    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541546/stas-receptionist": {
      "title": "STAS Receptionist",
      "description": "STAS Receptionist Apply now Job no: 541546 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 19140000 - EG-COMPUTER - INFO SCI & ENG Classification Title: STAS Front Desk Receptionist Classification Minimum Requirements: High School Diploma or equivalent. Dependable, punctual, and able to maintain a consistent work schedule. Current enrollment as an undergrad student in good academic standing. Must have the availability to work Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30. Job Description: Serves as the primary front-desk contact for the CISE administrative office, managing phones, greeting visitors, and responding to general inquiries. Oversees daily reception operations, including message handling, mail and package distribution, and maintaining an organized and professional front-desk area. Provides administrative and logistical support for departmental activities by assisting with events, creating flyers, maintaining conference rooms, managing office supplies, and supporting faculty and staff with office equipment. Maintains accurate directories and mail assignments, monitors and restocks supplies, and assists with departmental asset tracking as needed $15 per hour Required Qualifications: High School Diploma or equivalent. Dependable, punctual, and able to maintain a consistent work schedule. Current enrollment as an undergrad student in good academic standing. Must have the availability to work Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30. Preferred: Strong verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to interact professionally with students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Demonstrated reliability, punctuality, and ability to follow established procedures. Basic computer proficiency, including familiarity with Microsoft Office. Proficiency with basic office technology, including phones, printers, copiers and scanners. Ability to work independently while also collaborating effectively with administrative staff. Special Instructions to Applicants: Must have availability Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30 Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541548/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541548 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Applicants must have experience with manuscript preparation and submission and must be comfortable working with large animals (training will be provided upon hire). Applicants must also demonstrate the ability and willingness to mentor and manage graduate students. Experience with grant writing and prior formal mentoring of students is desirable. Job Description: The Orthopaedic Biomedical Engineering (OrthoBME) Laboratory is seeking a post-doctoral fellow to help lead an observational clinical study on osteoarthritic pain in veterinary equine populations. Osteoarthritis (OA) is common in horses, and like human OA, equine OA is often idiopathic, develops over years, and shows a poor match between the severity of joint damage and the severity of symptoms. These parallels make naturally-occurring equine OA a powerful population for developing pain measures that can translate to humans. As such, our study is being conducted entirely in privately-owned equine patients with naturally occurring fetlock OA, where the overall goal of the study is to determine the best and most reliable measures of naturally-occurring OA pain in horses. Following enrollment visits conducted by the UF College of Veterinary Medicine, our assessment team travels to the equine patient (farms and ranches near Gainesville) and then performs on-site measurements of neural processing, autonomic nervous system function, activity, and gait mechanics, among other physiologic tests. We are seeking a post-doctoral fellow to join this NIH-funded project, helping our team establish reliable and valid behavioral measures of OA pain in horses. Using psychometric statistical approaches, our team will then evaluate the reliability and validity of these measures and compare them with data from ongoing human OA pain studies at the UF Pain Research and Intervention Center of Excellence. Again, testing is conducted at the horses' housing sites in the Gainesville-Ocala area, in partnership with the UF College of Veterinary Medicine. The OrthoBME lab is focused on understanding how chronic joint degeneration leads to chronic pain and disability. To achieve this goal, our team works with samples and data collected from rodent models, large animal veterinary patients, and human patients ( www.orthobme.com ). A key tenet of our laboratory environment is team-based science. We feature a highly collaborative environment and often take on ambitious projects that integrate the skills and abilities of multiple scientific fields. As such, strong communication skills, willingness to learn, and the ability to work with and mentor others are highly important for all members of our team. The post-doctoral fellow will serve as a day-to-day scientific leader for this project and will work closely with a team of graduate students. Responsibilities will include coordinating study activities and timelines, mentoring graduate students in data collection and analysis, supporting the training and calibration of study personnel, and helping maintain data quality and protocol standardization. Ideally, the post-doctoral fellow will also lead the quantitative sensory testing (QST) portion of the project, adapting human QST protocols to the horse. Prior experience with QST is not required; training will be provided. This position is also an opportunity to expand the post-doctoral fellows own research program into veterinary populations, and as such, the post-doctoral fellow will be encouraged to develop independent research directions, contribute to manuscripts and grant proposals, and build skills for a career leading a research team. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering (UF BME) is housed in a state-of-the-art building that is located in the medical school and steps from engineering. The University of Florida is one of only a few comprehensive universities, having medical, veterinary, dental, nursing, public health, and engineering disciplines all co-localized on the same, contiguous campus. UF is a highly collaborative environment that provides researchers with many opportunities for learning (e.g., top seminar programs, excellent core research facilities) and research collaborations. Gainesville is located in the northern region of Florida, within 1-1.5 hours of each coast, and about 2 hours to Orlando and Tampa. It is a small to medium-sized city with a low cost of living, excellent public and private schools, and southern hospitality. While Gainesville is widely recognized as the home of the Gators, it is quickly becoming known as a center for innovation and a place with a lifestyle that's comfortable for families, yet attractive for young professionals. Further, the UF BME partners with many local research centers and institutes including the McKnight Brain Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory, and the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center. Expected Salary: $62,000-$70,000, depending on experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Applicants must have experience with manuscript preparation and submission and must be comfortable working with large animals (training will be provided upon hire). Applicants must also demonstrate the ability and willingness to mentor and manage graduate students. Experience with grant writing and prior formal mentoring of students is desirable. Preferred: Above all, we are looking for a colleague with strong team management and mentoring skills who is excited to work with graduate students and help them succeed. We are also looking for someone with an interest in expanding their research to large animals, and, ideally, an interest in performing quantitative sensory testing, as described above. Prior experience with large animals, pain research, sensory testing, behavioral assessment, or biomechanics would be useful but is not required, as we are able to train these skills in new staff. For this position, applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. The field of study is less important than a willingness to work with large animals, to interact with patient/clients of the UF College of Veterinary Medicine, and to mentor and lead a team of graduate students. A high-degree of professionalism during the on-site visits is required. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Special Instructions to Applicants: In addition to applying online to Careers at UF ( https://jobs.ufl.edu/ ), applicants send a CV, a brief statement of interest, and contact information for two references to Dr. Allen at kyle.allen@bme.ufl.edu . Review of applications will start immediately and continue until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541549/assistant-professor-in-the-field-of-population-health": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the field of Population Health",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in the field of Population Health Apply now Job no: 541549 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16920000 - LS-SOCIOLOGY CRIMINOLOGY&LAW Classification Title: Assistant Professor Job Description: The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing position beginning August 16, 2027, at the rank of Assistant Professor with specialization in population health broadly defined. We seek scholars whose research examines the social, economic, political, environmental, and/or demographic factors that shape population health outcomes and health inequalities. Applicants’ research programs may focus on areas including, but not limited to, health disparities and inequities; aging and health; family and health; mental health; disability; reproductive health; health behaviors; environmental influences on health; social epidemiology; or the consequences of social stratification for health and well-being. Researchers employing all types of methodological approaches are encouraged to apply. We seek applicants whose records show commitment to rigorous scholarship with the promise of external funding, as well as excellence in teaching. The ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students is highly desirable. The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law is a dynamic department with over 350 undergraduate Sociology majors and 46 Sociology graduate students. The Department has two undergraduate programs, sociology and criminology, both offered via in-person and online courses. We also offer minors in Sociology of Social Justice & Policy and Environmental Justice & Policy. Faculty conduct research in diverse areas, including criminology and criminal justice; psychology and law; families and gender; health, aging, and the life course; environment and resources; and other areas. Our commitment to methodological rigor and innovation is reflected by our investment in the department’s International Ethnography Lab and the Computational Sociology and Criminology Lab. Many faculty are affiliated with other academic programs at UF, including the School of Natural Resources and the Environment, the Center for Latin American Studies, the Center for European Studies, the Department of Gender, Sexuality and Women’s Studies, and the Health Science Center. More information about the Department is available at https://soccrim.clas.ufl.edu/ , including biographical information about our faculty, students, and staff; and a description of the various projects and research interests in which our members are involved. UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked Number 1 public university in the United States by Wall Street Journal in its “2024 Best Colleges in the U.S.” report with exceptional research and teaching. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience and includes a full benefits package. Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families, and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, maternity/paternity leave, and leave payouts. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Demonstrated expertise in population health, health disparities, social epidemiology, demography, medical sociology, aging, health policy, or related areas. Preferred: An established record of publication Evidence of rigorous scholarship and the potential to obtain external funding Evidence of excellence in teaching and the ability to mentor graduate students Ability to contribute to diverse theoretical viewpoints and open academic exchanges through scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, experience, and suitability for the position. (2) a complete curriculum vitae . (3) a statement on teaching and research goals. (4) the names and email addresses of three references. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning October 26, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541550/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541550 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Job Description: Job description/Research Training and Responsibilities : A full-time postdoctoral position is available to study immuno-engineering and develop novel, biomaterial-based immunotherapeutics is available in the laboratory of Dr. Jamal Lewis ( https://www.ufllewislab.com/ ) . This position is for an initial one-year appointment and has the potential to be renewed for an additional year based on performance and funding availability. We seek an enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to advance a novel platform of immune‑modulatory biomaterial particles designed to attenuate aberrant immune responses in rheumatoid arthritis. The candidate will work beside materials scientists, chemists, and immunologists on particle synthesis, in‑vitro and in‑vivo validation, and translational pathway design. For this highly collaborative position, the successful applicant will be expected to work closely with other faculty in the Biomedical Engineering department, and researchers at UF College of Medicine and the UF College of Pharmacy. The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Preference will be given to applicants with demonstrated expertise using various biomedical imaging modalities, small animal procedures, and those who have a strong track record as graduate students as evidenced by their published work. Attributes of UF and Gainesville : The BME Department is housed in a state-of-the art building that is located in the medical school and steps from engineering. The University of Florida is one of only a few comprehensive universities, having medical, veterinary, dental, nursing, public health, and engineering disciplines all co-localized on the same, contiguous campus. UF is a highly collaborative environment that provides researchers with many opportunities for learning (e.g., top seminar programs, excellent core research facilities) and research collaborations. Gainesville is located in the northern region of Florida, within 1-1.5 hours of each coast, and just 1.5-2 hours to Orlando and Tampa. It is a small to medium-sized city with a low cost of living, excellent public and private schools, and southern hospitality. UF BME and the IMBL : The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering is one of only a few departments in the nation to be co-localized with a top-ranked medical school, veterinary school and dental school. The department is housed in a state-of-the-art building that is co-located with the medical school and steps from engineering. The IMBL is housed in the brand new, Wertheim Engineering Building (opened in 2020), just a few steps away from the Reitz Union (the center of campus). Biomedical Engineering at UF partners with many local research centers and institutes including the McKnight Brain Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory and the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center. Expected Salary: $60,000.00 Required Qualifications: The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Preferred: Preference will be given to applicants with demonstrated expertise using various biomedical imaging modalities, small animal procedures, and those who have a strong track record as graduate students as evidenced by their published work. Special Instructions to Applicants: In addition to applying online to Careers at UF, Applicants should send a CV, a brief statement of interest, and contact information for three references to Dr. Jamal Lewis at jamalslewis@ufl.edu . Review of applications will start immediately and continue until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541553/ops-lab-field-technician-blueberry-breeding-and-genomics-lab": {
      "title": "OPS Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab)",
      "description": "OPS Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab) Apply now Job no: 541553 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Agricultural Sciences, Agriculture Operations Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: OPS-Spons Prjs Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency Commitment to the time requirements of this position Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season No bee or bug allergies Job Description: The UF Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab is currently seeking highly motivated individuals to assist the Blueberry Breeding Program with basic molecular work and field activities related to the program. These activities will take place in both the molecular lab and our field trial locations starting now until May 2027, with the possibility of extending the position upon performance. Essential Duties and Responsibilities for Field: Be available to work in 8-hour shifts Weekdays 8:00AM – 5:00PM Conduct basic molecular work, such as DNA extraction, PCR, gel electrophoresis, gene cloning and transformation Work outdoors or greenhouse in various weather conditions Assist with the collection of field samples and breeding seasonal work Load and unload field sample equipment and coolers from the truck bed Collect and document data accurately Be flexible with sudden changes to the schedule Expected Time Commitment: 20-40 hours/week Expected Salary: $15/Hour Required Qualifications: Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency Commitment to the time requirements of this position Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season No bee or bug allergies Preferred: Experience in molecular lab and field work Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541555/ops-student-assistant-lab-field-technician-blueberry-breeding-and-genomics-lab": {
      "title": "OPS Student Assistant - Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab)",
      "description": "OPS Student Assistant - Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab) Apply now Job no: 541555 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: STU AST - NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: · Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency · Commitment to the time requirements of this position · Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season · No bee or bug allergies Job Description: The UF Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab is currently seeking highly motivated individuals to assist the Blueberry Breeding Program with basic molecular work and field activities related to the program. These activities will take place in both the molecular lab and our field trial locations starting now until May 2027, with the possibility of extending the position upon performance. Essential Duties and Responsibilities for Field: · Be available to work in 8-hour shifts Weekdays 8:00AM – 5:00PM · Conduct basic molecular work, such as DNA extraction, PCR, gel electrophoresis, gene cloning and transformation · Work outdoors or greenhouse in various weather conditions · Assist with the collection of field samples and breeding seasonal work · Load and unload field sample equipment and coolers from the truck bed · Collect and document data accurately · Be flexible with sudden changes to the schedule Expected Time Commitment: 20-31 hours/week Expected Salary: $15.00/hour Required Qualifications: · Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency · Commitment to the time requirements of this position · Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season · No bee or bug allergies Preferred: · Experience in molecular lab and field work Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538108/astasofull-prof-cardiovascular-medicine": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiovascular Medicine",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiovascular Medicine Apply now Job no: 538108 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050400 - MD-CARDIOLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Cardiovascular Medicine Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Medicine, and Division of Cardiovascular Medicine is seeking a tenure/non-tenure, Assistant/Associate/Full Professor with innovative leadership and good interpersonal skills to become an active and integral member of the Division of Cardiology, Section of Heart Failure. Rank and Tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. The University of Florida has a long track record of being a national leader in advanced heart failure. With a celebrated transplant and mechanical circulatory support history, combined with unique opportunities for research and teaching, ours is a premiere academic program. We are seeking a dynamic physician to join our growing team. Applicants should have a record of distinguished accomplishments and proficiency in clinical duties, administration, and clinical investigation to include mentoring new physicians, fostering a progressive and dynamic work environment, and promoting teaching and research. Responsibilities will include heart failure services at the UF Health Shands Hospital at the University of Florida. Clinical duties will include performance and teaching in heart failure services. Coverage of inpatient heart failure services and general cardiology services as well as outpatient clinical duties and other duties as assigned. Individuals with expertise in Infiltrative Cardiomyopathies, Cardio Oncology, or Inheritable Cardiomyopathies are encouraged to apply. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have a medical degree and Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applications are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541145/ufhealth-physician": {
      "title": "UFHealth Physician",
      "description": "UFHealth Physician Apply now Job no: 541145 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Marion Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050604 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-OTHER Classification Title: UFHealth Physician Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes, plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of an accredited fellowship in Hematology and Oncology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology fellowship program may substitute for the required experience. Job Description: Provides comprehensive hematology and oncology patient care, including the evaluation, diagnosis, treatment, coordination of care, preventive care, and ongoing management of patients with hematologic and oncologic conditions in the outpatient clinic and infusion suite. Oversees the clinical care of patients receiving treatment in the outpatient infusion suite and/or clinic, including assessing treatment readiness, reviewing laboratory results, managing treatment related complications, and collaborating with nursing and pharmacy staff to facilitate the safe delivery of chemotherapy, immunotherapy, anti-neoplastic treatment, blood products, and supportive care. Works collaboratively with members of the multidisciplinary healthcare team, including pharmacists, advanced practice providers, nurses, social workers, and other support staff to coordinate comprehensive patient care. Orders, reviews and interprets diagnostic reports, including laboratory studies, pathology, imaging, and other diagnostic tests, and follows up in a timely manner. Prescribes chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapies, blood products, supportive medications, and other treatment regimens as appropriate to the patient's diagnosis and clinical condition. Develops individualized treatment plans aligned with clinical and institutional pathways and refers patients to appropriate specialists, clinical services, supportive care programs, and community resources as needed. Directs and coordinates the patient care activities of nursing, infusion, and support staff as appropriate. Follows established departmental policies, procedures, quality improvement initiatives, patient safety standards, regulatory and accreditation requirements, and infection prevention practices. Participates in tumor boards, multidisciplinary conferences, peer review, quality assurance activities, provider meetings, and other clinical and operational meetings as requested. Assists in the development, implementation, and updating of clinical protocols, treatment pathways, and standards of practice as requested. Identify, consider and enroll (or refer), when appropriate for clinical trials to support optimal cancer patient care. Engage in lifelong learning and demonstrate high-quality care delivery and decision making. Support an environment of inquiry, excellence, and collaboration. May manage the daily clinical operations of a hematology and oncology practice or patient care program. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE Monday through Friday, 7:30 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. Extended work hours may be required based on clinic, infusion suite, and patient care needs. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes, plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of an accredited fellowship in Hematology and Oncology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology fellowship program may substitute for the required experience. Preferred: Ambulatory clinical experience providing care to adult hematology and oncology patients. Experience managing patients receiving chemotherapy, immunotherapy, transfusions, and other systemic or supportive therapies. Experience participating in or leading a multidisciplinary oncology care team Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541156/assistant-director-of-accreditation-housestaff": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Accreditation - Housestaff",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Accreditation - Housestaff Apply now Job no: 541156 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 29012600 - MD-HOUSESTAFF Classification Title: AST DIR, Education/Training Programs Job Description: The Assistant Director of Accreditation provides operational leadership for the accreditation and continuous quality improvement functions of the University of Florida College of Medicine Graduate Medical Education (GME) Office. Reporting to the GME Director of Administration, this position manages a major segment of the institution's education and training operations and supports the oversight of ACGME-accredited residency and fellowship programs, including ACGME Non-Standard Training (NST) programs, within a large, complex academic medical center. The Assistant Director serves as a institutional resource for accreditation standards, program review, policy implementation, and program improvement. The position translates accreditation requirements into practical guidance, coordinates institutional review and monitoring activities, supervises assigned professional and support staff, and partners with program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME leadership to promote consistent and sustainable compliance. As part of the GME leadership team, the Assistant Director is cross-trained in the Director's core accreditation responsibilities and provides leadership continuity for assigned functions when delegated or during the Director's absence. About This Role: Accreditation Oversight and Program Consultation Direct day-to-day accreditation support and consultation for assigned GME programs and institutional functions. Interpret and apply ACGME Institutional, Common Program, and specialty-specific requirements; provide timely, consistent guidance to program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME staff. Manage routine accreditation monitoring activities, including review of program letters, citations, Areas for Improvement, accreditation statuses, action plans, and required institutional follow-up. Monitor institutional compliance with Program Letters of Agreement (PLAs) and provide oversight of the PLA process, including review of status reports, identification and escalation of gaps or expired agreements, and verification that designated staff and programs complete required actions. Provide institutional support and operational oversight for ACGME NST programs, including interpretation of applicable requirements, ongoing compliance monitoring, and guidance to program and department leadership. Develop and maintain accreditation resources, templates, calendars, standard operating procedures, and communication tools that support consistent institutional practices. Identify emerging compliance risks, recommend corrective strategies, and escalate significant or systemic concerns to the Director, Associate DIO, and DIO as appropriate. Serve as a liaison between the GME Office, clinical departments, program leadership, and institutional stakeholders on assigned accreditation matters. Program Review, Applications, and Site Visit Readiness Coordinates complex program review and accreditation-readiness activities from planning through follow-up. Coordinate institutional preparation for ACGME institutional and program site visits, focused reviews, self-studies, and other accreditation-related reviews. Review draft new program applications, complement increases, participating site additions, program changes, and related submissions for completeness, internal consistency, and alignment with institutional expectations. Assist with the development, institutional review, and submission of new ACGME NST program applications and renewals, and coordinate preparation for NST-related site visits, reviews, and required follow-up. Provide structured consultation to programs preparing responses to citations, requests for progress reports, adverse actions, or other ACGME communications. Conduct targeted audits of accreditation records and program documentation; track findings, responsible parties, deadlines, and completion of corrective actions. Maintain readiness materials and standardized processes that allow the GME Office to respond efficiently to planned and unplanned accreditation activity. Committee Operations and Institutional Governance Lead or support GME committee processes that advance institutional oversight and regulatory compliance. Manage the operational work of assigned GMEC subcommittees, including agenda development, document review, presentation of findings, action tracking, and maintenance of an auditable record. Support the Accreditation Requirements Review Committee (ARRC) and other assigned committees; chair meetings or workgroups when delegated by the Director. Oversee the operational work of the NST Committee, including application review, agenda and meeting coordination, documentation of decisions, action tracking, and communication with programs and institutional leadership. Prepare accreditation analyses, recommendations, and decision materials for GMEC and GME leadership. Partner with the Director in developing, reviewing, and implementing institutional GME policies and procedures. Ensure assigned committee actions and approvals are completed within applicable institutional and ACGME timelines. Data Monitoring and Continuous Quality Improvement Use institutional and program-level data to support early identification of risk and continuous program improvement. Monitor ACGME survey results, milestones, evaluation data, work hour information, accreditation trends, and other indicators for patterns requiring program or institutional action. Develop dashboards, reports, and concise leadership summaries that translate accreditation data into actionable recommendations. Support the Institutional Program Review Committee (IPRC), Annual Program Evaluation oversight, Special Review processes, and other institutional quality improvement activities. Establish performance measures for assigned accreditation functions and evaluate the timeliness, consistency, and effectiveness of processes. Collaborate with GME leadership, analysts, and institutional partners to improve data quality, reporting, documentation, and governance. Education, Training, and Resource Development Design and deliver education that strengthens accreditation knowledge and operational consistency across the institution. Develop and deliver onboarding, workshops, written guidance, and continuing education for program directors, associate program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME staff. Assess learning and resource needs and revise educational offerings based on accreditation changes, recurring findings, stakeholder feedback, and institutional priorities. Translate complex regulatory information into clear, practical tools and examples for varied audiences. Promote shared learning and dissemination of effective accreditation and program-management practices across programs. Leadership, Supervision, and Operational Continuity Provide direct leadership for assigned staff and contributes to the strategic and operational management of the GME Office. Supervise assigned managers, professionals, and support staff; establish priorities, assign work, provide coaching, evaluate performance, and support professional development. Participate as a member of the GME management team and contribute to strategic planning for accreditation, education, and program quality functions. Manage special projects and cross-functional initiatives delegated by the Director, Associate DIO, or DIO. Maintain cross-training and working knowledge of the Director's core accreditation portfolio; assume delegated authority for defined functions and provide operational leadership during planned absences or leadership transitions. Represent the institution at regional or national GME accreditation meetings as delegated Foster a collaborative, service-oriented, accountable, and inclusive work environment. We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! Expected Salary: $82,000- $90,000 annually Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience, or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of appropriate experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Master's degree in education, health professions education, higher education administration, healthcare administration, public health, business administration, or a related field. Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in graduate medical education, academic medicine, accreditation, education administration, or a related regulatory environment. Demonstrated knowledge of ACGME Institutional, Common Program, and specialty-specific requirements and experience supporting program or institutional accreditation activities. Experience coordinating accreditation reviews, new program applications, site visits, Special Reviews, Annual Program Evaluations, or responses to accreditation findings. Supervisory or team-lead experience, including prioritizing work, coaching staff, and managing performance. Experience using a residency management system such as New Innovations and accreditation systems such as the ACGME Accreditation Data System. Experience analyzing educational or compliance data and developing reports, dashboards, presentations, or recommendations for leadership. Strong consultation, writing, presentation, project management, judgment, and relationship-building skills in a complex, decentralized organization. Ability to manage multiple high-priority deadlines, exercise discretion with sensitive information, and communicate regulatory expectations clearly and diplomatically. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541428/biological-scientist-ii": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist II",
      "description": "Biological Scientist II Apply now Job no: 541428 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Orange Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60870000 - AG-MREC-APOPKA Classification Title: Biological Scientist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Job Description: Plant Breeding and Germplasm Management -Plan, modify designs, and implement breeding nurseries, field trials, greenhouse experiments, and controlled-environment experiments. Assist with the development, maintenance, evaluation, and advancement of germplasm. Driving to site locations will be required. Molecular and Genomic Research -Perform molecular biology procedures, including DNA/RNA extraction, PCR, genotyping, and related laboratory techniques. Perform sequencing, QTL mapping, GWAS, genomic prediction, and other quantitative-genetic analyses. Research Program Coordination -Coordinate day-to-day research activities with faculty PI, graduate students, undergraduate researchers. Maintain laboratory, greenhouse, field, and research equipment. Train and supervise students, technicians, and other research personnel. Data Management and Analysis -Maintain, organize research records and databases. Assist with statistical analysis and visualization of breeding and experimental data. Other -Collaborate with multidisciplinary research teams and contribute to grant-funded research objecti Expected Salary: $53,000; commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. A valid State of Florida Driver’s License is required. Preferred: PhD’s degree in plant breeding, genetics, horticulture, agronomy, or specialty-crop research. Experience with controlled crosses, plant propagation, seed handling, and breeding population management. Experience conducting field or greenhouse experiments. Experience with DNA extraction, PCR, genotyping, or other molecular biology techniques. Experience with plant phenotyping and image-based data collection. Experience using R, Python, or other statistical/data-analysis software. Experience working with QTL mapping, GWAS, genomic selection, genomic prediction, or other quantitative-genetic approaches. Good inter-personal skills and demonstrated ability to work in an interdisciplinary environment Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541500/postdoctoral-associate-developmental-programming-of-metabolic-disease": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Developmental Programming of Metabolic Disease",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Developmental Programming of Metabolic Disease Apply now Job no: 541500 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29060000 - MD-MOLECULAR GENTCS - MICROBIO Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant biomedical discipline. Job Description: The Karst laboratory in the Department of Molecular Genetics and Microbiology at the University of Florida College of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral associate to join a newly funded NIH R01 project investigating how the early-life environment programs long-term metabolic health. Our laboratory recently discovered that maternal bile acids delivered during lactation act as developmental signals that regulate neonatal growth through the bile acid receptor TGR5. Remarkably, transient manipulation of this pathway during lactation has lasting consequences: offspring exposed to reduced maternal milk bile acids are protected from diet-induced obesity, insulin resistance, hyperinsulinemia, and fatty liver in adulthood. The successful candidate will investigate the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying this developmental programming. Current studies focus on how bile acid signaling during early postnatal life establishes adipose tissue architecture and function, including effects on adipocyte progenitor populations and their developmental trajectories. Approaches will include mouse models, flow cytometry and cell sorting, lineage tracing, metabolic phenotyping, and single-cell genomic and epigenomic approaches. This is a new and rapidly developing research direction in the laboratory, providing substantial opportunity for the successful candidate to contribute intellectually to the project, develop independent questions, and shape its future direction. We are looking for a scientist who is curious, rigorous, collaborative, and interested in understanding how transient exposures during critical developmental windows can produce lifelong changes in physiology and disease susceptibility. Expected Salary: $58,656 - $63,900 and is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant biomedical discipline. Preferred: Candidates should have a strong background in biomedical or biological sciences and demonstrated experience conducting rigorous, hypothesis-driven research. Preferred applicants will have experience with molecular, cellular, or in vivo experimental approaches relevant to the project. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position and all applicants MMUST apply through Careers at UF at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. Search for posting #541500. In order to be considered, you must upload references, research interests, and resume/CV. A Criminal Background check is required, and this is not a remote work position. It is located on the University of Florida main campus in Gainesville, FL. We Offer Exceptional Benefits: • Low-cost State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance • Life and Disability Insurance • Generous Retirement Options to secure your future • Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages: (includes over 10 paid holidays, as well as paid family, sick, and vacation leave) • Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities: Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more • Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program • Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer To learn more about Gainesville and the surrounding areas, please visit the Guide to Greater Gainesville, which shares information about our neighborhoods, schools, zoning, culture, leisure, and more at https://guidetogreatergainesville.com/. Gainesville is a vibrant city with a low cost of living, diverse population, excellent public schools, Division I NCAA sports, and no state income tax. The area is known for its nature parks, bike trails, sporting events, museums, mild winters, and warm summers. Being centrally located, Gainesville is easily situated for a day trip to beautiful beaches along the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico, as well as to theme parks like Disney World. Other nearby cities include Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at https://www.visitgainesville.com/. To learn more about the University of Florida (UF), please visit https://www.ufl.edu/. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter describing their research interests and career goals, CV, and contact information for three references to skarst@ufl.edu. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (EDT) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541509/assistantassociate-professor-extension-agronomy": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor – Extension Agronomy",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor – Extension Agronomy Apply now Job no: 541509 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Gadsden Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60770000 - AG-NFREC-QUINCY Classification Title: Ast/Aso Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month tenure-accruing position that will be 40% research (Florida Agricultural Experiment Station) and 60% Extension (Florida Cooperative Extension Service), available in the North Florida Research and Education Center (NFREC), Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida. Tenure will accrue in the Agronomy Department . This assignment may change in accordance with the needs of the unit. The NFREC is one of the largest and the most diverse units of UF/IFAS, consisting of research and education campuses in Quincy, Marianna, and Live Oak, Florida. NFREC has faculty members representing 8 academic departments and the School of Forest, Fisheries, and Geomatics Sciences, 5 emeritus faculty members and a dynamic population of graduate students, post-doctoral associates, and scientific/farm/administrative staff. This variety of faculty, staff and programs provides a unique setting for interdisciplinary work within the NFREC campuses, with colleagues on the main UF campus and faculty at other Centers around the state. The incumbent is expected to develop a dynamic integrated research and Extension program focusing on the primary agronomic cropping systems in North Florida including peanut, cotton, corn, and soybean. This requires integral knowledge of whole plant response to agronomic practices from planting until harvest, as well as developing good understanding of off-season management options. Expertise in variety selection, planting methods and rates, irrigation, and cropping systems are essential for the successful candidate. Activities dealing with optimizing crop management and production, digital agriculture, alternative rotational and emerging crops, winter crops, and integrated crop livestock systems are potential research and Extension avenues. Future-focused skills in the use of artificial intelligence (AI) and/or machine learning to collect and integrate agronomic and environmental data towards specific objectives including the investigation of advanced technologies for optimization of crop inputs and cropping systems are encouraged. The incumbent is expected to develop an internationally recognized program while meeting the specific needs of the region. The successful candidate will develop collaborative projects with supporting UF/IFAS research and Extension faculty located at main campus and across North Florida including agronomists, plant pathologists, entomologists, breeders, economists, and scientists in water quality, soil science, as needed. The faculty member will actively seek contract and grant funding to support his/her program. The significant appointment in Extension means that the incumbent is expected to participate in Florida’s Cooperative Extension Strategic Initiatives and become a strong leader and resource for County Agents and Regional Specialized Agents across Florida’s northern tier who work in agronomic crops, by providing regular In-Service Training, writing Extension documents (EDIS articles), publishing in newsletters, etc. The incumbent will be expected to interact with stakeholders, including producers, industry representatives, commodity groups, and other clientele to further the UF/IFAS mission. The successful candidate will participate actively in undergraduate and graduate education by chairing and serving on graduate student committees, supervising thesis, dissertation and undergraduate research, and publishing research results with students. Faculty are encouraged to participate in professional development activities related to teaching and advising and may teach courses and seminars. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in Agronomy, Crop Science or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should have demonstrated skills in verbal and written communication, interpersonal relationships, and procurement of extramural funding. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Previous experience in Extension in a land-grant university setting Demonstrated ability to develop a robust educational program for stakeholder groups including the use of traditional and multimedia tools. An established network of collaborators in other states in agronomic crop systems. Demonstrated ability to work in a multidisciplinary, collaborative team environment. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by November 27, 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. Employment Conditions This position is available February 1, 2027 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. Nominee information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541509 Dr. Cheryl Mackowiak Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida North Florida Research and Education Center 155 Research Rd. Quincy, FL 32351 Telephone: 850-875-7126 Electronic Mail: echo13@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation from a professional perspective Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. NFREC-Quincy is situated about 20-30 minutes’ drive west of Tallahassee, Florida. The area is rich in natural resources, such as state parks, lakes, rivers, and forests. Entertainment and housing options range from rural to metropolitan with many events, restaurants, and social opportunities available. The area is near to some of the Gulf Coast’s best beaches for swimming, fishing and boating. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541519/the-agency-administrative-services-manager": {
      "title": "The Agency Administrative Services Manager",
      "description": "The Agency Administrative Services Manager Apply now Job no: 541519 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Human Resources, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 23140100 - CJC-THE AGENCY-ADMIN Classification Title: MGR, Administrative Services Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications — The Agency Bring Your Organizational Leadership to The Agency If you're a strategic organizer, collaborative problem-solver, and people-focused professional who enjoys turning complex operations into seamless experiences, we invite you to apply for The Agency Administrative Services Manager position at the University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications. The University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications is seeking The Agency Administrative Services Manager to provide operational and administrative leadership for The Agency, a dynamic environment where students gain hands-on experience working on real-world communications projects. The Agency Administrative Services Manager will oversee the administrative functions that support The Agency's day-to-day success. This position will manage office operations and physical space, coordinate student staff operations and human resources activities, oversee financial and personnel processes, support events and external communications, and serve as a central point of contact for students, clients, university partners, and other stakeholders. The ideal candidate is a highly organized and collaborative professional who can manage competing priorities, solve problems, coordinate resources, and keep complex operations running smoothly. This role is well suited for someone who enjoys working with people, supporting students, managing details, and contributing to a creative, goal-oriented team. What You'll Do Lead student staff operations by recruiting, hiring, onboarding, scheduling, supervising, and managing performance; develop staffing plans and allocate student resources based on project needs, skills, and capacity. Coordinate HR and payroll functions for student employees, including timekeeping, appointments, scheduling, budgets, and personnel matters, in partnership with the College HR team. Manage financial and administrative operations, including account reconciliation, invoices, reimbursements, travel expenses, purchasing, requisitions, encumbrances, and office supply needs. Support students and serve as a central point of contact for The Agency by coordinating immersion course registration, connecting students with academic resources, managing calendars and inquiries, and liaising with College departments, university partners, clients, and external stakeholders. Oversee Agency records, workspace, meetings, and events by maintaining organized files and physical space, coordinating stakeholder meetings and events, and supporting efficient day-to-day operations and workflows. Why Join The Agency? This is an opportunity to play a critical role in an environment where education, creativity, communications, and real-world experience come together. You will work closely with students, faculty, staff, clients, and university partners while helping create the operational foundation that allows creative teams to succeed. The Agency Administrative Services Manager will have the opportunity to make a meaningful impact by developing student talent, strengthening operational processes, coordinating resources, and supporting innovative communications work within one of the nation's leading journalism and communications programs. The Agency at UF, Marketing and Communications Agency Work Schedule Monday–Friday | 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. Eastern Time Schedule flexibility may be available based on business needs and with approval from the Assistant Director of Operations. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Strong project coordination, organizational, and time-management skills, with exceptional attention to detail and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Ability to thrive in a fast-paced, goal-oriented, and collaborative environment while effectively managing competing priorities and stakeholder needs. Experience supervising staff and supporting students, with the ability to coordinate resources, schedules, and cross-functional teams. Strong problem-solving, critical-thinking, decision-making, and communication skills, both written and verbal. Proficiency with Microsoft Outlook, Word, and Excel, with familiarity using project management tools such as Monday.com, Airtable, Asana, or Trello. Experience in resource planning, scheduling, or coordinating teams in a professional or academic setting; experience in communications, advertising, marketing, or a creative agency environment is a plus. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please submit your applications online via CAREER AT UF Applications must include: A cover letter detailing previous journalistic or public media experience. A current résumé. Name, email addresses, and phone numbers of three references. Any other supporting documentation that you deem relevant to the position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, creed, color, religion, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender identify and expression, marital status, national origin, political opinions or affiliations, genetic information and veteran status in also aspects of employment including recruitment, hiring, promotion, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits and training. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541523/director-of-advancement-george-a-smathers-libraries": {
      "title": "Director of Advancement, George A. Smathers Libraries",
      "description": "Director of Advancement, George A. Smathers Libraries Apply now Job no: 541523 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 55010600 - LB-LIB DEVELOPMENT-OB TRAVEL Classification Title: Director of Advancement Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Job Description: JOB SUMMARY Few areas of a university have the ability to impact students, faculty, researchers, and communities as broadly as their libraries. The George A. Smathers Libraries are seeking a Director of Advancement to lead the vision and strategy for a comprehensive philanthropy and external relations program. In partnership with the Interim Dean of University Libraries and UF Advancement leadership, the Director will drive major gift fundraising, cultivate meaningful donor relationships, oversee stewardship and volunteer engagement, and build a strong philanthropic pipeline in support of the Libraries’ strategic priorities. This is a unique opportunity for an accomplished advancement professional to shape a growing fundraising program and inspire philanthropic investment in the resources, collections, and initiatives that fuel discovery and learning across the university and beyond. RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Development Work closely with the Dean of Libraries and the Senior Assistant Vice President of Development to define, prioritize and develop plans to meet funding needs. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Within 18-24 months of the start date, develop a portfolio that will consistently raise $1.5 - $2 million per year or more in support of the George A. Smathers Libraries, averaging 120-160 personal visits annually and 10-15 gift solicitations per year of $25,000 or more, with emphasis on gifts of $100,000 or more. Build and manage a portfolio of major gift donors as well as manage a qualification pipeline. Serve as the primary fundraising strategist for Libraries, identifying, qualifying, and stewarding donor prospects up to the major gift level. Strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits with Special Collections donors, prospects, and friends. Actively identify and research alumni and friends in targeted areas; work with the SAVP to develop and implement strategy for securing gifts in these areas, including advancement plans to inform constituents in identified markets about the funding needs and gift opportunities. Actively build donor pipeline through in-person visits, volunteer engagement, and other strategies. Work closely with the Dean, SAVP and the Office of Estate & Gift Planning in identifying and qualifying prospects who have the potential of making a planned gift to Libraries. Actively qualify and manage major gift prospects in accordance with their interests, capacity, and propensity, consulting with the SAVP on philanthropic engagement strategy and key campus partnerships for collaboration. Work with University Initiatives to increase parent and family giving to Libraries. In collaboration with the Dean of University Libraries, develop solicitation strategies on case-by-case basis, being cognizant of development office and Library needs and procedures. Oversee all stewardship efforts for the Libraries. Coordinate stewardship efforts with colleges and support units as well as other colleagues within UF Advancement and University of Florida, as appropriate. Acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the vision, mission, and values of the Smathers Libraries and UF Advancement, ensuring the advancement team is providing timely recognition, appropriate and meaningful recognition. L eadership & Management Recruit, hire, retain and develop the Smathers Libraries Advancement team including a Development Coordinator and Assistant Director of Development. Provide leadership, counsel, and accountability on matters relating to fundraising, including establishing goals in alignment with library objectives, developing metrics and strategic planning. Provide ongoing leadership and direction to team members in building the Libraries’ donor pipeline through the qualification and solicitation of leadership annual giving donors to the Libraries. Lead the Libraries participation in university wide fundraising campaigns and initiatives. External Relations & Volunteer Management Implement a series of strategic events designed to promote Special Collections and other library priorities, to engage a larger audience and build a major gift pipeline. Oversee the design and execution of fundraising plans that would include special events, VIP and donor lunches and dinners, campus visits with the dean, catered events, Library tours with the dean, team retreats, and meetings and initiatives that promote the engagement of donors and friends. Develop support for the program by effectively involving stakeholders, such as volunteers, staff, and members of the Library Leadership Board, in efforts supporting the fundraising program. Understand and articulate the unique contributions of Smathers Libraries to education, research, and service on local, national, and international levels. Oversee and manage activities and public support programs and events for the Smathers Libraries. Coordinate development and alumni activities with Libraries’ public relations staff; assist with Libraries public relations and promotional activities. Attend and strategically engage and cultivate donors and prospects at events, providing effective follow-up. Provide leadership and direction to the Development Coordinator in the management of a successful Friends of the Libraries program to generate gifts between $1,000 and $25,000 that will generate unrestricted support for the library, steward and cultivate those closest to the library, and build a pipeline of new potential donors. Lead the advancement team in executing fundraising initiatives for the Smathers Friends of the Libraries and other donor groups, effectively building a pipeline of prospects to ensure future success of the fundraising programs. Engage with the UFA Annual Giving Office to ensure successful Friends of the Libraries prospect and pipeline management. Provide leadership and support strategic planning efforts as they relate to Smathers Friends of the Libraries. Administrative Responsible for effective and timely prospect tracking, record keeping, reporting and administration aligned with the metrics and reporting from UF Advancement. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Ensure best practices in office systems, practices, files and record keeping; ensure effective use of strategic tools and resources such as contact reports, prospect management, research, analysis, and the donor database. Assist in evaluating and analyzing programs using established attributes and metrics to produce accurate reports, to guide effective decision making. Supervise the Libraries’ Development Coordinator and, once hired, the Assistant Director of Development. Library Leadership Board Cultivate the Leadership Board, engaging a small group of individuals who can support at a high level or make significant connections on behalf of the library. With the Dean of University Libraries, plan, manage, and successfully execute all Library Leadership Board meetings, events, and activities; responsible for preparation of meeting materials and logistics. Engage with individual prospects, including Library Leadership Board members. Coordinate stewardship reports and activities for individual prospects including Library Leadership Board members with appropriate members of the administration. Develop support for Smathers Libraries by effectively involving stakeholders, including Library Leadership Board Members, and staff efforts supporting the fundraising program. Create a structured process for the identification, recruitment, evaluation, recognition, and replacement of volunteers to strengthen fundraising effectiveness. Collaborate with the Dean and the SAVP regarding overall gift strategies for Libraries Leadership Board members as appropriate. Expected Salary: $98,000 - $115,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer: Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Preferred: At least 4-6 years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual prospects with campaign experience. At least 4-6 years of external affairs experience with success in cultivating relationships with individuals and corporate prospects. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Proven experience leading a high performing team, including prior management of development officers. Track record of recruiting, developing, and retaining a high caliber staff, building a team, and creatively organizing and managing them to maximize the support of external constituencies. Proven track record of developing and implementing innovative fundraising strategies designed to maximize performance of a development team. This includes knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards; a demonstrated ability to construct, articulate, implement, and monitor an annual strategic development plan; and an excellent track record of entrepreneurship, innovation, and creative problem solving. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Business acumen and the ability to work collaboratively as a team player. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Process In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Apply by 11:55 pm (EST) on the application close date via the Jobs at UF online application system. Applications will be reviewed as received. If you have any questions or concerns about this process, please contact Tina Marie Litchfield, George A. Smathers Libraries Human Resources Office, at tlitchfield@uflib.ufl.edu . Work sponsorship is not available for this position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541524/assistant-director-of-bar-support": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Bar Support",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Bar Support Apply now Job no: 541524 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 24010000 - LW-DEANS OFFICE Classification Title: Program Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: A Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Program Manager of Bar Support helps provide strategic support for the Levin College of Law's academic success and bar readiness program, in collaboration with the Associate Dean for Academic Success and the Dean of Students, assisting in the assessment, enhancement, and ongoing development of program components to support students and graduates from matriculation through bar admission. The Program Manager works closely with faculty, administrators, staff, alumni, and external partners to identify and support students at risk of bar exam underperformance; helps design and deliver workshops, programs, and resources that promote student achievement, professional development, bar readiness, and licensure success; and supports the program's alignment with evolving accreditation standards and licensure requirements, including the NextGen bar exam. The Program Manager teaches academic success and/or bar preparation coursework, provides individualized advising and coaching to students and graduates, and contributes substantially to the structure and delivery of the Law School's bar readiness program. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Student Advising, Coaching & Bar Exam Preparation: The Program Manager provides individualized academic advising, coaching, and bar exam preparation support to law students and graduates, including those identified as at risk of underperformance. Working with faculty, administrators, staff, alumni, and external partners, the Program Manager helps identify students at risk of bar exam underperformance and connects them with appropriate resources and support. Teaching, Curriculum & Program Delivery The Program Manager teaches one or more academic success and/or bar preparation courses each year and designs and delivers workshops, seminars, and resources on study strategies, exam-taking skills, substantive bar subjects, and professional development. The Program Manager contributes substantially to the structure and delivery of the Law School's bar readiness program. Program Strategy, Data Analysis & Compliance The Program Manager assists in the strategic planning, implementation, and continuous improvement of the Law School's academic success and bar readiness program. This includes analyzing student performance data (e.g., grades, bar results, practice exam scores) to identify at-risk students and evaluate program effectiveness; monitoring developments in bar admission requirements, including the transition to the NextGen bar exam; helping ensure program alignment with accreditation standards; and maintaining accurate records and preparing reports on program outcomes. Collaboration, External Relations & Other Duties The Program Manager collaborates with faculty, administrators, and staff to integrate academic success principles into the broader curriculum, and builds and maintains relationships with alumni, bar preparation vendors, and other external partners. The College of Law is growing and changing daily, and all College of Law community members, including the Program Manager, are expected to perform other duties as assigned in furtherance of the Law School's academic success mission. Expected Salary: $90,000 minimum Required Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience Admission to a State Bar Preferred: J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school Florida Bar preferred, or willingness to obtain Demonstrated experience in academic support, bar preparation, legal education, or a closely related field. Strong understanding of bar exam content, format, and study methodologies Excellent teaching, coaching, and communication skills. Ability to work effectively and empathetically with a diverse student population Strong organizational skills and ability to manage multiple priorities Experience teaching in a law school academic success or bar preparation program Familiarity with the NextGen bar exam and current trends in bar licensure Experience analyzing student performance and outcomes data Experience developing curricula or programmatic content Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541525/visiting-assistant-professor-in-communication-design": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design Apply now Job no: 541525 Work type: Visiting Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 13020100 - COTA-ART-DIRECTOR Classification Title: VIS AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The School of Art + Art History in the College of the Arts at the University of Florida invites applications for a three-semester (Spring 2027, Fall 2027, Spring 2028) appointment as Visiting Assistant Professor of Communication Design. This position will contribute to a vibrant program with a forward-looking curriculum at a top public research university focused on cultivating research and the student experience. This position will teach undergraduate courses in the BFA and minor in Graphic Design, as well as our signature campus-wide UF Quest course on how design works as a tool for shaping, understanding, and communicating who we are in everyday life. Design studio courses focus on contemporary design practice and typically include design research, applying design principles to co-create solutions that meet users' needs, and interaction design. The Quest course is taught as a hybrid course, with students attending in-person sessions with a graduate student once per week to supplement asynchronous online content. As the lead instructor, this position’s role will be to manage the course, organize and deliver the asynchronous content, supervise and mentor graduate assistants, and design lesson plans for the in-person sessions. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists, designers, and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a changemaker. We seek a colleague who will position students as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities: Teach a 3-2 or 2-3 load in the Fall and Spring semesters We anticipate that 1–2 courses each year will be sections of UF Quest 1: Design + Identity in Everyday Life. We anticipate that the other courses will be upper-level courses in the BFA in the Graphic Design major or minor. Actively participate in our learning community, including finding innovative ways to connect design to critical discourse in other disciplines. Participate in shared governance through service to the design program, School, College, and University. SCHOOL OF ART + ART HISTORY: Organized within the College of the Arts, the School of Art + Art History nurtures a culture of critical inquiry in our scholarly and creative work. Our educational mission is to empower each student with knowledge, skills, and insight to engage thoughtfully with our changing world. The SA+AH believes that art, design, and scholarship are critical to our local, university, regional, national, and international communities. We pursue positive transformation and impact through socially engaged, local and global education, research, and creative works. Our community asks challenging questions, takes risks, and strives for excellence through an interdisciplinary, inclusive, and often collaborative practice. School of Art + Art History faculty publish, curate, and exhibit widely and internationally. They bring their scholarship and production into their teaching, offering innovative, engaged learning experiences to their students. We foster a diverse, equitable, and inclusive community through mutual respect and acceptance, assuming the best in others to enable a culture where everyone can flourish. Degree programs include the BA, BFA, MA, MFA, and PhD. Areas of study include art education, art history, design, museum studies, and studio art. The school has 400 undergraduate students enrolled in our majors and 190 graduate students in our residential programs and online art education MA. Also a part of the School of Art + Art History are the 4Most Gallery and the University Galleries—University Gallery, the Gary R. Libby Gallery, and Grinter Gallery — which provide exhibition space for contemporary art, including student work. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and the National Association of Schools of Art and Design. The art education program is accredited by NCATE. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/art . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. Nine-month faculty employees accrue approximately 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also gives you flexibility to handle life’s challenges with paid family leave: eight full weeks over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which can provide savings at various business and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: $70,000 annually with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following: Terminal degree (e.g., MFA, MDes, PhD) in Communication Design or closely related field. Experience as instructor of record in higher education beyond graduate student assistantship. Knowledge of contemporary design issues, conceptual approaches to design, design thinking, and design research. Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following: Evidence of success as an undergraduate instructor, preferably in a BFA or other bachelor’s program that prepares students for professional practice Experience teaching and/or professionally creating interaction design Experience with community-based projects that connect academic research with public interest, for example, co-design and/or participatory design projects. Evidence of a commitment to innovative and cross-disciplinary curricula. An active engagement with design research, creative work, or professional practice Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin October 13, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, submit all application documents by October 9, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the committee's and/or hiring authority's discretion. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: (1) a detailed cover letter that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a PDF portfolio (one file, 5 MB maximum) or a link to a website, which includes 10-15 examples of your creative research, scholarship, professional praxis, or related production, and a research statement; (4) a PDF teaching portfolio (one file, 5 MB maximum) or a link to a website, which includes a teaching philosophy and materials from 1–2 design courses a course syllabus, semester timeline, and 5–10 examples of student outcomes with assignment description(s); and (5) names and contact information of four professional references, among which one must be a staff member with whom you have worked in the past. The UF system will give you the option to upload letters of reference. We are not requiring letters of reference with initial application materials. Please ignore that option. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Professor Maria Rogal Chair, Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design Email: mrogal@ufl.edu Please keep in mind that search committee members, not AI or other software, review application materials. The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541528/provost-and-executive-vice-president-for-academic-affairs": {
      "title": "Provost and Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Provost and Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs Apply now Job no: 541528 Work type: Other Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 02010100 - PV-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Executive VP & Provost Classification Minimum Requirements: Must have a terminal degree and a strong academic record at the full-professor level, combined with substantial senior academic administrative experience. Job Description: Key Responsibilities This scholar-leader will combine distinguished academic credentials with exceptional institutional leadership in support of the following key responsibilities: Serve as the University’s chief academic officer and principal advisor to the President on academic matters. Develop, articulate, and execute a compelling academic vision aligned with UF’s mission, strategic priorities, and aspirations for national and international leadership. Work collaboratively with deans, faculty, academic leaders, students, and staff to establish university-wide academic priorities and translate them into measurable outcomes. Strengthen UF’s position as a premier public research university and enhance its academic reputation, impact, and competitiveness. Identify emerging opportunities for academic innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration, and institutional distinction. Recruit, retain, develop, and recognize a world-class faculty committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service. Champion excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, clinical practice, and public service. Strengthen faculty development, mentoring, leadership development, and career advancement. Ensure that promotion, tenure, evaluation, compensation, and recognition systems support academic excellence and institutional priorities. Foster a culture characterized by intellectual vitality, academic freedom, professional respect, transparency, collegiality, and shared responsibility. Work with the Board of Trustees, Florida Board of Governors, and other governing bodies on academic matters and regulatory requirements. Critical Leadership Capabilities The next Provost should demonstrate exceptional capability in the following areas: Visionary Academic Leadership: The ability to articulate a bold, coherent academic vision and translate that vision into priorities, investments, structures, and measurable results. Institutional Scale and Complexity: The capacity to lead across a large, comprehensive research university with wide-ranging academic disciplines, professional schools, health sciences, research enterprises, and external constituencies. Faculty Credibility and Trust: The intellectual standing and authenticity to earn the respect of faculty while engaging constructively with differing perspectives and maintaining strong standards of academic excellence. Strategic Resource Allocation: The ability to make difficult choices about people, programs, facilities, and financial resources while protecting academic quality and investing strategically in areas of opportunity. Talent Leadership: A demonstrated ability to identify, recruit, develop, retain, and empower exceptional faculty, deans, and academic leaders. Collaboration Across Boundaries: The ability to break down institutional silos and create productive partnerships among colleges, disciplines, research centers, health sciences, students, industry, government, and communities. Change Leadership: Organizational skills to lead significant changes while maintaining institutional stability, engaging stakeholders, communicating clearly, and preserving trust. Student-Centered Leadership: A genuine commitment to ensuring that academic excellence translates into exceptional educational experiences and outcomes for students at every level. Research and Innovation Leadership: The ability to identify emerging opportunities, make strategic investments, and position UF for greater research impact, innovation, entrepreneurship, and national leadership. Public and Land Grant University Stewardship: An appreciation for the distinctive responsibilities of a public land-grant university and its obligations to students, the state of Florida, and society, including a commitment to connecting the University’s academic and research strengths with the needs of communities across the state. Governance and Political Acumen: The judgment to navigate effectively among faculty governance, university administration, trustees, state-level governance, government partners, and external stakeholders while maintaining the integrity of the academic mission. Communication and Relationship Building: The ability to communicate a compelling vision, listen deeply, explain difficult decisions, build coalitions, and represent the University with credibility and confidence. Data-Informed and Technology-Enabled Leadership: The ability to use evidence, analytics, emerging technologies, and artificial intelligence thoughtfully to improve academic quality, institutional effectiveness, research productivity, and student success. Student Success and Educational Excellence: Advance an outstanding undergraduate, graduate, and professional educational experience. Leadership Priorities The successful candidate will be expected to work with the President and University community to: Define and execute a compelling next-generation academic strategy for UF. Further elevate UF’s standing among leading public research universities. Recruit and retain exceptional faculty and academic leaders. Strengthen research competitiveness and interdisciplinary collaboration. Advance educational innovation and student success. Identify strategic opportunities presented by artificial intelligence and other transformative technologies. Strengthen academic program quality, relevance, and sustainability. Build greater alignment among university-wide priorities, college strategies, and resource allocation. Foster a culture of academic excellence, innovation, intellectual openness, shared governance, and institutional trust. Advance UF's land-grant mission by strengthening the University's engagement and impact across the state of Florida, while extending its national and global reach. Total Rewards As one of the nation’s top public research universities, The University of Florida is dedicated to elevating benefits offerings that support the wellbeing of faculty and staff and to be responsive to a range of individual and family needs. The salary for this role will be commensurate with experience. About the University of Florida UF, the state’s flagship institution, is a top-ranked public research university located in Gainesville. With 16 colleges, a major academic health center, and championship-winning athletics, UF is a leader in education, research, and innovation. Its 2,000-acre campus integrates historic charm with state-of-the-art facilities and preserved natural spaces, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration. Ranked 7th among U.S. public universities in the 2026 U.S. News Best Colleges rankings, UF continues to drive advancements across multiple fields. As Florida’s land-grant university, UF has a distinctive responsibility to translate its strengths in education, research and innovation into meaningful impact for the people and communities of Florida. Through its statewide reach and partnerships, UF connects academic expertise and discovery with the needs and opportunities of a rapidly growing and changing state. With over 62,000 students, 33,000 employees, and a $10 billion annual budget, UF is among the largest and most well-funded universities in the nation. A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), UF boasts 6,600+ faculty, including National Academy members and award-winning scholars. In 2026, UF research expenditure surpassed $1.34 billion, demonstrating a strong commitment to discovery and technological advancement. UF’s entering Class of 2030 is the largest and most academically qualified in the university’s history, with 7,725 students selected from 96,000 applicants. The university leads in artificial intelligence education, incorporating AI into 200+ courses taught by 300 AI-focused faculty, supported by HiPerGator, the most powerful university-owned supercomputer in the U.S. UF’s strength lies in its comprehensive academic offerings, robust state support, and forward-thinking vision. With continuous growth and transformation, UF is dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation, and world look to for leadership.” For more information, visit www.ufl.edu . Expected Salary: Commensurate based on experience and education. Required Qualifications: Must have a terminal degree and a strong academic record at the full-professor level, combined with substantial senior academic administrative experience. Preferred: Experience overseeing academic programs, faculty and staff, budgets, strategic planning, and university-wide initiatives Demonstrate strong knowledge of shared governance, accreditation, faculty development, student success, and the ability to work effectively with presidents, faculty, deans, trustees, and other stakeholders. Significant experience in a research-intensive environment and a demonstrated record of institutional leadership is expected. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to accept applications and nominations until the position is filled. For full consideration, applications must be submitted by November 1, 2026, at 11:55 p.m. Applications should include a current curriculum vitae and a letter of interest describing relevant experience and interest in the position. Submission of materials via email is strongly encouraged. Nomination letters should include the name and contact information of the nominee. Application materials should be submitted to: Vickie Antolini, Senior Client Partner Anna Flagg, Senior Associate Korn Ferry Organization Consulting 2005 Market Street, Suite 910 Philadelphia, PA 19103 Email: UF-Provost@kornferry.com Refer to code “UF-Provost” in subject line The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541529/ast-prof": {
      "title": "AST PROF",
      "description": "AST PROF Apply now Job no: 541529 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 17020100 - BA-MANAGEMENT BUS OFFICE Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. degree or equivalent in Management Job Description: The University of Florida, in Gainesville, Florida is seeking an Assistant Professor for the Department of Management. Duties and responsibilities include teaching, research, and service. Teaching duties include teaching graduate-level courses, mentoring PhD students, providing effective instruction in strategic management, and participating in departmental service. Research duties center on the Future of Work, with expectations to conduct high-quality studies that advance understanding of anticipated changes in the workforce, work tasks, work environments, management practices, organizational adaptation, and strategic leadership. Position also requires impactful research published in top-tier journals while contributing meaningfully to the department and broader academic community. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree or equivalent in Management Special Instructions to Applicants: For more information about the position, including instructions on how to apply, please visit us on-line at UF Careers website, and reference job opening ID: 541529. UF is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Application should include the name and contact information of three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541530/uf-health-physician-hematology-medical-oncology-medicine": {
      "title": "UF Health Physician - Hematology & Medical Oncology  -  Medicine",
      "description": "UF Health Physician - Hematology & Medical Oncology - Medicine Apply now Job no: 541530 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: St. Johns Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 30050000 - JX-MEDICINE AT JAX Classification Title: UF Health Physician - Hematology/Oncology - Medicine Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Job Description: UF College of Medicine - Jacksonville is seeking to hire a Hematology & Medical Oncology Physician to join UF Health North and UF Health St. Johns, Florida. This is an outstanding opportunity to be a part of a patient-centered, collegial group committed to delivering high-quality clinical care. The selected candidate(s) will enjoy a market-based, competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. UF Health – The Power of Together! At the University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville and UF Health, together, we strive to create unstoppable momentum toward the goal of improving individual and community health through discovery, clinical and translational science and technology, exceptional education and patient-centered, innovative, high-quality health care. At the heart of our mission is to provide high-quality clinical care and to promise every patient their best experience possible. UF College of Medicine - Jacksonville is seeking to hire a Hematology & Medical Oncology Physician to join UF Health North and UF Health St. Johns, Florida. This is an outstanding opportunity to be a part of a patient-centered, collegial group committed to delivering high-quality clinical care. The selected candidate(s) will enjoy a market-based, competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. About UF Health: UF Health represents the commitment of more than 30,000 faculty and staff dedicated to delivering outstanding patient care, advancing medical research, and educating the next generation of health care professionals. With a network that includes 12 hospitals and numerous outpatient locations across Gainesville, Jacksonville, and Central Florida, UF Health is a leader in high-quality, patient-centered care. About UF Health Durbin: UF Health Durbin is a 42.5 acre health and wellness campus that will advance health care services as part of UF’s regional expansion plan for Jacksonville and St. Johns County. Located in northeast St. Johns County, UF Health Durbin Park will include a full-service 99-bed hospital with a focus on acute, intensive, emergency care, orthopaedics, neurosurgery, general surgery, cardiovascular medicine, and women’s services as well as a multidisciplinary medical office building. Future potential site development include the ability to expand hospital beds as well as an ambulatory surgical center, outpatient imagining and additional medial office building. We Offer Exceptional Benefits: Explore your benefit options at https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits/eligibility/staff-teams/ Low-cost State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision InsuranceLife and Disability Insurance Florida Retirement System Retirement Plans Paid Time Off including for vacations, holidays, paid family leave, and sick leave Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Sovereign immunity malpractice status No state taxes Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Federal DEA Certification Completion of an accredited Internal Medicine residency Completion of an accredited Hematology/Oncology fellowship Preferred: Specialty License or Certification; Board certified or Board Eligible in Specified Area of Medical Specialty; Certification/Licensure in the state of Florida and Federal DEA Certification Experience participating in multidisciplinary oncology programs Clinical research or quality improvement experience Interest in teaching medical students and residents Experience utilizing EPIC electronic health record Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please submit an application at Careers at UF with the following documents attached: Resume/Curriculum Vitae The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at https://naces.org/members/ The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541532/executive-vice-president-for-health-affairs": {
      "title": "Executive Vice President for Health Affairs",
      "description": "Executive Vice President for Health Affairs Apply now Job no: 541532 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 27000000 - OFFICE OF HEALTH AFFAIRS Classification Title: Executive Vice President for Health Affairs Classification Minimum Requirements: Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management. Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Job Description: KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Vision Develops, articulates, resources and implements a strategic vision for the University of Florida’s health colleges, centers and institutes that supports the university’s and UF Health’s strategic plans. Acts in all areas as a forward-thinking and transformational leader. Research and Academic Excellence Holds overall responsibility and authority for research and education across the health sciences (UF health educational programs include clinical education programs and undergraduate, graduate and professional education). Fosters and supports excellence in basic, clinical and translational research programs in the health colleges and their contributions across the university. Operations Leverages the educational and research strengths of UF health sciences in alignment with the clinical mission of UF Health. Ensures the university’s health colleges and related programs are operated in a financially sound manner, and that capital and other investments are used efficiently. Develops a structured process to optimize decision-making and outcomes. With the UF Health President/System CEO and the UF President, develops and administers financial models supporting the clinical, educational and research missions of the health sciences. Governance While reporting to the President, leads collaboration with the health sciences’ college deans and other senior leaders. Provides leadership and direction for UF’s academic programs in the health sciences. Culture Develops and fosters an environment of respect, competence, transparency, innovation and principle-driven management. Ensures that interdisciplinary collaborations enhancing educational and research programs in the health sciences are encouraged and supported. Supports and champions collaboration across all of UF. Advocacy Cultivates strong, credible and trusting relationships with alumni, donors, government leaders and related external stakeholders in support of the educational, research and clinical missions of UF and UF Health. Advocates for the initiatives of constituent units in the health sciences. Acts as an effective representative and spokesperson for the health sciences at the university. Plans and executes fundraising initiatives pertaining to the health sciences in support of college and university activities. Fundraising and Development The EVPHA will play a leadership role in fundraising for the academic health center (UF Health overall), leading efforts in the university’s capital campaign to support the organization’s growth and strategic initiatives. DESIRED OUTCOMES Growth Oversee successful expansion and successful transformation of the educational and basic, translational and clinical research enterprises of the health sciences at the university in alignment with UF and UF Health goals. Ensure growth is coordinated with inter-university initiatives and contributes to revenue-enhancing strategies, e.g., through new educational program offerings, strategic recruitment of outstanding research groups and integration of research opportunities into clinical care and population health. Research Improve quality and quantity of basic, translational, clinical and population-based health sciences research and expand research activity to increase the overall impact of UF’s research enterprise. Foster collaborative, programmatic research programs that encompass health science and non-health science colleges, institutes and centers. Education Continue development and implementation of an innovative and interprofessional educational strategy. Support the health colleges’ education and training initiatives. Enable greater achievement in workforce development through support of initiatives across the health sciences at the University of Florida. UF Health Alignment Promote the growth, reputation and impact of the health colleges and the clinical enterprise by enhancing education and research, including the development of innovative educational strategies and development of novel prevention and therapeutic programs. UNIVERSITY OF FLORIDA The University of Florida (UF) is the state’s flagship institution, a top-ranked public research university located in Gainesville. With 16 colleges, a major academic health system and championship-winning athletics, UF is a leader in education, research and innovation. Its 2,000-acre campus integrates historic charm with state-of-the-art facilities and preserved natural spaces. Ranked 7th among U.S. public universities in the 2026 U.S. News & World Report’s “Best Colleges” rankings, UF continues to drive advances across multiple fields. With over 62,000 students, 33,000 employees and a $10 billion annual budget, UF is among the largest and most well-funded universities in the nation. A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), UF boasts over 6,600 faculty, including National Academy members and award-winning scholars. In 2025, UF research expenditures surpassed $1.3 billion, demonstrating a strong commitment to discovery and technological advancement. UF’s entering Class of 2030 is the largest and most academically qualified in the university’s history, with 7,725 students selected from 96,000 applicants. The university leads in artificial intelligence education, incorporating AI into 200+ courses taught by 300 AI-focused faculty, supported by HiPerGator, the most powerful university-owned supercomputer in the U.S. UF’s strength lies in its comprehensive academic offerings, robust state support and forward-thinking vision. With continuous growth and transformation, UF is dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation and world look to for leadership.” Visit www.ufl.edu for more information. UF HEALTH UF Health, the world-class academic health system of the University of Florida, combines scientific innovation at campuses around Florida with outstanding compassionate care at a network of hospitals and clinical practices throughout the state. The $6.5 billion system optimizes collective expertise to improve patient care, education, discovery and the health of our communities. With main campuses in Gainesville and Jacksonville as well as satellite sites in Central Florida, St. Johns County, Ocala and several other locations, UF Health provides quality health care to patients across Florida. The country’s only academic health center with six health-related colleges (Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine) and a partner teaching hospital located on a single, contiguous campus, the system features 13 hospital facilities in total (including two teaching hospitals and five specialty hospitals), more than 100 physician medical practices and outpatient sites, and research centers and institutes, including The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation and Technology, located in Jupiter. UF Health is a world leader in interdisciplinary research, generating more than half of UF’s total research awards. Research activities reflect a depth of purpose by focusing on the translational nature of biomedical research, following fundamental research from clinical research to patient care. The backbone of UF Health is a talented and dedicated workforce of more than 32,000 faculty and staff who provide lifesaving care and research breakthroughs for more than four million patients who come to UF Health each year from around the U.S. and more than 40 countries. UF Health is committed to improving lives and health through a variety of community-based programs and activities as part of the state’s “safety-net’’ hospital system, often caring for people who have little or no medical coverage. Expected Salary: Commensurate based on experience and education. Required Qualifications: Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management. Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Preferred: IDEAL EXPERIENCE Executive Leadership in Complex Academic Environments Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management Research and Education Leadership Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Operational Acumen Demonstrated managerial abilities and enthusiasm for the administrative and financial responsibilities of the position, with understanding of the financial pressures facing academic health centers and health care delivery organizations and the principles of mission-based budgeting and funds flow. External Leadership and Engagement Demonstrated success representing a complex academic health enterprise and building strong relationships with external stakeholders, including donors, alumni, community partners, government leaders and other key constituencies. Experience leading philanthropic engagement and fundraising efforts while effectively advancing institutional priorities at the state and federal levels. Personal Scholarship Strong preference for a physician-scientist or clinician-scientist with a strong record of scholarship, including a record of substantial, extramural peer-reviewed funding and national/international recognition; academic credentials sufficient to engender respect within the University of Florida and UF Health and support appointment as a tenured full professor. CRITICAL LEADERSHIP CAPABILITIES Acting Strategically The EVPHA will partner with constituent deans and UF/UF Health senior leaders to develop, refine and execute a strategy that optimizes UF’s scholarship in the health sciences and contributions to the clinical mission by: Clearly articulating the future direction and ideal culture of the organization while leading by example and challenging established practices, as necessary. Identifying and prioritizing critical factors in making strategic decisions affecting the university’s health colleges and programs. Discovering, creating and expanding synergies between colleges in the health sciences. Pursuing revenue-enhancing opportunities through new or expanded education/research offerings. Addressing opportunities in the larger research landscape to enhance UF’s position as a leading AAU institution. Identifying and pursuing opportunities to advance the health sciences through philanthropy, government partnerships and other external sources of support. Collaborating and Influencing The EVPHA will develop relationships with all stakeholders to effectively guide the health sciences’ contributions to UF and UF Health by: Demonstrating appreciation for all the health sciences and supporting inter-college collaboration while championing collective activity across all UF entities. Connecting productively within the health sciences enterprise, with the broader university, and across UF Health to develop long-term collaborations and partnerships. Developing a culture that respects, values and promotes the dignity of UF’s students, staff, faculty and stakeholders. Building and maintaining productive relationships with donors, alumni, government leaders, policymakers and other external stakeholders, working in partnership with university leadership, advancement and government relations to advance UF and UF Health priorities. Leading Transformation The EVPHA should be able to thrive in a complex, fast-paced institution that is executing change on multiple fronts while balancing complementary and competing priorities and be prepared to lead the academic mission of UF’s health sciences by: Identifying and leading changes necessary to advance education and research in the health sciences, embracing transformational initiatives (e.g., Artificial Intelligence at UF, Drug Discovery at The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology) to elevate the stature and productivity of the university. Adapting agreed-upon plans to the political realities, strengths and constraints of the university, health system and state, engaging with all relevant stakeholders to gain necessary buy-in through customized influence efforts. Establishing practices that encourage and reinforce innovation and impact new thinking in UF’s health colleges, institutes and centers. Special Instructions to Applicants: The University of Florida is being assisted in this recruitment by Spencer Stuart. For questions about the opportunity, to nominate an individual or to indicate personal interest in being considered as a candidate, please email the search’s confidential mailbox at UF_EVPHealthAffairs@spencerstuart.com .For full consideration, applications must be submitted by November 1, 2026, at 11:55 p.m. FOR ADDITIONAL INFORMATION, PLEASE CONTACT: Chuck Jordan, Consultant Office telephone: 404.504.4429 E-mail address: cjordan@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Mary Elizabeth “M.E.” Welton Office telephone: 404.504.4463 E-mail address: mewelton@spencerstuart.com Kathryn Sugerman, Consultant Office telephone: 310.443.3525 E-mail address: ksugerman@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Kimberly Harvalis Office telephone: 310.443.3510 E-mail address: kharvalis@spencerstuart.com Shannon L. Yeatman, Consultant Office telephone: 404.504.4436 E-mail address: syeatman@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Melinda Lee Office telephone: 404.504.4437 E-mail address: mklee@spencerstuart.com Donna Russell, Senior Associate Office telephone: 617.897.3327 E-mail address: drussell@spencerstuart.com Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541534/streetlight-program-assistant-department-of-pediatrics": {
      "title": "Streetlight Program Assistant - Department of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Streetlight Program Assistant - Department of Pediatrics Apply now Job no: 541534 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29094400 - MD-PEDS-STREETLIGHT Classification Title: OPS Program Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements Classification Minimum Requirements are the baseline requirements for Staff Titles and Academic Titles found at the University of Florida. Job Description: The Streetlight Program Assistant (OPS) herein supports the Streetlight program’s mission to provide age-appropriate peer support to chronically ill adolescents as a model of pediatric palliative care. The Streetlight Program Assistant assists with administrative duties, patient support, and volunteer support. ADMINISTRATIVE SUPPORT Recruits, screens, and orients new volunteers Reviews EPIC charts and filters patient census and medical information into a Streetlight team-member appropriate \"need-to-know\" format Records daily patient referrals from medical staff and consolidates information into task-lists for team members Manages Streetlight encrypted SharePoint site featuring weekly team educational readings, announcements, and educational web links Manages REDCap database including transcription of patient demographics, diagnoses, and volunteer psychosocial chart entries Facilitates communication and collaboration with interdisciplinary team members to best meet the needs of patients enrolled in Streetlight VOLUNTEER SUPPORT Assists with new volunteer orientation and continued education in palliative care, chronic and terminal illness, HIPAA compliance and best practices to provide psychosocial care to the target population Conducts project-based volunteer meetings Organizes and archives team education materials Coordinates team member graduation ceremony and other social events PATIENT SUPPORT Introduces Streetlight program to new designated patients Provides psychosocial support to patients including inpatient room visits, outpatient appointment visits, and event planning Provides end-of-life support including creation of legacy projects Regularly coordinates activities for patients/families (milestone events, unique long-term patient events, etc.) including management of logistics, catering, and community involvement. Organizes weekly meal deliveries for Cystic Fibrosis patients through the BJ’s CF Dining Days Program PROGRAM PROMOTION AND EXPORTATION Maintains Streetlight active patient database, used for program census Expected Salary: $20 per hour Required Qualifications: Qualifications refer to evidence-based or demonstrated knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences required for the role. For TEAMS positions the required qualifications should reflect Classification Minimum Requirements. For Faculty roles, these qualifications may reflect college-specific requirements needed for success the role. Preferred Qualifications Graphic design, video/photo editing, additional languages, social media proficiency, video game proficiency, group facilitation Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/532862/clinical-coder-iii-department-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder III - Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Clinical Coder III - Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 532862 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Coder III Job Description: The Department of Neurosurgery is seeking an experienced Surgical Coder to provide specialized diagnosis and procedural coding for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services performed by our faculty in Gainesville and at Halifax Regional Medical Center. The ideal candidate will have strong knowledge of anatomy, medical and surgical terminology, coding practices, and insurance billing requirements. As part of the Lillian S. Wells Department of Neurosurgery at the University of Florida College of Medicine, this position supports a nationally recognized academic department providing comprehensive care for complex neurological conditions across adult and pediatric populations. The department combines advanced clinical care with innovative research, clinical trials, education, residency, and fellowship programs. This role offers an opportunity to contribute to a collaborative environment while developing specialized expertise in neurosurgical coding. Key Responsibilities Perform highly specialized diagnosis and procedural coding for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services provided by Department of Neurosurgery faculty in Gainesville and at Halifax Regional Medical Center. Review operative reports, progress notes, and other clinical documentation to identify billable services and accurately assign or verify ICD-10 and CPT codes. Verify physician attestation statements and other required documentation to support compliant coding and billing. Conduct comprehensive chart reviews to ensure all appropriate services are captured and meet regulatory, documentation, and coding requirements. Ensure proper documentation of teaching provider involvement for services performed with residents and fellows. Monitor operative documentation and follow up on unsigned or missing operative records to support timely charge entry. Enter and maintain accurate charges for surgical procedures and non-surgical inpatient services in Epic. Monitor, research, and resolve TES edits and other coding or billing issues within Epic. Verify patient demographics, guarantor information, insurance coverage, and claim data for accuracy and resolve discrepancies. Establish and maintain guarantor and insurance accounts in Epic, including subscriber relationships, policy information, claim addresses, authorizations, injury information, and other required billing data. Verify insurance eligibility through electronic systems and direct communication with insurance carriers. Review encounter information and identify emergency admissions, hospital-to-hospital transfers, endovascular cases, and other encounter characteristics for statistical and departmental reporting. Collaborate with the Assistant Director and departmental leadership to determine appropriate surgical charges and evaluate insurance reimbursement. Monitor and analyze denials, write-offs, reimbursement trends, and billing issues, identifying opportunities to improve revenue cycle performance and insurance collections. Assist department leadership in developing and implementing processes that improve coding accuracy, documentation, reimbursement, and overall revenue cycle performance. Research complex coding questions using authoritative resources, including professional coding organizations, federal and private payer guidelines, coding networks, and other industry resources. Provide coding and billing education and guidance to attending surgeons, fellows, residents, and other clinical staff. Keep faculty and trainees informed of changes to coding requirements, payer guidelines, documentation standards, and billing practices. Serve as a subject matter expert and resource for neurosurgical coding, developing and maintaining specialized knowledge of complex neurosurgical procedures and applicable coding requirements. Participate in monthly Coder Forum meetings to review Epic product-line performance, coding trends, and opportunities for improvement. Conduct independent research and prepare reports and analyses related to coding practices, reimbursement, compliance, and departmental performance. Participate in professional development activities, including coding meetings, seminars, classes, and continuing education, to maintain current knowledge and coding expertise. Meet regularly with the Department Chair, Administrator, Assistant Director, and/or other departmental leaders to address coding, documentation, reimbursement, and product-line issues. Serve as a liaison between the Department of Neurosurgery and internal and external partners, including University of Florida Physicians (UFP), the UF Compliance Office, and other clinical, coding, and revenue cycle stakeholders. Expected Salary: $25.40 - $28.38 per hour Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of experience in professional medical coding, with experience in two or three clinical specialties. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of medical terminology, surgical coding. Knowledge of business and office practice. Knowledge of Excel, and other applicable software. Ability to analyze financial data and able to generate understandable reports. Ability to communicate effectively, verbally and in writing. Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted and extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540384/maintenance-generalist-ii-jupiter-fl": {
      "title": "Maintenance Generalist II - Jupiter, FL",
      "description": "Maintenance Generalist II - Jupiter, FL Apply now Job no: 540384 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 37021000 - SR-FACILITIES Classification Title: Maintenance Generalist II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The full-time Maintenance Generalist II will support the demands of the Institute’s maintenance operations and perform services and repairs which include welding, plumbing, painting, carpentry and as a locksmith. The position is considered essential and must be available during emergencies, such as hurricanes. Responsibilities and Duties: General Maintenance Operations: Builds and/or installs shelves, cabinets, lab tops, doors and desk keyboard trays; hangs signs, pictures and boards; repairs door hinges, knobs and closures. Assist with event setup and general repairs. Replaces ballasts, electrical outlets, light switches and light bulbs; hangs light fixtures; repairs or replaces frayed power cords and plugs. Inspects sinks, drinking fountains, urinals, toilets, unclogs drains, install pipes, performs light repairs of sinks and garbage disposals. Replaces floor and ceiling tiles, caulks around bathroom fixtures. Works with specialized lab support equipment including hoods, incubators, freezers or other lab equipment as assigned. Moves equipment, freezers and furniture. Responds to emergency calls and equipment alarms; evaluates problem and makes repairs or requests appropriate specialist. Supply Maintenance: Estimates labor and material costs for work requests; may order supplies. Performs routine work orders and preventative maintenance tasks, as assigned. Accurately records daily hours worked and time spent on individual tasks. Works daily with the Facilities Computer Maintenance Management System (CMMS) Software (Micromain); BAS software, and the Footprints work order system. The ability to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing, is an essential function of this position. May perform related tasks and work of other Facilities department personnel, as required or assigned. Interprets blueprints, drawings, layouts or other specifications to determine installation or repair requirements. Expected Salary: $25.00 per hour. Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Previous experience working in a research, hospital, or university setting is preferred. Previous experience with plumbing, electrical, mechanical, carpentry, and/or welding experience is preferred. Previous trade experience (1 or more) in plumbing, electrical, pipefitting, HVAC, etc. is preferred. Excellent interpersonal communication and writing skills and the ability to evaluate mechanical and electrical systems. Must have a valid driver’s license issued from the state where the position is located. Must be available on holidays, weekends, and emergency responses as the property is open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Please provide names and email addresses of three to five references as part of the application process. When ready, the hiring department will contact the listed references via email requesting their reference letters to be uploaded directly to the application website. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See the Qualified Positions link on our applicant FAQs page for a list of eligible positions. Join our facilities team with the opportunity to receive a competitive sign-on bonus! We’re looking for dedicated professionals to help maintain and enhance our facilities. This limited-time bonus is available to new hires who join our team and meet eligibility requirements—apply today and take advantage of this exciting opportunity! This position is open until filled, and application review will begin July 3, 2026. The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540575/student-account-specialist-i-collections": {
      "title": "Student Account Specialist I, Collections",
      "description": "Student Account Specialist I, Collections Apply now Job no: 540575 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 64801000 - FA-UNIVERSITY BURSAR-ADMIN Classification Title: Student Account Specialist I Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor's degree, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: This position within the University Bursar is located in the CFO Division. The primary focus of this position is to assist with collection efforts for students who have become delinquent on loans administered by the University. Counsels borrowers about potential deferment, cancellation, and forbearance options. Submits economic hardship, deferment, or forbearance requests to the Accounting Manager for approval. Coordinates submission of student loan accounts to collection agencies. Assists with collection efforts prior to sending to collection agencies. Assists with timely placement to collection agencies, monitoring collection agency activity, and reviewing monthly payment processing. Provides account documentation when requested by collection agencies. Communicates with currently enrolled students with past due accounts receivable balances to enlist the student in a payment plan or to otherwise collect on the balance due. Monitors student holds to ensure service indicators are preventing students from progressing in their education without settling past due accounts. Creates proactive student communication to increase student retention while minimizing balances past due. Respond to student inquiries regarding credit bureau, delinquent status, academic transcript requests, or other disputes. Counsels' students and borrowers with financial holds about available options. Provides supporting documentation on charges, loans, and payments to outside agencies as appropriate. Assists current university or State of Florida employees to pay past due accounts via payroll deductions or the state wage withholding program. Performs all other duties as required. Expected Salary: $19.16 - $22.00 per hour Preferred: Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate files, records, and work assignments. Ability to effectively communicate verbally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to work independently and as a team. Ability to utilize problem-solving techniques Experience with MS Word and Excel Ability to determine work priorities, assign work, and ensure proper completion of work assignments. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540780/clinical-coder-iidepartment-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder II-Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Clinical Coder II-Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 540780 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Coder II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Job Description: The position is responsible for reviewing clinical documentation and entering accurate diagnoses and procedural codes for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services performed by the Department of Neurological Surgery faculty at both Gainesville and Halifax. Key Responsibilities Review operative reports, progress notes, and other clinical documentation to identify billable services and determine the appropriate ICD-10 and CPT codes. Verify physician attestation statements and ensure required documentation is complete and supports coding and billing requirements. Enter and maintain accurate charges for surgical procedures and non-surgical inpatient services in Epic. Perform comprehensive chart reviews to ensure all appropriate services are captured and coding and regulatory requirements are met. Monitor and resolve TES edits in Epic, addressing discrepancies and ensuring charges are accurately processed. Verify patient demographics, insurance coverage, and billing information and resolve discrepancies as needed. Establish and maintain guarantor and insurance information in Epic, including subscriber relationships, policy information, claim addresses, authorizations, dates of injury, and other required claim details. Verify insurance eligibility through electronic resources and direct communication with insurance providers. Review patient and encounter information to determine appropriate classifications, including emergency admissions, hospital-to-hospital transfers, and endovascular cases, for departmental reporting and statistical purposes. Maintain accurate databases containing surgical charges, diagnosis codes, CPT codes, dates of service, and attending physician information. Monitor operative documentation and follow up on unsigned or missing operative reports to support timely and accurate billing. Ensure appropriate documentation of teaching provider involvement for services involving residents and fellows. Work collaboratively with the Associate Director and departmental leadership to evaluate surgical charges, coding practices, and insurance reimbursement opportunities. Research complex or evolving coding questions using professional coding organizations, federal and private payer guidelines, coding networks, and other authoritative resources. Serve as a resource to attending surgeons, fellows, residents, and staff by providing coding education, guidance, and support. Keep faculty and trainees informed of coding, documentation, billing, and regulatory changes that may affect reimbursement. Participate in monthly Coder Forum meetings to discuss Epic product-line performance, coding trends, challenges, and opportunities for improvement. Collaborate with departmental leadership to develop and implement processes that improve coding accuracy, compliance, documentation, and reimbursement. Conduct independent research and prepare reports and analyses related to coding practices, reimbursement, and departmental performance. Participate in professional development opportunities, including coding seminars, classes, meetings, and continuing education, to maintain current knowledge and skills. Expected Salary: $24.50-$27.50 per hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred: Knowledge of medical terminology, surgical coding Certification as a coder, either CPC (American Academy of Professional Coders) or CCS-P (American Health Information Management Association). Knowledge of business and office practice; Knowledge of Excel, and other applicable software. Ability to analyze financial data and able to generate understandable reports. Ability to communicate effectively, verbally and in writing. Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. To claim veterans' preference, indicate the claim on the application and provide required documentation by uploading a DD-214 (or equivalent certification from the Department of Veteran Affairs) and any additional supporting documentation based on the type of claim. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted and extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541416/visiting-lecturer": {
      "title": "Visiting Lecturer",
      "description": "Visiting Lecturer Apply now Job no: 541416 Work type: Visiting Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16940100 - LS-WRITING PGM GENERAL Classification Title: Visiting Lecturer Job Description: The University Writing Program at the University of Florida invites applications for a Visiting Lecturer with the working title Assistant Instructional Professor, to begin January 1, 2027. This is a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-accruing faculty appointment with the possibility of renewal for a maximum of three years. The University Writing Program is a thriving, independent academic unit offering a wide range of writing-intensive courses in areas such as first-year composition, themed critical thinking and writing, discipline-focused writing, and professional writing. A significant goal of the University Writing Program is to cultivate effective visual and written communication in academic and professional contexts. The teaching assignment is 3/3. The successful candidates will teach writing-intensive courses, support students in developing effective written, visual, and rhetorical communication, and contribute to the instructional mission of the University Writing Program. Applicants should have rhetorical expertise and teaching experience. Advertised Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Minimum Requirements: PhD in Composition and Rhetoric or a related content area with teaching experience in composition. Preferred Qualifications: Rhetorical expertise and teaching experience in writing, composition, or communication. Relevant professional experience in writing, communication, or a related field. Strong interest or experience in professional, community-based, or discipline-specific communication. Interest or experience in experiential learning, including student exchanges, study abroad courses, or community-based outreach. Interest or experience with artificial intelligence, digital media, visual communication, or related areas of writing instruction. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three professional references Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on September 28, 2026 and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541448/assistantassociate-professorfamily-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Apply now Job no: 541448 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30160000 - JX-COMMUNITY MEDICINE-JAX Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must be MD/DO and have a Florida Medical License Job Description: The Department of Community Health and Family Medicine at the University of Florida College of Medicine-Jacksonville seeks a family medicine/internal medicine physician, MD/DO, to join us. The position is for Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor level on a clinical non-tenure accruing track at our outpatient care office - UF Health Family Medicine. This position is up to fulltime and will report to the medical director and chair of the department at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville. Base salary is based on experience and guaranteed with significant additional productivity incentives and benefits package. UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Negotiable based on Qualifications and Experience. Preferred: Board certified or board eligible. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on November 1, 2026. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541468/recsports-student-assistant-safety-instructor": {
      "title": "RecSports Student Assistant - Safety Instructor",
      "description": "RecSports Student Assistant - Safety Instructor Apply now Job no: 541468 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Recreation/Event Services, Student Services Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: STU-AST-NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for Safety Instructors to join our energetic team and move Gators toward lifelong wellbeing! The Recreational Sports Safety Instructor position is responsible for instructing safety courses on standards set by the American Red Cross. Successful candidates will begin work at the start of Spring of 2027. For questions or additional information, please contact Adam Caldwell, caldwelladam@ufl.edu . For more information about the role and onboarding process, visit: RecSports Student Employment Webpage. RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Teach all course materials to the standards set forth by the American Red Cross by following the Instructor Manual, while observing the set safety measures and guidelines. Arrive before the start of class, as scheduled, to collect supplies and prepare for class. Dress professionally and act responsibly. Provide a safe and welcoming environment, conducive to a productive learning environment. Use a variety of learning tactics to provide a learning opportunity for all participants. Be available to answer participant questions, demonstrate skills, and provide global and individual feedback. Clean and disinfect all supplies after use and return to appropriate storage location. Complete Safety Course Shift reports at the conclusion of each course, noting any issues with equipment, supplies, technology, and/or participants. Attend mandatory meetings and/or trainings. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures being courteous and helpful to individual students and classes. Maintain an acceptable level of knowledge through a personal commitment to learning new information, instructional techniques, and updating/obtaining new certifications. Participate in the facilitation of the Safety Audit program and in-service trainings, as needed. Perform all other duties as assigned by the Aquatics & Risk Management Administrative Team. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $16.50 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications University of Florida student Contribute 3-5 hours/month, dependent up on course schedule (evening and weekend hours) Required: Current American Red Cross CPR/AED/First Aid Certification or recognized equivalent or willing to obtain by start date Must demonstrate a basic understanding of effective instruction strategies and tools Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811 This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Special Instructions to Applicants: Questions: Contact Adam Caldwell at caldwelladam@ufl.edu. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541480/postdoctoral-scholar-in-natural-language-processing-and-mammalian-evolution": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution Apply now Job no: 541480 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 56021500 - NH-MAMMALS Classification Title: Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in natural language processing, computer science, data science, ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, bioinformatics, or a closely related field, completed or expected to be completed before the start date. Job Description: Centuries of field observations, dietary studies, and taxonomic accounts contain an extraordinary amount of information about what mammals eat. However, these data remain embedded in unstructured text, scattered across thousands of publications, and are reported using inconsistent terminology and formats. This project seeks to unlock these hidden data by developing a human-in-the-loop AI pipeline that uses LLMs to read scientific literature at scale, extract consumer-food relationships, and convert qualitative descriptions into standardized ordinal dietary rankings suitable for evolutionary biology research. Our central hypothesis is that the published literature contains a vast reservoir of latent dietary information that can be systematically transformed into reproducible quantitative datasets. Existing mammalian diet databases rely heavily on manual expert curation, limiting their scope and scalability. By combining AI-assisted information extraction with expert validation, this project will create high-resolution dietary datasets spanning the mammalian tree at a scale that would be impractical through manual curation alone. The Postdoctoral Researcher will play a leading role in developing the dietary ranking framework and constructing the resulting database. Working closely with an interdisciplinary team of ecologists, biodiversity scientists, and AI researchers, the successful candidate will contribute to the development and evaluation of LLM-based information extraction methods, analyze large-scale dietary datasets, and help advance computational approaches for ecological synthesis. The Postdoctoral Researcher will conduct independent and collaborative research, publish in leading scientific journals, contribute to grant proposals, mentor graduate and undergraduate students, and help shape the future direction of this growing interdisciplinary research program. The project also provides excellent opportunities to develop expertise in AI for biodiversity science while building a strong publication record and collaborative network for an academic or research career. This position will initially be awarded for one year and, contingent upon strong performance and conduct and the availability of funds, may be renewed for up to three years. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity institution. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Expected Salary: $60,000 Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in natural language processing, computer science, data science, ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, bioinformatics, or a closely related field, completed or expected to be completed before the start date. · Demonstrated research experience in at least one of the following areas: o Natural language processing, large language models (LLMs), information extraction, or machine learning; or o Ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, or similar, with a focus on large-scale, quantitative research. · Strong programming skills in Python and experience with scientific computing, data processing, and reproducible research workflows. · Experience designing, implementing, and evaluating computational methods or data analysis pipelines. · Excellent written and oral communication skills, with evidence of peer-reviewed publications or a strong publication trajectory. · Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to an interdisciplinary, collaborative research team. Preferred: · Experience with modern NLP and LLM techniques, including transformer models, prompting strategies, named entity recognition, relation extraction, or information extraction from scientific literature. · Experience working with biodiversity, ecological, evolutionary, or biological trait datasets. · Familiarity with annotation workflows, corpus development, or evaluation of machine learning models. · Experience with statistical analysis, multivariate methods, or comparative and phylogenetic analyses. · Experience using high-performance computing (HPC), cloud computing, or large-scale machine learning workflows. · Familiarity with open science, FAIR data principles, and research data management. · Experience mentoring undergraduate or graduate students and contributing to collaborative research projects. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. Application reviews will begin Dec 15, 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. A complete application includes: A complete curriculum vitae Letter of intent, summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, two page maximum. Names and contact information of three professional references We expect the Postdoctoral Researcher to begin in late 2026 or spring of 2027, with some flexibility in start date. Initial review of applicants will begin on 15 October 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541489/recsports-ops-massage-therapist": {
      "title": "RecSports - OPS Massage Therapist",
      "description": "RecSports - OPS Massage Therapist Apply now Job no: 541489 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Recreation/Event Services, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: OPS Time-Limited Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for a Massage Therapist to join our energetic team and move Gators toward lifelong wellbeing! Massage Therapists at the Department of Recreational Sports are provided with a room, electric massage table, sheets, oil, and a laundering service. Therapists will also have access to RecSports marketing channels and materials for promotional opportunities. Therapists are provided access to all RecSports facilities, fields, and programs, including group fitness, weight and cardio rooms, Lake Wauburg, intramural sports, and more! For questions or additional information, please contact Emma Edgeworth at edgeworthemma@ufl.edu . RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Provide Massage Therapy modalities to clients within the scope of the State of Florida Massage Therapy license. Maintain a clean work environment including treatment areas. Monitor Massage Therapy materials (sheets, oils, etc.), keeping treatment rooms well stocked at all times. Provide directions and information to valid users and guests regarding facilities and programs. Answer main phone lines and direct calls to appropriate personnel, take Massage Therapy reservations, and answer facility or program inquiries. Learn the responsibilities and tasks required of the supervising positions. Contribute to the promotion of the Massage Therapy Program with regards to activities, events, programs, and off-site services. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $34-$36 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications State of Florida Massage Therapy License and proof of professional liability insurance First Aid, CPR, AED certification or ability to attain it within the first week of employment Interest in health and wellness, recreation, fitness, and campus involvement Availability to work shifts as well as by appointment, including weekend hours Availability to work three permanent shifts, at minimum, each week, totaling at least 10 hours per week Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811 This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Preferred: Preferred Qualifications 1 to 3 years of hands-on experience Certifications in specialized modalities Experience with dynamic cupping and hot stone Background in a clinical or spa environment Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants applying for this requisition may be hired as either Student Assistants or OPS employees. If hired, applicants who are full-time University of Florida students will be hired as Student Assistants; all other applicants will be hired as OPS employees. This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. This is a time-limited position. For more information, please reach out to Emma Edgeworth at edgeworthemma@ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541494/recsports-student-assistant-outdoor-adventures-associate": {
      "title": "RecSports Student Assistant - Outdoor Adventures Associate",
      "description": "RecSports Student Assistant - Outdoor Adventures Associate Apply now Job no: 541494 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Recreation/Event Services, Student Services Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: STU-AST-NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for Outdoor Adventures Associates to join our energetic team and move Gators towards lifelong wellbeing! Outdoor Adventures Associates serve the department by working in a team environment to provide excellent customer service through outdoor gear rentals, merchandise sales, and ensuring a superior CORE facility for our users. Successful candidates will begin work at the beginning of Spring 2027. For questions or additional information, please contact Braja Smith at b.smith2@ufl.edu . For more information about the role and onboarding process, visit RecSports Student Employment Webpage . RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Assist patrons and participants with rentals and adventure trip registrations in-person and over the phone. Perform sales and rental transactions for outdoor gear and merchandise. Maintain cleanliness and organization of the Outdoor Adventures facility and warehouse. Assist with preparation and cleaning of adventure trip gear. Make appropriate referrals to other departments at UF RecSports Clean, maintain, and repair rental gear. Manage gear rental reservations, check-ins, and check-outs. Inspect rental gear for damages. Regularly communicate with administrative staff Willingness to innovate and develop new systems as Outdoor Adventures expands its scope of offerings. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $15.00 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications Successful completion of departmental training once hired. Shadow Shift Requirement: New Outdoor Adventures Associates are required to complete at least one shadow shift Interest in outdoor recreation and campus involvement Outdoor recreation knowledge/experience preferred Availability to work weekend and weekday facility hours. Ability to attend monthly Outdoor Adventures Associate meetings. Commitment of approximately 9 hours/week, or a minimum of two shifts per week. Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811. Successfully complete background check. This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must submit a resume to be considered. For more information, contact Braja Smith at b.smith2@ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541507/tenuretrack-assistant-or-associate-professor-in-media-and-technology": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor in Media and Technology",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor in Media and Technology Apply now Job no: 541507 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 23030000 - CJC-MEDIA PROD, MGMT, TECH Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Job Description: The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) at the University of Florida invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position (who currently has tenure) starting August 2027. We seek an energetic scholar with an active research program centered on any aspect of media and technology. We welcome candidates from a broad range of methodological traditions and theoretical perspectives. While the research program is open to any domain within media and technology, the candidate must be prepared to teach RTV 3432: Ethics and Problems in Media, a core course serving undergraduate majors in both Digital Film and Video Production and Media Management and Technology. Research & Teaching Responsibilities Conduct original research that advances scholarly understanding of media and/or technology including, but not limited to: media infrastructure technology practices sports media and communication media systems platform studies entertainment media digital culture and strategy social/mobile media science and technology studies Teach undergraduate core courses, specifically RTV 3432: Ethics and Problems in Media, bridging ethical, regulatory, and societal issues across both creative production and strategic media management contexts and other courses in the department, with opportunities to develop specialized upper-level electives and graduate seminars. Pursue internal and external research funding. Publish in leading academic venues. Mentor students and contribute to departmental, college, and university service. Minimum Qualifications A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Demonstrated experience for national and international scholarly impact. (Assistant level) Established record of national and international scholarly impact. (Associate level) Demonstrated success in research funding. Evidence of excellence in university teaching. Preferred Qualifications Ability to engage students across both creative production and media management specializations in ethical and critical decision-making. Commitment to supporting a collaborative academic culture where all faculty, staff, and students can thrive. Preference will be given to candidates whose research, professional experience, and teaching demonstrate the ability to bridge the department's two tracks: (1) Digital Film and Video Production and (2) Media Management and Technology. About the Department & College: The MPMT department serves approximately 500 undergraduate majors across two core specializations: Digital Film and Video Production and Media Management and Technology. Our faculty prepare students for a variety of careers in film and video production, social media strategy, audience analytics, UI/UX design, and digital marketing. Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) - UF College of Journalism and Communications The College of Journalism and Communications enrolls over 2,500 undergraduate and 700 graduate students across four departments. Faculty benefit from premier research hubs, including the Atlas Lab for social media listening, the Media Effects and Technology Lab, the Center for Public Interest Communications, and UF's university-wide AI Initiative. Homepage - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the University & Community The University of Florida is a top-tier public research university (AAU) with over $1 billion in annual research expenditures and state-of-the-art supercomputing infrastructure. Located in Gainesville, UF offers a vibrant university community surrounded by natural springs, a rich cultural scene, and excellent quality of life within two hours of major metropolitan areas and coastal beaches. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. https://youtu.be/e_7nUKAFanM?si=7Z4s36Q80iaj6Nwg The University of Florida offers a competitive salary commensurate with similar positions and includes a full benefits package. To view the university’s Total Reward benefit package, please click here: https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Preferred: Preference will be given to candidates whose research and teaching demonstrate the ability to bridge the department's two tracks: (1) Digital Film and Video Production and (2) Media Management and Technology. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu and include: A cover letter detailing your research agenda and teaching philosophy A current curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., student evaluations or teaching portfolio) Copies of two representative lead-authored publications or scholarly works Names, email addresses, and phone numbers for three professional references Review of applications will begin on October 23 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Jasmine McNealy, at jmcnealy@ufl.edu. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541508/assistant-professor-of-creative-production-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Creative Production (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Creative Production (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 541508 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Information Technology Department: 23030000 - CJC-MEDIA PROD, MGMT, TECH Classification Title: AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: A terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or equivalent) in film, media production, or a related field by August 2027 A strong record of creative achievement, such as award-winning films, documentaries aired on network or streaming services, VR experiences showcased at SIGGRAPH, or new media installations. Evidence of teaching/mentoring effectiveness The candidate should demonstrate their potential in establishing a national and/or international reputation. Job Description: The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) in the University of Florida's College of Journalism and Communications seeks to hire a tenure-track Assistant Professor with a demonstrated record of creative production to begin Fall 2027. We seek a dynamic scholar/practitioner whose creative output advances storytelling through innovative media formats. We value candidates who push boundaries in media innovation and foster content that addresses contemporary social issues, cultural narratives, or technological disruptions. The ideal candidate will demonstrate excellence in creative production while maintaining industry connections. Areas of expertise may include feature-length and short films, television series and specials, documentary filmmaking, emerging technologies such as augmented reality (AR) or virtual reality (VR), streaming series development and production, or hybrid formats that blend narrative with interactive elements. This role emphasizes a robust portfolio of creative work—such as films, documentaries, series, or interactive projects—alongside teaching and service contributions. The MPMT department recognizes the critical importance of creative tenure tracks where artistic output is valued as highly as traditional scholarly research. Tenure evaluation will prioritize impactful creative scholarship, evidenced by honors and awards at nationally recognized film festivals, publications in creative outlets, peer-reviewed exhibitions, grants from bodies like the National Endowment for the Arts, or collaborations with industry leaders. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses in media production (for example, filmmaking techniques; camera and lens systems; documentary storytelling; VR/AR production; streaming content creation; mentoring students in hands-on projects; supervising capstone productions) and integrating emerging technologies into the curriculum. Candidates are expected to maintain an active agenda of original creative work, secure external funding, and disseminate outputs through festivals, broadcasts, online platforms, or academic conferences. Service duties include department and college committees, advising student media organizations, and contributing to college initiatives and other duties as assigned. About the Department of Media Production, Management, & Technology The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology is one of the premier programs in offering courses related to mass media and emerging media. Our curriculum prepares students for the media industry through two specializations: Digital Film and Video Production, and Media Management and Technology. The department has about 500 student majors and 16 full-time faculty. Students from the department pursue careers in film production, TV program production, social media management, brand management, consumer analysis, UI/UX design, and digital marketing, among others. Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the College of Journalism and Communications We are recognized as a national leader in communication scholarship and professional skills development. Be part of an ambitious, progressive, and collaborative program at one of the top public research universities in the United States. Accredited by ACEJMC, the college has earned its reputation for excellence over the last half-century on the strength of its programs, faculty, students, and alumni; the stories they tell; and the difference they make around the world. With over 70 full-time faculty members and 175 part- time instructors serving in four academic departments— Advertising; Journalism; Media Production, Management, and Technology; and Public Relations —we enroll about 2,500 undergraduates both in-person and online. Additionally, more than 700 graduate students are earning master’s and doctoral degrees. The College of Journalism and Communications offers multiple research and teaching environments, including the Atlas Lab for social media listening; the Consortium on Trust in Media and Technology; the STEM Translational Communication Center; the Center for Public Interest Communications; the Media, Effects, and Technology Lab; the Institute for Public Relations; The Agency, a full- service student-run advertising and public relations agency; the Innovation News Center; Division of Media Properties; and more. The College is highly involved in UF’s AI Initiative. Homepage - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the University of Florida Our university ranked in the top 6 among public universities, in the top 28 overall, and as the number 1 in online undergraduate programs by the 2024 U.S. News and World Report. UF continues to expand its faculty ranks and the impact of its research and curriculum. UF faculty members annually generate more than $1 billion in research expenditures, with a comprehensive long-term AI initiative (https://ai.ufl.edu/about/) and access to state-of-the-art infrastructure, including one of the most powerful supercomputers in the U.S. The University of Florida is a member of the Association of American Universities and is categorized in the Carnegie Commission’s top tier of research universities. UF has a student body of more than 60,000 who come from all 50 states in the United States and more than 100 countries. The university and greater Gainesville communities enjoy a number of different cultural events, restaurants, year- round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. he University of Florida is situated in north-central Florida, a region renowned for its abundant natural beauty. Gainesville is within a two-hour drive of Tampa, Orlando, Jacksonville, the Atlantic Ocean, and the Gulf of Mexico. About Gainesville As home to Florida's largest and oldest university, Gainesville (http://cityofgainesville.org (http://cityofgainesville.org/)) is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville and the surrounding area is home to about 250,000 people, a robust arts community, several museums, a world-class health care system anchored at UF, a downtown core known for its restaurants and brew pubs, excellent public and private schools, and abundant nature exemplified by clear, natural springs. Additionally, within 2 hours are attractions such as Disney World, Universal Studios, Busch Gardens, and historic St. Augustine. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. (https://www.youtube.com/watch? v=e_7nUKAFanM) UF Benefits At the University of Florida, you will not only find competitive salaries, but you’ll enjoy elevated benefits and leave options, tuition assistance programs, and stability and support with opportunities for advancement and career growth that only UF can offer. To view the university’s Total Reward benefit package, please click here, https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or equivalent) in film, media production, or a related field by August 2027 A strong record of creative achievement, such as award-winning films, documentaries aired on network or streaming services, VR experiences showcased at SIGGRAPH, or new media installations. Evidence of teaching/mentoring effectiveness The candidate should demonstrate their potential in establishing a national and/or international reputation. Preferred: Expertise in software such as Adobe Premiere, After Effects, DaVinci Resolve, Unity for VR, or data-driven tools for audience engagement; experience in streaming series productions; screenings or selections at mid-major or major film festivals; familiarity with ethical considerations in AI-assisted production or sustainable filmmaking practices. Experience teaching at the university-level. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online via the UF Careers portal at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ . Submit: cover letter (be creative in explaining how your unique talents, creative vision, and professional experiences will advance MPMT's mission of valuing creative output as scholarship, enhance our curriculum, and benefit our students) curriculum vitae; portfolio links or samples of creative work (e.g., reels, scripts, VR demos); teaching statement; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications begins November 1, 2026 and continues until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. For inquiries, contact Search Committee Chair Yu-Hao Lee leeyuhao@jou.ufl.edu If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, creed, color, religion, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender identify and expression, marital status, national origin, political opinions or affiliations, genetic information and veteran status in also aspects of employment including recruitment, hiring, promotion, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits and training. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination, and reference, be available for public inspection. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541510/data-management-analyst-iii-uf-department-of-emergency-medicine": {
      "title": "Data Management Analyst III – UF Department of Emergency Medicine",
      "description": "Data Management Analyst III – UF Department of Emergency Medicine Apply now Job no: 541510 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29290100 - MD-EMERGENCY MED-AED Classification Title: Data Management Analyst III Classification Minimum Requirements Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida, College of Medicine, Department of Emergency Medicine (DEM), actively seeks a motivated and experienced individual to join us as the Data Management Analyst III . Leveraging advanced statistical methods and data visualization tools, this role will analyze performance data to identify trends, uncover opportunities for improvement, and generate insightful reports for senior leadership. By collaborating with cross-functional stakeholders, they tailor data insights to support informed decision-making across the organization. This role ensures data quality and integrity by establishing governance procedures, monitoring data consistency, and setting data collection standards. Additionally, this role will develop interactive performance dashboards, providing stakeholders with real-time access to key metrics in a clear and actionable format. Through trend analysis and forecasting, they will help anticipate future challenges and opportunities, enabling proactive strategy development to drive continuous performance improvement. The UF Department of Emergency Medicine proudly maintains an affiliation with UF Health Shands Hospital, a distinguished 1,162-bed teaching facility renowned for its Level 1 trauma center and cutting-edge burn center. As the primary referral center for North Central Florida, our institution stands as a beacon of excellence in healthcare. This affiliation underscores our commitment to top-tier medical care and innovation. Key Responsibilities: Performance Measurement and Analysis Lead AAAEM Benchmark Survey submissions utilizing Business Objects. Collaborate with the health system to ensure data consistency and alignment with submissions like Vizient. Support administration of Earned Variable Compensation (EVC) with required wRVUs and other associated data to accurately administer multiple plans. Develop and implement KPIs aligned with departmental goals to assess provider performance, operational efficiency, and productivity. Utilize statistical analysis and data visualization tools to identify trends, patterns, and areas for improvement. Conduct trend analysis and forecasting to anticipate challenges and opportunities, supporting proactive decision-making. Data Management and Visualization Establish and maintain data governance procedures to ensure accuracy, consistency, and integrity in performance data. Create interactive dashboards and reports that translate complex data into actionable insights for key stakeholders. Manage the end-to-end lifecycle of departmental data requests, including intake form administration, prioritization of requests, coordination with data resources, tracking deliverables, and communication of results to key stakeholders. Monitor data quality and refine data collection processes to enhance reporting accuracy. Stakeholder Collaboration and Reporting Work closely with leadership, clinicians, and operational teams to understand their performance needs and provide tailored analytical support. Liaise with Shands Decision Support Services (DSS) team to build and update Emergency Medicine data reports. Generate comprehensive reports to inform strategic decisions and operational improvements. Effectively communicate data insights through visual storytelling and presentations to diverse audiences. The City of Gainesville: Gainesville, home to the University of Florida, is ranked #1 best city to move to in Florida by USA Today. Centrally located in North Florida, it offers a blend of small-town charm and big-city amenities, with close proximity to major airports, family entertainment, and some of the best beaches in the world. Known for its beautiful landscapes and urban forest, Gainesville is a hidden gem that provides year-round outdoor activities, award-winning college sports, and a rich cultural scene. The University of Florida and UF Health are the leading employers in the area, making Gainesville an excellent place to work and raise a family. Learn more about Florida’s premiere health system, UF Health, and the advantages of living in Gainesville at UF Health Destination . Expected Salary: The salary range is $85,000 - $90,000 and is commensurate with education and experience. At the University of Florida, we are committed to the well-being and professional growth of our employees. Our benefits package is designed to support your health, work-life balance, and financial security: Generous Paid Leave: Enjoy 22 days of paid vacation, 13 days of paid sick leave, 11 paid Holidays, and 8 weeks of paid parental leave. Individual and Family Health Insurance Benefits State of Florida Retirement Plan Options State of Florida Basic Life Insurance Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Professional Development Opportunities Wellness Programs available to employees For more details on our comprehensive benefits, visit UF Benefits . Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred Strong expertise in performance measurement, data analysis, and statistical methods. Proficiency in data visualization tools such as Power BI, Tableau, or similar platforms. Experience in healthcare operations, clinical workflows, and data governance best practices. Excellent communication skills to translate complex data insights into actionable recommendations. Analytical problem-solving skills. Ability to work collaboratively with cross-functional teams and senior leadership. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, candidates must submit the following documents: A cover letter detailing relevant experience and qualifications. A current resume/CV. For questions, please reach out to EM-Careers@ufl.edu Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541513/assistant-vice-president-development": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice President, Development",
      "description": "Assistant Vice President, Development Apply now Job no: 541513 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Advancement Department: 30010100 - JX-DEAN-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: AST VP, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and eight years of appropriate experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and ten years of appropriate experience with demonstrated success soliciting gifts at $1 million and above in an institution of higher education. Job Description: The Assistant Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer, UF Health – Northeast will build and oversee a comprehensive philanthropy program for UF Health Northeast. The incumbent will report to the Sr. Vice President & Northeast Regional President, - UF Health (SVPNRP), the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, and the Senior Associate Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer – UF Health. The AVP/CAO-NE will oversee development, grateful patient fundraising, annual giving, stewardship, capital projects, and CMN programs for UF Health’s Northeast units, including UF Health Jacksonville and, UF Health St. Johns and UF Health Durbin Park. The incumbent will serve as the primary point of contact for academic and hospital stakeholders in the Northeast for development-related activity and will collaborate with other members of the HSC Development leadership team to foster a culture of philanthropy. This position will manage a high-level portfolio of leadership and principal gift prospects and will lead the UFHNE Advancement teams and inspire a culture of high performance, collegiality, and professionalism throughout the organization. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Leadership/Management Create and oversee new campaign strategic plans for UF Health Northeast, and annual work plans for the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville and SVPNRP – Northeast, providing support and ensuring implementation of key elements, and aligning with the newly integrated health system strategic plans for the Northeast. Meet regularly with UF Health leadership and partner on philanthropy-related presentations and initiatives. Serve as a strategic member of UF Health – Northeast hospitals’ collaborative senior leadership team. Lead and manage high-performing teams, ensuring alignment with the goals of annual performance and business plans and assuming overall responsibility for implementation of strategic initiatives for UF Health – Jacksonville hospital, the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, UF Health – St. Johns, and UF Health Durbin Park Provide direction, strategic planning, leadership, counsel on matters relating to fundraising and operations. Ensure accountability and oversee staffing, including establishing goals in alignment with unit objectives, and develop and implement appropriate metrics. Evaluate staff, develop a strong sense of teamwork, and establish an environment of mentoring and support. Coach the team and bring cohesion to the department. Participate as part of the senior leadership team in setting direction and developing strategy for UF Health in partnership with the Chief Advancement Officer for UF Health. Special Initiatives & Campaign Development Lead in the creation and promotion of a culture of philanthropy at UF Health-NE. Effectively involve stakeholders, including center/institute directors, department chairs, physicians, administrators, volunteers and donors in fundraising efforts. Support and enhance a dynamic grateful patient philanthropy program. Implement best practices, oversee development of programs and materials, and determine allocation of resources to generate maximum return on investment. Provide coaching for leadership on strategic plans, business plans, and campaign planning and development activities, including cultivation/solicitation strategies. Collaborate with the UF Advancement to maximize philanthropic opportunities. Support and partner with the Sr. Associate Vice President/ Chief Advancement Officer – UF Health and UF Health leadership in Northeast on all major fundraising initiatives. Develop and manage campaign prospect pipeline for UF Health – Northeast and the College of Medicine - Jacksonville. Meet annual targets for the number of donor visits, proposals submitted, and gifts closed. Monitor all donor information; provide and present statistical analysis to leadership. Successfully oversee the plan for raising funds to support campaign priorities, including scholarships, endowed professorships/faculty support and select capital projects. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Portfolio Management Develop and maintain a personal fundraising agenda focused on leadership and principal gift prospects and donors. Strategically managing relationships through face-to-face visits and cultivation and stewardship activities to bring solicitations to closure. Develop and present major gift proposals and stewardship programs to acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and align to the vision, mission, and values of the unit, the University of Florida, and UF Advancement. Represent UF Health at donor and business and community events as assigned. Effectively articulate and communicate UF Health needs and priorities, particularly those among the Northeast units, within the context of the overall mission, goals and objectives. Expected Salary: Salary is competitive and commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: A valid Florida driver license. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Eight to ten years of progressively responsible leadership experience in development with a demonstrated track record of successful fundraising in a large-scale university and medical services environment or one of similar complexity. Demonstrated ability and expertise in building fundraising and engagement programs. Track record of recruiting, developing, and retaining a high caliber staff, building a team, and creatively organizing and managing them to maximize the support of external constituencies. Excellent organizational and operational skills; confidence and independence in exercising leadership skills and in representing UF Health to external constituents. Ability to construct, articulate, and implement an annual strategic development plan for a department. Demonstrated ability to support UF Health’s mission, particularly in managing the responsibilities of the department, including strategic and operational planning at the department level as well as establishing metrics and processes to monitor and evaluate operational performance outcomes and implement performance improvement initiatives when required. Demonstrated ability to provide management oversight, leadership, and strategic and tactical direction. A strong understanding of budgeting practices and financial accountability and the ability to manage resources with prudence and efficiency. Ability to work both independently and as a team player who will productively engage with others at varying levels of seniority within and outside UF Health. Experience engaging, training, motivation, and staffing volunteers in the cultivation, solicitation, and closing of significant gifts; appreciation for the role of volunteers in building an effective development network. Ability to demonstrate the highest standards of ethics as accepted by the professional fundraising community. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. A commitment to and support for workplace inclusion in all its forms; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee is being assisted by the executive search firm ZRG Aspen Leadership Group. To view the position prospectus, please visit the ZRG Aspen Leadership Group website: Assistant Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer, Northeast Florida Region To nominate a candidate or inquire about the position, please contact Steven Wallace at stevenwallace@aspenleadershipgroup.com . In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541514/assistantassociate-professor-of-organic-chemistry": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541514 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Chemistry, invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Organic Chemistry, broadly defined. The appointments will commence on August 16, 2027. Successful candidates are expected to conduct innovative research at the highest level and to establish an outstanding, independent, externally funded research program. We seek candidates with a record of excellence in research and scholarship, the ability to excel in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and who will contribute to the service mission of the Department of Chemistry and the University of Florida. For more information about the Department of Chemistry, please visit our website, www.chem.ufl.edu . The University of Florida is a land grant, sea grant, and space grant university and home to the most comprehensive university health sciences facilities in the southeast. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with rank, qualifications and experience. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A complete curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. A brief research summary A description of proposed research projects. A statement of teaching philosophy. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540794/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 540794 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Artificial Intelligence, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29240101 - MD-HOBI-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: The Division of Biomedical Informatics and Data Science within the Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics (HOBI) at the University of Florida (UF) is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher with a strong background in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence, machine learning, or data science. The successful candidate will join a collaborative and interdisciplinary research team to contribute to cutting-edge research at the intersection of AI/ML, real-world data, and health outcomes. Ongoing projects leverage large-scale multimodal clinical data, including electronic health records (EHRs), medical imaging, genomics, wearable devices, and unstructured clinical text, to develop novel methods for disease prediction, precision medicine, and clinical decision support. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, or a related STEM field. Experience with EHR data, machine learning or deep learning, natural language processing, medical imaging, or large language models (LLMs) is highly desirable. Familiarity with causal inference, real-world evidence, multimodal data integration, or cloud/high-performance computing is a plus. The Postdoctoral Researcher will collaborate closely with biomedical informaticians, clinicians, statisticians, and translational researchers across the University of Florida. Responsibilities include developing and evaluating AI/ML methods, analyzing large-scale clinical datasets, building computational tools and software, preparing manuscripts for publication in high-impact journals, presenting research at scientific conferences, and contributing to NIH and other federal grant proposals. The position offers excellent opportunities to lead research projects, publish extensively, develop new methodological expertise, and establish an independent research program within a highly collaborative and well-funded research environment. • Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, biostatistics, or a related quantitative STEM field. • Strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience with SQL and Linux/Unix is desirable. • Demonstrated research experience in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, or data science, including the analysis of large-scale biomedical or healthcare datasets (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, or other real-world data). • Excellent written and oral communication skills. • Ability to work effectively in a collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment. Job Description: Conduct independent and collaborative research in biomedical informatics, including the development and application of artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and statistical methods to analyze large-scale, multimodal clinical data (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, wearable data, and unstructured clinical text). Design, implement, validate, and optimize computational methods and analytical pipelines to address clinically relevant research questions. Develop, implement, and maintain research software, data processing pipelines, and computational tools to support data integration, extraction, transformation, quality assurance, and analysis. Contribute to reproducible research practices, software documentation, and code sharing. Participate in the design, execution, analysis, and dissemination of research studies. Prepare scientific manuscripts, conference presentations, technical reports, and other scholarly products. Contribute to the interpretation of findings and collaborate with multidisciplinary teams on the publication of research results. Expected Salary: $61,008 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: • Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, biostatistics, or a related quantitative STEM field. • Strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience with SQL and Linux/Unix is desirable. • Demonstrated research experience in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, or data science, including the analysis of large-scale biomedical or healthcare datasets (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, or other real-world data). • Excellent written and oral communication skills. • Ability to work effectively in a collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment. Preferred: Experience working with electronic health records (EHRs), real-world data (RWD), clinical data warehouses, or other healthcare databases. Experience developing and applying machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing (NLP), computer vision, or large language models (LLMs) for biomedical or healthcare research. Experience integrating and analyzing multimodal data, including structured EHR data, clinical text, medical imaging, genomics, wearable devices, or other biomedical data sources. Familiarity with causal inference, statistical learning, clinical prediction modeling, or real-world evidence methodologies. Experience developing reproducible research software, computational pipelines, or open-source tools using modern software engineering practices (e.g., Git/GitHub, Docker, workflow management). Evidence of scholarly productivity through peer-reviewed publications and presentations at scientific conferences. Experience working on NIH-funded or other federally funded research projects and contributing to grant proposal development. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541386/assistant-professor-in-statistics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Statistics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Statistics Apply now Job no: 541386 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16480000 - LS-STATISTICS Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Statistics, Biostatistics, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Job Description: The Department of Statistics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for two full-time (1.0 FTE), nine-month, tenure-accruing Assistant Professor positions beginning August 16, 2027. We seek outstanding candidates with research interests in causal inference, Bayesian computation, statistics, machine learning, or related areas. Faculty responsibilities include developing and maintaining an active research program, teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, mentoring students, and contributing services to the department, university, and profession. The Department of Statistics has a long-standing reputation for excellence in research, education, and innovation, with a proud history of hosting distinguished scholars and producing impactful research. As one of the nation's leading public research universities, the University of Florida provides exceptional institutional support and fosters a highly collaborative academic environment. This search is part of the University of Florida's strategic initiative to expand the role of artificial intelligence in higher education and research. The university is home to HiPerGator , one of the most powerful high-performance computing systems at a U.S. public university, providing substantial computational resources to support cutting-edge research. Applicants are encouraged to learn more about the Department of Statistics and its programs by visiting https: Department of Statistics and about the University's AI initiative at https: Artificial Intelligence . UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked 7th in the country among public universities (US News and World Report 2025 rankings), and 1st among public institutions in the Wall Street Journal 2023 survey. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Statistics, Biostatistics, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae including statistics teaching background and experience A statement on teaching and research goals. Three confidential letters of recommendation. Note, the names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 3, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541387/lecturer-statistics": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Statistics",
      "description": "Lecturer - Statistics Apply now Job no: 541387 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16480000 - LS-STATISTICS Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: MS in Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Job Description: The Department of Statistics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time (1.0 FTE), 9-month, non-tenure-accruing Lecturer position beginning August 16, 2027. This is a renewable academic-year appointment. The successful candidate will primarily teach advanced undergraduate statistics courses for Statistics majors and advanced undergraduate courses for Data Science majors offered by the Department of Statistics. Experience or interest in online instruction is desirable. The department is home to thriving undergraduate programs in both Statistics and Data Science and is committed to excellence in teaching and student success. The University of Florida and the Department of Statistics highly value the contributions of teaching faculty. To support professional growth and long-term career development, lecturers are eligible for promotion through the ranks of Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, and Master Lecturer. This search is part of a university-wide initiative to expand the role of artificial intelligence in higher education and research. Applicants are encouraged to learn more about the University of Florida's AI efforts at Artificial Intelligence . The university is also home to HiPerGator , one of the most powerful high-performance computing systems at a U.S. public university. Additional information about the Department of Statistics, its academic programs, and faculty can be found at Department of Statistics . UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked top 10 public universities. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: MS in Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Preferred Qualifications: PhD. In Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae including statistics teaching background and experience A link to a short (5-10 minute) instructional video prepared by the applicant on a topic of their choice Three confidential letters of recommendation. Note: the names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541393/utilities-supervisor": {
      "title": "Utilities Supervisor",
      "description": "Utilities Supervisor Apply now Job no: 541393 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Environmental/Occupational Safety Department: 63700100 - FS-UTILITIES-ADMIN Classification Title: Utilities Supervisor Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma and five year of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Manages the Locates of all underground utility facilities for accuracy and within the state’s regulations. Responsible for field training and developing technical staff in highly technical work involving operating equipment and instruments in order to locate, mark and protect main and service lines used for electric, chilled water, steam, water, wastewater, fiber-optic, gas, and reclaimed water facilities. Coordinate resources to ensure the department meets required regulated response times and avoid reasonable contractors’ delays while protecting the university assets. Maintains equipment and coordinates supplies by following purchasing policies and monitoring inventory. Approves direct reports hourly and leave time. Develops, maintains, and enforces production, safety, and work standards. Maintains UF compliance with Utilisphere / Sunshine One Call regulations. Responsible for assisting with locating in the repair of all Utility systems during emergencies. Manages and coordinates with contractors dig permit applications. Responsible for assisting the applicant in the initiation the permit application by providing instructions regarding the process to the applicant, coordination of the process with other related University procedures, scheduling locale of University-owned underground utilities for the applicant. Reviews, approves and completes documentation related to work accomplished including UF Dig Permits. Ensures that all applicants follow the procedures according to the University of Florida Dig Permit, Sunshine One call and OSHA Excavation guide lines. Coordinates field appointments with contractors, engineers and other utilities to ensure locate and mark buried facilities in the vicinity of construction work are communicated effectively. The lead responsible for updating, maintaining, and coordinating Geographic Information System (GIS) utility records by coordinating field findings with mapping technicians. Responsible for reviewing and using utility mapping and record information through GIS, AutoCAD, and construction prints; identifying and communicating record discrepancies; and providing timely field information to support accurate utility locates, dig permits, maintenance, and construction activities. Assists the utilities staff and project managers on location of existing underground assets and assists in identifying underground assets as they are discovered. Ability to accurately distinguish and interpret colors as necessary to perform the essential functions of the position. Valid Florida Driver’s license required at time of appointment. Criminal Background Check and post offer physical assessment is required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: This position is an Exempt position with schedule reflecting 5 days a week; Monday – Friday; total hours per week 40. Rotating On-Call schedule within the group to cover emergency utility failures and emergency locate requests. Expected Salary: $55,000.00 to $75,000.00 Annually Required Qualifications: High school diploma and five year of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Five years’ experience in multiple utility facility location for the University of Florida. Knowledge of approved principles, methods, materials used in electric, water, wastewater, gas, reclaimed water and telecommunications construction, maintenance and repair work. Knowledge of occupational hazards and safety precautions of construction work. Must be able to interpret utilities systems construction blueprints and drawings. Must be able to work effectively with co-workers and the general public. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541411/assistant-professor-in-criminal-justice-system": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Criminal Justice System",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Criminal Justice System Apply now Job no: 541411 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16920000 - LS-SOCIOLOGY CRIMINOLOGY&LAW Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Criminology or Criminal Justice, Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Job Description: The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences invites applications for two full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing positions beginning August 16, 2027 at the rank of Assistant Professor with research and teaching interests related to US criminal justice systems broadly defined. These positions support the Crime and Justice component of our program in Criminology & Law. We seek scholars who study the practice and implementation of criminal and/or juvenile justice systems, for example, local, state, and/or Federal law enforcement, courts, sentencing and/or corrections, capital punishment, juvenile programming and prevention. Applicant research programs can emphasize theory development, methodological applications (quantitative, qualitative and/or mixed), and/or policy analysis (including program evaluation). We seek applicants whose records show commitment to rigorous scholarship with the promise of external funding, as well as excellence in teaching. The ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students is highly desirable. Advanced assistant professors are welcome to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to teach in their topical areas of interest and one or more of our required courses, which include research methods, criminological theory, and/or advanced principles of criminal justice. At the graduate level, successful applicants may also be able to teach required courses in our quantitative methods sequence or a criminal justice process course in addition to their area of interest. The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law is a large and dynamic department with nearly 700 undergraduate Criminology majors and roughly 20 Criminology, Law and Society graduate students. The Department has two undergraduate programs, both offered via in-person and online courses, and minors in Sociology of Social Justice & Policy and Environmental Justice & Policy. Faculty conduct research in diverse areas, including criminology and criminal justice; psychology and law; families and gender; health, aging, and the life course; environment and resources; and race and ethnicity. Many faculty are affiliated with other academic programs at UF, including the School of Natural Resources and the Environment, the Center for Latin American Studies, the Center for European Studies, the Department of Gender, Sexuality and Women’s Studies, and the Health Science Center. More information about the Department is available at https://soccrim.clas.ufl.edu/ , including biographical information about our faculty, students, and staff; and a description of the various projects and research interests in which our members are involved. Salary & Benefits: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Florida, a top-ranked public research institution, offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package to support employees’ health, financial security, and work-life balance. Comprehensive medical, dental, vision, and prescription coverage. Life insurance, long-term disability, accident, and illness insurance. Retirement savings and investment options with employer contributions. Generous paid time includes vacation, sick leave, annual holidays, personal holidays, and winter break. 8 weeks of paid parental and medical leave. Access to wellness programs, flexible spending accounts, and an employee assistance program. Discounts for on-campus and off-campus activities and services Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Criminology or Criminal Justice, Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Preferred: Evidence of rigorous scholarship and the potential to obtain external funding Evidence of excellence in teaching and the ability to mentor graduate students Ability to contribute to diverse theoretical viewpoints and open academic exchanges through scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, experience, and suitability for the position. (2) a complete curriculum vitae . (3) a statement on teaching and research goals. (4) the names and email addresses of three references. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning October 20, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541449/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541449 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Theoretical and computational quantum information Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Physics, Computer Science or closely related areas Job Description: A Postdoctoral Associate position in theoretical and computational quantum information science and condensed matter physics is available in the group led by Prof. Haining Pan in the Department of Physics at the University of Florida, starting Fall 2027. The initial appointment is for two years, renewed annually, and may be extended for a third year contingent upon strong performance, mutual satisfaction, and the availability of funds. The successful candidate will conduct numerical and theoretical research on quantum computing, AI-accelerated quantum simulation and materials discovery, quantum machine learning, topological quantum materials, and/or related areas. The successful candidate will have access to HiPerGator, the fastest university-owned supercomputer in the United States (TOP500), including 504 NVIDIA Blackwell B200 GPUs. These resources are provisioned directly through the group's institutional allocation. The University of Florida is the state's flagship land-grant, Carnegie R1, and AAU research university, ranked among the top 10 U.S. public universities (U.S. News & World Report, 2027). The Department of Physics hosts a large condensed matter and quantum information community of roughly twenty-five faculty across theory and experiment. The department operates the High B/T Facility of the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory through its Microkelvin Laboratory. Faculty also participate in the Center for Condensed Matter Sciences and the Quantum Theory Project, among the world's largest centers for the theory and computation of complex molecular and materials systems. Gainesville offers a substantially lower cost of living than most major research hubs and lies within about two hours of Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa; Florida levies no state income tax Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Physics, Computer Science or closely related areas Preferred: Demonstrated strength in one or more of the following. - Quantum many-body numerics: tensor-network methods, variational Monte Carlo (including neural quantum states), stabilizer and near-Clifford simulation, Gaussian/free-fermion methods, open-system dynamics (Lindblad master equations). - Machine learning for physical systems: deep learning, reinforcement learning, transformer architectures and large language models. - Analytical many-body and quantum information theory: replica field theory approaches to monitored and noisy quantum circuits, symmetry-protected topological phases, topological quantum computation, quantum error correction. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a cover letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae, (3) a statement on research goals, and (4) a list of at least three references. Application reviews will begin Dec 15, 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Haining Pan at panhaining@ufl.edu . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Jun 2027 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541469/postdoctoral-associate-crop-transformation-center-ctc": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Crop Transformation Center (CTC)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Crop Transformation Center (CTC) Apply now Job no: 541469 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is Postdoctoral Associate position available in the Baysal Lab within the Horticultural Sciences Department and the Crop Transformation Center (UF/IFAS CTC). The Baysal Lab seeks a motivated Postdoctoral Associate to advance plant transformation and genome engineering. The position focuses on developing virus-mediated delivery systems, with the goal of achieving tissue-culture-free, transgene-free, heritable edits in planta . The successful candidate will design and execute experiments in citrus and other crop species, using viral vectors. The position also includes coordinating day-to-day lab operations and contributing to experimental design, data analysis, and manuscript preparation. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The Horticultural Sciences Department is a unit within IFAS at the University of Florida and has diverse teaching, research, and extension education programs with 34 faculty in Gainesville and 26 faculty at various Research and Education Centers throughout the state. The Horticultural Sciences Department is currently ranked top 9 in the center for world university rankings. The Center for Crop Transformation , founded in 2023, addresses the need for rapid development and commercialization of genetically improved crops. The CTC is directed and led by Dr. Charlie Messina, a professor of predictive breeding specializing in symbiotic AI with over 16 years in the private sector. The center aims to accelerate genetic gain in specialty crops, ultimately improving human and environmental health. Their primary focus is creating citrus varieties that can tolerate or eventually resist citrus greening disease (HLB). The CTC harnesses core capabilities to activate an end-to-end approach to translate these novel ideas into products for Florida farmers and consumers. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in plant molecular biology, plant biotechnology, biochemistry or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Experience with molecular cloning and vector construction. Proficiency in standard molecular analyses: PCR, qPCR, DNA/RNA extraction, Sanger and amplicon sequencing, and analysis of editing outcomes. Track record of first-author peer-reviewed publications. Strong written and oral communication in English, and the ability to work independently and collaboratively. Experience with plant RNA or DNA virus vectors, VIGS, or virus-induced gene editing. Experience with CRISPR-Cas reagent design and delivery in plants. Familiarity with NGS data analysis and command-line bioinformatics (e.g., CRISPResso, Python or R). Experience mentoring students or technicians and coordinating lab operations. Special Instructions to Applicants: Employment Conditions This position is available November 2026 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF by 11:55 PM of the closing date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541470/postdoctoral-associate-nursery-science-production-and-agrophysics": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Nursery Science - Production and Agrophysics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Nursery Science - Production and Agrophysics Apply now Job no: 541470 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . A postdoctoral Research Associate position is available in the Environmental Nursery Production lab at the University of Florida with an emphasis on establishing fundamental research that will unlock insight into the relationship between plants, substrate and environment in outdoor nursery crop production. This position will focus on developing and publishing high-caliber research that will expand our understanding of the substrate-plant-atmosphere continuum in commercial nurseries and lead to improved production practices for ornamental horticulture. The postdoctoral associate will spend approximately 50% of their time on original, fundamental research designed collaboratively with the PI to advance our understanding of the connectivity between nursery production, the environment, and the physical world. This includes developing concepts, initiating trials, collecting, analyzing and interpreting data, with the expectation of publishing in Q1 journals. The remaining 50% of the postdoctoral associate’s time will be dedicated to advancing ornamental production practices and moving applied nursery research forward. This includes analyzing data, generating reports, and writing manuscripts for publication with the PI, mentoring junior scientists and supporting additional lab activities. These endeavors involve exploration of innovative concepts and ideas to advance production techniques, understanding of plant stress response, substrate science, irrigation technologies, and fertilizer efficiency. Thus, this position will divide their time between fundamental nursery-based agrophysics research and supporting the lab’s motto of “Applied Solutions” all with a focus on container-grown ornamental plants in open-air and protected nursery cultivation systems. Overall, this position will participate in projects focusing on plant physiology, abiotic stress, water relations, biophysics, root zone temperature management, understanding the substrate-plant-water continuum, substrate engineering, and environmental modeling. This is an in-person position in Dr. Jeb S. Fields’ lab, which focuses on developing production applications for the ornamental horticulture industry. All facilities and space necessary for success will be provided. To accentuate the postdoctoral experience, we encourage and support participation in additional collaborative research, program development, grant writing, extension, and mentoring of junior scientists. There will be opportunities to present data and build industry networks. Moreover, the postdoctoral associate will have regular dedicated collaborative mentoring time with PI for support in research and career opportunities, with the aim of securing gainful employment after the program. The postdoctoral associate position is a 12-month full-time position, and renewable annually for up to three years depending on productivity. The pay is set at $58,656 / year and comes with associated benefits. An option for 0.75 FTE is also available. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The Horticultural Sciences Department is a unit within IFAS at the University of Florida and has diverse teaching, research, and extension education programs with 34 faculty in Gainesville and 26 faculty at various Research and Education Centers throughout the state. The Horticultural Sciences Department is currently ranked top 9 in the center for world university rankings. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in horticulture, soil science, environmental science, biophysics or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Experience with plant production Aptitude in scientific writing, data visualization and interpretation Advanced modeling/programming skills Proficiency with environmental remote sensing and interpretation Familiarity with HYDRUS computer models Self-sufficiency with statistical concepts and programs Self-motivated, well-organized, team player, and critical thinker Good written and verbal communication skills in English Driver’s license Ability to lift 30 lbs. Special Instructions to Applicants: Employment Conditions This position is available November 2026 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go to Careers at UF by 11:55 PM of the closing date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541471/ast-prof": {
      "title": "AST PROF",
      "description": "AST PROF Apply now Job no: 541471 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Construction/Design/Planning, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 15060000 - DCP-URBAN REGIONAL PLANNING Classification Title: Assistant Professor in Urban and Regional Planning Classification Minimum Requirements: The candidate must have a PhD in urban and regional planning, urban studies, geography, public policy, finance and real estate, public administration, or related field. A successful candidate must have expertise in housing and a strong background in the planning field, either through education or experience, to enable them to teach specialized and core courses for the MURP and BUSP programs. The PhD must be completed prior to the start date of August 16, 2027. An ABD at the time of interviews will be considered with a letter from the candidate’s supervisor confirming the candidate’s date of graduation. Job Description: The Department of Urban and Regional Planning within the University of Florida’s College of Design, Construction and Planning invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-accruing faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in August 2027. The Department encourages applications from candidates with a background in the planning field and expertise in housing . The Department has particular interest in candidates whose work incorporates housing policy and finance, resiliency and disaster recovery in housing, socio-economic sustainability, and/or land use . Preferred candidates will have a proven track record of engaging with public interest concerns and interdisciplinary scholarship. Key Responsibilities The selected candidate will conduct research and publications commensurate with a tenure-track position and be entrepreneurial in obtaining research grants. The candidate should be open to collaborating with other faculty and researchers, including the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies, Florida Institute for Built Environment Resilience (FIBER), GeoPlan (for more, see below), and establishing relationships with related discipline programs on campus, such as real estate and political science. The candidate should also engage with housing innovation and practitioners working with Florida communities and beyond. The position requires teaching a total of four courses per year (two in fall and two in spring), which may be graduate and upper-level undergraduate courses delivered in-person and online in the candidate’s areas of expertise and in general planning topics. Anticipated courses include introduction to housing, housing finance, advanced housing topics, and at least one core general planning course. The successful candidate should have a well-developed pedagogy that includes methods to engage a multicultural student body. The candidate would also be expected to conduct service and community outreach consistent with the missions of the Department and the land-grant University. The Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning consists of a collegial faculty of fourteen core members and an additional eighteen members affiliated with research centers and other academic programs. Our faculty is the most productive in the College and consistently attracts the highest amount of research funding, with close to $4 million in new grants secured in the past year. We actively support mentorship of junior faculty, promote career development at every level, and foster a family-friendly environment. The Department, founded in 1975, offers an accredited Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), a Master of Science in Urban Analytics (MSUA), a PhD URP concentration with STEM designation, a graduate minor in URP and certificate in UA, an undergraduate minor in URP, and a combined (4+1) bachelor-master degree. The Department’s fully online MURP was the country’s first and remains the only accredited online program. In 2023, the MURP program (campus and online) was ranked 22 nd in the nation, sixth in the South, and the 10 th largest by enrollment. The Department recently established a Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning (BUSP) that began in Fall 2026. The School and College As one of the two units in the School of Landscape Architecture and Planning , the Department of Urban and Regional Planning has a strong relationship with the Department of Landscape Architecture, as well as other units in the College, which include Architecture, Construction Management, Interior Design, Historic Preservation, and Sustainability and the Built Environment. Located in the Rinker School of Construction Management, the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies is the state’s primary public source of housing data for policymakers and local governments, and conducts wide-ranging research on housing topics such as affordability, preservation of assisted housing, and the intersections of housing with disaster recovery and climate resilience. In addition, the School and College are home to a range of research institutes and centers, including the GeoPlan Center, the Center for Health and the Built Environment, the Center for Landscape Conservation Planning, and FIBER. Other collaboration opportunities exist with the School of Architecture, which operates two off-campus CityLab programs in Orlando and Jacksonville to enhance access to highly urban contexts. The University of Florida The University of Florida is ranked #8 among the nation’s public universities according to the 2027 U.S. News & World Report “Best Colleges” list, and #4 among public universities in the inaugural TIME magazine “America’s Best Colleges of 2026-2027” ranking. As the flagship university of the state of Florida, and as a major land-grant research university in the Association of American Universities (AAU), the University offers many opportunities for faculty to develop a national and international reputation for research on issues that showcase Florida and the Southeastern United States as a living laboratory, and to shape the next generation of urban and regional planning professionals. Opportunities exist for cross-disciplinary appointments and collaboration, such as with the Warrington College of Business (including Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate), the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (including the Political Science and Geography), the Levin College of Law, the School of Natural Resources and Environment, and UF’s six health-related colleges on campus, as well as university-wide institutes like the Water Institute and the Florida Climate Institute. In addition, UF’s HiPerGator, the nation's fastest university-owned supercomputer, is available to faculty for large-scale data analysis, spatial modeling, and AI research. The Campus and Gainesville The College is located on the University’s historic main campus, which is characterized by gothic brick buildings and expansive oak trees. The broader campus boasts world-class museums, a performing arts center, a childcare center, and a nationally ranked hospital complex. Situated within the city of Gainesville , the campus is surrounded by historic neighborhoods and a vibrant downtown, and the campus connects to the larger community and beyond by over one hundred miles of bike paths, a regional transit system, and a regional airport. The Gainesville metropolitan area within Alachua County is home to 280,000 residents and serves as the commercial, cultural, educational, and medical center of the north central Florida region. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of specialized programs. The County’s nature parks, waterways, and freshwater springs provide nature-lovers with abundant outdoor activities. The major cities of Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville, and the Gulf and Atlantic coasts, are all two hours or less away. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Compensation includes a full benefits package with health insurance, retirement options, and paid leave. The University has a dual career academic hire program. Required Qualifications: The candidate must have a Ph.D in urban and regional planning, urban studies, geography, public policy, finance and real estate, public administration, or related field. A successful candidate must have expertise in housing and a strong background in the planning field, either through education or experience, to enable them to teach specialized and core courses for the MURP and BUSP programs. The Ph.D must be completed prior to the start date of August 16, 2027. An ABD at the time of interviews will be considered with a letter from the candidate’s supervisor confirming the candidate’s date of graduation. Preferred: Preferred candidates incorporate housing policy and finance, resiliency and disaster recovery in housing, socio-economic sustainability, and/or land use into their research, teaching, and service; connections with related discipline programs are also desired. Candidates should have a demonstrated track record or potential to publish in peer-reviewed journals and attract extramural research funding, particularly nationally competitive grants. Candidates should also have evidence of student-centered classroom instruction at graduate or undergraduate levels. An interest in incorporating online modalities and a willingness to serve the academy, the profession, and communities are also desired. Lastly, professional planning experience is desired, with AICP or other relevant credentials preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, submit a complete application with the following: Letter of interest describing your: Vision for the roles that housing and its intersection with planning will play in the future, and how you expect your work to contribute to this vision. Research agenda, including your goals and how you will build upon past work, including publications, projects, funding, and partnerships. Teaching philosophy and pedagogies; a list of courses of interest in teaching; a summary of courses taught including evidence of teaching effectiveness; and any instructional trainings and certifications received. Academic leadership, professional experience, and community service in relation to future academic goals. Specific interests in joining the Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Florida. Writing sample or products demonstrating your research interests and strengths such as a portfolio of relevant research, design/creative, or professional work. Teaching evaluations (if received). Curriculum vitae. List of three references, including affiliations, phone numbers, and email addresses. To ensure full consideration, a complete application should be submitted by October 26, 2026 . Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and/or hiring authority. Review of complete applications will begin upon receipt and will continue until the position is filled. Three letters of reference will be required for shortlisted candidates. Submittal instructions will be provided upon notification. Inquiries can be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Kristin Larsen, klarsen@ufl.edu . If accommodations due to a disability are needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. All candidates selected for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide official transcripts to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541472/lecturer": {
      "title": "LECTURER",
      "description": "LECTURER Apply now Job no: 541472 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 26040100 - HH-SPM ADMIN-INSTRUCTION Classification Title: LECTURER Classification Minimum Requirements: The position requires a doctorate in sport management or related discipline or a master’s degree and up to 10 years of sport industry experience. The successful candidate must have experience teaching sport management undergraduate and/or graduate students or extensive experience training sport industry professionals Job Description: The successful candidate will primarily be responsible for coordinating engaged learning and outreach activities in the online and residential programs, teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses in the fall, spring, and summer, and participating in service at the department, college, university, and professional levels. The candidate will lead and collaborate in a variety of activities, including developing and nurturing new industry partnerships, maintaining alumni databases, mentoring and onboarding all incoming online master’s sport management students and guiding their career development, creating and supporting networking efforts for students, arranging professional industry trips, collecting post-trip data from experiential activities, facilitating and executing the SPM Voices From the Field Podcast, and overseeing final exams and/or capstone projects. The position will be in Gainesville, Florida, and performance effort is service (75%) and teaching (25%) responsibilities. The University of Florida is one of the top ranked Public Universities in the United States 1 and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 61,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. There is a vibrant culture of research, with over $1 billion in research expenditures last fiscal year. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 145,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 85 miles from the Georgia border, 50 miles from the gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean. It has abundant wildlife, mature forests, prairies, lakes, and opportunities for many outdoor activities. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: The position requires a doctorate in sport management or related discipline or a master’s degree and up to 10 years of sport industry experience. The successful candidate must have experience teaching sport management undergraduate and/or graduate students or extensive experience training sport industry professionals. Preferred: Candidates will bring experience in sport industry engagement and networking, student mentoring, and teaching. Online teaching experience in sport management research methods, statistics, marketing, management and leadership, sociology, finance, or sport law courses is preferred Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu (job #541472) and include (a) an application letter summarizing qualifications; (b) a teaching philosophy; (c) a curriculum vitae or resume; and (d) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. Review of complete applications begins October 20, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Send inquiries and nominations to Dr. Cynthia Willming, Search Committee Chair, willming@hhp.ufl.edu. This is an on-campus, 12-month, full-time, non- tenure-accruing Lecturer position, beginning August 16, 2027. The University of Florida is an equal employment institution and is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy, gender identity, and sexual orientation), parental status, national origin, age, disability, genetic information (including family medical history), political affiliation, military service, or other non-merit-based factors. These protections extend to all management practices and decisions, including recruitment and hiring, appraisal systems, promotions, training, and career development programs. Selected candidates will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluations Services (NACES) which can be found at http://www.ncas.org. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-87. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541476/postdoctoral-associate-astrophysics": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics Apply now Job no: 541476 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment Job Description: The Department of Physics at the University of Florida invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate to conduct research on topics related to supermassive black hole astrophysics under the mentorship of Prof. Laura Blecha. Preference will be given to candidates with experience and/or interest in modeling gravitational-wave or multi-messenger source populations, the cosmological evolution of black holes and galaxies, or other topics that overlap with the research of Blecha’s group. The preferred start date is August 16, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment. The position is renewable annually for two years and may be extended to a third year, subject to satisfactory performance and the availability of funds. A salary of $60,000 and travel funding will be provided, as well as a full benefits package. The successful candidate will have access to the state-of-the-art HiPerGator computing cluster: ( it.ufl.edu/rc/hipergator/ ). The Physics and Astronomy Departments at the University of Florida have active research groups in numerous areas of astrophysics. The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. If an accommodation due to disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Expected Salary: $60k Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment. Preferred: Experience and/or interest in modeling gravitational-wave or multi-messenger source populations, the cosmological evolution of black holes and galaxies, or other topics that overlap with the research of Blecha’s group. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must include: (i) a cover letter, (ii) a statement of research interests and plans, and (iii) a CV that includes a list of publications. In addition, names and email addresses for three references must be provided. After initial review, letters of recommendation will be requested for selected applicants. To ensure full consideration, application materials should be received by December 4, 2026. Please contact Laura Blecha at lblecha@ufl.edu with any questions. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Feb 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541433/program-coordinator-collegiate-veterans-success-center": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center",
      "description": "Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center Apply now Job no: 541433 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 04011000 - SL-DISABILITY RESOURCE CENTER Classification Title: Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience consistent with University requirements. Job Description: Military-Connected Student Outreach and Engagement • Coordinate outreach and engagement strategy that increases awareness of CVSC resources, programs, space, student organizations and opportunities for prospective, incoming, current, online, and graduating military-connected students. • Supervise peer mentoring, student advisory, ambassador, volunteer, and/or student employment initiatives, including recruitment, onboarding, training, scheduling, coaching, and recognition for student assistants supporting the CVSC area. • Conduct targeted outreach at key transition points, including admission, orientation, return from deployment or military leave, academic milestones, graduation, and post-graduate transition. • Serve as a point of contact for military-connected students and families seeking information about University resources, transition, engagement, academic success, wellbeing, career development, and community support. • Develop accessible and timely communications across digital platforms, presentations, tabling, campus events, and partner channels to promote CVSC resources and opportunities. • Plan, implement and assess programs, workshops, recognition activities, peer connections, and signature events that foster community, resilience, leadership, and engagement, synthesizing student feedback to identify needs, reduce barriers, and recommend program or service improvements. • Expand outreach through relationships with military installations, veteran-serving organizations, alumni, employers, families, and community partners. • Create opportunities for military-connected students and others interested in supporting this community to participate, lead, volunteer, and mentor through CVSC initiatives. • Refer education-benefit questions to the designated School Certifying Official or responsible office unless separately designated and trained to perform certification duties. Program Coordination, Experiential Learning, Career Design, and Campus Partnerships • Design and coordinate the development, logistics, delivery, and evaluation of CVSC programs, workshops, mentoring initiatives, leadership experiences, and collaborative events. • Incorporate experiential learning through defined learning outcomes, meaningful responsibilities, reflection, feedback, and opportunities for students to demonstrate and articulate their learning. • Develop and facilitate educational programs and resources that strengthen campus understanding of military-connected student experiences, needs, resources, and effective referral practices. • Collaborate with campus and community partners to connect students with career exploration, professional networks, internships, employment, graduate education, and other post-graduate pathways. • Maintain collaborative relationships with campus and community partners supporting admissions, financial aid, veteran education benefits, academic success, accessibility, wellbeing, career development, housing, legal services, alumni engagement, and community resources. • Represent the Student Engagement and CVSC at assigned at partner meetings, resource fairs, committees, various events, and professional development opportunities. • Maintain accurate and accessible resource guides, referral protocols, websites, contact lists, and partner communications. • Coordinate assigned collaborations with Veterans Affairs, state and federal contacts, veteran-serving organizations, and community partners under the direction of leadership. • Develop resources and activities that help students translate military service and campus involvement into transferable leadership and career-readiness competencies. • Manage program calendars, reservations, vendors, purchasing documentation, marketing, staffing, accessibility, risk-management requirements, and post-program evaluations. • Research best practices and recommend approaches that strengthen military-connected student engagement within the UF context. • Support evening, weekend, and campus-wide events, as assigned. Student Support, Resource Navigation, and Student Organization Advising • Advise assigned registered student organizations, student leaders, and organization advisors on operations, leadership transitions, membership engagement, event planning, budgeting, risk management, and University policies. • Assist in the management of the student organization program and database (GatorConnect) and work with software provider to make continued improvements to the database and technology. • Maintain accurate records of student contacts, outreach, referrals, and follow-up in accordance with privacy requirements and approved University practices. • Provide logistical and administrative support for departmental priorities, student organization needs, and major campus-wide events as assigned. • Interpret and communicate University policies and procedures related to students, student organizations, events, campus safety, risk, and liability. Assessment, Administration, and Operations • Collect, organize, and report participation, outreach, referral, student feedback, learning, engagement, and career-related data using approved tools and definitions. • Assist with reporting, dashboards, annual reports, needs assessments, program reviews, presentations, budget tracking, purchasing documentation, and other operational records. • Support daily Student Engagement, Community and Belonging, and CVSC operations, including desk coverage, event support, space readiness, service standards, student employee schedules, inventory, technology, records, communications, accessibility, safety, and continuity procedures. • Use assessment findings and student feedback to recommend and implement improvements to programs, outreach, communications, and resource coordination. • Support grants, partnerships, sponsorships, and gift-funded initiatives as assigned, including documentation and reporting consistent with University requirements. Departmental, Divisional, and University Contribution • Contributes to the Division and University through active participation on committees and division-wide initiatives (example opportunities): Emergency dean on call, event support team, conduct committee, screening committees, student welcome events, etc); and other duties as assigned • Position receives regular direction, coaching, and feedback from the Associate Director of the Collegiate Veterans Success Center. Work is guided by established goals, applicable policies and procedures, scheduled meetings, and performance review. The coordinator is expected to exercise initiative and sound judgment in coordinating assigned programs, outreach, communications, referrals, and daily responsibilities, and to consult the supervisor on significant policy, student welfare, fiscal, reputational, or operational matters. • The position may provide day-to-day direction, training, scheduling, and coaching to assigned student assistants, interns, ambassadors, peer mentors, and volunteers. The position does not directly supervise professional staff. • Normal work schedule is Monday through Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., 40 hours per week, including frequent evening and weekend hours as assigned. Expected Salary: $48,000 to $50,000 Annual Salary Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience consistent with University requirements. Preferred: Master’s degree in higher education/student affairs, counseling, social work, public administration, communications, education, leadership, or a related field with experience working in a higher education setting, particularly in Student Affairs. Knowledge of military culture, transition from military service to higher education and career, and campus and community resources for veterans and military-connected students. Veteran or military-connected lived experience is valued but not required. Experience advising student organizations, mentoring student leaders, coordinating peer programs, or supervising student employees or volunteers. Experience with events and event management, and compliance-based systems or protocols Experience with outreach strategy, digital communications, program marketing, presentations, event planning, and partnership development. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541451/astasofull-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF Apply now Job no: 541451 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050100 - MD-MEDICINE-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Ast/Aso/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants should hold a PhD, MD or MD/PhD in the biomedical sciences or a related field of study. Job Description: The Department of Medicine at the University of Florida (UF) College of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. We are seeking an outstanding, creative investigator in any area of Immunology. Our faculty include some of the brightest and most innovative scholars spanning a broad variety of disciplines and disease models. The Department of Medicine has established the Research Hub to foster cross-disciplinary collaborations and continue a major expansion of the faculty as part of a 5-year strategic plan. We invite you to learn more about us by visiting https://medicine.ufl.edu/research-new/ . UF has more than 30 immunology research laboratories situated across multiple colleges and institutes that are supported by state-of-the-art resources and centers in advanced imaging, genomics, cytometry, virology, vector biology, metabolomics, lipidomics, and oncogenesis. UF is also developing a world-class artificial intelligence (AI) research infrastructure to support investigators. Research spans innate and adaptive immunity, with existing strengths in infectious disease, organ-specific immunology of lung and kidney, autoimmunity, cancer immune therapies, and antiviral immunity. We are particularly interested in recruiting candidates who will build a research program that complements these existing strengths. The successful candidate will be expected to develop or have created a vibrant, externally funded research program, participate in graduate-level teaching, and recruit and mentor outstanding trainees. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education. Required Qualifications: Applicants should hold a PhD, MD or MD/PhD in the biomedical sciences or a related field of study. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications should include cover letter, CV, research statement, and contact information for three references. The position offers outstanding research space, a highly collaborative environment, and a substantial start-up package. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 23 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541459/software-licensing-coordinator": {
      "title": "Software Licensing Coordinator",
      "description": "Software Licensing Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541459 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Computer Science, Information Technology, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 27010701 - HA-INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: End User Computing Spec III Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree and four years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and 6 years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate of required experience. Job Description: Software Fulfillment Fulfills new software licensing requests submitted through the service management system. Actively manages annual licensing periods for existing applications and coordinates with departmental administration as required. Coordinates with IT asset management as required to maintain accurate licensing asset records. Departmental Software Liaison Serves as the departmental liaison for software requests in coordination with UF Software Licensing services. Utilizes the UF Liaison Dashboard to obtain and update licensing information as required. Software Installation Coordination Coordinate with customers and support teams regarding the installation and activation of software applications. Process optimization, risk mitigation, strategic initiatives Works closely with organizational leadership to optimize software licensing and deployment processes, identify and mitigate risks, and facilitate strategic initiatives. Expected Salary: $61,000 Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and four years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and 6 years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate of required experience. Preferred: Demonstrated experience with the licensing, deployment and support of common administrative, educational and research (statistical analysis, reference management, etc.) applications. Knowledge of service management, asset management and software management / deployment systems. Experience with supporting enterprise environments (10,000+ devices) and/or supporting academic health centers / clinical care. The successful candidate will have a strong customer-centric service orientation and excellent troubleshooting, communication, organizational and collaboration skills. Discretion, sound judgment, confidentiality, initiative, and interpersonal skills are critical to the success of this position. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540493/director-of-development-college-of-veterinary-medicine": {
      "title": "Director of Development, College of Veterinary Medicine",
      "description": "Director of Development, College of Veterinary Medicine Apply now Job no: 540493 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 50010000 - AD-VP ADVANCEMENT Classification Title: DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Job Description: Portfolio Management Responsible for the identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of major and principal gift prospects as well as grateful clients. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Focus on individual prospects rated at a capacity of $100,000 or above. Assess and refine the portfolio by identifying new high-net-worth individuals and families with the capacity and affinity to support college priorities. Develop and implement tailored fundraising strategies for grateful client donors, aligning philanthropic interests with the College's priorities in research, education, patient care, and capital initiatives. Collaborate with the Office of Estate & Gift Planning team to develop and implement a gifting strategy for qualified donor portfolios at the planned giving level. Development Strategy & Donor Engagement Must develop comprehensive understanding of the college mission, strategy, and goals as they relate to fundraising. Partner with college leadership and unit/program directors to create and execute tailored, multi-year engagement and solicitation strategies for each assigned prospect, leveraging a moves management framework. Each plan should be rooted in the donor's philanthropic interests and aligned with university strategic priorities. Develop and present concept papers and gift proposals to donors. Organize and facilitate high-level engagement experiences, including campus visits, personalized briefings and engagement opportunities to deepen prospect relationships. Attend and support activities and events involving volunteers and prospects. This may include pre-and post- event publicity, as well as event set-up, break-down, and serving as a host. Deliver thoughtful, personalized donor stewardship by recognizing gifts in meaningful ways aligned with the mission and values of CVM and UF Advancement, while providing customized reporting, facilitating naming opportunities, managing long-term relationships, and ensuring donors are appropriately engaged and recognized through events and communications. Communications Develop and implement a comprehensive communications plan to inform constituents and identify markets about the mission, vision, and values of the unit, its funding priorities, and gift opportunities. Build and maintain effective relationships with college administration and faculty through regular, consistent, and relevant communication. Work with marketing and communications to design and produce collateral or promotional materials that relate to events, including programs, pledge forms, signage, etc. Administrative Ensure best practices in office systems, files and record keeping. Provide research, market, and prospect information for UFA’s integrated database, and handle confidential prospect information in a sensitive, ethical manner. Contribute to the planning and monitoring of the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Expected Salary: $85,000-95,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: At least 4-6 years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual prospects with campaign experience. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and a team player Competency in problem solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work sponsorship is not available for this position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541430/astaso-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO PROF Apply now Job no: 541430 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 26040100 - HH-SPM ADMIN-INSTRUCTION Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Job Description: The Department of Sport Management at the University of Florida (UF) invites applications and nominations for one, full-time, tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor position. This is a 9-month, tenure-track, on-campus appointment scheduled to begin August 16, 2027. Qualifications We are seeking candidates who will pursue an innovative, rigorous research program; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; and provide department, college, and professional service. An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Candidates should have the ability or experience in conducting high-impact research, publishing in top sport management journals, and seeking external funding to support their scholarship. They should be passionate and enthusiastic toward student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence. We are seeking applications from candidates with teaching and research expertise related to sport law, risk management or other related research area (e.g., policy, organizational behavior). Preference will be given to applicants who have demonstrated ability to publish in high-impact journals and pursue external funding to support their scholarship. The Department of Sport Management The Department of Sport Management (SPM) is committed to excellence and impact through (a) innovative, socially impactful research; (b) the preparation and training of future industry and academic professionals; (c) engaging with the community, and (d) being stewards of the future. The department includes 9 instructional and 8 tenured or tenure-track faculty who are supported by award winning advisory and administrative staff. Across residential and online programs, SPM reaches more than 900 students (over 600 undergraduate majors, 350 master’s students, and 14 PhD students). The Sport Management online program was one of the first in the country and is a thriving part of the department. Our website ( https://hhp.ufl.edu/about/departments/spm/ ) expands upon our research and teaching activities. The University of Florida The University of Florida is one of the top ranked Public Universities in the United States 1 and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 61,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. There is a vibrant culture of research, with over $1 billion in research expenditures last fiscal year. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 145,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 85 miles from the Georgia border, 50 miles from the gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean. It has abundant wildlife, mature forests, prairies, lakes, and opportunities for many outdoor activities. Alachua County Public Schools is an excellent school system, with many public schools ranked highly in the state and nation. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Preferred: We are seeking candidates who will pursue an innovative, rigorous research program; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; and provide department, college, and professional service. An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Candidates should have the ability or experience in conducting high-impact research, publishing in top sport management journals, and seeking external funding to support their scholarship. They should be passionate and enthusiastic toward student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence. We are seeking applications from candidates with teaching and research expertise related to sport law, risk management or other related research area (e.g., policy, organizational behavior). Preference will be given to applicants who have demonstrated ability to publish in high-impact journals and pursue external funding to support their scholarship. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu (reference job #541430) and include (a) an application letter summarizing qualifications; (b) a research statement and evidence of research proficiency; (c) a teaching statement and evidence of teaching proficiency; (d) a curriculum vitae; and (e) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. To receive full consideration, complete applications must be received by October 30, 2026, however, the position will remain open until filled. Send inquiries and nominations to Dr. Dan Connaughton, Search Committee Chair, at: danc@hhp.ufl.edu. The University of Florida is an equal employment institution. Selected candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluations Services (NACES) which can be found at http://www.ncas.org. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541431/astasofull-prof-division-of-surgical-oncology-bmse": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Division of Surgical Oncology - BMSE",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Division of Surgical Oncology - BMSE Apply now Job no: 541431 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29141201 - MD-SURGERY-SURGONC-BMSE Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery. Job Description: The Department of Surgery at the University of Florida is recruiting a tenure or non-tenure track surgeon to join the Division of Surgical Oncology – Breast, Melanoma, Sarcoma and Endocrine (BMSE). A devotion to endocrine surgery is required. The academic rank will be based upon the career stage and development of the candidate. This new physician will join the division’s five full-time and one part-time surgeons with a reputation of excellence in patient care. The division has a long-standing commitment to medical student, resident and fellow education and the new surgeon will be involved in this effort. Special consideration will be given to applicants with a commitment to research. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery. Preferred: Fellowship training in Endocrine Surgery Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
