    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541434/assistant-director-for-programs-community-and-belonging-student-life": {
      "title": "Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life",
      "description": "Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life Apply now Job no: 541434 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 04080602 - SL-SE-COMMUNITY & BELONGING Classification Title: Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and four years of experience. Job Description: Provide leadership, management, and oversight for registered student organization support services, advisor resources, and student organization operations. Manage the coordination, education, and processing of student organization registration, event permitting, risk management, on-site event management, officer eligibility, space allocations, and related administrative processes. Develop and implement training, resources, consultations, and educational opportunities for student organization leaders and advisors related to leadership development, event planning, budgeting, policy interpretation, risk management, and program assessment. Serve as a primary resource to student leaders, advisors, and campus partners regarding student organization policies, procedures, systems, and best practices. Support the coordination and implementation of student organization management systems, including GatorConnect, and maintain accurate records, resources, and operational processes. Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with campus partners, including Event Services, University Police Department, Student Conduct and Conflict Resolution, venue managers, and other university departments that support student organization activity. Interpret university policies for students and student organizations relating to the management of programs, contracts, safety, liability and risk management, and fiscal management. Lead, coordinate, and elevate Community and Belonging programs that promote community development, student engagement, involvement, leadership development, and student connection. Provide leadership for the planning, implementation, supervision, and assessment of programs within the Community and Belonging portfolio, including but not limited to Speed Friending, individual and group involvement consultations, Break Buddies, MLK campus celebration, Gator Wellness Day, and other emerging initiatives. Develop and refine programs that foster meaningful student connection, reduce isolation, support leadership development, and increase student engagement across campus. Coordinate program timelines, logistics, budgets, staffing plans, marketing needs, risk considerations, and assessment strategies to ensure high-quality and sustainable program delivery. Identify opportunities to create, elevate, and implement new programs that respond to student needs and advance the goals of the Office of Community and Belonging. Collaborate with campus departments, student leaders, and community partners to strengthen programmatic efforts and expand student participation. Serve as a resource for event planning and management for students, staff, and faculty as needed. Assist with budget planning, monitoring, and reporting for events. Assist in developing and maintaining collaborative relationships in programming efforts with other UF departments, including academic departments. Assist with administrative leadership, supervision, assessment, and strategic operations for the Office of Community and Belonging. Provide supervision, training, and support for professional staff, graduate assistants, and student employees assigned to student organization support and community programming functions. Assist with strategic planning, goal setting, budget planning, financial monitoring, reporting, and resource allocation for assigned programs and services. Develop, conduct, and analyze assessment efforts related to student organization services, program effectiveness, student satisfaction, learning outcomes, and emerging student needs. Contribute to comprehensive marketing, communication, and outreach strategies that promote student involvement opportunities, programs, services, and resources. Stay informed of trends and issues related to student engagement, student organizations, community development, and leadership development; make recommendations for improvements to programs, services, and operational practices. Contributes to the Division and University through active participation on committees and division-wide initiatives (example opportunities: emergency dean on call, event support team, conduct committee, student welcome events, etc; and other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $58,000 to $60,000 Annual Salary Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and four years of experience. Valid driver's license. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/537055/assistantassociateprofessor-nephrologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor - Nephrologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Professor - Nephrologist Apply now Job no: 537055 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29051300 - MD-NEPHROLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Board certified/board eligible and eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: Faculty Position – Division of Nephrology, Hypertension, and Renal Transplantation University of Florida, Department of Medicine The University of Florida (UF) Department of Medicine invites applications for a faculty position in the Division of Nephrology, Hypertension, and Renal Transplantation. We seek a board-certified or board-eligible nephrologist eager to contribute to a thriving quaternary academic medical center, at the rank of tenure /non-tenure Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. We are looking for passionate physician-leaders who demonstrate teamwork, service, and a commitment to training the next generation of physicians. Why UF Nephrology? Our Division is nationally recognized for excellence in research, education, and clinical care. Faculty members have the opportunity to: Practice world-class nephrology across varied settings, including inpatient and subspecialty outpatient clinics. Contribute to programs in onconephrology, glomerulonephritis, stone disease, lupus nephritis, CRRT, home dialysis, and transplant services. Collaborate on innovative basic, clinical, and AI research with strong institutional support. Advance their careers in an environment that values innovation, scholarship, and mentorship. What We Offer: Competitive compensation with a strong base salary, productivity incentives, and excellent benefits. Flexible career pathways, including VA affiliation if desired. A culture that promotes work-life balance in sunny Florida. Rank and tenure status commensurate with experience. About UF Health: UF Health is one of the Southeast’s premier academic health systems and home to UF Health Shands Hospital, a 1,000-bed quaternary referral center ranked among the Top 50 hospitals in multiple specialties by U.S. News & World Report. With six health colleges and nine institutes on a single campus, UF fosters unparalleled collaboration and discovery. As a Top 5 public research university, UF provides abundant resources to support teaching, research, and clinical excellence. Life in Gainesville: Gainesville offers the energy of a university town with the warmth of a welcoming community, including: Affordable living and nationally ranked schools. Year-round outdoor activities such as hiking, biking, kayaking, snorkeling, and cave diving. Easy access to pristine beaches, state parks, and world-famous theme parks. Convenient travel via Gainesville Regional Airport with direct flights to major hubs. Join us at UF to transform lives, advance nephrology, train future nephrologists, and thrive in a supportive environment for both your career and well-being. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Board certified/board eligible and eligible for a Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted and will be used to fill multiple positions. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541383/forensic-pediatrics-clinical-assistantassociate-professor": {
      "title": "Forensic Pediatrics- CLINICAL ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR",
      "description": "Forensic Pediatrics- CLINICAL ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR Apply now Job no: 541383 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO/FULL PROF Minimum Requirements: Applicants must possess a MD/DO degree and possess or be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: The Department of Pediatrics at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville has an opening for a full-time position in the Division of Forensic Pediatrics and Child Protection. The position will be at the non-tenure accruing level of Assistant/Associate Professor and will assume the role of Medical Director of the University of Florida’s Child Protection Team (CPT) and fellowship program. Major responsibilities will include directing the day-to-day clinical operation of the Child Protection Team, clinical care to victims of child abuse, and teaching of fellows, residents and medical students. Conducting community trainings on child abuse is also required. The Director supervises the largest CPT in Florida, in what is an advanced statewide Child Protection Team system. This is a large busy clinical and academic team that includes 10 child abuse examiners of different disciplines, 23 case coordinators/interviewers, a supervising Team Coordinator to meaningfully manage contracts, reports, hiring and scheduling, a sexual abuse trauma therapy program, and focused senior CPT assistants working on death reviews and Death Review Teams for multiple counties. Services are provided at CPT Clinics as well as to area hospitals. Including Wolfson Children’s Hospital (Level I Pediatric Trauma Center) and the University of Florida Health – Jacksonville (Level II Trauma Center). This position will report to the Chief of the Division of Forensics and Chairman of the Department of Pediatrics at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville. Following is a general description of duties and responsibilities: Provide overall direction and supervision of the CPT team for program service delivery and administration. Provide leadership in establishing an effective and coordinated response to Child Abuse Hotline reports with the designated area Department of Children and Families representatives. Participate and represent the team in the designated community service area as appropriate. Provide advanced medical consultations and supervision, interfacing with 8 different county child protection agencies (Department of Children and Families), the medical examiners, and officers of the State Attorney. Provide medical evaluations of children, and supervise and review the work of the provider agency’s team medical practitioners for the provision of team medical evaluations of children suspected to be abused or neglected. Arrange for and provide in-service training and other professional development for team staff and medical personnel. Assist the statewide CPT Medical Director in the planning and provision of training for regional and statewide training sessions. Participate as a committee member (or appoint a medical designee) in the local Child Abuse Death Review (CADR) Committee. Annually, plan and conduct a minimum of two training sessions in the detection of child abuse and neglect for area physicians and other medical staff, particularly those working in hospital emergency rooms. Provide guidance and oversee the team’s screening of child abuse reports made to the Florida Abuse Hotline, and provide selected direct screening as required by law, or when otherwise determined appropriate. Be available 24 hours a day, seven days a week for consultation to team members and team physicians including telemedicine consultation, when available and appropriate, for medical assessment needs. Arrange coverage by another CMS approved CPT Medical Director when unavailable. Assure medical representation for team staffing. Participate in peer reviews with other team physicians and other medical providers. If designated and approved, participate in monitoring of child protection teams as a member of the statewide-designated monitoring team. Attend statewide child protection team meetings and other meetings, and training sessions as required. Provide medical consultation in an area of expertise to other teams when requested. Provide depositions and expert court testimony when appropriate. Provide teaching of fellows, residents and students. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Negotiable base on experience and academic appointment. Preferred: BE/BC Pediatrician Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541417/lecturerinstructional-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Instructional Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Lecturer/Instructional Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541417 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60150000 - AG-FOOD SCIENCE - HUMAN NUTR Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall have received the academic degree appropriate to the assignment of duties. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month, non-tenure-accruing faculty position that will be 100% teaching, available in the Food Science and Human Nutrition Department within the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences and the Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences at the University of Florida. The salary is competitive and comes with a full benefits package. Duties will include teaching undergraduate and graduate food science courses in both in-person and online formats. Teaching responsibilities may include courses such as Food Microbiology, Food Product Development, and other core food science courses, as well as the development and delivery of upper-level undergraduate courses aligned with the successful candidate’s area of expertise and interests. The faculty member is expected to contribute to excellence in teaching through innovative instructional approaches and curriculum development. The successful candidate will also mentor and advise undergraduate students and support student professional development through participation in experiential learning and competitive activities. This includes coaching and mentoring students involved in organizations and competitions such as the Food Science Club, FFA-related activities, IFT Student Quiz Bowl, Product Development Competitions, and other regional and national student competition events. The successful candidate is encouraged to engage in scholarly activities related to instruction, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, actively seeking funding for the teaching program, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the CALS Honors Program, distance education, and international education. A successful candidate with a Ph.D. degree would be eligible to use the ‘Instructional Assistant Professor’ working title. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The Food Science and Human Nutrition Department (FSHN), a large interdisciplinary unit within IFAS, comprises three distinct undergraduate programs: Food Science, Nutritional Sciences, and Dietetics. The Food Science program is an approved curriculum with the Institute of Food Technologists (IFT). The department has approximately 30 full-time faculty members, including a significant group of federally funded researchers with a rich history of national research awards. The undergraduate student enrollment in Food Science and Human Nutrition is approximately 550, including 72 Food Science students. The graduate program enrolls over 60 M.S. and Ph.D. students in Nutritional Sciences, Dietetics, and Food Science. Within walking distance are superb opportunities to participate in basic and translational research with the UF Diabetes Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, and collaborate with faculty from the Colleges of Medicine, Health and Human Performance, Public Health, Pharmacy, Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A master’s degree (foreign equivalent acceptable) in food science or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should have demonstrated skills in teaching, verbal and written communication, and interpersonal relationships. The successful candidate must be able to work effectively with students, faculty, and staff and demonstrate a commitment to student success. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system and committed to the University of Florida’s core values. Preferred: A Ph.D. degree (foreign equivalent acceptable) in Food Science or a closely related discipline is preferred, but not required (only required for use of the ‘Instructional Assistant Professor’ working title). Preference will be given to candidates with experience teaching food science courses in both in-person and online learning environments, including course development and curriculum enhancement. Familiarity with the Institute of Food Technologists (IFT) Higher Education Review Board (HERB) approval process assessment, or securing extramural funding to support educational programs is also desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by November 1, 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. Employment Conditions This position is available after January 1st, 2027 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541417 Soohyoun Ahn, Ph.D. Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida Food Science and Human Nutrition P.O. Box 110370 Gainesville, FL 32611 Telephone: 352-294-3710 Electronic Mail: sahn82@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit the following documents: Cover letter that states the applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above (2-page limit) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539938/astasofull-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF Apply now Job no: 539938 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29350000 - MD-DERMATOLOGY Classification Title: Ast/Aso/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have an M.D. degree and be board-certified or board-eligible in Dermatology. Applicant must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: The Department of Dermatology invites applications for a Medical Dermatologist to pursue a career with the Department of Dermatology in the College of Medicine at the University of Florida. This is a part-time .60 FTE clinical, non-tenure-track position. Primary duties include providing care for dermatology patients at the UF Health Springhill in Gainesville, FL. It is expected that the candidate participates in teaching dermatology residents/fellows, medicine residents, and medical students. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Applicants must have an M.D. degree and be board-certified or board-eligible in Dermatology. Applicant must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload a CV, cover letter, and three letters of recommendation to be considered for this position. Application review will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"issued to student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541251/research-coordinator-ii": {
      "title": "Research Coordinator II",
      "description": "Research Coordinator II Apply now Job no: 541251 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 29240105 - MD-HOBI-BIOMED INFORMATICS Classification Title: Research Coordinator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Biomedical Informatics and Data Science Program (BIDS), hosted within the Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics and closely affiliated with the Clinical and Translational Science Institute (CTSI), engages in diverse informatics research and service across the University of Florida. Service initiatives include, but are not limited to, supporting the informatics infrastructure for the CTSI, the OneFlorida+ Clinical Research Network, and the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI). The Biomedical Informatics team is seeking a qualified and enthusiastic individual to serve as a Research Coordinator. The Coordinator will play an important role in the management and execution of cutting-edge research studies at the intersection of health care delivery and information technology to enhance the quality of care and population health. The Coordinator will work under the supervision of the Associate Director of Project and Program Management of the Biomedical Informatics (BMI) Program, and will support projects led by BMI faculty. The job responsibilities include a full range of research-related and organizational support efforts across multiple initiatives. The Coordinator will facilitate team communication, coordinate research study execution, and support grant proposals and research dissemination. The Coordinator will collaborate with internal and external research partners, clinical providers, and other stakeholders. Within these activities, opportunities will be available for the Coordinator to foster their professional interests and develop new skills. Successful candidates must be committed to working with various professional and community populations. Duties: Biomedical Informatics Research Study Coordination: Track objectives, milestones, deliverables, status reports, study results. Translate study design decisions into concrete tasks, and assist in setting implementation priorities. Manage communication among staff, study sponsors, healthcare professionals, and investigators, to ensure on-time reports and other deliverables. Work with investigators and administrative staff to maintain budgets, monitor project spending, and develop and maintain IRB protocols, prepare and submit progress reports, and other research-related documentation. Communication: Organize and lead project and team meetings. Track and communicate project updates to team members, leadership, and other internal and external stakeholders. Grant Proposal Development: Coordinate grant proposal development with investigators and grants administrators. Collaboratively manage submission milestones and deadlines. Identify and review extramural funding opportunities and share opportunities with investigators and other partners. Research Protocol Implementation: Work collaboratively with investigators and project teams to conduct all phases of quantitative and qualitative research including study recruitment, informed consent, retention, and data collection and analysis. Communicate with study participants to address participant questions/issues and implement study protocols Dissemination: Work with Investigators to produce publications in peer-reviewed journals and presentations to academic, organizational, and community audiences. Expected Salary: $60,000 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Master’s Degree with at least two years of experience in conducting health services, informatics, or other health-related research. Excellent attention to detail, particularly with regard to interpreting and implementing protocols and in terms of collecting data to ensure accuracy and integrity Excellent written (in English) and oral communication skills. Ability to work independently and to collaborate with faculty, staff, and healthcare professionals at multiple levels Demonstrate strong skills and proficiency in Excel, Word, Powerpoint, Adobe, and Project Management software (Asana and Atlassian products preferred) Working knowledge of informatics and/or health-related research Ability to handle protected health information and human subject issues Ability to interpret basic statistical results Ability to work with investigators to draft project proposals and plans, manuscripts for publication in peer-review journals, and project reports Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541379/ops-learning-technologies-specialist": {
      "title": "OPS Learning Technologies Specialist",
      "description": "OPS Learning Technologies Specialist Apply now Job no: 541379 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 18300000 - ED-LASTINGER CENTER Classification Title: OPS - Learning Technologies Specialist Job Description: LMS and Course Design & Development Design, develop, and maintain online courses and learning experiences within D2L Brightspace or Articulate Rise. Apply instructional design, accessibility, and visual design principles when developing and updating courses and learning experiences. Utilize Brightspace/Articulate Rise features, HTML, CSS, and other approved technologies to create and enhance online learning experiences. Create, organize, and maintain course content, learning materials, resources, and digital assets. Collaborate with instructional designers, subject matter experts, and other project stakeholders to develop and improve courses and learning experiences. Conduct quality assurance and accessibility checks to ensure courses meet established standards for content, functionality, navigation, and accessibility. Ensure integrated tools/applications and SCORMs perform as intended. Course Project Management and LMS Support Take ownership of assigned course development and revision projects, working with project stakeholders to complete deliverables and meet established timelines. Manage and maintain assigned courses and learning experiences, including routine updates, revisions, and improvements. Communicate with instructional designers, content reviewers, subject matter experts, and program staff regarding course development and updates. Maintain course documentation, version history, and other records needed to support course continuity and ongoing maintenance. Gather and incorporate feedback from stakeholders to support improvements to existing courses and learning experiences. Assist the Instructional Design team with the development of learning materials, course activities, and other project needs as assigned. Troubleshooting and Stakeholder Collaboration Troubleshoot LMS, course, and learning experience issues and escalate more complex concerns to appropriate team members when needed. Respond to internal stakeholder questions and requests related to LMS functionality and assigned courses. Document reported issues, resolutions, and course changes according to established team processes. Participate in user acceptance testing and quality assurance activities for course launches, updates, and LMS enhancements. Collaborate with team members to identify and resolve issues affecting course quality or functionality. Other tasks as assigned Expected Salary: $31.00/hour Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in Instructional Design, Educational Technology, Education, or a related field. Experience with instructional design principles, adult learning practices, Universal Design for Learning (UDL), and accessibility standards for online learning. Experience designing and developing online courses or learning experiences using D2L Brightspace, Articulate Rise, or similar learning technologies. Working knowledge of HTML and CSS, as well as eLearning standards and technologies such as SCORM. Strong organizational, project coordination, communication, and interpersonal skills, with the ability to manage multiple projects and meet established deadlines. Experience working in an academic or educational setting and collaborating with subject matter experts, instructional designers, program staff, and other stakeholders. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is soft-funded and time-limited. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is a full-time position, working Monday - Friday 8am - 5pm Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541403/ops-secretary": {
      "title": "OPS Secretary",
      "description": "OPS Secretary Apply now Job no: 541403 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Dentistry Department: 34170100 - DN-RESTORATIVE DENT GEN DEPT Classification Title: OPS Secretary Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience Job Description: Serve as primary administrative support for the department faculty. Responsibilities include receptionist duties (phones, front desk, building issues), scheduling, producing reports and correspondence, and rendering consistent office support as needed. Assist in the coordination and be primary contact in scheduling department meetings/lunches and events (reserving conference rooms, place catering orders, event set up/clean, etc.) Handle routine requests for information for faculty, staff, and outside inquiries via phone, mail, and email. Daily pick-up and delivery of mail. Assist guests and delivery personnel. Place information technology and maintenance tickets. Lab support/chemical inventory/training/maintenance. Maintain office and break room supplies/cleanliness. Manage department conference room reservations. Assist with various onboarding/offboarding activities. Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $15.50 - $16.50/hr Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family's needs, and Vision. Build your retirement with the FICA Alternative Plan and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. Optional insurance plans such as life, disability, legal, and accident insurance are also available. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF's benefit options here: OPS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Preferred: The successful candidate will have experience with Microsoft Office AS or AA degree Experience with Adobe Acrobat Experience working in a healthcare or educational setting Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541413/resfellowship-prog-assistant-infectious-diseases-global-medicine": {
      "title": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant - Infectious Diseases & Global Medicine",
      "description": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant - Infectious Diseases & Global Medicine Apply now Job no: 541413 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 29050800 - MD-INFECTIOUS DISEASES Classification Title: Res/Fellowship Program Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Job Description: This position is located in the Department of Medicine, Division of Infectious Diseases and Global Medicine. Under the direction of the Division Administrator, the Program Assistant is responsible for providing administrative support and coordination to the Division Fellows, as well as the Fellowship Director and Assistant Director. The position must provide a high level of customer service both in person and on the telephone to all patients, faculty, referring physicians, fellows, staff, and other visitors. The Program Assistant performs responsibilities by anticipating needs and proactively providing support with minimal direction. The position is responsible for maintaining sensitive and confidential materials that are received and/or developed during the course of the position’s job duties. Position reports to the Division Administrator. The Program Assistant will serve as the fellowship program’s fiscal manager, responsible for assisting in the fiscal management and maintenance of the program. Responsibilities include understanding basic financial and operational concepts and trends, identifying financial results that do not meet expectations and working with the Chief/Administrator to determine the reason for the variance, and translating analyses into actionable information for decision making. Requires ability to effectively use New Innovations, interpret and apply scheduling principles to work hours reports; to work accurately with numerical detail; and to analyze, consolidate, and interpret accounting data. This position requires on campus presence. General Fellowship Program Duties Schedules all academic conferences and fellowship meetings, including maintaining conference room calendar, individual fellow/faculty calendar invites, sending weekly reminders and taking notes Maintains an electronic progress log and files on all applicants (and other files as requires) as Fellowship Program moves into the match Develop and maintain a system to assist the faculty with review of applicant packets and creation of the final match list Schedule interviews (virtually or in person depending on the programs’ preference) and create itinerary for fellowship applicant interviews with appropriate faculty, Chief, etc. Confirms date, time, and location of all appointments in the week prior to the interview. Ensures the applicants’ interview day flows properly and is presented in a professional manner Create annual fellow schedule and ensure divisional scheduling system and EPIC On-Call schedules are up-to-date and accurate. Including VA schedule Responsible for typing and mailing all correspondence related to the Fellowship Program in a timely manner with attention to spelling, grammar, punctuation, and formatting Organizes new fellow orientation annually, including but not limited to assisting Program Director with orientation handbook, scheduling onboarding sessions with UF Housestaff Office and VA Hospital, obtaining network access, ACLS training, electronic medical record training, security and confidentiality training and research orientation Annual Fellowship Graduation, including securing a venue, catering, event set-up and teardown, running the event, and adhering to a modest budget Create budget tracking spreadsheets for fellows, monitor spending and ensure that expenses are tracked and all purchases are within budget. Expected to understand basic financial and operational concepts and trends, identify financial results that do not meet expectations and work with the Chief/Administrator to determine the reason for the variance, and translate their analyses into actionable information for decision making Fellowship Accreditation Activities Serve as the primary point of contact for all inquiries of the Infectious Diseases Fellowship Program Creates, maintains, and updates all fellowship documents required by the ACGME guidelines. Ensures that the Training Program adheres to ACGME standards and brings any concerns to the attention of the Program Director Upkeep of New Innovations evaluation system, including entering schedules, running reports, and monitoring the compliance of fellows, faculty and staff to evaluation requests Ensure fellows schedules are accurately reflected in necessary systems and rotation assignments align with fellow funding lines and program requirements Maintains filling system for all fellowship documents and correspondence Compiles necessary information for the ACGME site visits in an accurate and timely manner Attends DOM and COM meetings regarding fellowship activities Responsible for monitoring updates and changes in University and ACGME requirements for fellowships through email, list serves, websites, and official correspondence Other Responsibilities Division Admin office coverage, including consult distribution Clinical learner rotation management Prioritize assignments and messages based on urgency or deadlines and work cooperatively with other Division, Department, and University staff to ensure that all assignments are completed in a timely manner and meet or exceed the standards for quality and performance, as established by the Division Serve as a liaison to multiple internal and external entities, including but not limited to: Departments in the College of Medicine or Department of Medicine and its Divisions, Fellowship Office, Division Clinics and VA Medical Center. Consistently strive to represent the Division, Department, and University in a positive and professional manner Work closely with administrative support staff in the Division to ensure that all faculty are supported in their clinical and academic needs Provide direct back-up support when absences occur Adhere to all office policies, procedures, and protocols established by the Division Expected Salary: $22.11 - $27 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Minimum Qualifications: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Preferred Qualifications: Proficiency in Word, Excel, PowerPoint, E-mail and Internet Demonstrated outstanding ability to work under pressure in a busy office with frequent interruptions, changing priorities, and short deadlines Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others Ability to function under stressful circumstances always maintaining respectful and supportive interactions Ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing Ability to work independently Strong interpersonal and telephone skills Knowledge of ACGME rules and guidelines Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been extended and reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541418/senior-compensation-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Compensation Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Compensation Analyst Apply now Job no: 541418 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Human Resources Department: 62010900 - HR-CLASSIFICATION Classification Title: Senior Compensation Analyst Classification Minimum Requirements Masters degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelors degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Human Resources is seeking a strategic thinker who pairs deep compensation expertise with exceptional analytical, consulting, and executive communication skills. You should be comfortable navigating complex structures, working with sensitive information, challenging conventional approaches, and translating sophisticated compensation data into practical recommendations leaders can act on. Reporting under the general direction of the Vice President for Human Resources, you will work with significant independence and collaborate with senior leaders, HR and Finance partners, colleges and administrative units, external consultants, and other key stakeholders. You will influence some of UF’s most important compensation decisions while leading high-visibility initiatives that affect employees across the university. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Shape enterprise compensation strategy : Develop and evaluate compensation philosophies, programs, salary structures, and practices that strengthen UF’s ability to compete for and retain exceptional talent. Advise on executive compensation: Provide sophisticated market analysis and strategic recommendations related to executive and senior leadership compensation, including recruitment, retention, market positioning, peer groups, and compensation practices. Turn data into strategy: Lead complex market benchmarking, financial modeling, compensation analytics, and workforce analyses, transforming data into clear recommendations for senior decision-makers. Lead university-wide salary initiatives: Guide the planning, design, implementation, and evaluation of annual merit and salary increase programs in partnership with university leadership, Finance, HR, colleges, and administrative units. Help transform the future of compensation at UF: Provide strategic compensation leadership as the university transitions to Workday and identify opportunities to strengthen compensation programs through technology, analytics, automation, standardization, and improved governance. Lead through influence: Manage complex cross-functional initiatives and partner with external compensation consultants, survey organizations, and market data providers while providing strategic direction and expertise across organizational boundaries. If you’re ready to bring your compensation expertise to a complex, high-impact environment where your insights can shape consequential workforce decisions, we invite you to explore this opportunity with a University dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation, and world look to for leadership.” For more information, visit www.ufl.edu . WHY GAINESVILLE As Senior Compensation Analyst working from the University's main campus in Gainesville, FL, you'll enjoy a vibrant hub of education, healthcare, culture, and sports in the state. The Greater Gainesville area serves as the cultural, educational, and commercial heart of the north central Florida region and offers a full range of municipal services, including cultural and administrative support. Renowned for its preservation of historic landmarks and natural beauty, the city boasts numerous parks, museums, and lakes that attract thousands of visitors. With its lush urban forest and Tree City USA® designation, Gainesville stands out as one of Florida's most picturesque cities. Its central location provides convenient access to both the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean, offering easy exploration of Florida's famed beaches, springs, and nature reserves. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. OUR COMMITTMENT The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Hiring is contingent on eligibility to work in the U.S. The University of Florida is a public institution and is subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . Salary $100,000.00 with consideration to market, education and experience. Required Qualifications: Masters degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelors degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Preferred Highly competitive candidates may also bring experience in several of the following areas: Enterprise-wide compensation strategy and program design Executive compensation and market benchmarking Large-scale merit or salary planning programs Advanced compensation analytics and financial modeling Executive-level consultation and presentations Complex, cross-functional project leadership Compensation surveys, market data, and external consulting partnerships Higher education or another large, complex, decentralized organization CCP, SHRM-SCP, SPHR, or a comparable professional certification Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Inquiries can be sent to stg@hr.ufl.edu. Candidates must apply online at Careers at UF. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/535112/post-doctoral-associate-in-multiomics": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Associate in Multi-Omics",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Associate in Multi-Omics Apply now Job no: 535112 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Allied Health, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 36020000 - PHHP-COM EPIDEMIOLOGY Classification Title: Post Doctoral Associate Job Description: The Department of Epidemiology ( http://epidemiology.phhp.ufl.edu/) is strategically situated within the College of Public Health and Health Professions (PHHP) and the College of Medicine. Faculty members are jointly appointed to create synergies and collective strength, resulting in a competitive research profile and highly trained students who receive exceptional exposure to a variety of expertise. Our faculty and students have received numerous national awards in research and mentoring and are committed to excellence in training. The department offers an epidemiology PhD, Master of Science, and concentration in the MPH program. It is also home to two National Institutes of Health Training grants (T32). Faculty members are strongly supported by our college-level instructional design team for course development and delivery and by our research core for grant development and submission. Dr. Jinling Liu’s laboratory ( https://phhp.ufl.edu/profile/liu-jinling/ ) at the University of Florida's Department of Epidemiology and The Cener for Genetic Epidemiology and Bioinformatics (GeneBio; https://genebio.phhp.ufl.edu/ ) is accepting applications for 1-2 fully-funded postdoctoral associate positions, starting immediately. Dr. Liu has extensive experience in big data analytics, systems biology, probabilistic graphical models, causal inference and machine learning. Dr. Liu's research interests lie in modeling the rapidly-accumulating big data (e.g., muti-omics) in biology and medicine for precision medicine via a variety of statistical and machine learning techniques – one of her projects along these lines has been recently funded by a prestigious NIH Career Development Award for young investigators. She is committed to advancing precision medicine through her work. She is also collaborating with researchers in other domains for data analytics and decision making. Liu lab has access to rich high-dimensional multi-omics data, including WGS, WES, DNA methylation, RNA-seq, proteomics, lipidomics and metabolomics in large-scale human populations. The lab is interested in integrating and mining the different types of data to understand the genomic causes and other biomarkers as well as the underlying signaling network for a variety of diseases including aging, cardiovascular diseases, brain diseases, cancer, et al., through novel statistical or deep learning methods development and application. The postdoc associates will be exposed to rich multi-omics data, a variety of diseases, advanced statistical and machine learning methods and wide collaborations with experts from different fields. Key responsibilities for the postdoctoral associates include multi-omics data analysis, novel method development and application, manuscript writing, research presentation at conferences, and assistance with grant applications and student mentoring. Additional Information: The University of Florida ( www.ufl.edu ) is a comprehensive research-extensive university that includes a full range of academic departments and programs. The College of Public Health and Health Professions ( www.phhp.ufl.edu ) is part of the University’s Health Science Center. The UF Health Science Center ( https://ufhealth.org/about-us ) is comprised of six health-related colleges located on a single, contiguous campus. They include the colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine. The Health Science Center also partners with the immediately adjacent UF Health Shands Hospital, the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center, and the UF Health Science Center Regional campus located in Jacksonville, FL. Additional major institutes and centers located at the University of Florida include the Emerging Pathogens Institute, the Institute of Child Health Policy, the UF Health Cancer Center, the UF Genetics Institute, the Clinical and Transactional Science Institute and the McKnight Brain Institute, all of which provide state-of-the-art environments and access to large data sets for faculty collaboration. Expected Salary: $63,480 annually; commensurate with experience level and in accordance with current NIH postdoctoral salary guidelines. Postdoctoral associates are eligible for comprehensive health and leave benefits and have access to libraries and other research resources available for faculty and postdoctoral associates. Additionally, postdoctoral trainees may receive funds each training year to support professional development (e.g., travel to workshops or national conferences, purchase of education materials). Minimum Requirements: PhD degree in computer science, statistics, biostatistics, biomedical informatics or related fields OR a PhD degree in biology or related fields. Preferred Qualifications: A strong background in programming (e.g., Python or R or C++) is essential. Research experience utilizing skills of programming, statistics, and AI are essential. The ideal candidate should demonstrate independent and critical thinking abilities as well as good communication skills. Prior research experience in analyzing biomedical data is preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants should submit an application, including a CV, statement of interest, and contact information for three references. Substantive queries may be addressed to Dr. Jinling Liu at jinling.liu@ufl.edu (Assistant Professor and Principal Investigator). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position has been reposted; previous applicants remain under consideration and need not re-apply. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity institution. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 17 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/536501/assistantassociatefull-professor-classical-hematology": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor -  Classical Hematology",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Classical Hematology Apply now Job no: 536501 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050604 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-OTHER Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Job Description: Hematologist at the University of Florida. The University of Florida Department of Medicine is seeking applications for a full or part-time Classical Hematologist for a clinician-educator or clinician-researcher track position in the Division of Hematology and Oncology at the Assistant Professor rank or higher. This position seeks a board-certified/board-eligible Hematologist. Applicants must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. This individual will have a primarily clinical assignment but will also be expected to develop, participate in, and lead clinical/educational/translational research projects; embrace team-oriented research; engage in teaching students, housestaff, and fellows in the clinic and inpatient settings; and provide direct patient care at our Gainesville campus. A track record of prior clinical research and scholarly activity is desirable, but not required. Academic interests could include translational research, early-phase clinical trials, symptom management, observational studies, and/or health disparities. The UF Division of Hematology and Oncology faculty are closely engaged and partners with the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI), the only NCI-Designated Cancer Institute based at a public university in Florida. The UFHCI blends comprehensive patient care and innovative research in a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment, with members from 11 of the 16 colleges at the University of Florida (UF). UF, ranked #7 among public universities by U.S. News & World Report and #4 by Forbes in 2025, stands as a premier research institution, driving innovation and discovery while delivering exceptional value and academic excellence. Resources available for professional development at the University of Florida Health Sciences Center include leadership, education, and research training through support from the UF Health Cancer Institute, Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI), formal mentorship programs, and supported opportunities for teaching and research. Foreign national candidates whose employment conditions meet federal and University requirements under an immigrant classification are eligible to apply. Tenure accruing and non-tenure accruing tracks are available. Rank and tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. About Gainesville, Florida Gainesville ( http://cityofgainesville.org ) is home to Florida's largest and oldest university and is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville is also the largest city in Alachua County and is the county seat, with approximately 269,000 residents county-wide. The city provides a full range of municipal services, including cultural and nature services and necessary administrative services to support these activities. Additionally, Gainesville is home to a regional transit system, a regional airport, and a 72-par championship golf course. Gainesville is known for the preservation of historic buildings and the beauty of its natural surroundings, and the city's numerous parks, museums, and lakes provide entertainment to thousands of visitors. Because of its beautiful landscape and urban \"forest,\" Gainesville is one of the most attractive cities in Florida. It is a hidden gem in North Central Florida, within a couple of hours of major cities such as Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa. We are in a central location providing easy access within a couple of hours' drive to beaches, nature preserves, and water-based activities. We have warm weather much of the year, allowing for year-round outdoor activities. Due to the vibrant atmosphere generated by The University of Florida and UF Health Shands, the leading employers in Gainesville, the city offers a range of amenities for residents. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Hematology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540414/end-user-computing-spec-2": {
      "title": "END USER COMPUTING SPEC 2",
      "description": "END USER COMPUTING SPEC 2 Apply now Job no: 540414 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 27010701 - HA-INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: End User Computing II Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree and two years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Job Description: Provides Tier-2 hardware and operating system support for end user computing equipment including workstations, printers, scanners, and mobile devices. May also include specialty computing devices which interface with clinical or research equipment. Provides Tier-1 support for telephony, network, and audio-visual devices or services. Support includes standard configuration and/or troubleshooting steps as documented in process documentation and the knowledgebase. Escalates unresolved incidents to the appropriate Tier-2 support team. Provides Tier-2 support for enterprise and unit level applications which support clinical, educational, research, and administrative functions. Support includes client installation and configuration, basic functionality troubleshooting, and assistance with application features or interfaces Documents all work and processes in service management systems, performs initial hardware and software setup and reimages production systems; provides customers guidance and instruction on the use of hardware and software Contributes to the improvement of workflow processes, knowledge support systems, and IT provided services through active participation in team meetings. Participates in technical and customer service training initiatives and inter-team technical projects as directed Expected Salary: $53,000 Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and two years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Class E Driver's License is required. Preferred: Demonstrated knowledge of current Windows and/or Mac OS X operating systems, network troubleshooting (LAN/wireless), and performing hardware diagnostics and repair. Experience with supporting enterprise environments (10,000+ devices) and/or supporting academic health centers / clinical care; Working knowledge of common administrative, educational and research applications and services and familiarity with service management industry best practices is strongly preferred. The successful candidate will have a strong customer-centric service orientation and excellent troubleshooting, communication, organizational and collaboration skills. Discretion, sound judgment, confidentiality, initiative, and interpersonal skills are critical to the success of this position Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541001/clinical-research-coordinator-ii-epilepsy-division": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator II - Epilepsy Division",
      "description": "Clinical Research Coordinator II - Epilepsy Division Apply now Job no: 541001 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration Department: 29180200 - MD-NEUROLOGY-EPILEPSY Classification Title: Clinical Research Coordinator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Clinical Research Coordinator II will support clinical research activities within the iBRAIN Laboratory in the Department of Neurology at the University of Florida. This position is responsible for coordinating human subjects research focused on neurological disorders, including epilepsy and dementia. The incumbent will work closely with the Principal Investigator and research team to ensure efficient, compliant, and timely implementation of study protocols. More About This Role Participant Recruitment and Study Coordination Recruit, screen, consent, enroll, and retain participants Schedule and coordinate study visits, assessments, imaging, electrophysiology, biospecimen collection, and follow-up activities Maintain screening/enrollment logs and communicate with participants, caregivers, clinical teams, and investigators Data Collection and Management Collect, enter, verify, and track clinical, behavioral, imaging, electrophysiology, and biospecimen data Maintain accurate source documents, case report forms, and coded records Complete REDCap/Epic data entry, quality checks, query resolution, secure data transfer, and archiving Regulatory Compliance, Quality Assurance, and Study Operations Prepare and maintain IRB submissions, consent documents, regulatory files, deviations, adverse-event records, and required reports Track study milestones, support monitoring and audits, coordinate start-up and closeout activities, and ensure compliance with protocols, Good Clinical Practice, and UF/UF Health requirements Research Team and General Support Participate in research team meetings and complete required training and relevant professional development activities Provide general research and administrative support and perform other related duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator Managing Clinical Trial funding Coordinate and process participant payments for clinical research study visits in accordance with study protocols, sponsor requirements, institutional policies, and regulatory guidelines Verify visit completion and participant eligibility for reimbursement, maintain accurate payment documentation, reconcile payment records, resolve payment-related inquiries, and ensure timely and compliant disbursement of participant compensation while maintaining confidentiality and data integrity About the UF Division of Epilepsy in the Department of Neurology The University of Florida’s Epilepsy Division is dedicated to improving the lives of individuals affected by epilepsy and seizure disorders, conditions that impact millions of people across the United States. Epilepsy is a complex neurological condition that can range from well-controlled seizures with medication to cases that require advanced diagnostic evaluation and specialized treatments, including surgical and device-based therapies. UF Epilepsy Neurology is committed to delivering patient-centered care by integrating innovative research with comprehensive clinical treatment. Through multidisciplinary collaboration and access to advanced neurodiagnostic technologies, the division works to develop new approaches to seizure management and improve outcomes for patients living with epilepsy. For more information about UF Epilepsy Neurology, visit UF College of Medicine’s Epilepsy Neurology page here . About the City of Gainesville Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, and thriving farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds Expected Salary: $53,000 - $60,000 annually Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Three (3) or more years of human-subjects or clinical research experience Proficiency with REDCap, Epic, OnCore, or similar systems Familiarity with Good Clinical Practice and research requirements Experience with clinical, behavioral, neuroimaging, electrophysiology, or biospecimen data Strong organizational and communication skills Ability to manage multiple studies with minimal supervision Clinical research certification, such as CCRC Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please upload the following documents with your application: Cover Letter Resume Contact information for three professional references This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 17 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541363/lecturerinstructional-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "LECTURER/Instructional Assistant Professor",
      "description": "LECTURER/Instructional Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541363 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 15060000 - DCP-URBAN REGIONAL PLANNING Classification Title: Instructional Assistant Professor/Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: The candidate must have an advanced degree in urban and regional planning, landscape architecture, urban design, or closely related field. Job Description: School of Landscape Architecture and Planning Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning within the University of Florida’s College of Design, Construction and Planning invites applications for a 12-month, nontenure-accruing faculty position at the rank of Instructional Assistant Professor to begin in January 2027. The Department encourages applications from candidates with background and expertise in the urban and regional planning field. The Department has particular interest in candidates whose teaching contribute to future-oriented, innovative urban and regional transformation and the empowerment of communities and residents. We seek a high-caliber candidate with the planning-based skills to develop interdisciplinary lecture and studio-based courses related to sustainability and resilience delivered on-campus and online. The successful candidate is anticipated to have a well-developed pedagogy that includes methods to engage a multicultural, interdisciplinary student body and a capacity to teach in the professional, accredited MURP degree program. The candidate would be expected to engage in service consistent with the missions of the Department, the School and the land-grant University. Key Responsibilities The successful candidate is anticipated to lead community-engaged, interdisciplinary teaching initiatives for the PAB-accredited Master of Urban and Regional Planning program and the new Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning program. These will center around a course that involves students in planning project-based work of a professional nature for paying and/or pro bono clients. Teaching duties will include a total of nine credit hours per semester (Fall, Spring, Summer), which may be graduate and upper-level undergraduate courses delivered in-person and online, in the candidate’s areas of expertise and in general planning topics. Anticipated courses include at least one core general planning course to include at least one urban planning project course. The additional courses may include topics such as Neighborhood Planning, Sustainability and Resilience Planning, Planning Information Technologies, or other courses that fill specific departmental needs. The candidate will establish collaborative relationships within the college and with related discipline programs on campus towards developing interdisciplinary and experiential learning opportunities for undergraduate and graduate planning students. The Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning consists of a collegial faculty of thirteen core members and an additional eighteen members affiliated with research centers and other academic programs. Our faculty is the most productive in the College and consistently attracts the highest amount of research funding, with close to $4 million in new grants secured in the past year. We foster a family friendly environment and promote career development at every level, including mentorship of junior faculty. The Department, founded in 1975 and first accredited in 1978, offers a Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP) with in-person and online deliveries, a Master of Science in Urban Analytics (MSUA), a PhD URP concentration with STEM designation, a graduate minor in URP and certificate in UA, an undergraduate minor in URP, and a combined (4+1) bachelor-master degree. The Department’s fully-online MURP is the country’s first and remains the only accredited such program. In 2023, the MURP program (campus and online) was ranked 22nd in the nation, sixth in the South, and the 10th largest by enrollment. The Department recently established a Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning (BUSP) and accepted its first cohort in Fall 2026. The School and College As one of the two units in the School of Landscape Architecture and Planning, the Department of Urban and Regional Planning has a strong relationship with the Department of Landscape Architecture, as well as other units in the College, which include Architecture, Construction Management, Interior Design, Historic Preservation, and Sustainability and the Built Environment. The School of Architecture has established two off-campus CityLab programs in Orlando and Jacksonville to enhance access to highly urban contexts. The School and College are home to a range of research institutes and centers, including the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies, the GeoPlan Center, the Center for Health and the Built Environment, the International Center for Adaptation Planning and Design (iAdapt), the Center for Landscape Conservation Planning, and the Florida Institute for Built Environment Resilience (FIBER). The University of Florida The University of Florida is ranked #7 among the nation’s public universities according to the U.S. News & World Report Best Colleges 2025 list. As the flagship university of the state of Florida, and as a major land-grant research university in the Association of American Universities (AAU), the University offers many opportunities for faculty to develop a national and international reputation for research on issues that showcase Florida and the Southeastern United States as a living laboratory, and to shape the next generation of urban and regional planning professionals. Opportunities exist for cross-disciplinary appointments and collaboration, such as with the Warrington College of Business, the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, the Levin College of Law, UF’s six health-related colleges on campus, the School of Natural Resources and Environment, the Water Institute, and the Florida Climate Institute. In addition, the University’s AI initiative includes access to Nvidia’s HiPerGator supercomputer. The Campus and Gainesville The College is located on the University’s historic main campus, which is characterized by gothic brick buildings and expansive oak trees. The broader campus boasts world-class museums, a performing arts center, a childcare center, and a nationally ranked hospital complex. Situated within the city of Gainesville, the campus is surrounded by historic neighborhoods and a vibrant downtown, and the campus connects to the larger community and beyond by over one hundred miles of bike paths, a regional transit system, and a regional airport. The Gainesville metropolitan area within Alachua County is home to 280,000 residents and serves as the commercial, cultural, educational, and medical center of the north central Florida region. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of specialized programs. The County’s nature parks, waterways, and freshwater springs provide nature-lovers with abundant outdoor activities. The major cities of Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville, and the Gulf and Atlantic coasts, are all two hours or less away. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Compensation includes a full benefits package with health insurance, retirement options, and paid leave. Required Qualifications: The candidate must have an advanced degree in urban and regional planning, landscape architecture, urban design, or closely related field. We will consider a wide range of backgrounds in filling this position; however, candidates should have a record of excellence in education, research, creative works, or professional practice in urban and regional planning. Preferred: Professional planning experience is desired, with AICP or other relevant credentials preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, submit a complete application with the following: Letter of interest describing your: Vision for the roles that planning will play in the future, and how you expect your work to contribute to this vision. Teaching philosophy and pedagogies, a list of courses of interest in teaching, a summary of courses taught including evidence of teaching effectiveness, and instructional trainings and certifications received. Academic leadership, professional experience, and community service in relation to future academic goals. Specific interests in joining the Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Florida. Writing sample demonstrating research interests and strengths, and/or a portfolio of relevant research, design/creative, or professional work. Teaching evaluations (if received). Curriculum vitae. List of three references, including affiliations, phone numbers, and email addresses. Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu using the requisition number TBD. To ensure full consideration, a complete application should be submitted by October 15, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and/or hiring authority. Review of complete applications will begin upon receipt and will continue until the position is filled. Three letters of reference will be required for shortlisted candidates. Submittal instructions will be provided upon notification. Inquiries can be sent to Search Committee Chairs, Dr. Emre Tepe, emretepe@ufl.edu , and Prof. Kyle Dost, kyledost@ufl.edu . If accommodations due to a disability are needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. All candidates selected for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. The selected candidate will be required to provide official transcripts to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541371/assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541371 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Alachua Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16320000 - LS-MATHEMATICS Classification Title: Assistant Professor – Mathematics Department Classification Minimum Requirements Ph.D. degree in Mathematics or a closely related field is required. Job Description: The Department of Mathematics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor position, beginning August 16, 2027. This is a nine-month, full-time (1.0 FTE), position. The search is open to all areas of mathematics, with candidates in partial differential equations (PDE) and algebra especially encouraged to apply. Candidates should demonstrate a strong and active research program, a compelling vision for future scholarship, the ability to mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers, and excellence in teaching. The successful candidate will join a vibrant department of approximately 60 faculty and 85 graduate students conducting research across a broad spectrum of pure and applied mathematics. For more information, visit Department of Mathematics . The University of Florida offers exceptional support for research and scholarships through significant investments in faculty, facilities, and technology. Resources include HiPerGator, one of the leading university supercomputers in the United States, as well as modern classroom and laboratory facilities that enhance both teaching and research endeavors. More information is available at HiPerGator . The University of Florida is consistently ranked among the nation’s leading public universities and offers an exceptional academic environment. Gainesville provides a high quality of life, with a rich array of cultural opportunities and year-round outdoor recreation. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree in Mathematics or a closely related field is required. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applicants must submit their application materials via MathJobs and separately through the University of Florida online application system. Click the link above to apply. A complete application must include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae, including a complete list of publications Research statement describing the applicant’s qualifications, prior research accomplishments, and future research plans Teaching statement outlining teaching experience, interests, and philosophy Contact information for at least three references, including names and email addresses. References will be contacted directly and asked to upload confidential letters of recommendation through the University of Florida application system. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Questions may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Professor Lei Zhang ( leizhang@ufl.edu ). All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541375/compliance-analyst-ii": {
      "title": "Compliance Analyst II",
      "description": "Compliance Analyst II Apply now Job no: 541375 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Legal/Audit Services/Compliance Department: 11150000 - RE-RESEARCH INTEGRITY Classification Title: Compliance Analyst II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Perform investigator duties for the Research Misconduct (RM) program, working independently. Including the following: identify documents for review, catalog evidence, and maintain chain of custody. Conduct review and analysis of pertinent materials, in accordance with UF and federal regulations. Conduct analysis of source data, other evidence and interview transcripts. Draft assessments and reports for RIO approval and maintain compliant templates. Maintain internal logs and records related to the RM program. Perform data entry, assist with scheduling management plan meetings, and maintain files, including record retention and destruction requirements. Maintain timelines for cases, including federal deadlines and communications. Manage faculty committees, including appointments, reviewing for conflicts of interest, facilitating meetings, scheduling witness interviews, recording interviews, reviewing and correcting transcriptions and providing committees catalogued and organized evidence. Maintain an understanding of research regulations, ethics, and guidelines to ensure requirements are met for each case depending on its disposition under the relevant UF regulation and sponsor-specific requirement. Maintain a familiarity with UF Research contracting, award negotiation and financial management, including an understanding of 2 CFR 200. Expected Salary: Salary Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience understanding complex regulations and applying them in an academic setting. Experience working with university faculty and staff. Research administration or compliance experience in an academic or federal regulatory setting. Experience writing reports and creating original analysis based on multiple sources. Special Instructions to Applicants: • In order to be considered, applicants must provide a cover letter, resume, and list of professional references. • Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. • This is not a remote position and is located on the University of Florida main campus in Gainesville, FL. • A criminal background check is required. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541380/biological-scientist-ii": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist II",
      "description": "Biological Scientist II Apply now Job no: 541380 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29020100 - MD-NEUROSCIENCE-GENERAL Classification Title: Biological Scientist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Job Description: The Department of Neuroscience and the McKnight Brain Institute at the University of Florida is seeking applications for a Biological Scientist II in the laboratory of Dr. Ziyuan Guo. The Guo Laboratory focuses on human stem cell biology, 3D brain and barrier organoid/assembloid modeling, microfluidic platforms, and molecular/epitranscriptomic mechanisms of neurodegenerative and neurodevelopmental diseases. The incumbent will work closely with the PI and laboratory personnel to conduct research, assist with experimental design and multi-omic data analytics, oversee day-to-day laboratory operations, and mentor student research assistants. Duties include: Conducting Laboratory Experiments: Perform mammalian cell culture, maintenance, and directed differentiations of human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) into neural and vascular lineages. Generate and maintain 3D brain organoids, blood-brain barrier (BBB) / blood-spinal cord barrier (BSCB) assembloids, and organ-on-a-chip platforms. Execute molecular protocols including qPCR, DNA/RNA extractions, molecular cloning, CRISPR/Cas9 manipulations, Western blotting, tissue sectioning (cryostat/vibratome), immunofluorescence staining, and confocal microscopy. Experimental Design & Data Analytics: Collaborate with the PI to design, optimize, and execute experimental protocols. Perform quantitative data analysis, image quantification, and statistical analysis. Organize, curate, and maintain large data repositories (including single-cell transcriptomic, genomic, and imaging datasets) required for manuscript preparation. Laboratory Management & Maintenance: Manage day-to-day laboratory ordering of supplies and reagents, record keeping, and inventory tracking via UF purchasing portals. Maintain stem cell culture suites, biosafety cabinets, incubators, and common reagent stocks. Maintain active safety logs, equipment certifications, and compliance documentation under EH&S, IBC, and IRB/SCRO guidelines. Scientific Reporting & Trainee Mentoring: Assist with literature searches, figure preparation, and scientific progress summaries for grant applications and manuscripts. Assist with onboarding new lab personnel and teaching fundamental stem cell and molecular biology skills to undergraduate research assistants. Expected Salary: $53,000-$56,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Preferred: Direct hands-on experience with mammalian cell culture (hiPSC/hESC culture experience is highly preferred). Experience with 3D tissue culture, organoids, microfluidic/organ-on-a-chip platforms, or tissue sectioning and immunofluorescence staining. Technical proficiency in molecular biology workflows (qPCR, molecular cloning, Western blot, CRISPR editing). Familiarity with computational data analysis tools in R, Python, or MATLAB. Demonstrated desire to learn new neuroscience and stem cell engineering techniques. Excellent organizational, record-keeping, and interpersonal communication skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and Contact Information for Three (3) References: Names, email addresses, and phone numbers of professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541388/sponsored-programs-officer-iiiiii": {
      "title": "Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III",
      "description": "Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III Apply now Job no: 541388 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 11060000 - RE-SPONSORED PROGRAMS Classification Title: Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III Classification Minimum Requirements: Level I : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience in research administration. Level II : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Level III : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and three years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The position is located within UF's Division of Sponsored Programs responsible for learning and facilitating a wide range of sponsored programs administration activities, primarily proposal submission on behalf of the University of Florida. Coordinate and support faculty and staff in the preparation of proposals being submitted to federal, state, corporate, and other funding agencies. Review proposals for accuracy and compliance with federal, state, university and sponsor regulations. Ensure that university policies, such as the F&A Policy, Cost Sharing Policy, PI Status Policy, Effort Policy and others, are followed. Review and analyze proposed budgets for compliance with guidelines; check budget details for accurate reflection of line items and proper indirect cost rates. Learn and maintain knowledge with university and sponsor electronic research administration systems in order to ensure timely and compliant submission of proposals. Act as a liaison between Principal Investigator (PI), campus administrators, agency officials, as well as federal, state, and local offices to resolve problems or issues that develop during course of proposal review; interacts closely with PI and their associates to provide technical assistance during the development process of proposals; and provides guidance to departmental staff, investigators, and research administrators on agency policy and requirements. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Level I : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience in research administration. Level II : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Level III : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and three years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Level I : Facility to learn electronic research administration software preferred. In addition, skills required include: the ability to provide excellent customer service; excellent verbal and written communication skills; ability to work independently and with others ability to search out federal, sponsor and university policy and guidance and apply critical thinking skills to make effective decisions; negotiation skills; ability to maintain positive and effective working relationships; effectiveness in use of electronic business applications with other systems; experience in the Microsoft Office suite; experience in PeopleSoft grants; experience at the University of Florida; and being a strong problem solver, seeking win-win answers. Level II : At least three years of sponsored programs administration experience that demonstrates knowledge of federal policies governing the administration of grants, contracts, and cooperative agreements at educational institutions, including but not limited to the Federal Uniform Guidance, Federal Acquisition Regulations, Code of Federal Regulations, NIH Grants Policy Statement, NSF Grants Policy Manual, knowledge of contracting and regulations applicable to the University of Florida. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to be an effective communicator verbally and in writing. Ability to critically think and use good judgment. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to interface with electronic business applications with other systems. Ability to use with personal computers, Windows, Word, Access Database, Excel, UFIRST and myUFL Grants. Level III : At least three years of sponsored research administration experience that demonstrates knowledge of federal policies governing the administration of grants, contracts, and cooperative agreements at educational institutions, including 2 CFR 200, Federal Acquisition Regulations, Code of Federal Regulations, NIH Grants Policy Statement, NSF Grants Policy Manual, and knowledge of State regulations applicable to the University of Florida. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to be an effective communicator verbally and in writing. Ability to critically think and use good judgment. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to interface with electronic business applications with other systems. Ability to use enterprise applications and various personal computing tools. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is not a remote work position, and it is located on the University of Florida's Main Campus in Gainesville, Florida. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and a list of three professional references. This position is open until filled. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable pool of candidates is established. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541315/ops-physician-prn-pulmonary-critical-care-uf-health-st-johns": {
      "title": "OPS Physician - PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care - UF Health St. Johns",
      "description": "OPS Physician - PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care - UF Health St. Johns Apply now Job no: 541315 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: St. Johns Categories: Allied Health, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30010100 - JX-DEAN-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience Federal DEA Certification Job Description: UF Health St. Johns is seeking PRN Critical Care Physicians to join our growing critical care and pulmonology team in Northeast Florida. This opportunity offers the flexibility to maintain your existing practice or professional commitments while providing clinical coverage within the UF Health system. Physicians will have the opportunity to provide coverage across hospital-based critical care settings and, depending on training and departmental needs, outpatient pulmonology. Clinical Responsibilities Provide clinical coverage in one or more of the following settings based on departmental needs, physician qualifications, and availability: UF Health Flagler Hospital – Intensive Care Unit -12-hour clinical shifts UF Health Durbin Park Hospital – Intensive Care Unit - 10-hour clinical shifts UF Health St. Augustine – Pulmonology Outpatient Clinic - 8-hour clinical shifts Shift Requirements This position requires a minimum of one clinical shift per month, with scheduling coordinated based on departmental staffing needs and physician availability. About St. Johns County St. Johns County is one of the fastest-growing and most desirable counties in Florida, consistently ranked for its exceptional quality of life, strong public schools (#1 in the state of Florida), family-friendly communities, and easy access to the Jacksonville metro area. About UF Health Flagler: UF Health St. Johns is a total health care enterprise aimed at advancing the physical, social and economic health of Northeast Florida communities. From serving as the lead agency for St. Johns County’s Continuum of Care with an aim to end homelessness, to bringing a new concept in health villages throughout the region, UF Health St. Johns is your caring partner throughout the journey of life. UF Health St. Johns is an extension of UF Health Flagler Hospital, which has a 130-year legacy of caring for the community. The 335-bed hospital has been named among America’s 50 Best hospitals out of nearly 4,500 nationwide. About UF Health Durbin: UF Health Durbin is a 42.5 acre health and wellness campus that will advance health care services as part of UF’s regional expansion plan for Jacksonville and St. Johns County. Located in northeast St. Johns County, UF Health Durbin Park will include a full -service 99-bed hospital with a focus on acute, intensive, emergency care, orthopaedics, neurosurgery, general surgery, cardiovascular medicine, and women’s services as well as a multidisciplinary medical office building. Future potential site development include the ability to expand hospital beds as well as an ambulatory surgical center, outpatient imagining and additional medial office building. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Federal DEA Certification Preferred: MD or DO degree Board certification or board eligibility in Critical Care Medicine Pulmonary Medicine training/certification preferred for physicians interested in outpatient pulmonology coverage Active or eligible for an unrestricted Florida medical license Active or eligible for federal DEA registration, as applicable Ability to obtain appropriate UF Health medical staff privileges and credentialing Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please submit an application at Careers at UF with the following documents attached: Resume/Curriculum Vitae Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and consideration will continue until the positions are filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at https://naces.org/members/ The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes (Patient Contact and Bloodborne Pathogens) Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541373/assistant-director-of-provider-workforce-strategy-and-administration-uf-emergency-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration – UF Emergency Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration – UF Emergency Medicine Apply now Job no: 541373 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29290100 - MD-EMERGENCY MED-AED Classification Title: AST DIR, Health Care Admin Classification Minimum Requirements Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida, College of Medicine, Department of Emergency Medicine (DEM), actively seeks a motivated and experienced individual to join us as the Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration . The Assistant Director of Healthcare and Provider Workforce Administration leads workforce strategy and operations for the Department of Emergency Medicine, ensuring 24/7/365 coverage across all practice locations. Reporting to the Vice Chair of Administrative Affairs, this role manages scheduling approximately 60 daily shifts for physicians, fellows, clinical faculty, and APPs with a focus on optimizing utilization, productivity, documentation compliance, and performance outcomes. The UF Department of Emergency Medicine proudly maintains an affiliation with UF Health Shands Hospital, a distinguished 1,162-bed teaching facility renowned for its Level 1 trauma center and cutting-edge burn center. As the primary referral center for North Central Florida, our institution stands as a beacon of excellence in healthcare. This affiliation underscores our commitment to top-tier medical care and innovation. Key Responsibilities: Key responsibilities include managing the PRN physician group, OPS, and locum tenens operations (onboarding, integration, and utilization); leading workforce analytics and cross-functional projects; overseeing APP recruitment and the scribe program; and enforcing timely note completion to support billing and compliance. The Assistant Director also supports strategic planning, workforce projections, and emergency preparedness, and advises on workforce policy to ensure alignment with institutional and regulatory standards. Provider Workforce Strategy and Leadership Lead strategic workforce planning for physicians, faculty, fellows, and APPs Align workforce utilization with departmental, organizational, and patient care goals Oversee workforce analytics and scheduling performance metrics to guide provider deployment Lead the PRN physician group (~25% of workforce), ensuring effective deployment and integration Drive long-term workforce planning through forecasting and staffing projections Partner with departmental and health system leadership to align staffing with clinical priorities Oversee provider documentation compliance, monitoring timely note completion with clinical leadership Direct cross-functional projects to improve workforce efficiency, documentation compliance, and operational workflows Participate in emergency preparedness and disaster response planning for provider deployment Co-lead the Provider Workforce Clinical Scheduling and Utilization Committee with the Vice Chair of Clinical Affairs Clinical Scheduling and Operations Management Oversee scheduling operations ensuring 24/7/365 coverage across five clinical sites Manage approximately 60 daily shifts across emergency, observation, and inpatient care Oversee real-time staffing adjustments based on patient volume, acuity, and operational needs Review and approve leave requests per guidelines while maintaining site coverage Collaborate with the Administrator on Call and site/clinical leadership to resolve urgent staffing issues Oversee OPS and locum tenens scheduling, onboarding, and utilization tracking Ensure accurate implementation of faculty effort allocations and assignment changes in schedules Coordinate scheduling workflows that reinforce timely, complete provider documentation Program Oversight and Operational Support Lead operational reporting on staffing utilization, coverage trends, and workforce analytics Direct recruitment, onboarding, and education processes for APPs Supervise the departmental scribe program and associated staff Lead special projects within provider workforce administration Manage day-to-day PRN and locum administration, including compensation support and serving as primary liaison with staffing vendors Drive continuous improvement in workforce operations through data analysis and process optimization Monitor note completion trends and address barriers with clinical leadership Schedule: 8:00 am - 5:00 pm Monday through Friday, with additional hours depending upon specific deadlines. The City of Gainesville: Gainesville, home to the University of Florida, is ranked #1 best city to move to in Florida by USA Today. Centrally located in North Florida, it offers a blend of small-town charm and big-city amenities, with close proximity to major airports, family entertainment, and some of the best beaches in the world. Known for its beautiful landscapes and urban forest, Gainesville is a hidden gem that provides year-round outdoor activities, award-winning college sports, and a rich cultural scene. The University of Florida and UF Health are the leading employers in the area, making Gainesville an excellent place to work and raise a family. Learn more about Florida’s premiere health system, UF Health, and the advantages of living in Gainesville at UF Health Destination . Expected Salary: The annual salary ranges between $105,000 - $110,000 and is commensurate with education and experience. At the University of Florida, we are committed to the well-being and professional growth of our employees. Our benefits package is designed to support your health, work-life balance, and financial security: Generous Paid Leave: Enjoy 22 days of paid vacation, 13 days of paid sick leave, 11 paid Holidays, and 8 weeks of paid parental leave. Individual and Family Health Insurance Benefits State of Florida Retirement Plan Options State of Florida Basic Life Insurance Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Professional Development Opportunities Wellness Programs available to employees For more details on our comprehensive benefits, visit UF Benefits . Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred Master’s in Healthcare Administration (MHA), Business Administration (MBA), Public Health (MPH), or related field Progressive leadership experience in healthcare operations, physician workforce management, or academic medicine Expertise in complex clinical scheduling and provider workforce planning in a multi-site, 24/7 environment Experience managing physician, APP, PRN, and/or locum tenens workforce operations Strong analytical skills with experience in workforce data, productivity metrics, and operational reporting Proven ability to lead staff, manage teams, and oversee operational programs Experience collaborating with physician leadership and senior executives Knowledge of emergency medicine, acute care operations, or similar high-acuity clinical environments Familiarity with workforce management systems, EMRs, and reporting/analytics tools Demonstrated ability to lead process improvement, change initiatives, and operational optimization efforts Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, candidates must submit the following documents: A cover letter detailing relevant experience and qualifications. A current resume/CV. For questions, please reach out to EM-Careers@ufl.edu Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541321/assistant-instructional-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Instructional Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Instructional Professor Apply now Job no: 541321 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related discipline, and demonstrable expertise in teaching at the university level. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry at the University of Florida, one of the nation's leading public research universities, invites applications for a full-time (1.0 FTE), 12-month, non-tenure-track faculty position in Organic Chemistry at the rank of Lecturer (Assistant Instructional Professor). The successful candidate will join a dynamic academic community with an anticipated start date as early as January 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach Organic Chemistry lecture and laboratory courses, maintain instructional laboratory instrumentation, and contribute to the development and implementation of innovative pedagogical materials and undergraduate curriculum. Responsibilities also include managing electronic course content and online learning resources, supervising and training graduate teaching assistants, fostering a strong culture of laboratory safety and ethical conduct, mentoring undergraduate students, and collaborating with faculty within and beyond the department. The Department encourages the submission of proposals focused on chemistry education and supports faculty engagement in the scholarship of teaching and learning. Teaching faculty are fully integrated members of the department and have opportunities to participate in departmental, university, and professional service activities. Applicants must demonstrate a sustained record of teaching and a strong commitment to undergraduate chemistry education. The successful candidate will have evidence of scholarly and professional achievement, a passion for student success, and a demonstrated ability to develop and implement innovative, evidence-based instructional approaches. The ideal candidate will also exhibit a commitment to pedagogical innovation and a commitment to service, including active involvement in professional organizations and the broader chemistry education community. Expected Salary: $80,000 - $85,000 Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related discipline, and demonstrable expertise in teaching at the university level. Preferred: We seek candidates who have demonstrated creativity in chemistry education and a strong commitment to pursuing a career dedicated to the education and mentorship of exceptional undergraduate students within one of the largest chemistry departments in the country. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Complete applications include the following files in PDF format: A cover letter Curriculum vitae A statement of teaching practices and philosophy Names and contact information for three professional references (the search committee will request confidential letters of recommendation only from candidates selected for its long list) Additional documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. This position is open until filled. Review of applications will begin on October 8, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541328/assistant-associate-professor-of-inorganic-chemistry": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Inorganic Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Inorganic Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541328 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Chemistry, invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Inorganic Chemistry, broadly defined. The appointments will commence on August 16, 2027. Successful candidates are expected to conduct innovative research at the highest level and to establish an outstanding, independent, externally funded research program. We seek candidates with a record of excellence in research and scholarship, the ability to excel in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and who will contribute to the service mission of the Department of Chemistry and the University of Florida. For more information about the Department of Chemistry, please visit our website, www.chem.ufl.edu. The University of Florida is a land grant, sea grant, and space grant university and home to the most comprehensive university health sciences facilities in the southeast. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A complete curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. A description of proposed research projects (maximum 5 pages). A statement of teaching philosophy. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541331/postdoctoral-associate-in-artificial-intelligence-and-quantum-chemistry": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541331 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Artificial Intelligence, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in physical/theoretical chemistry or artificial intelligence/machine learning (or related fields). Job Description: A Post-doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry position is available to work in a project exploring the interface of AI for QC led by Dr. Thorpe in the Department of Chemistry. The research will focus on the development and implementation of AI/ML techniques to extend and accelerate quantum chemical simulations of complex molecules and reactions. Must have PhD in Physical/Theoretical Chemistry or Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning or related fields. This position will be initially awarded for one year, and, contingent upon strong performance and conduct and availability of funds, may be renewed for up to three years. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. To see more, visit, benefits.hr.ufl.edu. Required Qualifications: Experience in the implementation and application of machine learning to quantum chemistry and a programming background in Python and C++. Preferred: Familiarity with wavefunction-based quantum chemistry. Experience and interest in fundamental studies of chemical reactions, kinetics, thermochemistry, and spectroscopy. Interest in mentoring graduate students. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae , (3) a statement on teaching and research goals, and (4) a list of at least three references. After initial review, letters of recommendation will be requested from the references for selected applicants. Applications will be reviewed beginning 10/1/2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541334/assistantassociate-full-professor-neuroimmunology-neurologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Neuroimmunology Neurologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Neuroimmunology Neurologist Apply now Job no: 541334 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29181100 - MD-NEURO-MULTIPLE SCLEROSIS Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Neuroimmunology, Multiple Sclerosis, or a related subspecialty Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Job Description: The Department of Neurology invites applications for a full-time Neuroimmunology Specialist at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor , commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek an outstanding clinician-scholar committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary neuroscience team dedicated to the diagnosis, treatment, and research of neuroimmunologic disorders. Responsibilities include providing comprehensive outpatient and inpatient neuroimmunology services, caring for patients with multiple sclerosis and related autoimmune neurologic diseases, contributing to educational programs, and engaging in scholarly activities that advance the field of neuroimmunology. Essential Responsibilities Clinical Duties Provide expert care for patients with multiple sclerosis (MS), neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD), myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein antibody-associated disease (MOGAD), autoimmune encephalitis, and other neuroimmunologic disorders Deliver comprehensive outpatient and inpatient neuroimmunology consultation services Develop and implement individualized treatment plans utilizing current evidence-based therapies and disease-modifying treatments Collaborate with specialists in neuroradiology, neuro-ophthalmology, rehabilitation medicine, rheumatology, immunology, and related disciplines to provide multidisciplinary care Participate in departmental call schedules and clinical coverage responsibilities Contribute to quality improvement initiatives and the growth and development of the Neuroimmunology and Multiple Sclerosis Program Academic Responsibilities Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other healthcare trainees Develop and participate in educational conferences, lectures, and curriculum activities Engage in scholarly activities, including clinical, translational, immunologic, outcomes, or health services research Pursue and maintain an active academic portfolio appropriate to faculty rank Participate in departmental, institutional, and professional service activities Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Neuroimmunology, Multiple Sclerosis, or a related subspecialty Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ and should include a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation may be attached to the application or sent directly to Polly Glattli, glattpa@ufl.edu. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The Search Committee will accept applications until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis by the committee. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541337/assistantassociate-full-professor-vascular-neurologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Vascular Neurologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Vascular Neurologist Apply now Job no: 541337 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29180700 - MD - VASCULAR NEUROLOGY Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Vascular Neurology Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Board certified or eligible in Vascular Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in the state of Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Job Description: The Department of Neurology invites applications for a full-time Vascular Neurologist at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor , commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek an outstanding clinician-scholar committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary neuroscience team dedicated to the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of cerebrovascular diseases. Responsibilities include providing comprehensive inpatient and outpatient vascular neurology services, participating in acute stroke management, contributing to educational programs, and engaging in scholarly activities that advance the field of vascular neurology. Essential Responsibilities Clinical Duties Provide expert care for patients with stroke, transient ischemic attack (TIA), cerebrovascular disorders, and related neurological conditions Participate in acute stroke evaluation and management, including telestroke and inpatient consultation services as applicable Collaborate with neurosurgery, neurointerventional radiology, emergency medicine, rehabilitation medicine, and other specialties to deliver comprehensive stroke care Participate in departmental call schedules and clinical coverage responsibilities Contribute to quality improvement initiatives and stroke program development Academic Responsibilities Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other healthcare trainees Develop and participate in educational conferences, lectures, and curriculum activities Engage in scholarly activities, including clinical, translational, health services, or outcomes research Pursue and maintain an active academic portfolio appropriate to faculty rank Participate in departmental, institutional, and professional service activities Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Vascular Neurology Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Board certified or eligible in Vascular Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in the applicable state Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ and should include a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation may be attached to the application or sent directly to Polly Glattli, glattpa@ufl.edu. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The Search Committee will accept applications until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis by the committee. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541340/associate-director-of-development-fredric-g-levin-college-of-law": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Development, Fredric G. Levin College of Law",
      "description": "Associate Director of Development, Fredric G. Levin College of Law Apply now Job no: 541340 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 24030100 - LW-DEVELOPMENT - ALUMNI AFFRS Classification Title: ASO DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and three years of relevant experience. Job Description: The future of legal education is built by those who invest in the next generation of leaders, advocates, and changemakers. The Fredric G. Levin College of Law is growing its Advancement team and seeking an Associate Director of Development to help strengthen donor relationships and expand philanthropic support. Working closely with alumni, donors, college leadership, and advancement colleagues, this role cultivates meaningful partnerships, advances major gift opportunities, and helps bring the college's strategic priorities to life. This position focuses on securing and stewarding gifts of $100,000 and above while fostering lifelong connections with the law school community. If you enjoy building authentic relationships and inspiring generosity, this role offers the chance to make a lasting impact on the next generation of legal leaders. RESPONSIBILITIES Prospect Cultivation & Portfolio Management Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Build and manage a robust relationship portfolio targeting small and mid-level gift donations that support the strategic goals of the College of Law. Maximize travel plans to strategic geographic regions for major gift solicitation/donor engagement. Research, identify, and cultivate individual alumni and friends as prospective donors to the College of Law. Collaborate with the Office of Estate & Gift Planning team to develop and implement a giving strategy for qualified donors at the planned giving level. Qualify prospects by making contact with them, both virtually and in person, to evaluate their readiness to engage with the law school and their interest in making a gift. Development Strategy Set and implement strategies for identification, qualification, relationship-building, and solicitation to close and steward major gifts. Collaborate with team members to create and implement annual fundraising initiatives that support the strategic goals of the law school including end-of-fiscal-year and end-of-calendar-year campaigns and other special fundraising activities created by the College of Law and the UF Foundation. Collaborate with college partners to understand and formulate priorities and opportunities related to individual gifts at the appropriate level. Create gift proposals, concept papers, and other documents used for soliciting restricted and unrestricted contributions to the College of Law. Develop comprehensive understanding of the UF Law mission, strategy, and goals as they relate to fundraising. Acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the mission, visions, and values of UF Law and UF Advancement. Stewardship Communications, Events, and Administration Serve as the liaison to the Law Alumni Council Membership Committee, working directly with alumni committee members as a representative of UF Law. Attend and support alumni events and initiatives, partnering with colleagues to involve alumni when appropriate. Plan and execute select regional alumni engagement events. Support College of Law and UF functions to help steward donors and move prospects through the gift continuum toward closure. Events include, but are not limited to, class reunions, alumni receptions, alumni board meetings, local and state bar conferences and bar exams, and other special occasions. Develop and implement communication and engagement plans to keep prospects connected to the law school and informed about the vision, goals, and values of the College of Law, including its funding priorities and current giving opportunities. Ensure best practices in office systems, files, and record keeping. Ensure effective use of strategic tools and resources, such as contact reports, prospect management, research analysis, and UFF database. Contribute to the planning and monitoring of event and travel budget. Steward gifts by completing thank you cards and other gift recognition with donors and prospects. Other Duties Perform related duties as required or deemed appropriate to the accomplishment of the responsibilities and functions related to this position. Expected Salary: $65,000-75,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and three years of relevant experience. A valid Florida driver’s license is required. Preferred: The successful candidate will possess: One to three years of fundraising, development, sales, or related experience. Prior experience in a university or nonprofit environment. General knowledge and understanding of business/economic environment. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Demonstrated successful experience in prospect identification, relationship building, solicitation, and stewardship. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and be a team player. Competency in problem-solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver's license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work sponsorship is not available for this position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541341/assistant-professor": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR Apply now Job no: 541341 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16400100 - LS-PSYCHOLOGY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants will have a doctorate degree in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field by the start date of the position, August 16, 2027. Job Description: The Department of PSYCHOLOGY at the UNIVERSITY OF FLORIDA, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing Assistant Professor in the field of Developmental Science. The specific area of research is open but should focus on developmental research in both brain and behavior that complements and expands existing strengths in the department and the university at large, including the development of cognition, perception, attention, memory, language, decision making, emotion, and/or social functioning. Priority will be given to candidates with research expertise in children from early childhood through middle childhood. Research and training in our developmental psychology program encompasses the lifespan; hence, we will also consider candidates whose expertise spans multiple life stages, including childhood. Applicants should have an outstanding record of research achievement and a commitment to undergraduate and graduate instruction in developmental psychology and neuroscience. The successful candidate will be expected to (1) maintain a program of research with high potential for external funding, (2) teach graduate and undergraduate courses in developmental psychology, (3) mentor students, and (4) provide service to the institution and academy. The Psychology Department offers doctoral programs in behavioral and cognitive neuroscience, behavior analysis, counseling psychology, developmental psychology, and social psychology. We currently have 44 full-time faculty, ~ 100 PhD students, and > 2,000 undergraduate majors. About the University of Florida A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), the University of Florida is the state’s oldest and most comprehensive land-, sea-, and space-grant university, with an enrollment of over 50,000 students. For 2026, the University of Florida was ranked the seventh-best public university in the United States by U.S. News & World Report, with exceptional research, teaching, and a university-wide commitment to open intellectual discourse. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs, including magnet schools and an International Baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . The Department of Psychology is committed to promoting an environment that welcomes all abilities, classes, ethnicities/races, religions, gender identities and expressions, etc. We particularly welcome applicants who can contribute to such an environment through their scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. Required Qualifications: Applicants will have a doctorate degree in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field by the start date of the position, August 16, 2027. Preferred: Candidates with post-doctoral experience will be prioritized. Candidates with evidence of methodological expertise in human neuroscience or modeling/AI will be prioritized. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at [insert link]. A complete application includes: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement Statement of teaching experience, interests, and philosophy Three letters of recommendation. Please provide the names and email addresses of three references and the application system will automatically request letters at the time of application submission. Review of applications will begin on October 15th, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to search committee co-chairs Lisa Scott ( lscott@ufl.edu ) and Natalie Ebner ( natalie.ebner@ufl.edu ). Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541342/assistant-professor-in-history-of-modern-philosophy": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in History of Modern Philosophy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in History of Modern Philosophy Apply now Job no: 541342 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16340000 - LS-PHILOSOPHY Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants will have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or complete the Ph.D. by date of hire, will have an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy, and will provide evidence of effective teaching. Job Description: The Department of Philosophy at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for a nine-month, full-time, tenure-accruing position at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 16, 2027, in the History of Modern Philosophy. The successful candidate will pursue an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy and provide service to the department, the university, and the profession. Although candidates must have Early Modern Philosophy as their core area of research, additional expertise in either Medieval Philosophy or 19 th -Century Philosophy would be useful as the department has teaching needs in all these areas. Tenure-line faculty are typically assigned four courses per academic year at the undergraduate and graduate levels. In addition to regularly teaching required courses for the undergraduate Philosophy major and minor, the ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students will also be expected. The department has recently grown with multiple hires in the last several years and currently has a significant number of faculty working primarily in the history of philosophy. It is committed to ensuring a robust coverage of all the core areas of philosophy, though it has developed special strength in the ethics of technology and the philosophy of artificial intelligence. The department also has a good working relationship with the newly established Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education, which has hired several faculty members with interests in the history of philosophy during the past few years. To learn more about the Department of Philosophy, applicants are encouraged to visit the departmental website ( Department of Philosophy ). Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Applicants will have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or complete the Ph.D. by date of hire, will have an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy, and will provide evidence of effective teaching. Preferred: Additional expertise in either Medieval Philosophy or 19 th -Century Philosophy would be useful as the department has teaching needs in all these areas. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae A statement regarding the applicant’s research agenda A statement and supporting materials regarding teaching effectiveness A sample of written work of moderate length Names and contact information for three professional references Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541347/assistantassociate-professor-of-maternal-and-child-nutrition": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Maternal and Child Nutrition",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Maternal and Child Nutrition Apply now Job no: 541347 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60150000 - AG-FOOD SCIENCE - HUMAN NUTR Classification Title: Ast/Aso Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF. IFAS and the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition invites applications from qualified individuals for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. The successful candidate will develop a collaborative, externally funded, and internationally engaged research program focused on maternal and child nutrition, with particular emphasis on improving health and developmental outcomes for women, infants, and young children in global and underserved settings. This is a 9-month, tenure-accruing position that will be 70% research ( Florida Agricultural Experiment Station ) and 30% teaching ( College of Agricultural and Life Sciences ) available in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition, Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida, based in Gainesville, FL, expected to start in Spring 2027. The position is supported by a competitive salary with benefits including health, life, dental and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; paid holidays and wellness benefits. This assignment may be revised in accordance with the unit's needs. Tenure will accrue in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition. The faculty member will seek contract and grant funding actively to support their program. Expected duties We seek candidates who conduct research and integrative studies in global nutrition and health, public health nutrition, food and nutrition security, and maternal and child health. Relevant areas may include the role of nutrient-dense foods and dietary patterns—including animal-source foods, fruits, vegetables, legumes, nuts, fortified foods, and complementary foods—in supporting maternal health, infant growth, early childhood development, and long-term health outcomes. Successful candidates will use interdisciplinary, systems-based, and population-level approaches and may integrate primary data collection, epidemiology, intervention research, implementation science, dietary assessment, nutritional biomarkers, data science, and predictive modeling. The position will include responsibilities in research and teaching, with opportunities to mentor undergraduate and graduate students and contribute to courses in nutrition, public health, global health, and food systems. The successful candidate will engage in scholarly activities related to instruction. Examples of these scholarly activities include teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, advising and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, seeking funding for the teaching program, supervising undergraduate and graduate research and creative work, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences Honors Program, distance and international education. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background information This position is one of two new faculty positions which will seed a nexus in global health nutrition and food security between Global Food Systems Institute (GFSI) within IFAS and the One Health Center of Excellence (OHCE) within the College of Public Health and Health Professions ’ (PHHP) Department of Environmental and Global Health (EGH). These synergistic positions, a food safety epidemiologist in EGH and a public health nutritionist in Food Science and Human Nutrition, will complement the existing expertise in interdisciplinary research on global food security and nutrition across the lifespan. The position will be housed in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition, strengthening existing expertise in maternal and child nutrition, community and public health nutrition, nutritional sciences, food systems, food safety, food processing, and global nutrition. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with faculty across the department and throughout UF/IFAS, PHHP, the Emerging Pathogens Institute , and other university programs engaged in global health, One Health, agriculture, nutrition, and international development. The Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition is a unit in CALS. The department has approximately 27 full-time faculty members including a significant group of federally funded researchers with a rich history of national research awards. The graduate program enrolls over 50 M.S. and Ph.D. students in Nutritional Sciences, Dietetics, and Food Science. The doctoral program in Nutritional Sciences is a part of the Center for Nutritional Sciences , which is interdisciplinary and includes a number of faculty from different departments across campus, along with a vibrant seminar series. The Department enrolls more than 450 undergraduate students across its three majors: Nutritional Sciences, Food Science, and Dietetics. Nutritional Sciences faculty conduct research in multiple areas that span micronutrients, molecular nutrition, human metabolism, physiology, clinical, and community nutrition. Our faculty collaborate with colleagues from the Colleges of Medicine, Health and Human Performance, Public Health, Pharmacy, Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. Within walking distance are superb opportunities to participate in basic and translational research with the UF Diabetes Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a doctoral degree, or a foreign equivalent, in nutrition, public health nutrition, nutritional epidemiology, community nutrition, global health, epidemiology, public health, or a closely related field by the position’s start date. Candidates must demonstrate the ability to develop a research program involving human populations and to work effectively in multidisciplinary and cross-cultural collaborations. Relevant research may include maternal and child nutrition, community or population nutrition, global nutrition, food and nutrition security, dietary interventions, or the evaluation of nutrition-related health outcomes. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in scholarship, teaching, and student mentoring. Applicants at the Assistant Professor level should show strong potential to secure external research funding and develop an independent research program. Applicants at the Associate Professor level should demonstrate an established record of scholarly productivity, external funding, student mentorship, and teaching appropriate to rank. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Preferred candidates will have experience conducting nutrition or public health research in low- and middle-income countries or other resource-constrained settings. Additional preferred qualifications may include experience in one or more of the following areas: Designing and implementing human nutrition interventions, community-based trials, randomized controlled trials, or longitudinal studies involving women, infants, children, families, or communities; Conducting community-engaged, implementation, translational, or population-based nutrition research; Working with international universities, governments, nongovernmental organizations, community organizations, or other local partners; Using dietary assessment methods, nutritional biomarkers, anthropometric measurements, developmental or health outcomes, or food-security indicators; Applying quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods approaches, including nutritional epidemiology, meta-analysis, population-level modeling, data integration, or implementation evaluation; Managing research activities across international sites, including ethical review, participant recruitment, research staff training, data quality assurance, and culturally appropriate community engagement; Developing externally funded, interdisciplinary research programs addressing maternal and child nutrition, global health, or food and nutrition security. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by October 15 th , 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. This position is available January 1, 2027, and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541347 Dr. Juan E. Andrade Laborde Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida, IFAS Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition 572 Newell Dr. Gainesville, FL 32611-0620 Email: jandrade2@ufl.edu Applicants should go to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position, background, and qualifications relative to duties, responsibilities and qualifications listed above (2-page limit) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Statement of research activities and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Current curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541316/assistant-professor-astrophysics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Astrophysics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Astrophysics Apply now Job no: 541316 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Professor in Physics Job Description: The Department of Physics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida has an opening for a full-time (1.0 FTE), nine-month, tenure-accuring Assistant Professor in experimental particle astrophysics to begin August 16, 2027. We seek highly qualified candidates from areas related to current and novel experimental dark matter direct detection searches. This position offers the opportunity to interact with and build upon the Department’s extensive research efforts in related and complementary directions, including dark matter detection (ADMX, ALPS, SuperCDMS), experimental particle physics (Belle II, COHERENT, CMS, DUNE, SBND), gravitational wave detection (LIGO, LISA, NANOGrav), theoretical astrophysics, and condensed matter physics. It also provides access to the world-class HiPerGator research computing center. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Minimum Requirements: The successful candidate must hold a Ph.D. degree. Potential for establishing a well-recognized research program, excellence in teaching, and mentoring undergraduate and graduate student body is essential. Preferred: Relevant postdoctoral research experience is preferred About University of Florida UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked 7th in the country among public universities (US News and World Report 2025 rankings), and 1st among public institutions in the Wall Street Journal 2023 survey. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu and should include: (1) a cover letter, (2) a curriculum vitae with publication list, (3) a research statement, (4) a teaching statement (in which you are welcome to discuss outreach activities and other contributions you’ve undertaken to enhance student experience), (5) a statement of broader impacts (as described by the National Science Foundation ) of past or planned future activities, (6) at least three confidential letters of reference. Reference providers will only be notified and provided an upload link once the application is submitted in full, so early completion of an application is highly recommended. To ensure full consideration, applications and letters of reference must be submitted by November 8, 2026. For more information, please contact Prof. Tarek Saab ( tsaab@ufl.edu ). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541320/postdoctoral-fellow-hamilton-school": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Hamilton School",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Hamilton School Apply now Job no: 541320 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Humanities Department: 57301000 - HS-HAMILTON SCHOOL ADMIN Classification Title: Postdoctoral Fellow Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree by June 2027. Candidates who received the doctorate after June 2023 are eligible to apply. Job Description: Position Description The Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education at the University of Florida invites applications for two Postdoctoral Fellowships in English literature, intellectual history, political theory, theology and religion, philosophy, political science, and strategic studies. Fellows will pursue ambitious research and participate in an interdisciplinary community devoted to serious inquiry into enduring questions of human nature, political order, religion, literature, philosophy, history, war, statecraft, and the ideas and institutions that have shaped Western civilization and the American experience. The Fellowship is a two-year, residential appointment beginning August 2027. It is designed to provide an optimal context for pursuing research toward publications. Consequently, while Fellows have the option of teaching a course in their areas of expertise, teaching does not constitute a requirement for the Fellowship. Applications from scholars whose work transcends traditional disciplinary boundaries are particularly welcome. Fellows can expect structured mentoring and job-market advising. Fellows are expected to participate in regular Fellows and Faculty Seminars as well as visiting speakers sponsored by the Hamilton School. About the Hamilton School The Hamilton School is a multi-disciplinary academic unit at the University of Florida, devoted to excellence teaching and research in the humanities and social sciences. Its work engages the ideas and institutions, traditions and texts that have shaped Western civilization and the American experience, while fostering open inquiry, civil discourse, and robust engagement across disciplinary boundaries. Fellows will join a rapidly growing community of scholars working in history, literature, philosophy, political thought, religion, political science, international affairs, and war, statecraft, and strategy. Expected Salary: Salaries begin at approximately $80,000, and include benefits, and $5,000 for research support. Fellows are eligible for UnitedHealthcare Student Health Insurance Plan. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree by June 2027. Candidates who received the doctorate after June 2023 are eligible to apply. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: A complete application must include: A cover letter A curriculum vitae A research statement of approximately 3-5 single-spaced pages describing the project to be pursued during the fellowship A writing sample of no more than 40 single-spaced pages Three names and contact information for referees who can be reached for reference letters Successful applicants will undergo two-stages of zoom interviews. The appointment will begin in August 2027. Appointments will be made on the basis of exceptional scholarly achievement and promise; the significance, originality, and feasibility of the proposed research; evidence of excellent research; intellectual range; and the potential to contribute to a demanding and collegial interdisciplinary community. This position is open until filled. Review of Applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. Questions may be directed to: Paul C.H. Lim Professor of Humanities Associate Director for Faculty Affairs Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education University of Florida paul.lim@ufl.edu Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539497/clinical-programs-coordinator-i-peds-id": {
      "title": "Clinical Programs Coordinator I- Peds ID",
      "description": "Clinical Programs Coordinator I- Peds ID Apply now Job no: 539497 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Allied Health, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Clinical Programs Coordinator I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Clinical Programs Coordinator I under the supervision of the Quality Officer will provide support to the UF CARES CQI Plan. This position will coordinate requirements and logistics for Patient Centered Model Home-PCMH annual recognition. Coordinate the patient services and administrative activities of clinical data and monitor ongoing clinical activities for NCQA regulations. Develops and coordinate patient services to review NCQA standards Assists and support the Quality Officer to review and monitor COI strategic plan Assists providers by monitoring patient care plan, and treatment problems Works collaboratively with clinical team members of the patient’s treatment team. Maintains all records and reports related to NCQA/PCMH. and keeps track of data in an Excel spreadsheet. Coordinates PCMH committee meetings, serves on health-related task forces and advisory committees, including the Clinical Effectiveness Group (CEG). Attending NCQA workshops and webinars 5% Provides administrative services for assistance with any additional needs. Provides health education to patients, staff, and practitioners within area of specialty as it relates to NCQA compliance Expected Salary: $43,888 to $55,000 annually depending on education and experience Required qualifications Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: 1-year experience with HIV population Adult/ Pediatric outpatient clinic, National Continuing Quality Assurance (NCQA) PCMH and Quality Improvement Projects Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Level II Background required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541305/director-tropical-conservation-and-development-program": {
      "title": "Director, Tropical Conservation and Development Program",
      "description": "Director, Tropical Conservation and Development Program Apply now Job no: 541305 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management Department: 57140100 - LT-CTR-LT AMER STUDIES-ADMIN Classification Title: PRG DIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field Job Description: The Center for Latin American Studies of the University of Florida (UF) welcomes applications for the position of Director for its Tropical Conservation and Development (TCD) Program . We seek a dynamic and innovative leader in the field of conservation and development to build on the strengths of this internationally recognized program of training, research, and practice. The mission of the TCD Program is to bridge theory and practice to advance biodiversity conservation, sustainable resource use, and human well-being. The program: offers a cross-campus graduate certificate which complements the knowledge and skills that students develop in their home units, adding significant value to their graduate education; promotes cross-national, interdisciplinary, integrative, and comparative problem-centered research at UF; and embraces collaborative learning and action with partners involved in day-to-day conservation and development realities around the world. The TCD Director will: lead and advocate for this cutting-edge, internationally recognized interdisciplinary research and training program with over 120 faculty affiliates and over 80 enrolled graduate students; direct budget allocations of investment returns on ~$6 million in endowments (most of which directly supports students); secure extramural funding to support research and training activities; oversee the innovative TCD graduate certificate program, maintaining academic rigor and relevance to conservation and development practice; support the vibrant TCD community of students, faculty, alumni, and partners through multiple and varied events; fortify existing and build new relationships with units and constituencies across the UF campus; strengthen and expand collaborations with organizations and partners across the globe, particularly with the more than 550 TCD alumni; spearhead shared program governance and a long-range TCD vision for the future; and commensurate with candidate background and interest, maintain a teaching/mentoring and/or research portfolio. The incoming TCD Director will have an appointment and be housed in the UF Center for Latin American Studies. We anticipate an appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. The Director may also be appointed jointly with another campus unit, depending on candidate profile, preferences, and university considerations. About the Center: The UF Center for Latin American Studies (LAS) is the oldest and one of the largest programs of research and education about the region in the United States, established as the Institute of Interamerican Affairs in the 1930s and renamed the Center for Latin American Studies in 1963. Today, the Center is one of the top-ranked centers in the world. In addition to the TCD certificate, the Center offers a Masters in Latin American Studies (MALAS) degree, an undergraduate minor, and graduate and undergraduate certificates. Jointly with the UF Center for African Studies, LAS offers the Master of Sustainable Development Practice (MDP) degree. The Center for African Studies and other campus units also collaborate in the TCD program. About the University: Founded in 1853, the University of Florida (UF) is the state’s flagship public research university and a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution. One of the nation’s most academically diverse universities, UF offers virtually every academic and professional discipline and is a member of the Association of American Universities. With more than 56,000 students across 16 colleges, a major academic health center, and championship-winning athletics, UF provides an exceptional environment for learning, discovery, and innovation. Consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities, UF was ranked the nation’s No. 1 public university by The Wall Street Journal in 2024 and among the top public universities by U.S. News & World Report , Forbes , and TIME . UF faculty conducted a record $1.33 billion in research in fiscal year 2025, including a record $817 million in federal research funding, underscoring the University’s growing national impact and commitment to advancing knowledge and addressing complex challenges. About the Area: As home to Florida's largest and oldest university, Gainesville is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville and the surrounding area are home to about 250,000 people, a robust arts community, several museums, and a world-class health care system anchored at UF. The area offers a low cost of living, strong public schools, and year-round outdoor recreation with easy access to Florida's coasts and major metropolitan areas, such as Jacksonville, Orlando, and Tampa, making it an attractive location for candidates and their families. https://www.visitgainesville.com/ Expected Salary: Commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. In addition to a competitive salary, the university offers a comprehensive benefits package. Explore more here https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/ . Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field; Demonstrated record of relevant research and professional experience in tropical conservation and development, including significant field experience in the Global South; Experience with program management, administration, and fundraising; Experience with leading facilitative decision-making processes and shared governance; and The ability to build collaborative relationships across disciplines and communicate effectively with diverse constituencies. Preferred: Fluency in Spanish and/or Portuguese or another language of the tropics. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at jobs.ufl.edu . For full consideration applicants must include: Complete curriculum vitae; A letter of application describing experience, qualifications, and vision related to this position; and The names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For inquiries concerning this position, contact search committee chair, Dr. Karen Kainer at kkainer@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The successful candidate will be required to provide official transcript(s) upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://naces.org/ . The University of Florida is an equal-opportunity institution. If an accommodation, due to disability, is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541306/student-assistant-innovation-academy-prototyping-studio-technical-assistant": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Innovation Academy Prototyping Studio Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Student Assistant - Innovation Academy Prototyping Studio Technical Assistant Apply now Job no: 541306 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 02070400 - PV-INNOVATION ACADEMY Classification Title: STU AST NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: • This position is open to students who are eligible to work on campus, be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS), and maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. • Registered students are employed on a part-time basis • Demonstrated experience with prototyping technologies. Job Description: Innovation Academy Technical Assistants support the day-to-day operation of the IA Prototyping Studio and help IA students safely and effectively use Studio equipment and prototyping technologies. Technical Assistants staff scheduled Studio hours, assist students with technical and fabrication questions, verify required training, oversee approved equipment workflows, maintain Studio records, and enforce Studio safety and access requirements. Current activities include 3D printing and related prototyping workflows, but equipment, software, and supported technologies may change as the Studio develops. Technical Assistants may also provide technical assistance for Innovation Academy courses, programs, workshops, and projects. This position does not include grading student coursework. Expected Salary: $16.00 per hour Required Qualifications: • This position is open to students who are eligible to work on campus, be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS), and maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. • Registered students are employed on a part-time basis • Demonstrated experience with prototyping technologies. Preferred: Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills and a willingness and ability to learn new technologies. Ability to follow and consistently enforce established safety procedures and Studio rules. Confidence assisting students in one-on-one and group settings. Excellent communication, customer service, and teamwork skills. Reliability, punctuality, and ability to commit to an established work schedule. Previous teaching, tutoring, TA, makerspace, or technical-support experience. Additional experience with 3D printing, CAD, electronics, programming, or fabrication. Successful completion of IDS 1940 Creativity and Design Thinking for Innovation. Enrollment in or completion of the Innovation Minor. Special Instructions to Applicants: Required Portfolio Submission Contents: Submit a small portfolio containing 2–3 examples of technical or prototyping work completed primarily or substantially by you. Examples may include: A 3D modeling/3D printing project, including an image and model file. An Arduino or electronics project, including an image or video and code. A laser-cutting or Adobe Illustrator project, including an image and original design file. Another project demonstrating relevant technical, fabrication, or problem-solving skills. Team projects are acceptable if your individual contribution is clearly identified. In order to considered, applicants must include a cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541310/ops-research-assistantscientist": {
      "title": "OPS Research Assistant/Scientist",
      "description": "OPS Research Assistant/Scientist Apply now Job no: 541310 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28100000 - VM-INFECT DISEASE & IMMUNOLOGY Classification Title: OPS Research Assistant/Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: MSc plus relevant research experience or PhD in immunology, reproductive biology, or a related life science field Flow cytometry experience Mouse handling experience Basic molecular technique experience Job Description: Design and execute experiments across mouse and human systems, including primary cell and organoid work Perform and support rodent surgeries, injections, and colony management Run and troubleshoot flow cytometry, PCR, Western blot, and electroporation protocols Perform animal work, in accordance with IACUC compliance Contribute to data analysis, and manuscript drafting Mentor junior lab members as needed Expected Salary: $25.00 - $30.00 / hourly Required Qualifications: ● MSc plus relevant research experience or PhD in immunology, reproductive biology, or a related life science field ● Flow cytometry experience ● Mouse handling experience ● Basic molecular technique experience Preferred: General reproductive biology experience CRISPR/Cas9 experience Electroporation experience Reproductive immunology background, particularly ovarian or uterine tissue Primary cell culture experience Histology experience Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541311/assistant-professor-in-ai-and-advertising": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising Apply now Job no: 541311 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 23020100 - CJC-ADVERSTISING-GENERAL Classification Title: AST Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment. Job Description: The Department of Advertising in the College of Journalism and Communications (CJC) at the University of Florida invites applications for a nine-month tenure-track Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising position to begin in Fall 2027. Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment. We seek a scholar whose research makes a strong contribution to the field of advertising, with a focus on artificial intelligence and its implications for the industry, professional practice, and pedagogy. Relevant areas for research specialization include but are not limited to AI-mediated advertising and persuasion, human-AI interaction, generative AI, algorithmic and automated communication, AI-driven social media and consumer insights, and the broader implications of emerging technologies for consumers, brands, media, and society. This faculty member will work in partnership with UF’s Consortium on Trust in Media and Technology, which is based at CJC. The Consortium connects scholars and thought leaders across disciplines to develop applications supporting media and technology that is credible and equitable. The Consortium’s work is part of UF’s commitment to artificial intelligence as the centerpiece of a major, long-term initiative combining world-class research infrastructure, cutting-edge research, and a transformational approach to curriculum. The successful candidate also will have opportunities to collaborate across the College and University on research related to artificial intelligence, emerging media technologies, consumer behavior, and strategic communication. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate courses in the advertising curriculum and established graduate courses approved by CJC’s Graduate Division. They may also be given the opportunity to teach specialized course offerings such as AI and Advertising and AI-Driven Social Media Insight, as well as develop new courses in their area of expertise. The successful candidate will also mentor doctoral and master’s students and participate in service to the department, college, university, and discipline. Candidates will be evaluated on the quality of their current research output and momentum, capacity for scholarly development and advancement, potential to cultivate a nationally and internationally acclaimed research agenda, and demonstrated contributions to teaching, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment; demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, original scholarly research relevant to advertising and strategic communication; demonstrate a research program that substantially engages artificial intelligence or related emerging technologies; demonstrate potential to secure external research funding; and demonstrate the ability to contribute effectively to undergraduate and/or graduate teaching. About UF’s College of Journalism and Communications The University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications, accredited by ACEJMC, is a nationally recognized leader in journalism and communications education and research. The College serves nearly 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students across four academic departments and is committed to attracting and retaining top talent through a culture that values diverse perspectives, skills, and experiences. Learn more at the College of Journalism and Communications and Department of Advertising. https://www.jou.ufl.edu ) and the Department of Advertising ( https://www.jou.ufl.edu/advertising/) for more information. About the University of Florida The University of Florida is a member of the Association of American Universities and a nationally recognized public research university. UF faculty conducted a record $1.33 billion in research in fiscal year 2025, including $817 million in federal research funding. UF offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. Learn more about UF Total Rewards . About Gainesville, Florida Gainesville is a vibrant college community of approximately 250,000 residents and home to the University of Florida. Known for its arts and culture, excellent schools, healthcare, restaurants, athletics, and abundant natural beauty, Gainesville offers an exceptional quality of life. The city is also within a two-hour drive of Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville, and St. Augustine. Learn more about Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment; ● Demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, original scholarly research relevant to advertising and strategic communication; ● Demonstrate a research program that substantially engages artificial intelligence or related emerging technologies; ● Demonstrate potential to secure external research funding; and ●Demonstrate the ability to contribute effectively to undergraduate and/or graduate teaching. Preferred: Preference will be given to candidates who provide evidence of: A strong trajectory of scholarly research Excellence or demonstrated potential in undergraduate and/or graduate teaching; effective mentorship of students Willingness to collaborate on research within and beyond the University of Florida Relevant professional experience in advertising, marketing, artificial intelligence, or related fields. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please submit your application online via Careers at UF at https://jobs.ufl.edu . Applications should include: A cover letter describing the candidate’s research program, including its connection to artificial intelligence and advertising or strategic communication, as well as teaching and mentoring experience and interests A complete curriculum vitae A teaching philosophy statement (evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course evaluations, is recommended where available) A research statement Names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Review of applications begins October 15, 2026, and continues until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Won-Ki Moon, at wonkimoon@ufl.edu . If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Degrees earned from educational institutions outside of the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). All candidates for employment are subject to pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. This position is open until filled. Review of application materials will begin immediately, and continue until a suitable pool of applicants has been established. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541319/research-assistant-scientist": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Research Assistant Scientist Apply now Job no: 541319 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19050000 - EG-ELECTRICAL - COMPUTER ENG Classification Title: Research Assistant Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field. Job Description: The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering (HWCOE) at the University of Florida is soliciting applications for a full-time, nine-month, time-limited, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Research Assistant Scientist. The Research Assistant Scientist position will provide strategic leadership in establishing and expanding a nationally recognized, externally funded research program at the intersection of artificial intelligence (AI) and microelectronics failure analysis (FA), inspection, metrology, reliability, and advanced manufacturing. A central expectation is to identify emerging research opportunities and lead the development and submission of competitive proposals to federal agencies, industry sponsors, consortia, and other funding organizations. The successful candidate will define new research thrusts and long-term technical roadmaps; build multidisciplinary proposal teams; and develop sustained collaborations with academic institutions, government agencies, national laboratories, semiconductor manufacturers, equipment vendors, and other industry partners. Research directions may include machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), large language models (LLMs), vision-language models (VLMs), large multimodal models (LMMs), agentic AI, federated learning (FL), physics-informed AI, and related emerging technologies applied to advanced semiconductor packaging, integrated circuits, printed circuit boards, and broader microelectronics manufacturing and reliability challenges. The position will also provide intellectual and technical leadership for interdisciplinary research programs in AI-enabled microelectronics inspection and failure analysis, with responsibility for guiding projects from concept development and experimental design through model development, validation, deployment, publication, and technology transition. Research may address multimodal defect detection and classification, computational imaging and reconstruction, anomaly detection, automated root-cause analysis, predictive reliability, multimodal data fusion, digital twins, trustworthy and explainable AI, autonomous and agentic inspection systems, and privacy-preserving collaborative learning. Relevant modalities may include X-ray imaging and computed tomography, scanning acoustic microscopy, optical microscopy, infrared and terahertz imaging, SEM/FIB, electrical measurements, and manufacturing or process data. Additional responsibilities include building and mentoring research teams; supervising graduate and undergraduate researchers and research staff; contributing to instruction, curriculum development, seminars, workshops, and workforce-training activities; disseminating research through high-impact publications and technical presentations; advancing intellectual property and technology transfer; and supporting the continued growth, visibility, and research infrastructure of the laboratory and associated academic–government–industry programs. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field. Preferred: Demonstrated expertise in AI/ML, including deep learning, computer vision, LLMs, VLMs, multimodal AI, agentic AI, physics-informed AI, or related emerging technologies. Research experience applying AI to microelectronics, semiconductor manufacturing, advanced packaging, failure analysis, inspection, metrology, reliability, or related engineering challenges. Experience with multimodal imaging or characterization techniques such as X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, optical microscopy, infrared/terahertz imaging, SEM/FIB, or electrical measurements. Strong record of peer-reviewed publications and demonstrated potential to develop an externally funded research program. Experience developing proposals and multidisciplinary collaborations with federal agencies, industry, national laboratories, or academic partners. Experience mentoring graduate and undergraduate researchers and working effectively in interdisciplinary teams. Familiarity with large-scale multimodal datasets, computational imaging, digital twins, autonomous inspection, or AI-enabled experimental systems is highly desirable. Demonstrated potential for technology translation, intellectual property development, industry engagement, and workforce training. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately, with the first full committee screening occurring on September 20th, and will continue to review applications until the position is filled. You must apply by submitting an application through UFCareers via https://jobs.ufl.edu/ . ( Job 541319 ) The application consists of attached PDF files of the following required documents: 1. Cover letter (summary, introduction related to hiring emphasis areas) 2. Curriculum vitae (including a 1-page CV highlights) 3. Names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of no less than three and up to five references. Additional documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. 4. A personal research program vision statement detailing short-and long-term goals. 5. A vision statement for area/unit detailing short-and long-term goals (identify any synergies with personal research program). 6. Encouraged, but not required , A personal teaching statement describing the applicant’s teaching experience and interests. The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2026. For further questions, please contact Navid Asadi nasadi@ece.ufl.edu. The University of Florida (UF) is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities. UF is home to HiPerGator, the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education, and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering is undergoing a major expansion in faculty, research, and facilities with a strong focus on artificial intelligence. In Fall 2023, UF opened Malachowsky Hall for Data Science & Information Technology, a 260,000-square-foot facility that serves as the hub for AI. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Additional Requirements The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. Transcripts marked “Issued to Student” will not be considered official. Degrees earned outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Additional Information The University and College count among its greatest strengths a community of faculty, students and staff who support a culture of respect and dignity where learning and research thrive. If you require a reasonable accommodation to apply, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) . Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. All searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541284/business-admin-specialist-iii-jupiter-fl": {
      "title": "Business Admin Specialist III - Jupiter, FL",
      "description": "Business Admin Specialist III - Jupiter, FL Apply now Job no: 541284 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 37010100 - SR-HA-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Business Admin Specialist III Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: This position works in The Wertheim UF Scripps Institute shared services center (SSC) to provide oversight and coordination of the administrative and fiscal operations by gathering critical information necessary to complete tasks in a variety of administrative areas, such as fiscal, human resources, commitment accounting, or grants processes. This includes providing guidance to end users and acting as a subject matter expert for the relevant system functionality. Performs administrative and clerical support for the acquisition of goods and services. This position also facilitates advancing service delivery and efficiency by reviewing current processes for efficiency and effectiveness and exploring innovative solutions. Responsible for building relationships with the units served, supervising the daily transactions of the SSC, and supporting a culture centered on delivering excellent customer service. Responsibilities and Duties: Fiscal Operations & Financial Support Analyzes financial operational problems and implements appropriate resolutions. Assists with monitoring budgetary performance of various operational and scientific laboratories, such as tracking/reconciling certain accounts and initiating follow-up discussions with specific functional areas regarding budget variances. Oversees and ensures the full procure-to-pay lifecycle is completed. Interprets, explains, and applies complex procure-to-pay policies and procedures while monitoring compliance with UF, State, and Federal regulations. Serves as a resource and effective liaison to internal and external constituents and customers. Maintains transactional databases and records. Finance Transaction Processing Responsible for analyzing, preparing, and entering both routine and complex transactional activities, including fiscal, human resources, commitment accounting, and grants processing. Examples include processing personnel reimbursements and labor account distribution work by completing PDRR forms. Reconciles and approves purchasing transactions. Review and research transactions to provide information as requested. Researches, analyzes, and resolves complex accounts transaction problems. Will work frequently with the accounting team, laboratory administrators, principal investigators, the Office of Sponsored Programs, and the Controller’s Office to provide administrative and clerical support for the successful acquisition of goods and services. Administrative Operations & Supplier Management Responsible for managing the purchasing of commodities by processing purchase orders and auditing invoices. Confers with labs and department personnel to determine their needs and budgetary requirements to purchase materials, supplies, and equipment, and makes recommendations in terms of quality, quantity, and type of product or order. Serves as a resource and customer liaison for supplier add requests and information gathering. Assists in the management of onsite contracted suppliers to ensure compliance and effectiveness, including meeting with key personnel on a regular basis. Responsible for managing the campus asset program, including disposals and transfers. Performs other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Analytical and problem-solving skills, including researching and analyzing complex data. Understand and practice excellent customer service. Possess a professional attitude which is conveyed in all interactions, communications and appearance. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with faculty, administrative staff, and other campus departments. Work well in a team environment. Ability to take ownership of the resolution of problems, be flexible and accountable, manage conflicting responsibilities, prioritize tasks, and consistently meet deadlines. Self-motivated and capable of working independently. Strong people and team management skills. Knowledge of and experience with university and state regulations regarding human resources, and fiscal and budget matters. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Please provide names and email addresses of three to five references as part of the application process. When ready, the hiring department will contact the listed references via email requesting their reference letters to be uploaded directly to the application website. This is a time-limited position. The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This position is open until filled. Application review will begin September 17, 2026. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541282/clinical-assistant-or-associate-professor-of-veterinary-clinical-pathology": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Clinical Pathology",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Clinical Pathology Apply now Job no: 541282 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28060000 - VM-COMP, DIAG & POP MEDICINE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Assistant Professor Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Associate Professor Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Job Description: The Bronson Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory (BADDL) and the Department of Comparative, Diagnostic, and Population Medicine (CDPM) at the University of Florida-College of Veterinary Medicine (UF-CVM) invite applications for a full time, Clinical Asst/Assoc/Professor of Clinical Pathology. This position is a multi-mission non-tenure track position in the CDPM that is promotable following UF-CVM guidelines. The position will be headquartered in Kissimmee, Florida and will perform clinical (80%) and extension (20%) duties at the BADDL. The candidate will function primarily as a clinical pathologist at the BADDL. The diagnostic caseload includes a variety of companion animals, livestock, avian and wildlife species. Interaction with veterinarians, owners, and livestock producers is an integral part of this position. Creative use of diagnostic case material including publication of results in appropriate journals is encouraged. The successful candidate will have daily interaction with the current pathologists and the resident at the BADDL. Additionally, the candidate may have limited opportunities to teach DVM and/or graduate students or engage in research at the UF-CVM depending on the candidate’s qualifications and interests. University and public service through departmental, college and university committee work, participation in professional organizations, continuing education and other appropriate activities is expected. The faculty hire must have experience in diagnostic cytopathology, hematology, clinical chemistry, and urinalysis, and have the ability to independently sign out cases, in support of the clinical pathology service. The BADDL clinical pathology service is supported by a high quality, high volume, and diverse caseload with submissions from outside practices. The clinical pathology laboratory is well-equipped and includes dedicated sections for cytopathology, hematology and clinical chemistry, endocrinology (Siemens Immulite® 2000xpi), urinalysis, and other specialty analyses. Current tests offerings are listed on the BADDL website. The incumbent will provide diagnostic and consultative services to veterinary practitioners, animal owners and producers, and regulatory officials. The incumbent is expected to develop a working knowledge of routine laboratory assays and obtain proficiency for testing in the assigned area, maintaining a working knowledge of the Laboratory Information Management System (LIMS) and utilizing this tool for case coordination and reporting. The incumbent is also expected to provide mentorship, coaching, training, and guidance to residents, and will train employees who are assigned to clinical pathology section, as needed. Consultation with in-house pathologists, professional laboratory staff, the National Veterinary Services Laboratory (NVSL), and university pathologists, is anticipated. Fully accredited by the American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians (AAVLD), the BADDL provides quality diagnostic services for all animal species, including necropsy, bacteriology, serology, histopathology, virology, parasitology, and molecular diagnostics. As a national leader and the premier animal disease diagnostic laboratory in the state of Florida, it serves veterinarians, animal owners, livestock and poultry industry, educators and researchers to protect animal and human health. BADDL processes about 34,000 cases each year and conducts more than 80,000 tests annually. The incumbent will be responsible and accountable for managing budget/dollars/resources (staff, vehicles, computers, cell phones, etc.) of any assigned programs in an efficient and accountable manner. They will perform other duties as assigned. The UF-CVM is the sixth-ranked College of Veterinary Medicine in the US and is the only one that is part of a top 10 public research university The CVM is located on campus in close proximity to the College of Medicine and numerous other Health Sciences units that provide ample opportunities for translational research and collaboration. Expected Salary: Rank and salary will be based upon the successful candidate’s professional qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: Required qualifications for this position are a DVM or equivalent degree from an approved institution and completion of a veterinary clinical pathology residency training program or equivalent training. ACVP or ECVP board eligibility or certification is required. Experience in clinical pathology cases, and the ability to independently sign out cases, is required. Preferred: None Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, all applicants must apply online through Careers at UF. Applicants must submit the following items as a PDF file for full consideration: (1) a letter outlining clinical/diagnostic service, teaching and research experiences as well as professional goals, (2) a current curriculum vitae (CV), and (3) names and contact information for three professional references able to speak to all aspects of the candidate’s professional and personal abilities as they relate to this position’s responsibilities. E-mail inquiries to Dr. Justin Stilwell ( stilwellj@ufl.edu ), Search Committee Chair, are welcome. Review of applications for job number 541282 will begin immediately and will continue until a candidate is recommended for appointment. Candidates may be invited to interview prior to the application review date, though a hiring decision will not be made until after that date. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is An Equal Employment Opportunity Institution. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541290/postdoctoral-research-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate Apply now Job no: 541290 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29020100 - MD-NEUROSCIENCE-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Research Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Neurobiology, Stem Cell Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or a closely related ﬁeld. Job Description: The Guo Laboratory at the University of Florida is seeking a highly motivated, talented, and creative Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our dynamic multi-disciplinary team. Our research sits at the cutting edge of regenerative medicine and neurobiology, utilizing state-of-the-art human induced pluripotent stem cell (hiPSC) models, 3D tissue engineering, and advanced multi-omic / bioinformatics integrations to model and dissect human diseases. Our lab focuses heavily on inventing and reﬁning Next Approach Methodologies (NAMs)—speciﬁcally, brain organoids, Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB) / Blood-Spinal Cord Barrier (BSCB) assembloids, and spinal cord/motor neuron models—to investigate the molecular and epitranscriptomic mechanisms driving neurodegenerative conditions like Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) and Alzheimer’s Disease (AD), as well as neurodevelopmental conditions including autism and Fragile X Syndrome (FXS). The Guo Lab is exceptionally well-funded through major federal agencies and foundation awards—including NINDS, NIMH, NIDA, NIA, and the NSF—and regularly publishes in high-impact journals such as Nature, Cell Stem Cell, Nature Neuroscience, Nature Communications, Science Advances, Molecular Psychiatry, Advanced Science, The Journal of Clinical Investigation, Cell Discovery, and Protein & Cell. This position oﬀers an unparalleled opportunity to spearhead high-proﬁle projects, leverage robust resources, and work within the deeply collaborative, state-of-the-art academic ecosystem at UF Health and the McKnight Brain Institute. Key Responsibilities: • Advanced Cellular Engineering: Lead the directed diﬀerentiation of hiPSCs into highly enriched neural and vascular lineages (e.g., cortical neurons, motor neurons, astrocytes, microglia, brain endothelial cells, pericytes) to construct complex 3D assembloids and organ-on-a-chip platforms. • Mechanistic Dissection: Deploy CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing, shRNA/ASO tools, and pharmacological screens to interrogate pathological cellular axes. • Multimodal Analytics: Perform and integrate high-resolution functional readouts, including whole-cell patch-clamp electrophysiology, quantitative confocal/super-resolution microscopy, single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), and spatial transcriptomics. • Scientiﬁc Leadership: Independently manage project milestones, draft high-impact manuscripts, present ﬁndings at premier international conferences, and mentor graduate and undergraduate students within a supportive lab ecosystem. Expected Salary: $63,480-$65,000 commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Neurobiology, Stem Cell Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or a closely related ﬁeld. Preferred: • Strong technical background in mammalian cell culture (experience with hiPSC/hESC maintenance and directed diﬀerentiation is highly preferred, but not required for candidates with exceptional molecular biology or electrophysiology expertise). • Hands-on experience with 3D organoid, assembloid, or microﬂuidic/organ-on-a-chip modeling. • Experience with whole-cell patch-clamp electrophysiology or multi-electrode arrays. • Familiarity with computational biology pipelines (e.g., handling transcriptomic or scRNA-seq datasets using R or Python). Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. What We Oﬀer: • Comprehensive University of Florida beneﬁts package. • State-of-the-Art Facilities: Access to a brand-new, modern laboratory space within the Department of Neuroscience and McKnight Brain Institute, equipped with dedicated stem cell suites, high-resolution imaging cores, and advanced electrophysiology rigs. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/523259/federal-work-study-landscaper-kanapaha-botanical-gardens": {
      "title": "Federal Work Study - Landscaper (Kanapaha Botanical Gardens)",
      "description": "Federal Work Study - Landscaper (Kanapaha Botanical Gardens) Apply now Job no: 523259 Work type: Student Ast Location: Alachua Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Agriculture Operations Department: 06030000 - EM-FWS-COMMUNITY SERVICE Classification Title: STU AST - FEDERAL COMMUNITY SERVICE Job Description: Federal Work Study Position is with the Kanapaha Botanical Gardens. Looking for 3-9hrs per week. Landscaping including weeding, mulching, planting, trimming, blowing sidewalks and rental set up. Any day Monday, Tuesday and Friday mornings, with a 3-4 hour shift on days coming in (8:30am or 9am-12 or 12:30pm). Must be able to work on Saturday October 17th to help with the Fall Plant Sale. Please include your current available days and times you are available to work. Expected Salary: $15/hour Minimum Requirements: Federal Work Study required, no exceptions. Must have a good work ethic. Preferred Qualifications: Must have a good work ethic. Must have Federal Work Study award. * Students seeking FWS jobs should print/screenshot a copy of their Financial Aid Award Page, which contains the Federal Work Study award as proof of FWS eligibility. To locate your Financial Aid Award Page, go to https://one.uf.edu/financialaid/aidyear Special Instructions to Applicants: DO NOT APPLY TO THIS POSTING, APPLY DIRECTLY TO EMAIL. Applicants should send resume, cover letter, and references to Alexis at kbotanical@gmail.com to apply. Applicants should list their available schedule to work. PLEASE INCLUDE UFID IN APPLICATION Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541274/assistant-curator": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT CURATOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT CURATOR Apply now Job no: 541274 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Humanities Department: 56020500 - NH-ENVIROMENTAL ARCHAEOLOGY Classification Title: Assistant Curator Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Anthropology or a related field by the date of appointment; evidence of collections-based research using archaeological collections; and evidence of excellence or strong potential for excellence in research through publication record, grant record, or potential to conduct grant-funded research. Job Description: Assistant Curator of Environmental Archaeology The Florida Museum of Natural History at the University of Florida (UF) seeks qualified applicants for a full-time,12-month, tenure-accruing, Assistant Curator of Environmental Archaeology. This position will be filled at the Assistant faculty level in the Florida Museum’s Department of Natural History. For over 40 years, the faculty and staff of the Environmental Archaeology Program (FM-EAP) at the Florida Museum of Natural History have played a foundational role in the development of zooarchaeology, with secondary emphases in archaeobotany and archaeopedology. We curate archaeological animal, plant, and soil assemblages from across the Americas, including the southeastern United States, insular Caribbean, Mesoamerica, and South America. Several of the collections from sites are co-curated and managed across other archaeological museum collections (e.g., Florida Archaeology, Historical Archaeology, Caribbean Archaeology). The collections represent more than 1.7 million specimens from over 800 sites, including the largest zooarchaeological comparative collection in the southeastern USA. Through museum research, student mentorship, and collaboration, the FM-EAP is an international leader in zooarchaeological methods of identification and analysis. The zooarchaeological collections are complemented by macrobotanical resources, including comparative wood and seed collections from the southeastern United States. The FM-EAP faculty, staff, and collections support research on a wide range of questions related to human-environment interactions, including subsistence, domestication, coastal adaptations, migration, trade and exchange, resource management, landscape use, and cultural and environmental change. The program also supports interdisciplinary research and training that incorporates geochemical and biochemical analyses as complementary approaches to these questions. The FM-EAP provides an exceptional opportunity for a curator to build on a strong tradition of collections-based research, especially in the circum-Caribbean region, while developing innovative approaches to environmental archaeology that will continue to advance our anthropological understanding of human-environment relationships through time and across the globe. About the Florida Museum The Florida Museum of Natural History is the official state museum of Florida. It is located on the campus of the University of Florida, which is ranked no. 7 among the nation’s public research universities by U.S. News and World Report, and is the flagship of the state university system. The Florida Museum’s mission includes stewardship of 40 million specimens and artifacts, award-winning exhibitions, public programs, and emerging virtual and digital engagement. The research and collections programs of the Department of Natural History are world class and attract about $10 million annually in government and philanthropic support. The Florida Museum is a national and international leader in AI and biodiversity informatics, enhanced by its formative role in iDigBio, the national hub for digitization of natural history specimens. The Department of Natural History, a college-level unit within UF, is a vibrant community of about 300 employees, including 30 full-time faculty, ~80–100 graduate students, ~20 postdoctoral research associates, hundreds of undergraduates engaged in research, curation, and education, as well as administrative, collections and research support staff. The department has dedicated labs for digital imaging, molecular genetics and genomics, and preparing skeletal specimens. Natural History enjoys cross-campus collaborations with many of the 16 UF colleges, including those of potential relevance to this new faculty position: Liberal Arts and Sciences, Agricultural and Life Sciences (Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences), Education, Engineering, Libraries, and Veterinary Medicine. Other campus resources relevant to this position include the UF Museum Studies Program, FM Randell Research Center, UF Genetics Institute, UF Biodiversity Institute. Expected Salary: The starting salary range for this position is $90,000 to $105,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Florida (UF) offers an exceptional benefits package, including health, dental and vision insurance; state retirement plans; a generous leave program; and tuition assistance. UF is a Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) eligible employer. For more information on benefits, please visit UF HR Benefits and Rewards at https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/. Required Qualifications: We seek to hire an innovative environmental archaeologist whose research and teaching will foster an interdisciplinary approach to natural history scholarship, including collections-based research design and student mentorship. We invite candidates who address questions in the broad field of environmental archaeology and who will contribute to, and make use of, the Florida Museum’s extensive and world-renowned environmental archaeology collection. We anticipate that the successful candidate will have the opportunity to both bolster and expand the FM-EAP’s collections through sustained research, formal academic advising, teaching and service. An ideal candidate will contribute to the museum’s goals of understanding, preserving, and interpreting biological and cultural diversity, and conduct research with opportunities for integrative collaborations with other faculty and divisions within the Florida Museum and across the 16 colleges of the University of Florida. Candidates using multidisciplinary approaches are highly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must have a Ph.D. in Anthropology or a related field, conduct collections-based research on archaeological collections, and demonstrate excellence or strong potential for excellence in research through publication record, grant record, or potential to conduct grant-funded research. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at: careers.ufl.edu. For full consideration, applications must include: • Cover letter • Full curriculum vitae, including lists of peer-reviewed publications and grants received • Research statement describing the applicant’s current and planned research • Statement describing the applicant’s experience with museum/research collections and vision for curating, managing, and using FM-EAP collections • Statement describing the applicant’s experience or potential in teaching, mentorship, and outreach • List of the names and email addresses of three professional references. Letters of recommendation are not required at this stage. Review of applications will begin on October 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered official if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). For inquiries concerning this position, contact Dr. Michal Kowalewski, Search Chair, at kowalewski@ufl.edu. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541275/emergency-medicinecommunity-faculty-physician-clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Emergency Medicine/Community Faculty Physician - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Emergency Medicine/Community Faculty Physician - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541275 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Duval Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30290000 - JX-EMERGENCY MEDICINE-JAX Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD/DO - be able to obtain a Florida State Medical License Job Description: The University of Florida Department of Emergency Medicine - Jacksonville is recruiting a motivated and energetic board certified or board-eligible emergency medicine physician to join our Faculty in a Community Faculty position. This will be a non-tenured position up to a 1.00 FTE. Clinical activity occurs at a variety of sites and works alongside colleagues in other specialty services. Successful candidates will receive an appointment as either a Clinical Assistant, Clinical Associate or Clinical Professor. Qualified candidates must be ABEM/ABOEM board eligible or certified. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD/DO - be able to obtain a Florida State Medical License Preferred: ABEM/ABOEM board eligible or certified. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on September 14, 2026 . Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/537337/assistant-associate-full-professor-sleep-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant/ Associate/ Full Professor - Sleep Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant/ Associate/ Full Professor - Sleep Medicine Apply now Job no: 537337 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050900 - MD-PULMONARY MEDICINE Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Job Description: The University of Florida Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care & Sleep Medicine is seeking a board-eligible or board certified Sleep Medicine physician to become an active and integral member of the team. This is a full-time appointment in the Department of Medicine at the rank of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor level for a tenure/non-tenure track position. Rank and tenure status will be determined based on education and experience. The successful candidate must have a strong interest in developing an academic career. General Duties and Responsibilities: The primary duties will include seeing sleep medicine clinic patients and interpreting sleep studies, with opportunities for clinical research and teaching. Requisite attributes include a commitment to service, a strong sense of teamwork, and a desire to train tomorrow's doctors. About us: UF Health is one of the Southeast’s most comprehensive academic health centers and includes UF Health Shands, an 1,100-bed quaternary referral hospital, which consistently ranks among the top 50-hospitals by U.S. News & World Report in multiple specialties. In addition, the University of Florida is a top 5 public research university and includes substantial resources to support teaching, research, and collaboration, including one of only two Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI) hubs in the state of Florida. The CTSI features a Training and Research Academy for Clinical and Translational Science for early career faculty seeking to develop research skills and pursue careers as clinical investigators. Benefit highlights - Faculty compensation plan that includes quarterly incentives and annual salary increase opportunities University employer-paid benefits include individual and family health insurance, life insurance and long-term disability insurance Retirement benefits include participation in 403(b) and 457 plans as well as an employer match Professional liability protection and sovereign immunity administered by the Florida Board of Governors self-insurance program Relocation costs assistance About the area: The Gainesville, Florida region has an inexpensive cost of living, nationally ranked public middle and high schools, and superb weather with multiple year-round recreational opportunities including biking, hiking, kayaking, snorkeling, and cave diving. Pristine wilderness areas, coastal communities, famous theme parks, and other attractions are within a short driving distance. MSA Population: 330,000 Average High Temp in January: 66 F No State Income Tax Strong public and private schools 1 hour to Gulf of Mexico and Atlantic Ocean 2 hours or less to Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa Expected Salary: Salary and academic appointment are commensurate with training and level of experience. Minimum Requirements: MD or DO with Board Certification/Board Eligibility in Sleep Medicine. Candidates must be eligible for licensure in the State of Florida. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants remain under consideration and do not need to reapply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540775/assistant-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor",
      "description": "Assistant / Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 540775 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29030000 - MD-BIOCHEM - MOLECULAR BIOL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology in the University of Florida (UF) College of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track appointment at the Assistant Professor level. The successful candidate must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Biochemistry, Molecular Biology, or a broadly related field. The successful candidate will conduct cutting-edge, exceptional research within the broadly constituted disciplines of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology that would synergize with current UF research strengths. Demonstrated ability to obtain external funding will enhance the application. Exceptional candidates at the Associate Professor level will be considered. Biochemistry and Molecular Biology offers a growing and vibrant research atmosphere with a newly appointed chairperson and 15 research-intense tenure-track faculty members, including 4 recent hires. Current strengths of the department that we seek to complement or expand include: metabolism (spatial-biology, metabolomics, metabolic flux analysis, and artificial intelligence), structural biochemistry (macromolecular structure using cryo-EM, X-ray crystallography, and NMR), and molecular biology (RNA structure/function, epigenetics, gene regulation, DNA repair, and replication). UF offers world-class facilities for NMR/MRI (AMRIS center), structural biology (CSB), mass spectrometry (CASBR), epigenetics (CEG), cancer and neuroscience (McKnight Brain Institute), and computing that are readily accessible to researchers. In partnership with Nvidia, UF built HiPerGator, one of the largest supercomputing facilities sited at a university in the world with a particular emphasis on the use of AI in biomedical research. Notable achievements include the National Cancer Institute designation for the UF Health Cancer Institute, UF surpassing $1.33 billion in research spending, and UF ranks number 4 among public universities in TIME magazine’s America’s Best Colleges 2027. Biochemistry and Molecular Biology is part of a larger Biomedical Sciences program within the College of Medicine that includes eight fundamental science departments. Educational activities of research faculty include mentoring graduate students and research fellows and team-teaching at graduate/professional levels. Our Commitment Being part of the UF College of Medicine means joining a community that fosters the personal and professional goals and achievements of its employees. UF and COM offer multiple tools, resources, and training to help you learn and grow. When you rise, we do too – and we welcome applications from individuals who can contribute to the university’s vision and mission, including the strategic plan of the College of Medicine. To learn more about the College of Medicine’s strategic plan “Engage, Innovate, Excel 2026”, please visit: https://med.ufl.edu/about/strategic-plan/. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Biochemistry or Molecular Biology, or a related field. Preferred: Demonstrated proficiency in obtaining grant funding from the NIH, NSF, or DOD. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed beginning on October 14, 2026, and the search will continue until the position is filled. Applications and nominations will be kept confidential. Inquiries and nominations can be sent to Professor Linda Bloom, lbloom@ufl.edu . Please plan to submit the following in PDF format to the sections of the online application system listed below: (The job portal does not offer the flexibility to alter the fields for the application components. Therefore, please upload the components below in the sections that are indicated.) A curriculum vitae, including contact information for three references. References will only be contacted for those in final list of top candidates. (Upload your CV to the “Curriculum Vitae or Resume” Section) Statement of research plans (2-3 pages) (Make a combined document that contains your research plans and your teaching philosophy. Upload this combined PDF document to the “Other Document” section.) Citations and description of significance for up to three publications (total of 200 words or less each) (Upload this PDF document to the “Work Sample” section.) Include PDF copies of the above-mentioned three publications. (Upload these PDFs into the “Letter of Recommendation” section. Add one publication to each Letter of Recommendation section.*) Statement of teaching/mentoring philosophy (1 page) (Make a combined document that contains your research plans and your teaching philosophy. Upload this combined PDF document to the “Other Document” section.) *Note: Our application system has a maximum attachment size limit of 5mb. If any of your application materials exceed this limit, please email your materials to our HR Departmental Associate, Alex Franklin, at alexander.franklin@medicine.ufl.edu . Final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an institution outside the U.S. must be evaluated by a credentialing service approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES): http://naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541189/cardiothoracic-radiologist-clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Cardiothoracic Radiologist - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Cardiothoracic Radiologist - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541189 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30130000 - JX-RADIOLOGY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO /FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidate must have a MD/DO in Diagnostic Radiology Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville (UF COM-J) Department of Radiology is seeking a fulltime Cardiothoracic Radiologist at the non-tenure accruing level of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Job responsibilities include: Cardiothoracic Imaging Radiologist with academic focus at UF Jacksonville Health System at the Assistant, Associate Professor/Full Professor level with 100% specialization. Full-time academic position with UF Radiology and Imaging, UFJPI, Non-Tenure Accruing. Provide high quality service in a friendly academic setting. Serve as a mentor to train a great group of Residents & Fellows. Work with colleagues in surgery, oncology, radiation oncology & pathology, via daily interactions/multidisciplinary meetings. Have the drive and leadership skills necessary to be an integral part of the Cardiovascular & Thoracic Imaging Division. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Candidate must have a MD/DO Preferred: Board Certified / Board Eligible in Diagnostic Radiology. The successful candidate will be fellowship trained in Cardiothoracic Imaging and experienced in all aspects of Cardiothoracic. The candidate should be a team player and expect to join a pleasant group of colleagues Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is open until filled. The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541247/chair-associateprofessor-obstetrics-and-gynecology": {
      "title": "Chair & Associate/Professor - Obstetrics and Gynecology",
      "description": "Chair & Associate/Professor - Obstetrics and Gynecology Apply now Job no: 541247 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30070000 - JX-OB-GYN-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Obstetrics - Gynecology - CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in Obstetrics and Gynecology. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville (UF COM-J) invites applications and nominations for the position of Chair, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. The Department The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is a dynamic and growing academic department committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and community engagement. The department is staffed by University of Florida physicians and encompasses specialty divisions in Maternal-Fetal Medicine, Female Pelvic Medicine and Reconstructive Surgery, Gynecologic Oncology, and General Obstetrics and Gynecology. The department provides comprehensive women’s health services across Northeast Florida through a growing network of clinical locations, including UF Health Jacksonville, the region’s premier academic health center and tertiary referral hospital; UF Health North, a full-service medical campus in North Jacksonville offering inpatient and outpatient women’s health services; and multiple ambulatory practices throughout the region. These include the Emerson Women’s Health Center, the Elizabeth G. Means Center, Generation-to-Generation Women’s Care in Orange Park, and the expanding UF Health St. Johns (Durbin Park) campus, serving one of the fastest-growing communities in Northeast Florida. The department has a robust research enterprise supporting faculty scholarship, clinical trials, and translational research. It also sponsors an Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)-accredited Obstetrics and Gynecology Residency Program and is committed to preparing the next generation of physicians, educators, and physician leaders. With expertise spanning the full spectrum of women’s health, advanced clinical capabilities, and a highly accomplished faculty, the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, and research missions. The Opportunity The Chair will join a strong and collaborative leadership team committed to advancing the academic, clinical, and research missions of UF Health Jacksonville and the University of Florida. The continued growth of Northeast Florida, combined with the statewide reputation, resources, and academic strength of UF Health, creates significant opportunities for the Chair to expand clinical programs, develop innovative models of care, strengthen research and scholarship, enhance graduate medical education, and advance the department’s regional and national reputation. The Chair will provide strategic and operational leadership for the department and will be responsible for advancing its clinical, academic, research, and educational missions. The Chair will work closely with faculty, department leadership, hospital and health system leaders, and University partners to identify opportunities for growth, strengthen collaboration, and ensure alignment with the broader priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. A particular opportunity for the next Chair will be to lead the continued development and strategic expansion of women’s health services throughout Northeast Florida, including growth within the rapidly expanding UF Health St. Johns market. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three University of Florida health colleges located on the approximately 110-acre UF Health Jacksonville campus. The College’s 16 clinical science departments include more than 500 faculty members and 450 residents and fellows. The College sponsors 39 accredited graduate medical education programs and serves as a major regional campus for the University of Florida College of Medicine, providing third- and fourth-year medical student clerkships and electives. The College is an integral part of UF Health Jacksonville, an academic health center providing tertiary and quaternary care to patients throughout Northeast Florida and the surrounding region. The College’s faculty are engaged across the full continuum of clinical care, medical education, research, and community service. Responsibilities Reporting directly to the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, the Chair will have overall responsibility for the leadership, strategic direction, administration, and performance of the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Key responsibilities include: Developing and advancing a compelling strategic vision for the department that aligns with the priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. Leading the continued growth and integration of clinical services across Northeast Florida. Advancing the department’s academic and research missions, including scholarly activity, clinical research, and translational research. Fostering excellence in undergraduate and graduate medical education and strengthening the department’s educational programs. Recruiting, retaining, mentoring, and developing talented faculty. Promoting a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, and continuous improvement. Strengthening partnerships across departments, specialties, the College, UF Health, and the broader Jacksonville medical community. Providing effective fiscal and administrative oversight of the department and ensuring responsible stewardship of resources. Supporting quality, safety, patient experience, and operational excellence across the department’s clinical enterprise. Representing the department effectively within the University, health system, professional organizations, and broader community. Identifying opportunities to enhance the department’s regional, national, and academic reputation. Expected Salary: Negotiable Required Qualifications: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in Obstetrics and Gynecology. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Preferred: The ideal candidate will be an accomplished physician leader with a clear vision for the future of academic obstetrics and gynecology and a demonstrated ability to translate that vision into meaningful clinical, academic, and organizational results. Demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in patient care, medical education, research, and faculty development. Have a track record of recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining high-performing faculty. Bring experience leading growth and transformation within a complex academic or integrated health system environment. Demonstrate strong financial, operational, and organizational leadership skills. Foster a culture of collaboration, transparency, accountability, and innovation. Champion interdisciplinary and cross-organizational partnerships. Demonstrate a commitment to quality improvement, patient safety, and health equity. Have experience advancing graduate medical education and developing future physician leaders. Be well positioned to expand the department’s clinical, academic, and research footprint throughout Northeast Florida. Possess national recognition and active engagement in professional societies and academic organizations, which are highly desirable. The next Chair will have an exceptional opportunity to build upon the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology’s strong foundation while shaping its future growth and impact across Northeast Florida and beyond. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee is assisted by the executive search firm Spencer Stuart. Applications, nominations and comments may be sent to SBryant@SpencerStuart.com . The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541257/chair-associatefull-professor-department-of-surgery": {
      "title": "Chair & Associate/Full Professor - Department of Surgery",
      "description": "Chair & Associate/Full Professor - Department of Surgery Apply now Job no: 541257 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30140000 - JX-SURGERY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must possess: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in a surgical specialty appropriate to the department. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville (UF COM-J) invites applications and nominations for the position of Chair, Department of Surgery. The Department The Department of Surgery is a dynamic and nationally recognized academic department committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service to the community. The department provides a broad range of surgical services and serves as a major referral resource for patients throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. The department encompasses seven major divisions: Acute Care Surgery, Cardiothoracic Surgery, General and Gastrointestinal Surgery, Surgical Oncology, Otorhinolaryngology, Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery, and Vascular Surgery. With expertise spanning general and specialty surgery, trauma, surgical oncology, and complex surgical care, the department is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, and research missions. The department provides inpatient and outpatient surgical services at multiple locations and is closely integrated with UF Health Jacksonville, the region’s academic health center and tertiary referral hospital. A defining strength of the department is its leadership of UF Health TraumaOne, which is the only adult and pediatric Level I trauma program in Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia and provides comprehensive care for patients with complex and life-threatening injuries. The Department of Surgery has a robust educational mission. It sponsors a five-year, ACGME-accredited General Surgery Residency Program that provides broad operative and clinical experiences, including trauma, emergency general surgery, surgical critical care, vascular surgery, surgical oncology, colorectal surgery, breast surgery, minimally invasive surgery, pediatric surgery, and plastic surgery. The department also offers fellowship training in complex gastrointestinal surgery, surgical critical care, advanced rhinology and endoscopic sinus and skull base surgery, laryngology, and medical otorhinolaryngology. The department is also committed to advancing surgical knowledge through clinical research, faculty scholarship, translational research, and collaboration across the University of Florida. Its research enterprise includes clinical research and opportunities for faculty and trainees to engage in innovative investigations that improve surgical care and patient outcomes. With a broad clinical footprint, nationally significant trauma and surgical programs, a strong graduate medical education enterprise, and a highly accomplished faculty, the Department of Surgery is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, educational, and research missions. The Opportunity The Chair will join a strong and collaborative leadership team committed to advancing the academic, clinical, educational, and research missions of UF Health Jacksonville and the University of Florida. The continued growth of Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia, combined with the statewide reputation, resources, and academic strength of UF Health, creates significant opportunities for the Chair to expand surgical services, strengthen centers of excellence, develop innovative models of care, advance surgical research, enhance graduate medical education, and elevate the department’s regional and national reputation. The Chair will provide strategic and operational leadership for the department and will be responsible for advancing its clinical, academic, research, and educational missions. The Chair will work closely with faculty, division chiefs, department leadership, hospital and health system leaders, and University partners to identify opportunities for growth, strengthen collaboration, and ensure alignment with the broader priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. A particular opportunity for the next Chair will be to lead the continued development and strategic growth of the department’s surgical enterprise throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. This includes strengthening existing surgical programs, expanding access to advanced and specialized surgical care, optimizing the integration of inpatient and outpatient services, and identifying opportunities for growth across the department’s diverse clinical divisions. The next Chair will also have the opportunity to build upon the department’s significant trauma and acute care surgery infrastructure, including UF Health TraumaOne, while strengthening multidisciplinary partnerships and developing innovative approaches to complex surgical care. The Chair will be expected to foster an environment that supports clinical excellence while advancing the department’s academic and research profile, strengthening surgical education, and recruiting and developing the next generation of surgical leaders. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three University of Florida health colleges located on the approximately 110-acre UF Health Jacksonville campus. The College’s 16 clinical science departments include more than 500 faculty members and 450 residents and fellows. The College sponsors 39 accredited graduate medical education programs and serves as a major regional campus for the University of Florida College of Medicine, providing third- and fourth-year medical student clerkships and electives. The College is an integral part of UF Health Jacksonville, an academic health center providing tertiary and quaternary care to patients throughout Northeast Florida and the surrounding region. The College’s faculty are engaged across the full continuum of clinical care, medical education, research, and community service. Responsibilities Reporting directly to the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, the Chair will have overall responsibility for the leadership, strategic direction, administration, and performance of the Department of Surgery. Key responsibilities include: Developing and advancing a compelling strategic vision for the department that aligns with the priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. Leading the continued growth, integration, and strategic development of surgical services across Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. Strengthening the department’s clinical programs and centers of excellence while identifying opportunities for new programs and services. Advancing the department’s academic and research missions, including clinical research, translational research, scholarly activity, and external funding. Supporting the continued development and excellence of UF Health TraumaOne and the department’s acute care, trauma, and surgical critical care programs. Fostering excellence in graduate medical education and strengthening the department’s residency and fellowship programs. Recruiting, retaining, mentoring, and developing talented faculty and future surgical leaders. Promoting a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, inclusion, and continuous improvement. Strengthening partnerships across surgical divisions, departments, specialties, the College, UF Health, and the broader Jacksonville medical community. Providing effective fiscal and administrative oversight of the department and ensuring responsible stewardship of resources. Supporting quality, safety, patient experience, access, and operational excellence across the department’s clinical enterprise. Developing strategies to enhance faculty productivity, engagement, professional development, and academic advancement. Representing the department effectively within the University, health system, professional organizations, and broader community. Identifying opportunities to enhance the department’s regional, national, and academic reputation. The next Chair will have an exceptional opportunity to build upon the Department of Surgery’s strong clinical, academic, and educational foundation while shaping its future growth and impact across Northeast Florida, Southeast Georgia, and beyond. The Chair will lead a highly specialized surgical enterprise with the opportunity to further strengthen its reputation for clinical excellence, surgical education, research, innovation, and service to the region. Expected Salary: Salary commensurate with experience and training. Required Qualifications: Candidates must possess: An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. Board certification in a surgical specialty appropriate to the department. Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Preferred: Desired Attributes The ideal candidate will be an accomplished physician leader with a clear vision for the future of academic surgery and a demonstrated ability to translate that vision into meaningful clinical, academic, and organizational results. The successful candidate will: Demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in patient care, medical education, research, and faculty development. Have a track record of recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining high-performing faculty. Bring experience leading growth and transformation within a complex academic or integrated health system environment. Demonstrate strong financial, operational, and organizational leadership skills. Foster a culture of collaboration, transparency, accountability, and innovation. Champion interdisciplinary and cross-organizational partnerships to improve patient care and advance the academic mission. Demonstrate a commitment to quality improvement, patient safety, and health equity. Have experience advancing graduate medical education and developing future physician and surgical leaders. Demonstrate an understanding of the unique clinical, educational, and operational requirements of a comprehensive academic surgical department. Have experience developing or expanding clinical programs and centers of excellence within a competitive healthcare environment. Be well positioned to expand the department’s clinical, academic, research, and educational footprint throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. Possess national recognition and active engagement in professional societies and academic organizations, which are highly desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: The executive search firm Spencer Stuart has been retained to assist in this recruitment. Application, nominations and inquiries may be sent to TSorota@SpencerStuart.com . Review of application will begin immediately and will continue until position is filled. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541261/extension-agent-iiiii-environmental-horticulture": {
      "title": "Extension Agent II-III – Environmental Horticulture",
      "description": "Extension Agent II-III – Environmental Horticulture Apply now Job no: 541261 Work type: Permanent Status Faculty Location: Broward Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60355006 - AG-BROWARD Classification Title: EXT AGENT II-III Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall hold a master’s degree or 50% of hours completed towards a master’s degree along with qualifying experience or accomplishments. Job Description: You Belong at a University that’s Leading the Way The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (UF/IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Opportunity This 12-month, permanent-status accruing Extension Agent II-III position provides leadership for Environmental Horticulture programming in Broward County and reports directly to the County Extension Director. The successful candidate will develop, implement, deliver, and evaluate a UF/IFAS Extension Pathway Initiative 4 aligned environmental education program to serve landscape professionals, arborists, nursery operators, municipal employees, property managers, pest management professionals, youth audiences (5% programmatic appointment), and other green industry stakeholders. The agent will identify community and industry needs then deliver impactful educational programs and services that address emerging issues affecting Broward County's green industry sector while promoting environmental stewardship, urban forest sustainability, and responsible resource management. Environmental Horticulture Program Development and Delivery Develop and implement a comprehensive environmental horticulture Extension program based on stakeholder needs, county priorities, and statewide Extension Pathway statewide action plans. Deliver educational workshops, demonstrations, field days, webinars, certification training, and technical assistance programs. Prepare fact sheets, newsletters, digital resources, social media content, and other educational materials. Evaluate program effectiveness and document outcomes and impacts in accordance with Extension reporting requirements. Utilize innovative technologies and educational methods to expand program reach and effectiveness. Green-Industry Best Management Practices Provide leadership in Green Industries Best Management Practices education, outreach and testing for certification. Conduct training programs focused on nutrient management, irrigation efficiency, integrated pest management, and environmental compliance. Promote practices that improve landscape performance while protecting water quality and natural resources. Collaborate with industry professionals and regulatory agencies to encourage adoption of science-based management practices. Urban Forestry and Tree Management Develop and deliver educational programs related to tree selection, planting, pruning, maintenance, risk reduction, and storm resilience. Promote proper tree trimming standards and arboricultural best practices. Assist municipalities, landscape professionals, and arborists in addressing urban forestry issues. Support initiatives that enhance Broward County’s urban forest health, sustainability, and resilience. Invasive Species Management Develop educational programs addressing invasive plant identification, prevention, monitoring, and management. Promote integrated management strategies that protect native ecosystems and urban landscapes. Collaborate with local, regional, and state partners to address invasive species concerns and emerging threats. Partnerships, Leadership, and Administration Establish and maintain effective working relationships with industry organizations, governmental agencies, educational institutions, and community groups. Facilitate advisory group participation to determine educational needs and service gaps. Participate in county, district, and statewide Extension Initiative Teams Seek external support, sponsorships, and collaborative funding opportunities that enhance program capacity. Incorporate youth education and career awareness opportunities into programming activities, with youth engagement representing a minimum of five percent of the appointment. Prepare annual reporting, impact summaries, and other administrative documents as required. Supervision Directly supervise at least one Program Assistant. Provide leadership, performance coaching, work planning, and professional development opportunities to Program Assistant. Coordinate support activities that ensure effective program delivery and customer service. Working Conditions The position requires a combination of office, classroom, and field-based responsibilities. A significant portion of the assignment involves conducting educational programs, demonstrations, and site visits in outdoor settings throughout Broward County. Candidates should be comfortable working in South Florida environmental conditions, including heat, humidity, direct sunlight, rain, and exposure to insects and other natural elements. The position requires the ability to walk on uneven terrain, stand for extended periods, and transport educational materials and equipment necessary for program delivery. Travel within the county and occasional travel elsewhere in Florida will be required. About UF The University of Florida is one of the nation’s leading public land-grant universities, recognized for excellence in teaching, research, and service. As a member of the Association of American Universities, the University of Florida is committed to addressing critical societal challenges through innovation, scholarship, and community engagement. The university provides students, faculty, and staff with opportunities to contribute to meaningful work that improves lives locally, nationally, and globally. About UF/IFAS Extension UF/IFAS Extension is the statewide outreach arm of the Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences. Through county Extension offices, research and education centers, and statewide program teams, UF/IFAS Extension delivers research-based information and educational programs that help individuals, businesses, communities, and governments make informed decisions. Extension professionals serve as trusted resources, translating university research into practical applications that address agricultural, environmental, economic, and social issues affecting Florida’s residents. UF/IFAS Extension reaches communities throughout Florida by providing educational leadership in agriculture, horticulture, natural resources, youth development, family and consumer sciences, sustainability, water resources, and community development. Extension programs are developed through stakeholder input and are designed to create measurable impacts that improve quality of life, strengthen industries, and support environmental stewardship. Extension professionals work collaboratively with local governments, industry organizations, schools, nonprofit partners, and residents to identify challenges and develop innovative solutions. Educational efforts include workshops, demonstrations, consultations, field days, online training, publications, applied research, and technical assistance programs. This position provides a unique opportunity to serve one of Florida’s largest and most dynamic urban green industry markets. The successful candidate will play a critical role in supporting green industry professionals, municipalities, arborists, landscape contractors, and other stakeholders through science-based education focused on sustainable landscape management and natural resource protection. The University of Florida offers a competitive compensation package and excellent benefits. Information regarding employee benefits is available at UF Benefits . About Broward County Broward County is located in southeastern Florida between Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties and encompasses approximately 1,323 square miles. With a population exceeding two million residents, Broward is one of Florida’s most populous counties and includes 31 municipalities ranging from highly urbanized coastal communities to western areas adjacent to the Everglades. The county supports a substantial urban horticulture industry that includes landscape maintenance companies, arboriculture firms, municipal parks departments, golf courses, nurseries, property management organizations, and environmental consulting businesses. Year-round growing conditions create significant opportunities for the green industry while also presenting challenges associated with tropical pests, invasive species, water conservation, fertilizer management, urban forestry, and hurricane preparedness. UF/IFAS Extension Broward County maintains strong partnerships with local governments, state agencies, industry associations, schools, environmental organizations, and community groups. The county’s urban landscape, extensive tree canopy, and environmental resources provide exceptional opportunities for innovative Extension programming that supports both economic vitality and environmental sustainability. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: A master’s degree in environmental Horticulture, Horticultural Sciences, Plant Sciences, Urban Forestry, Arboriculture, Natural Resources, Agriculture, or a closely related field. Applicants with at least 50 percent completion toward a master’s degree may be considered for appointment at the Extension Agent II level in accordance with university requirements. Demonstrated knowledge of horticulture, nursery management, landscape management, arboriculture, or related green industry disciplines. Experience planning, implementing, and evaluating educational, outreach, training, or technical assistance programs. Knowledge of landscape best management practices, nursery production, irrigation principles, nutrient management, and environmental stewardship. Knowledge of tree care, pruning standards, urban forestry, and arboriculture concepts. Knowledge of invasive species identification and management principles. Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, facilitation, and public speaking skills. Ability to establish and maintain productive relationships with stakeholder groups and partner organizations. Experience using technology and digital communication tools for educational programming, reporting and evaluation. Ability to collect, analyze, and communicate program outcomes and impacts. Ability to supervise support personnel and manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to obtain a valid driver’s license and ability to travel throughout the county and state as required. Because youth engagement will comprise at least five percent of the appointment, successful completion and maintenance of applicable UF youth protection requirements, including Level 2 DCF screening, is required. Candidates must also have a commitment to the University of Florida's core values. The successful candidate must obtain and maintain the appropriate Pesticide Applicator license and Green Industry/Best Management Practices certification . Preferred: Doctoral degree in Horticulture, Environmental Horticulture, Urban Forestry, Arboriculture, Plant Sciences, Natural Resources, or a closely related discipline for applicants seeking appointment at the Extension Agent III level. Experience working with Florida’s landscape, nursery, arboriculture, or green industry sectors. Experience delivering Green Industries Best Management Practices training programs. Experience conducting arboriculture, urban forestry, tree risk management, or storm preparedness education. Experience implementing invasive species management or environmental stewardship initiatives. Experience supervising staff, managing budgets, or coordinating volunteers. Demonstrated success securing grants, sponsorships, or external funding. Experience developing partnerships with municipalities, homeowner associations, industry groups, schools, and youth-serving organizations. Ability to communicate effectively in English and Spanish. Experience using evaluation methods to measure program effectiveness and impact. Professional certifications such as International Society of Arboriculture Certified Arborist and Commercial Pesticide Applicator certification are highly desirable and will strengthen the candidate’s ability to serve Broward County’s green industry clientele. Special Instructions to Applicants: Apply at Careers at UF by 11:55 p.m. (Eastern) of the posting end date, and attach: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541154/womens-imaging-radiologist-clinical-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Women's Imaging Radiologist - Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Women's Imaging Radiologist - Clinical Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541154 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30130000 - JX-RADIOLOGY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates should have an MD/DO and specialty in Diagnostic Radiology Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville, Department of Radiology is seeking a full-time Women’s Imaging Radiologist at the non-tenure clinical accruing level of Assistant Full Professor. Job Responsibilities Include: Women's Imaging Radiologist with academic focus at UF Jacksonville Health System at the Assistant Professor level with 100% specialization. Full-time academic position with UF Radiology and Imaging, UFJP Provide high quality service in a friendly academic setting. Serve as a mentor to train a great group of Residents & Fellows. Work with colleagues in surgery, oncology, radiation oncology & pathology, via daily interactions/multidisciplinary meetings. Have the drive and leadership skills necessary to be an integral part of the Women's Imaging Division. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates should have an MD/DO and specialty in Diagnostic Radiology Preferred: The candidate be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Diagnostic Radiology. The successful candidate will be fellowship trained in Women’s Imaging and experienced in all aspects of Woman's Imaging. The candidate should be a team player and expect to join a pleasant group of colleagues. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: \"Yes\" Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541155/psychologysurgical-psychology-clinical-assistant-or-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Psychology/Surgical Psychology - Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor",
      "description": "Psychology/Surgical Psychology - Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 541155 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30140000 - JX-SURGERY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Doctoral Level Licensed Psychologist (or license-eligible), Doctorate in Clinical and/or Counseling Psychology, with clinical experience working within an acute care hospital setting. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville, Department of Surgery, Division of Acute Care Surgery, seeks a full-time faculty position for a Surgical Psychologist at the non-tenure accruing level of Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor. The position will include overall responsibility for the on-going, day-to-day operations of the psychological services/family management component of the trauma system, inclusive of crisis interventions, grief counseling, individual, family and group therapies, stress debriefings, psychological assessments and diagnoses, in-patient consultation/liaison services, and out-patient follow-up/referral as needed. Also provide on-going stress management consultation and debriefings as needed for surgical residents, medical students, nursing staff, and other allied health professionals. Must have experience in program development and initiative and must be familiar working with a culturally diverse patient population that will include adults and children. Teaching responsibilities include co-direct supervision of the psychology post-doctoral fellow within the Division of Acute Care Surgery, and clinical teaching to medical residents, students, allied professionals and nursing staff on the psychological aspects of physical injury and other related topics specific to a collaborative care model, i.e. effective communication, capacity issues, stress management. Expected administrative responsibilities include membership on various committees at the hospital, local and University level. Also responsible for consistently obtaining and maintaining extra-mural funding for research activities, development and administration of research studies, scholarly presentations at the local, regional and national level, and publications in peer-reviewed journals. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/. Expected Salary: Salary and academic appointments are commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Doctoral Level Licensed Psychologist (or license-eligible), Doctorate in Clinical and/or Counseling Psychology, with clinical experience working within an acute care hospital setting. Preferred: Clinical experience working within an acute care, hospital setting, as part of an interdisciplinary treatment team; experience in teaching within an academic medical setting; research experience; versed in clinical interventions with patients across the lifespan and also within a diverse patient population. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
