    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541706/chair-asofull-prof-department-of-religion": {
      "title": "CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF - Department of Religion",
      "description": "CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF - Department of Religion Apply now Job no: 541706 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management, Humanities Department: 16420000 - LS-RELIGION Classification Title: Chair and Associate/Full Professor in Department of Religion Job Description: The Department of Religion in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for a 1.0 FTE, 9-month, tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor to serve as Department Chair. We seek a distinguished scholar with substantial administrative and leadership experience who can provide a clear vision for the department and guide its continued growth in research, teaching, and service. This is a senior appointment, with preference given to candidates qualified for appointment at the rank of Professor with tenure. Outstanding candidates at the Associate Professor rank will also be considered, provided they demonstrate a strong record of scholarly productivity, relevant administrative experience, and a clear interest in academic leadership. Research specialization is open. However, preference may be given to candidates whose scholarly expertise complements existing departmental strengths or expands the department's areas of distinction. The successful candidate will provide strategic leadership for the department; oversee undergraduate and graduate academic programs; manage budgets, personnel, and departmental operations; promote interdisciplinary collaboration; cultivate fundraising and external engagement opportunities; and support faculty development, recruitment, and retention. Established in 1946, the Department of Religion is committed to excellence in research, teaching, and public service. It is home to internationally recognized scholars whose fields of academic expertise include Buddhism, Islam, Hinduism, Judaism, ethics, and the intersections of religion and ecology. The University of Florida is a prominent research university that offers exceptional opportunities for innovative teaching, cutting-edge research, interdisciplinary collaboration, and external funding. As Florida’s flagship university, UF is committed to strengthening its reputation as a top public university in the country, while fostering a vibrant and inclusive academic community. Gainesville is known for its great quality of life, easy access to natural resources, and vibrant cultural scene. The Department of Religion anticipates hiring an accomplished scholar and effective administrator who can build upon recent successes in improving departmental functions and provide visionary leadership for the program’s future. The position offers excellent professional opportunities to shape department’s strategic position, work closely with supportive administration within the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, foster innovation in teaching and research, and instigate new interdisciplinary initiatives. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with rank and experience. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Religion or a related field. Demonstrated administrative and leadership experience. Strong record of scholarly achievement and publication. Qualifications commensurate with appointment as a tenured faculty member. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. A complete application includes: A Curriculum Vitae. A cover letter, which should address administrative experience, leadership philosophy, research accomplishments, and demonstrated excellence in teaching. Names and contact information for three external references. One representative example of a scholarly publication from the past 3 years. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541707/administrative-specialist-ii": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist II",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist II Apply now Job no: 541707 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 28011100 - VM-DEAN Classification Title: Administrative Specialist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelors degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The incumbent within the College of Veterinary Medicine's Office of the Dean functions as the lead member of the administrative team. This position administers most operations within the College of Veterinary Medicine's Dean's office, including the supervision of a Receptionist. Determine issues and recommend solutions for workflow policies and procedures. Oversees, evaluates and maintains office procedures related to filing, record-keeping, mail distribution, use of telephones, use of e-mail, use of computers for personal, office, and room scheduling, handling of confidential materials, and handling of students and other visitors to the college. Responsible for scheduling and maintaining multiple calendars. Prioritizes numerous appointment and meeting requests for the Dean, and Executive Associate Dean and Executive Director. Provides administrative support for travel and purchasing for the Dean and Executive Associate Dean. Supports searches for administrative leadership positions, including direct communication with candidates, scheduling dates, arranging travel, and creating itineraries. Oversees the scheduling of events on the CVM campus and manages contract execution with UF general Counsel. When new tasks are requested of the Dean's office, s/he composes office policies and procedures as necessary. Works closely with Dean and Executive Associate Dean to oversee work flow issues. Gathers information and prepares reports which may include contacting various university, local, state and national agencies, gathering and relaying information, data and statistics for the college. Examples of these reports include the Annual Interim Report to the AVMA Council on Education, AAVMC Comparative Data Survey, AAVMC Faculty Salary Comparative Data Report, HSC Annual Program Report, Activity Reports for the Dean and Associate Deans, and Provost Report. Many of these reports directly affect funding, salaries, negotiations in retaining faculty members, and utilize data that is compiled at the university and national levels. Oversees college level deadlines and works with department chairs and associate deans to make sure those deadlines are met. Coordinates scheduling of shared conference rooms overseen by the college, processes event requests, and manages submission of contracts and agreements to the General Counsel’s office on behalf of the College. Conducts and/or oversees special projects, including compiling, evaluating, and analyzing data (such as salary counter offers) for the Dean as requested. Acquires background information, citations, and statistics to provide necessary documentation for reports. Prepares reports with short turn-around times. Takes initiative in dean's absence, or at his/her request, to initiate preliminary report responses. The Dean's office is responsible for major gift fund raising among the college's alumni, hospital clients, friends, as well as corporations and foundations throughout the United States; as such this individual must be capable of effectively communicating the college's overall expertise among these stakeholders. The individual must work very closely with the dean, associate deans, department chairs, directors and administrative assistants, as well as with the college’s coordinators of Budget and Personnel. This person, in addition, is the liaison for the Dean's office to the President, Provost, Vice Presidents of both HSC and IFAS, Vice President for Governmental Affairs, Deans of both HSC and IFAS, and the Deans of the veterinary colleges throughout the US and Canada. This position routinely requires decisions to be made with limited supervision, interaction with a wide spectrum of personalities, supervision of staff and programs, preparation of reports, carrying out duties under pressure and with short deadlines, and review of university and college policies as needed. This position assists the Dean and Executive Associate Dean relative to various college issues that may pertain to hiring additional staff to budget needs on a routine basis. Prepares and administers annual operating budget of the Office of the Dean. Responsible for interpreting administrative and fiscal rules and policies and recommending new and/or updates needed to existing policies. Expected Salary: $69,000 - $75,000 / annually Required Qualifications: Bachelors degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Knowledge of MS Office, WordPress, Adobe CS, UFGO. Ability to work in a highly confidential environment, able to handle multi-tasking in a fast paced atmosphere. The individual must be skillful and effective when collaborating with administrators, faculty, students, and staff. Must have excellent writing and verbal skills. Must possess a high level of professionalism at all times. Ability to establish and maintain good working relationships with others. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and list of references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541712/ast-research-scientist-rs": {
      "title": "AST Research Scientist (RS)",
      "description": "AST Research Scientist (RS) Apply now Job no: 541712 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: In the State of Florida Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Computer Science, Grant or Research Administration Department: 18050000 - ED-STL SCHL-TEACH - LEARN Classification Title: RES AST SCTST Classification Minimum Requirements: Doctoral degree in Computer Science Education, educational technology, or a related field. Job Description: Works collaboratively with CS Everyone faculty and staff to: Lead a program of applied research focused on computer science and artificial intelligence education, with particular emphasis on teacher professional learning, students with disabilities, South Florida school districts, and statewide initiatives. Conceptualize, design, and conduct research studies examining the development, implementation, effectiveness, and scaling of CS/AI professional learning models and instructional approaches. Lead the research and development of evidence-informed professional learning, including microcredentials, short courses, and other scalable models for preparing educators to teach CS and AI. Lead or contribute substantially to scholarly dissemination, including peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, research reports, briefs, and other products that communicate findings to research and practitioner audiences. Lead and contribute to the development of competitive external grant proposals, including identifying funding opportunities, conceptualizing research aims, developing research designs and evaluation plans, coordinating proposal components, and contributing to grant writing and submission. Supervise and mentor undergraduate and graduate research assistants in research design, data collection, analysis, scholarly writing, and responsible conduct of research. Because this position involves regular travel to partner districts and educational communities throughout Florida, particularly in South Florida, the successful candidate should be based in, or have ready access to South Florida to support frequent statewide travel. Expected Salary: $72,000 Required Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Computer Science Education, educational technology, or a related field. Preferred: A minimum of 3 years of experience in K‑12 CS/AI professional development for in‑service teachers. Experience working with South Florida districts or leading statewide PD initiatives. Experience designing and managing micro‑credential or short‑course offerings. Experience with writing and managing externally funded projects. Ability to translate and communicate complex topics across a variety of forums, tailoring communications to effectively fit and influence the targeted audience. Agility in making and following up on strategic decisions. Ability to plan for long‑term sustainability and statewide capacity building. Experience supporting students with disabilities in CS/AI contexts. Ability to work independently as well as in a team structure; flexibility and multi‑task management skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541717/assistant-professor-of-movement-acting": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Movement & Acting",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Movement & Acting Apply now Job no: 541717 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 13040100 - COTA-THEATRE-CHAIR Classification Title: Ast Prof in Movement Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank must hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: Assistant Professor of Movement & Acting Position: Full-time, 9-month tenure track, promotion-accruing faculty position Date of Expected Hire: August 16, 2027 Salary: $68,000-$70,000 with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package The School of Theatre + Dance seeks an Assistant Professor of Movement & Acting with a specialization in movement and physical approaches to actor training. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate students, with primary responsibility for movement training that develops the actor’s physical, expressive, and embodied skills. The candidate will also teach undergraduate and graduate stage combat courses in a variety of theatrical weapon styles and may teach acting courses and professional development labs. Additional teaching responsibilities may include non-majors courses such as Oral Interpretation of Literature, and courses in the University of Florida Quest program. The successful candidate will also provide mentoring to BFA and MFA students to prepare them for successful careers in the industry. The School is especially interested in candidates who bring a progressive perspective to actor training and who integrate movement, embodiment, and physical approaches into contemporary performance training. We seek an individual who will contribute to a student-centered community committed to professional development and scholarship, innovation and flexibility, and teamwork and collaboration. The successful candidate will contribute to the recruitment and retention of BFA and MFA acting students within a vibrant professional theatre training program at one of the nation’s leading public research universities. Additional creative responsibilities include movement coaching for School productions, as well as fight direction and theatrical violence and/or intimacy direction for approximately four productions each academic year. There is also an opportunity to direct School productions. The position also includes participation in school, college, and/or university service and other related duties as assigned. Typical teaching loads for tenure-line faculty in Theatre Studies & Performance are 5–9 credits per semester, generally distributed as 5–6 credits in one semester and 7–9 credits in the other. Most courses in the School are 2 or 3 credits. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a change-maker. We seek a colleague who will position emerging artists and researchers as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities: Teaching and Student Mentorship Develop, revise, and deliver undergraduate and graduate curricula, with primary responsibility for movement and physical approaches to actor training. Teach undergraduate and graduate stage combat courses in a variety of theatrical weapon styles. Advise graduate and undergraduate capstone projects. Contribute to curricular efforts in performance-related areas. Contribute to the recruitment and retention of graduate and undergraduate theatre students. Participate in School productions through movement coaching, fight direction, and/or theatrical intimacy direction. Responsibilities may also include teaching acting courses, professional development labs, non-majors courses such as Oral Interpretation of Literature, and/or courses in the University of Florida Quest program, as appropriate to the candidate’s expertise and the School’s curricular needs. Creative Research and Professional Engagement Maintain an active national and/or international creative research profile in movement, physical approaches to acting, stage combat, or related areas of specialization, consistent with expectations for tenure and promotion. Contribute to the School’s vibrant professional theatre training environment through creative practice, collaboration, and professional engagement. Engage in innovative approaches that connect theatre and the performing arts to critical discourse and research across disciplines. Service and Community Engagement Actively participate in the School’s learning community and contribute to a student-centered environment committed to teaching excellence, professional development and scholarship, innovation and flexibility, and teamwork and collaboration. Participate in the organizational and administrative activities of the Theatre Studies & Performance Area Participate in shared governance through service to the School, College, and University. Contribute to recruitment, outreach, and other activities that support the School’s academic and artistic programs. Perform other related duties as assigned. SCHOOL OF THEATRE + DANCE: The light-filled Nadine McGuire Theatre and Dance Pavilion is home to the School of Theatre + Dance (SoTD). The School’s international full-time faculty of 35 recognized artists and researchers, complemented by adjunct and guest faculty and staff, including full- and part-time professionals. The School offers MFA curricula in acting and in design, which includes scene design, lighting design and costume design; BFA programs in acting, musical theatre, and theatrical production with concentrations in costume, digital media, lighting, scenic, and sound; and a BA with concentrations in general theatre, stage management, and theatre management. The School features two degree programs in dance designed to develop the individual voice of the dance artist, a BFA and a BA. Approximately 200 students major in the artistically diverse curricula of the School of Theatre + Dance. Through its production program, outreach and innovative programming, the School of Theatre + Dance provides multiple cultural opportunities annually to thousands of members of the University and the greater community. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Dance and the National Association of Schools of Theatre. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/theatreanddance . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Explore UF’s benefits options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: $68,000-$70,000 with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following: Terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or PhD ABD) in Theatre or closely related field, or equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. Potential for record of achievement in the field at a national or international level, in alignment with the unit’s tenure and promotion criteria. Evidence of successful collaboration and collegiality with faculty, students, and staff. Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following: Teaching experience of 3 years minimum beyond graduate assistantship at the university level, or equivalent. Demonstrated experience integrating movement, physicality, and embodied practices into actor training. Specialization, experience, and/or certification in one or more established movement-based approaches to actor training, such as Michael Chekhov Technique, Margolis Method, Laban Movement Analysis, Black Acting Methods, Grotowski-based training, Theatrical Biomechanics, or other recognized physical approaches. Candidates who demonstrate an eclectic or interdisciplinary approach to physical actor training are also encouraged to apply, with evidence of effectiveness in practice. Credentials and demonstrated experience teaching stage combat and/or conducting skills proficiency tests through recognized stage combat organizations. Evidence of leadership potential and/or a national or international professional reputation in movement, physical actor training, stage combat, or related area. Evidence of a commitment to innovative and cross-disciplinary curricula. Demonstrated commitment to teaching practices that reflect current and evolving professional practices and prepare students to navigate a changing field. Experience navigating a multi-disciplinary higher education setting. Evidence of success in collaboration toward creative and curricular goals. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin November 17, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, all application documents must be submitted by December 7, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: (1) a detailed letter of application that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a research narrative; (4) statement of teaching philosophy; (5) links to research, production and/or teaching sample (Note that the UF application system only allows for one 5 MB maximum file size PDF document); (6) names and contact information of three professional references. The UF system will give you the option to upload letters of reference. We do not require letters of reference with initial application materials. Please ignore that option. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Tim Altmeyer Chair, Assistant Professor of Movement & Acting Email: taltmeyer@ufl.edu The search committee does not utilize AI or other software to provide any level of applicant assessment. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541726/health-promotion-specialist-harm-reduction": {
      "title": "Health Promotion Specialist - Harm Reduction",
      "description": "Health Promotion Specialist - Harm Reduction Apply now Job no: 541726 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Student Services Department: 04052000 - SL-GATORWELL Classification Title: Health Promotion Specialist Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Job Description: GatorWell Health Promotion Services seeks a Health Promotion Specialist, to develop, implement and evaluate theory-based and evidence-informed health promotion strategies focusing on harm reduction efforts. GatorWell’s mission is to empower UF students to pursue lifelong wellbeing through prevention, advocacy, and sustainable behavior change. Report to the Director Provide Wellness Coaching for Academic Success Support student success through programming and outreach Target materials and outreach to specific student populations Advocate for holistic student wellbeing by supporting strategic planning efforts. Collaborate with key stakeholders to develop and evaluate a comprehensive harm reduction approach through needs assessments, intervention development, implementation and evaluation of theory-based and evidence-informed health promotion strategies, targeted programs, outreach, and policies. Provide individual student services as aligned with the department, including but not limited to Brief Alcohol Screening Intervention for College Students (BASICS), Cannabis Screening Intervention for College Students (CASICS), Alcohol Skills Training Program (ASTP), and Wellness Coaching for Academic Success. Explore, create, and develop additional harm reduction services as needed. Hire, supervise and manage various student staff and interns. Contribute, collect, and maintain data tracking and report development related to health promotion strategies, trends, programs, education, and services at the University of Florida and across the country; and support grant funding and reporting. Contributes to the division and university through active participation on committees and division-wide initiatives. Perform other duties as assigned by supervisor. Expected Salary: $54,000.00 Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Preferred: Master's degree in public health, health promotion, health policy or health education; degrees in other health related fields may be considered. Experience in a higher education environment. Certified Health Education Specialist (CHES) or Certified in Public Health (CPH). Certified Wellness Coach (or eligible and intent to become certified). Experience conducting needs assessments and using results to guide programmatic decisions. Experience with design and implementation of health promotion programs. Knowledge of the American College Health Association’s Standards of Practice for Health Promotion in Higher Education (ACHA SPHPHE) and Healthy Campus Framework. Demonstrated knowledge of health promotion, behavior change, population-based theories and models, and evidence-based health promotion programs/initiatives. Demonstrated skills in developing and targeting health promotion initiatives and educational materials for various groups of college students. Proficiency in data collection and assessment tools such as Qualtrics. Supervisory and organizational skills to collaborate effectively with stakeholders, on campus and in the community. Willingness to be an active member of at least one relevant professional organization. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Required Documentation: Resume Cover Letter 3 References Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538930/dental-assistant-i": {
      "title": "Dental Assistant I",
      "description": "Dental Assistant I Apply now Job no: 538930 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry Department: 34010703 - DN-FACULTY PRACTICE Classification Title: Dental Assistant I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma and one year of appropriate experience and Basic Life Support (BLS) certification. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The College of Dentistry is seeking candidates for two Dental Assistant I positions. The Dental Assistant works in the Faculty Practice Clinic with members of the dental faculty to provide four-handed dentistry at the chair. This position assists dentists or dental students in dental examinations and in the treatment of patients; assists faculty in the instruction of dental students; prepares patients, operates equipment, sterilizes instruments, and mix amalgam. Assists dentist during examination and treatment of patients: Prepares patient, maintains and sterilizes instruments, sets up instrument trays, prepares materials, and assists dentist during dental procedures. In the laboratory the Dental Assistant pours dental models and trims them. Also he or she helps in the fabrication of whitening trays and in any other laboratory procedure that is required. Takes and processes dental X-rays; maintains and sterilizes dental instruments; organizes inventory and orders supplies. Takes and records medical and dental histories and vital signs of patient. Sets up and breaks down dental operatories. Organizes inventory and orders supplies. When assigned as rover, the Dental Assistant monitors clinical activity as well as the patient schedule to provide backup or assistance to dentists and primary assistants when needed. Responds to oral, telephonic and written communication related to patient care (appointments, consultations, emergencies) and performs related clerical duties, i.e. appoints patients in computer, copies and files charts. Assists with other duties as required to maintain efficient flow of patient care. Helps the dental assistants with setting up and breaking down dental operatories. Expected Salary: $20.00 - $21.00 per hour; commensurate with skills and experience Sign-on bonus available Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma and one year of appropriate experience and Basic Life Support (BLS) certification. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Expanded Functions Dental Auxiliary (EFDA) Certification demonstrating the successful completion of training as specified in Chapter 64B5-16, Florida Administrative Code. Dental radiology certificate/permit Thorough knowledge of dental terminology, equipment, instruments, materials and procedures. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload a cover letter, resume and a list of three references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540059/veterinary-technician-iii-small-animal": {
      "title": "Veterinary Technician I/II, Small Animal",
      "description": "Veterinary Technician I/II, Small Animal Apply now Job no: 540059 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Marion Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28360000 - VM-WORLD EQUESTRIAN CENTER Classification Title: Veterinary Technician I/II (CARE) Classification Minimum Requirements Veterinary Technician I - High school diploma or equivalent and one year of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II - Credentialed veterinary technician. Job Description: The University of Florida Veterinary Hospital at the World Equestrian Center (WEC), provides Small Animal primary care and emergency services and Equine sports medicine, rehabilitation, and diagnostic imaging services. A Veterinary Technician based in Small Animal services at the UFVH at WEC, assist clinicians with routine and urgent examinations, treatments, clinical procedures, and hospitalization nursing care. An individual in this position must possess a good base knowledge of veterinary medicine and enjoy learning new things. Why work for UF? We know you care about your own pets as much as we do our patients, so we offer discounts on treatments, procedures, and even food for all our employees’ pets. We encourage you to join our team and grow with us to make a difference. Benefits: Competitive Salary Extensive Medical Benefits (including health, dental and vision) Shift Differentials CE Opportunities Paid Time Off & Paid Holidays Licensing Fees Paid Employee Discount Available Shifts: The hospital requires coverage 24 hours/7 days a week. Typically schedules consist of 8 or 10 hour shifts. Will be assigned to a primary shift, generally: Day shift, Evening Shift or Night Shift. Reassignment to a different primary shift, rotating shifts, split shifts and extended shifts may be required and assigned depending on hospital needs. Assigned days may vary Sunday through Saturday, weekends and holidays included. This position also requires working alternate shifts, holidays, weekends, or “on-call duty.” This individual is considered essential personnel which may include reporting to work during Main Campus closures. Minimum Qualifications: Veterinary Technician I, Small Animal: High school diploma and two years of veterinary experience or one year of veterinary experience and currently enrolled in a veterinary technician program or graduate of a veterinary technician program Veterinary Technician II, Small Animal: Credentialed veterinary technician and four years of veterinary experience Expected Salary: $18 - 25 /hour Required Qualifications: Veterinary Technician I - High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II - Credentialed veterinary technician. Preferred Veterinary Technician I, Small Animal: High school diploma and two years of veterinary experience or one year of veterinary experience and currently enrolled in a veterinary technician program or graduate of a veterinary technician program Veterinary Technician II, Small Animal: Credentialed veterinary technician and four years of veterinary experience Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
