    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541358/administrative-specialist-ii-pediatric-neurology": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist II - Pediatric Neurology",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist II - Pediatric Neurology Apply now Job no: 541358 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29091800 - MD-PEDS-NEUROLOGY Classification Title: Administrative Spec II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Administrative Specialist II provides advanced administrative support within an assigned division in the Department of Pediatrics in support of clinical, research, and academic programs. This position manages a broad range of operational, fiscal, human resources, and recruitment, while establishing work priorities, coordinating daily administrative operations, and exercising considerable judgment and discretion in interpreting and applying University and departmental policies and procedures. The incumbent serves as a liaison between departmental leadership, faculty, staff, functional support teams, University departments, and external stakeholders to ensure efficient, compliant, and effective operations. The Administrative Specialist II provides comprehensive administrative support to all faculty and staff within the assigned division(s). The position interprets and applies departmental and institutional policies and standards, including those related to fiscal administration, procurement, and human resources, while coordinating activities associated with faculty and provider recruitment and onboarding, provider scheduling, payroll administration, purchasing and procurement, travel coordination, facilities and information technology services, and meetings and events. About this Role: Administrative Coordination and Division Support: Provides advanced administrative support for divisional operations and serves as a primary administrative resource for divisional leadership, faculty, staff, trainees, and stakeholders. Coordinates schedules, meetings, conferences, and events; prepares agendas, correspondence, presentations, reports, and meeting minutes; and manages travel arrangements and reimbursement processes. Provides logistical and administrative support before, during, and after divisional meetings and events. Monitors shared divisional email accounts, responds to routine and moderately complex inquiries, and ensures timely communication and appropriate routing of requests. Maintains confidential records and communications in accordance with university policies and procedures. Assists with divisional initiatives, projects, and operational activities by coordinating administrative processes, tracking assignments, and facilitating the completion of tasks and deadlines. Exercises independent judgment in prioritizing workload, resolving administrative issues, and serving as a liaison among faculty, staff, departmental leadership, university offices, and external organizations to support efficient divisional operations. Administrative Coordination & Division Support: Coordinates provider call schedules in collaboration with Division Chiefs and clinical leadership to ensure appropriate clinical coverage. Develops, maintains, and updates provider call schedules within Epic On-Call and other scheduling systems, ensuring schedules accurately reflect clinical assignments and coverage needs. Proactively reviews and monitors provider schedules to ensure accuracy and identify conflicts. Communicates provider schedule changes, absences, and clinical coverage updates with ACU, clinical leadership, Division Administrators, Clinic Managers, centralized scheduling teams, and other stakeholders in accordance with departmental and institutional policies and procedures. Identifies scheduling conflicts, coordinates schedule changes, and collaborates with leadership to maintain continuity of patient care and efficient clinical operations. Fiscal Administration & Procurement: Coordinates divisional fiscal and purchasing activities in accordance with University and departmental policies. Utilizes UFGO to process travel requests, expense reimbursements, and user access requests. Serves as a PCard holder and/or reconciler by processing and reconciling transactions, submitting exemption requests, and ensuring compliance with purchasing requirements. Processes purchases through UF Marketplace; obtains quotes, purchase orders, verifies funding sources, submits invoices for payment, tracks operational expenses, orders office and operational supplies, and interprets University procurement, fiscal, and purchasing policies to ensure compliance. Recruitment, Human Resources & Faculty Affairs: Coordinates faculty, provider, and staff recruitment and onboarding activities, including interview scheduling, candidate itineraries, travel arrangements, onboarding documentation, workspace preparation, information technology requests, and access credentials. Serves as a departmental resource regarding hiring, onboarding, payroll deadlines, leave administration, PTO, FMLA, and other human resources processes. Enters faculty time, reviews payroll reporting for providers and staff, and supports faculty affairs activities including annual evaluations, promotion and tenure, credentialing, licensure renewals, and educational programs. Cross-Divisional Administrative Support & Special Projects: Provides advanced administrative support and cross-coverage for other divisions within the Department of Pediatrics assigned by the Division Administrator. Assists with workload redistribution during staffing shortages, employee leave, vacancies, and other operational needs to ensure continuity of departmental operations. Participates in departmental initiatives, performs complex administrative projects, supports process improvement efforts, and completes additional assignments as directed by the Division Administrator and/or Division Chief. Expected Salary: $55,000 - $60,000; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred Qualifications: Administrative experience in a complex healthcare, academic, or higher education environment, with demonstrated experience in fiscal administration, human resources coordination, and proficiency with University of Florida administrative systems (e.g., UFGO, myUFL, UF Marketplace, and Epic) preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541457/scientific-laboratory-manager-pediatric-child-health-research-institute-chri": {
      "title": "Scientific Laboratory Manager - Pediatric Child Health Research Institute (CHRI)",
      "description": "Scientific Laboratory Manager - Pediatric Child Health Research Institute (CHRI) Apply now Job no: 541457 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29092100 - MD-CHILD HEALTH RES INSTITUTE Classification Title: Scientific Laboratory Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The Scientific Laboratory Manager is a research-focused laboratory role centered on studying the microbiota of clinical and environmental samples to better understand diarrheal diseases and UTIs caused by Gram-negative bacteria. This position will use molecular and chemical analysis techniques to explore how antimicrobial factors like antibiotics, bacteriophages, and host immunity influence bacterial communities, with findings applied to diagnostics and public health interventions. Beyond benchwork, the role includes substantial lab management responsibilities such as supervising students and staff, maintaining safety compliance, managing budgets and supplies, and coordinating with the PI on grants and publications. The position also involves coordinating international clinical studies and managing associated regulatory documents, with the possibility of intermittent fieldwork in Bangladesh or Kenya. Duties include but are not limited to: Laboratory Research Processing human-derived samples containing bacterial pathogens for molecular analysis (e.g., metagenomic sequencing, qPCR) and chemical analysis (mass spectrometry). Research, develop, and implement new laboratory protocols to achieve the experimental goals of funded projects. Maintain and iterate developed research protocols. Work with the PI and external collaborators to coordinate all phases of research related to funded projects and contribute to grant protocols, progress reports, pilot studies and research publications. Oversee the collection of samples and data generated by all collaborators and be responsible for gathering, organizing, and storing all data related to funded projects. In partnership with the PI, serve as the local administrator for management of UF Environmental Health and Safety and UF IRB protocols and compliance activities. Laboratory Management Maintenance and operation of the laboratory. Supervise research activities and provide training for paid and unpaid laboratory staff, including undergraduate and graduate students. Perform day-to-day laboratory management duties, including ordering supplies, and ensure compliance with all safety and regulatory requirements for the safe and proper functioning of the laboratory. Assist the PI with coordination of invoicing and purchasing activities between the departmental fiscal office and subprojects/independent contractors. Manage supplies and budgets to achieve the goals of funded research. Clinical Study Management With close guidance and support with the PI, coordination with domestic and foreign partners to manage clinical samples and clinical data. This includes maintenance of associated data and documents (e.g., MTAs and data management agreements). This responsibility requires excellent communication skills across different languages and cultures. Miscellaneous Duties as Assigned Performs additional responsibilities as determined by departmental needs and supervisor direction Mentoring graduate and undergraduate students. Continuing education (e.g., on- and off-campus conferences) will be encouraged. Individual must be willing to handle live pathogens within a BSL2 environment. Expected Salary: $70,000 - $80,000; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred Qualifications: Doctoral degree in molecular biology, microbiology, chemistry, biochemistry, or a closely related field and two years of experience in the field or in a related area; or a master’s degree in one of the same fields listed above or a closely related field and five years of experience in the field or in a related area; or a bachelor’s degree in biology or a closely related field and seven years of experience in the field or in a related area. Expertise in basic molecular microbiology laboratory techniques. Must be meticulous in recording, tracking, and reporting collected data. Experience with preferred with basic methods of bacterial genetic manipulation (e.g. gene cloning and gene knockouts). At least five publications (at least one as first author) in indexed journals required. Experience with quantitative PCR, next-generation sequencing and associated bioinformatics, phage biology (lytic and lysogenic), fluorescence microscopy, and mass spectrometry. Passion for making the world a better place through the tools of science and medicine, especially for marginalized populations globally who are vulnerable to infectious diseases because of extreme poverty. Experience in animal colony management and handling of rodents (optional). Special Instructions to Applicants: Normal work hours are Monday through Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm. Nights & weekends as needed for on-going research. To be considered for this position, please upload your cover letter or letter of interest and resume. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541697/surgical-oncologythoracic-surgery-clinical-assistantassociate-professor": {
      "title": "Surgical Oncology/Thoracic Surgery - Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor",
      "description": "Surgical Oncology/Thoracic Surgery - Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 541697 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 30140000 - JX-SURGERY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates should have a MD/DO or equivalent and must have completed an accredited thoracic surgery training program. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville Department of Surgery invite applications for a faculty Thoracic Surgeon at the non-tenure accruing levels of Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor to join our growing surgical program in Northeast Florida. We are seeking a thoracic surgeon with expertise across the full spectrum of general thoracic surgery and thoracic surgical oncology. Candidates should have experience with minimally invasive surgical approaches, including robotic surgery, and demonstrate a strong commitment to multidisciplinary, patient-centered cancer care. This position provides an opportunity to help shape the continued development of thoracic surgery and thoracic oncology while working alongside a multidisciplinary team committed to expanding access to advanced surgical and cancer care. The Department of Surgery at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville is a dynamic academic department committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service to the community. The department provides a broad range of complex surgical services and serves as an important referral resource for patients throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. Faculty work collaboratively across specialties to provide high-quality, evidence-based care while advancing the academic missions of the College of Medicine. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates should have a MD/DO or equivalent and must have completed an accredited thoracic surgery training program. Must be able to obtain a Florida Medical License. Preferred: Eligible candidates should be board certified/eligible from the American Board of Surgery and the American Board of Thoracic Surgery. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on a rolling basis until a competitive candidate pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541702/academic-assistant-iii": {
      "title": "Academic Assistant III",
      "description": "Academic Assistant III Apply now Job no: 541702 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 60030000 - AG-DEAN FOR ACADEMIC PROGRAMS Classification Title: Academic Assistant III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Process all transfer student applicants to the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences (CALS), and the School of Natural Resources and Environment. Evaluate transcripts, identify substitutions in the UF student records systems and maintain a file of admitted and denied students. Inform students and departments of course work that must be completed before acceptance. Assist with updating of the CALS Transfer Guide. Interact with students, parents and advisers (both UF and community college) regarding admission requirements, admission status, referral status, transferability of classes, etc. Diplomatically work with students who have been denied and give alternative options. Assist students during registration periods. Monitor and interpret changes in college and university policies. Update necessary information to be sent to students and advisers. Evaluate and verify admissibility to all AG (Agriculture) and NE (Natural Resources & Environment) majors for current students (residential and UF Online), readmission and post-baccalaureate applicants. Evaluate transcripts, identify substitutions in the UF student records systems, update major designation, evaluate progress, and change Universal Tracking status as necessary, monitor CHANGE majors and their progress. Verify, check for accuracy, and process college forms, including change of major, application for minors, and S/U (satisfactory/unsatisfactory) requests. Monitor and evaluate all undergraduate AG and NE students (residential and UF Online) for admission conditions, academic performance, and Universal Tracking status. Add and remove holds and change tracking status as needed. Serve as a liaison to various University Offices (Registrar, Admissions, etc.) and provide back-up to other CALS staff. Expected Salary: $19.16 - 31.13 per hour; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Bachelor's Degree Command of Microsoft Office products and proficient in basic computer tasks. Experience dealing with the public. Ability to communicate effectively and professionally, in both verbal and written form. Previous experience working with students. Knowledge of university and college policies and procedures related to undergraduate admissions and academic programs. Ability to properly complete and process all relevant forms used in undergraduate academic programs. Knowledge of student admissions procedures. Knowledge of UF student records systems. Ability to use UF student records systems to monitor a student’s academic progress and certify students for graduation. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541709/clin-svc-representative-i": {
      "title": "Clin Svc Representative I",
      "description": "Clin Svc Representative I Apply now Job no: 541709 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29081100 - MD-PATH - UF PATH LABS Classification Title: Clinical Service Representative I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent Job Description: This position supports the clinical, research, and educational missions of the UF Health Pathology Laboratories by coordinating the receipt, triage, accessioning, registration and reporting of incoming pathology specimens, slides, and tissue blocks. The position maintains consultation materials, records, data, files, and related documentation to ensure accurate specimen processing, information management, and compliance with laboratory procedures and regulatory requirements. This is an evening shift position. Triage: Receive and record all specimens/slides/blocks into daily receipt log. Prioritize and sort specimens appropriately for accessioning to include checking Clinical Lab bin periodically throughout the day for additional specimens. Verify specimens and requisitions meet requirements for labeling and are problem-free. Documents any discrepancies and forwards to Client Services. Registration: Collects patient and insurance information and ensure required details are included on the forms. Perform data entry of patient demographics for outreach biopsies and consult. Document any discrepancy and update in a timely manner. This position also verifies insurance information through LIS and may use Availity for confirmation. Anatomic Pathology Accession: Prepare Anatomic Pathology laboratory specimens for designated departments/locations prior to laboratory analysis and testing. Unpacks specimens and routes specimens by type to various staging areas. Prepares all specimens received for testing in designated laboratory departments or locations such as staging of slides, blocks, and other specimens. Reviews specimen requisitions against data entry of information for accuracy, documents error, corrects error and escalates to supervisor or team lead as necessary. Perform data entry of specimen source, test order and requisition into LIS according to given protocol with minimal errors and scan requisitions with associated paperwork. Accurately label specimen(s)/slide(s) and timely distributes to the grossing area or pathologist. Prepares excess specimen samples/slides for storage. Accurately documents any orders / materials lacking required information or documentation and all problems cases appropriately. Sends issues to necessary department and records for follow-up. CP Accessions: Prepare Clinical Pathology laboratory specimens for designated departments/locations prior to laboratory analysis and testing. Unpacks specimens from branches or ports and routes specimens by type to various staging areas. Prepares all specimens received for testing in designated laboratory departments or locations such as staging of specimens, centrifuge, separate serum, and blood smears. Aliquots sample for departments. Prepares excess specimen samples for storage and resolves and document problem specimens Reporting: Prepare draft reports in Laboratory Information System using established departmental procedures; tasks include report formatting and proofing. In addition, staff updates reports with Addendum or results from quantitative pathology, molecular pathology, or outside test results as requested. Intake/Slide control: All materials are tracked from site to site and to internal departments. Receive and track slides/cases from UF Health Pathology satellite laboratories. Distribute slides to the appropriate Pathologists. Maintain records of slides received and their distribution points. Assist UF Health Pathology Laboratories intake personnel in locating slides that have been distributed to Pathologists. Return and track slides to UF Health Pathology satellite laboratories. Maintain records of slides returned from outside facilities. Expected Salary: Salary range is between $17.86 - $18.77, plus evening shift differential Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: Communication skills which will allow the prospective employee to interact with UF physicians, client physicians, and other health professionals in a professional manner. Ability to work in a potentially stressful environment. Six month lab accessioning experience preferred. Knowledge of Medical terminology. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541713/assistant-scientist": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Assistant Scientist Apply now Job no: 541713 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16400100 - LS-PSYCHOLOGY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: Doctorate from an accredited institution in psychology, cognitive neuroscience, or a closely related health‑sciences discipline. Candidates with experience in multiple of these areas will be prioritized. Job Description: The Department of Psychology in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences invites applications for a full-time, twelve-month Assistant Scientist position. This is a non-tenure-accruing faculty appointment. Duties Include: Research Design and Implementation Designing Research Studies: Develop detailed research protocols and experimental designs focused on perception, attention, and emotion. Ensure that methodologies are scientifically rigorous and appropriate for the research questions and include detailed recruitment, analytic & results dissemination plans. Conducting Research: Carry out experiments, surveys, and assessments according to protocols. This includes conducting assessments, training staff, and ensuring the collection of high-quality data. Managing Data: Support the development and maintenance of databases for research data and/or the development and integration of data collection tools. Ensure data is stored securely and is accessible for analysis. Collecting and Analyzing Data: Oversee the collection of research data, ensuring accuracy and reliability. Reporting Results: Prepare visualizations, summary reports, journal publications and presentations to communicate findings effectively. Share results with the research team and external stakeholders to advance the field. Conducting Systematic Literature Reviews: Regularly review recent literature related to perception, attention, and emotion. Summarize and synthesize findings to inform ongoing and future research projects. Integrating New Knowledge: Apply insights from recent research to refine hypotheses, adapt methodologies, and enhance the relevance of research projects. Stay informed about emerging trends and technological advancements. Outreach and Networking Community Engagement: Participate in the development and implementation of community outreach activities, e.g., public lectures, to promote research and foster public understanding of health-related topics. Professional Networking & Stakeholder Engagement: Help organize and/or attend professional meetings, networking and stakeholder events to build relationships with research stakeholders including academic, industry, clinical, and community partners to facilitate research and integrate research into day-to-day settings. Team Collaboration: Work collaboratively with interdisciplinary teams. Participate in regular team meetings to discuss progress, challenges, and strategies. Scientific Communication: Prepare and deliver presentations of research findings at conferences, workshops, and meetings. Contribute to writing and revising peer-reviewed publications and peer reviewing manuscripts for the journal in the research community. Provide Service to the University and Profession: participate in department, university, and professional organizations at the local, national, international level. Grant Writing and Funding Develop Proposals: Draft and collaborate with team members to develop comprehensive and competitive grant proposals for a wide variety of funders, including government, industry and non-profit organizations. Obtain Funding: Identify potential funding sources and opportunities. Contribute to the development of project budgets and funding requests. Special Projects Assisting in Projects: Contribute to special projects or initiatives that arise within the department, which may include administrative or operational tasks outside of primary research responsibilities. Training and Supervision Mentoring: Provide guidance and support to junior researchers, students, interns, and lab assistants. Train new team members and learners in areas of expertise and/or on research protocols and techniques. Supervising: Oversee day-to-day activities of support staff and graduate/undergraduate students involved in research projects, ensuring tasks are completed effectively on time and in budget. Ethical and Regulatory Compliance Monitoring Compliance: Participate in developing and executing compliance monitoring plans. Provide oversight to ensure that all research activities adhere to institutional, ethical, and regulatory guidelines. Obtain necessary approvals from ethics review boards and regulatory bodies. Ensuring Participant Confidentiality: Maintain confidentiality and privacy of research participants. Follow protocols for data security and ethical handling of sensitive information. The Psychology Department offers doctoral programs in behavioral and cognitive neuroscience, behavior analysis, counseling psychology, developmental psychology, and social psychology. We currently have 44 full-time faculty, ~ 100 PhD students, and > 2,000 undergraduate majors. About the University of Florida A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), the University of Florida is the state’s oldest and most comprehensive land-, sea-, and space-grant university, with an enrollment of over 50,000 students. For 2026, the University of Florida was ranked the seventh-best public university in the United States by U.S. News & World Report, with exceptional research, teaching, and a university-wide commitment to open intellectual discourse. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs, including magnet schools and an International Baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at visitgainesville . The Department of Psychology is committed to promoting an environment that welcomes all abilities, classes, ethnicities/races, religions, gender identities and expressions, etc. We particularly welcome applicants who can contribute to such an environment through their scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. Benefits: This faculty position is eligible for a comprehensive benefits package that includes state medical, dental, and vision plans; life and disability coverage; multiple retirement options; and paid time away and university holidays. Learn more about UF faculty benefits here. Required Qualifications: Minimum of 2 years of research experience as a postdoctoral researcher. Publications in high‑quality, peer‑reviewed journals, as first author. Experience in university teaching, mentoring, and supervising graduate and undergraduate students. Demonstrated success in writing grant proposals. Record of refereed peer-reviewed publications and presentations. Experience of delivering presentations at academic conferences. Advanced proficiency in designing, conducting, and analyzing neuroimaging data (e.g., EEG, ERPs, fMRI), programming skills in Python and/or MATLAB, and applied experience using machine‑learning techniques (e.g., pattern‑recognition algorithms) for neuro‑signal processing. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. A complete application includes: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement Statement of teaching experience, interests, and philosophy Three letters of recommendation. Please provide the names and email addresses of three references and the application system will automatically request letters at the time of application submission. Review of applications will begin on October 30th, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to search committee chair Andreas Keil ( akeil@ufl.edu ). Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541715/phlebotomist-pediatric-infectious-diseases": {
      "title": "Phlebotomist - Pediatric Infectious Diseases",
      "description": "Phlebotomist - Pediatric Infectious Diseases Apply now Job no: 541715 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Allied Health, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Phlebotomist-Peds ID Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and completion of a phlebotomy course; or a high school diploma or equivalent and one year of relevant experience. Job Description: Provides medical support activities for clinicians in a multi-disciplinary outpatient clinic serving pediatric and adult patients, including HIV infected and affected populations. Works with integrated patient-centered medical home team to provide patient care and perform administrative, clinical and laboratory duties in support of clinic and providers in a friendly and helpful manner. Under general supervision, following established standards and practices, collect blood from patients; instruct patients in the procedure for the collection of specimens Match laboratory requisition forms to specimen tubes. Dispose of contaminated sharps, in accordance with applicable laws, standards, and policies. Draw blood from veins by vacuum tube, syringe, or butterfly venipuncture methods. Dispose of blood or other biohazard fluids or tissue, in accordance with applicable laws, standards, or policies. Performs Rapid HIV testing as needed, including pre and post-test counseling as required. Performs venipuncture and lab processing including completing requisitions and preparation for lab delivery. Perform basic laboratory tests (HIV Rapid tests, finger-stick glucose, hemoglobin, urine pregnancy test, rapid strep, u/a, GC/Chlamydia, etc). Reviews all lab work before it is sent out to labs, organizing and maintaining existing lab forms, scanning and data entry of lab results, etc. Draw blood from capillaries by dermal puncture, such as heel or finger stick methods. Organize or clean blood-drawing trays, ensuring that all instruments are sterile and all needles, syringes, or related items are of first-time use Collect fluid or tissue samples, using appropriate collection procedures. Communicates inventory and equipment needs to assigned staff. Expected Salary: $17 - $19/hr Preferred: Prefer Certified Medical Assistant and two years serving HIV/AIDS population and experience drawing blood from infants and children Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Background Check is Required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and contact information for 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work Visa sponsorship is not available for this position Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541718/dental-hygienist-ii": {
      "title": "Dental Hygienist II",
      "description": "Dental Hygienist II Apply now Job no: 541718 Work type: Staff Part-Time Location: Nassau Categories: Allied Health, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry Department: 34010705 - DN-WILDLIGHT DENTAL PRACTICE Classification Title: Dental Hygienist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed as a dental hygienist in accordance with Chapter 466, Florida Statutes, Basic Life Support (BLS) certification and two years clinical work experience as a licensed dental hygienist. Job Description: The College of Dentistry is recruiting for a Dental Hygienist II at the Wildlight Dental Clinic in Yulee, Florida. This position will work under the direction of dental faculty members in oral hygiene instruction, dental cleanings, and related treatments; and administer local anesthesia (as necessary). Perform oral prophylaxis, periodontal maintenance procedures, scaling, and root planning procedures for patients of the Wildlight Dental Clinic, as permitted by Florida law. Instruct, evaluate, and supervise patients in oral hygiene education and preventive dental techniques. Perform all other related duties. Complete appropriate extra- and intra-oral exam, including probing and charting. Maintain current medical and dental history, blood pressure, radiographs, progress notes, and charge entry. Keep dental faculty members informed of patient appointments and coordinate scheduling between doctors and patients. Support the patient recall system and scheduling, in cooperation with the front office staff members to assure timely care and scheduling of patients. Perform other administrative duties as necessary (i.e., copying, filing patient charts, etc.). Supervise sterilization of dental instruments or sterilize them (when necessary). Maintain clinic and operatory equipment in accordance with Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) guidelines. Order and stock hygiene supplies. Expose, develop, and mount standardized and other special radiographs. Participate in in-service training and lectures. Maintain current cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) certification and Registered Dental Hygienist (RDH) licensure. Perform other duties (as necessary). Attend meetings (as required). Work in other College of Dentistry (CoD) departments (when needed) Expected Salary: $40.00 - $45.00 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: Licensed as a dental hygienist in accordance with Chapter 466, Florida Statutes, Basic Life Support (BLS) certification and two years clinical work experience as a licensed dental hygienist. Preferred: Exceptional interpersonal skills. Good organizational skills. Ability to communicate orally, by phone, and in writing with faculty members, staff members, and patients. Ability to follow verbal and written instructions. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to prioritize and work independently. Exceptional compassion. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and a list of three references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Open to current UF employees only. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541719/directorsenior-director-of-development-college-of-liberal-arts-sciences": {
      "title": "Director/Senior Director of Development, College of Liberal Arts & Sciences",
      "description": "Director/Senior Director of Development, College of Liberal Arts & Sciences Apply now Job no: 541719 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management Department: 16010500 - LS-COLLEGE DEVELOPMENT Classification Title: SR DIR, Dev Classification Minimum Requirements: Director: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Senior Director: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. Job Description: The Senior Director of Development or Director of Development is responsible for a broad range of fundraising activities, but is primarily responsible for planning, organizing, and directing programs to identify, cultivate, and secure major gifts for the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences. Reporting to the Executive Director of Advancement, this position will assist in developing and executing plans and policies for the general administration of the College’s development program. Additionally, the incumbent will develop and execute the unit’s fundraising program to fulfill the financial needs as identified by the Executive Director and in alignment with the strategic initiatives of UF Advancement (UFA). Candidates located in metropolitan cities across Florida may be considered. Fundraising & Portfolio Management Will independently perform identification, cultivation, solicitation and stewardship of major gift prospects; focuses on individual prospects where gift/grant is assessed at $100,000 and above. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring major gift solicitations to closure. Administer strategic plans for major gift/leadership/principal gift prospects. Identify, cultivate, solicit and steward prospects that align with the needs, priorities, and research of CLAS. Proactively engage CLAS deans, directors, faculty, and development officers to identify needs and priorities. Research and make independent judgments about which prospects are to be the primary targets for potential cultivation and solicitation, and devise strategies for each foundation prospect. Provide leadership and support to CLAS development officers to derive strategies for increasing support, determining the size and appropriateness of gift requests. Work with the faculty and department leadership and lead the solicitation effort when appropriate. Proactively engage CLAS deans, directors, faculty, and development officers to identify needs and priorities. Leadership & Strategic Initiatives In partnership with Executive Director, track and report campaign progress in team meetings and as needed for College and UFA leadership. Participate in recruiting experienced leadership for various boards and/or committees to ensure that these groups are representative of, and responsive to, the communities served. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty, and administration. Work with Executive Director to build, manage and lead a strong team of development professionals. Identify and recruit top talent and provide leadership, counsel on matters relating to fundraising, and accountability including establishing goals in alignment with unit objectives, developing metrics and strategic planning. In addition, incumbent will attend senior development meetings. Strategy & Planning Provide support for several departmental developmental development/advisory committees. Develop annual and individual strategic plan including goals and objectives. Work with individual departments Chairs or Center Directors on alumni activities and raise the level of support directed to the different units of the college. Work closely with Executive Director and CLAS Leadership to develop strategy for the college’s next fundraising campaign. Provide support to the Executive Director of Advancement for staffing the Dean’s Leadership Council. Special Projects/Initiatives Responsible for fundraising for special projects. Effectively assist with maintaining files and records, planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, and administration. Expected Salary: Annual salary commensurate with education and experience. The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package including: -Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance -Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance -Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future -Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave -Professional and Personal Growth -Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program -PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Director: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Senior Director: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. Valid driver license Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Three to five years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual and corporate prospects with campaign experience. Prior fundraising experience within a liberal arts college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and as a team player, competency in problem-solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this opening, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Application materials must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work sponsorship is not available for this position. This position is open to current UF employees only. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541720/federal-work-study-student-assistant-receptionist": {
      "title": "Federal Work Study - Student Assistant (Receptionist)",
      "description": "Federal Work Study - Student Assistant (Receptionist) Apply now Job no: 541720 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 60210000 - AG-SOIL AND WATER SCIENCE Classification Title: STU AST – Clerical & Admin Classification Minimum Requirements: The Department of Soil, Water, and Ecosystem Sciences (SWES) is seeking one (1) Federal Work Study students who are eligible to work beginning Spring 2027. These positions will provide clerical and front desk support to the department’s main administrative office in McCarty Hall A. Applicants must be able to commit to work 10-20 hours per week and be dependable, detail-orientated, and exhibit professionalism. Job Description: PLEASE NOTE: Soil, Water, and Ecosystem Sciences Majors/Minors and IS:EMANR majors are not eligible for these positions. Acts as SWES receptionist, answers all incoming calls, transferring and taking messages as needed. Handles incoming and outgoing mail and package deliveries. Manages department mailboxes. Makes copies, scans, and sends faxes, digitizes documents as needed. Maintains reservation calendars for two (2) departmental conference rooms. Issuing parking permits, tracking usage and returns. Straightens and maintains front office and common areas throughout the day. Monitors department office supplies. Provides general information and guidance to students, faculty, staff, guests, and visitors. Helps create promotional materials. Assists with other front office/clerical duties as assigned by Office Manager and Administrative Support Assistant. Work Schedule: Applicants are preferred to work 15-20 hours per week. Our operating hours are from 8:30am-5:00pm. Shifts are scheduled around course schedules. Expected Salary: $15 per hour; commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: Must receive or be eligible to receive Federal Work Study Must be in good academic and conduct standing with the University of Florida Willingness to make a commitment of 10 hours a week, for at least 2 consecutive semesters. Preferred: Computer skills; Proficient in use of MS Word, Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint Strong verbal and written communication skills Experience with MyUFL modules. Successful completion of the required FERPA training. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, please upload a copy of your resume, three (3) references, and your Spring 2027 schedule of classes of list of available schedule to work. * Students seeking FWS employment should print/screenshot a copy of their Financial Aid Award Page, which contains the Federal Work Study award as proof of FWS eligibility. To locate your Financial Aid Award Page, go to https://one.uf.edu/financialaid/aidyear Preference will be given to applications submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 11 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541721/stuastclerical-admin": {
      "title": "STU-AST-CLERICAL & ADMIN",
      "description": "STU-AST-CLERICAL & ADMIN Apply now Job no: 541721 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 17030100 - BA-FSOA-BUSINESS OFFICE Classification Title: STU-AST-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: Must be a currently enrolled student (non-business major) at the University of Florida, with at least 1 full academic year prior to graduation. 3.5 minimum UF GPA. Availability to work up to 20 hours a week during business hours (Monday–Friday, 8 AM–4:30 PM). Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to communicate effectively. Reliable, punctual, and able to work both independently and as a team. Proficiency in basic computer applications (i.e., Microsoft Outlook, Excel, etc.). Job Description: The Fisher School of Accounting is seeking to hire a UF student to serve as a part-time receptionist for the office’s front office. As a receptionist, you will be the first point of contact for many visitors to the college, assist in scheduling advising appointments, and assist FSOA staff and faculty via additional duties as needed. Key Responsibilities: Greet and assist visitors in a friendly and professional manner. Manage incoming phone calls, take messages, and direct inquiries to the appropriate staff members as needed. Manage calendars for the FSOA Academic Advising team for scheduling student appointments for in person and online. Maintain cleanliness and organization of the front desk area. Assist FSOA staff and faculty to provide additional support as necessary. Expected Salary: $15.00 Per hour Required Qualifications: Must be a currently enrolled student (non-business major) at the University of Florida, with at least 1 full academic year prior to graduation. 3.5 minimum UF GPA. Availability to work up to 20 hours a week during business hours (Monday–Friday, 8 AM–4:30 PM). Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to communicate effectively. Reliable, punctual, and able to work both independently and as a team. Proficiency in basic computer applications (i.e., Microsoft Outlook, Excel, etc.). Preferred: Previous experience with filing, typing, and answering phones. Individuals who are highly organized and have a high attention to detail that can effectively communicate to a wide variety of parties and backgrounds. Individuals with flexible schedules who may be willing to work when class is not in session, but the University is open (between semesters, Spring Break, etc.). Individuals who plan to stay in Gainesville over the summer semester. Special Instructions to Applicants: Upload cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541723/uf-health-regional-physician-primary-care-physician-uf-health-central-florida-lake-and-marion-counties": {
      "title": "UF Health Regional Physician (Primary Care Physician) - UF Health Central Florida (Lake and Marion Counties)",
      "description": "UF Health Regional Physician (Primary Care Physician) - UF Health Central Florida (Lake and Marion Counties) Apply now Job no: 541723 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Lake Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29760900 - MD-RPN CENTRAL FLORIDA HEALTH Classification Title: UFHealth Physician (Primary Care Physician) Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes* plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Job Description: UF Health Central Florida, formerly known as Central Florida Health and acquired by the University of Florida Health in January 2020, is an award-winning, locally owned and governed not-for-profit healthcare system and the largest, most comprehensive provider of healthcare services in the region. We care for patients in Lake, Sumter, and Marion counties through inpatient acute hospital services at UF Health The Villages Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital, inpatient rehabilitation services at UF Health The Villages Rehabilitation Hospital, adult inpatient psychiatric services at the UF Health Leesburg Hospital Senior Behavioral Health Center and diagnostic laboratory services at several locations. As a premier healthcare provider, UF Health Central Florida takes pride in providing progressive, innovative technology, along with building strong relationships with patients, families, physicians, and residents of the communities we serve. UF Health Central Florida is currently seeking multiple full-time Primary Care Physicians. These physicians will provide and manage direct patient care, including physical examinations, evaluations, assessments, diagnoses, and treatment for a specified patient population. This Physician provides a full scope of clinical patient care and services that fall under the field of training including but not limited to diagnosis, treatment, coordination of care, preventative care, and health maintenance to patients at UF Health Regional Network clinical sites. Duties will include but are not limited to: Working collaboratively with members of the healthcare team including pharmacists, nurse practitioners, and nursing staff Reviews incoming reports (e.g. lab, x-ray, EKG) and follow up in a timely manner Prescribing pharmaceuticals, other medications, and treatment regimens as appropriate to assessed medical conditions Refer patients to specialists and to relevant patient care components as appropriate May manage the daily operations of a specific medical program or patient care unit Directing and coordinating the patient care activities of nursing and support staff as required Following established departmental policies, procedures, and objectives, continuous quality improvement objectives, and safety, environmental, and/or infection control standards As appropriate to the position, participating in specified health promotion, education and/or prevention programs Participating in peer review, quality assurance, provider meetings, and other clinical and operational meetings as requested Assisting in updating protocols and principles of practice, as requested Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education. Required Qualifications: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes* plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Preferred: Specialty License or Certification; Board Certified or Board Eligible in Specified Area of Medical Specialty; Certification/Licensure in the state of Florida and Federal DEA Certification. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until a sufficient applicant pool has been established. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540497/certified-medical-assistant": {
      "title": "Certified Medical Assistant",
      "description": "Certified Medical Assistant Apply now Job no: 540497 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29130200 - MD-RADIOLOGY-ANGIO-INTERVENT Classification Title: Certified Medical Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements Successful completion of the Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) exam offered by an appropriate accredited institute. Appropriate institutes include the American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA), the National Center for Competency Testing (NCCT), the American Medical Technologists (AMT), the National Healthcareer Association (NHA), and the American Registry of Medical Assistants (ARMA). Job Description: Patient Communication and Care Coordination - Answers and triages telephone calls from referring providers, patients, and caregivers using established clinical protocols. Provides patients with instructions regarding procedures, treatments, medications, and follow-up care. Coordinates communication between patients, providers, and clinical staff to facilitate continuity of care. Clinical Support and Patient Care Activities - Assists physicians, advanced practice providers, and residents with outpatient clinic procedures and patient examinations. Administers injections and performs approved clinical support activities, including specimen collection and dressing application, in accordance with established policies and protocols. Prepares treatment and examination rooms and maintains clinical supplies and equipment necessary for patient care activities within the Vascular and Interventional Radiology & VIR clinic. Medical Documentation and Electronic Health Record Management - Enters and processes orders within the electronic medical record system. Maintains accurate and timely patient documentation in accordance with departmental procedures, institutional standards, regulatory requirements, and applicable policies. Imaging and Procedure Coordination - Coordinates intake, scheduling, and follow-up imaging appointments for Y-90 patients. Assists with arranging inpatient admissions, coordinating procedural logistics, and ensuring required documentation and imaging are completed prior to treatment. Professional Development and Compliance - Participates in continuing education and training activities to maintain competency and remain current within area{s) of responsibility. Completes all required certifications, annual trainings, and compliance requirements in a timely manner. Administrative and Team Collaboration - Attends departmental meetings and participates in regular meetings with supervisor to exchange information, discuss workflow, and receive guidance and direction regarding clinic operations and patient care activities. Operational Support and Workflow Coordination - Collaborates with faculty, residents, and clinic staff to facilitate efficient patient flow and timely delivery of interventional Radiology services. Assists in coordinating clinic workload and communicates pertinent patient information to healthcare providers to support quality patient care. Expected Salary: $16.76 - $20.42, commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Successful completion of the Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) exam offered by an appropriate accredited institute. Appropriate institutes include the American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA), the National Center for Competency Testing (NCCT), the American Medical Technologists (AMT), the National Healthcareer Association (NHA), and the American Registry of Medical Assistants (ARMA). Preferred Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) certification through an approved accrediting organization. Previous experience working in an outpatient clinical setting, preferably within Interventional Radiology, Radiology, Surgery, Oncology, or another procedural specialty. Knowledge of medical terminology, clinical documentation, and electronic medical record systems. Experience coordinating patient scheduling, referrals, imaging, and follow-up care in a fast-paced healthcare environment. Ability to effectively communicate with patients, physicians, advanced practice providers, residents, and multidisciplinary healthcare teams. Experience assisting with minor procedures, specimen collection, injections, and patient preparation activities. Demonstrated ability to prioritize multiple responsibilities while maintaining accuracy and attention to detail. Strong organizational, customer service, and problem-solving skills. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive patient information in accordance with HIPAA and institutional policies. Experience working in an academic healthcare environment is preferred. Knowledge of regulatory and compliance standards applicable to ambulatory clinical care settings. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications and electronic scheduling systems. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. The application period for this position has been extended. Please note that this recruitment is specifically limited to candidates who hold a current Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) credential. A Registered Medical Assistant (RMA) credential does not meet the requirements for this position. Applicants whose only medical assistant credential is an RMA will not be considered and are encouraged not to apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540660/clinical-coder-ii": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder II",
      "description": "Clinical Coder II Apply now Job no: 540660 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 34080103 - DN-ORAL SURGERY RESIDENT Classification Title: Clinical Coder II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Job Description: The Department of Oral & Maxillofacial Surgery is recruiting for a Clinical Coder II. This position is responsible for charge entry for both Medical and Dental procedures, CPT/ICD-10/HCPCS/ADA coding, and queuing insurance claims for services rendered. Duties Include: Extract billable services from the patient charts, apply appropriate Current Procedural Terminology (CPT), International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10) codes, Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System (HCPCS) and American Dental Association Dental Procedure Codes and enter these services in AxiUm for submission. Work closely with faculty, fellows and the Residents in the respective Divisions to provide education on correct coding, proper documentation to maximize physician reimbursement, and ensure compliance with federal and state regulations. Inform financial counselors and faculty of important changes in coding and reimbursement. Schedules and facilitates education sessions to providers in regards to documentation, reimbursement, and compliance. Develops and maintains documentation standards for coding and documentation training to communicate to the providers individually and/or in groups. Provides feedback to provider regarding billing and coding and works with provider to resolve these issues to ensure accurate coding. Works closely with treating providers to research problems and maximize revenues and preventing errors. Researches coding issues through all available resources, including but not limited to professional associations, federal and private payer guidelines, and coding networks- Attend continuing education courses, seminars, conferences, and meetings Responsible for filling in during an OMFS financial office staffing shortage for financial counseling of patients and other assigned responsibilities as deemed necessary. Expected Salary: $22.12 - $25.00 per hour Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred: Knowledge of various rules, regulations, and procedures for billing private insurance companies, Medicare, Medicaid for patient treatment. Experience with insurance billing. Ability to deal with the public in a tactful and courteous manner. Aptitude for effective verbal and written communication. Ability to plan, organize and coordinate work assignments. Capacity for understanding and applying applicable rules, regulations and policies and procedures. Ability to work independently and to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See the Qualified Positions link on our applicant FAQs page for a list of eligible positions. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541243/gatorone-guest-service-associate": {
      "title": "GATORONE Guest Service Associate",
      "description": "GATORONE Guest Service Associate Apply now Job no: 541243 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 68010000 - BS-ID CARD SERVICES Classification Title: GATOR ONE Guest Service Associate Minimum Requirements Flexibility to work at least 2 hours in the mornings (between 8am-12pm) on Tuesdays and Thursdays is preferred. Valid Florida Driver License. Job Description: Guest service responsibilities include: Answer general questions about GATOR ONE use, account balances, and transaction activity. Assist at University functions and events (i.e. Preview Days, Move-In, Tabling, etc.). Perform general office duties such as filing, photocopying, preparing mailings, making on-campus deliveries walking or in State Vehicle, posting flyers, or assisting technical staff. Serve as a positive host by welcoming guests approaching or passing by the GATOR ONE office. Provide accurate information as requested by customers at the desk or over the phone. Research and have knowledge of a variety of sources of University and community information. ID production responsibilities include: Create and/or replace GATOR ONE cards, ID badges/Overlays, and Novelty Cards while verifying ID’s through ID systems. Mobile ID Support Assist students, faculty, and staff with provisioning mobile IDs (e.g., adding credentials to digital wallets), troubleshooting activation issues, and ensuring successful access across supported devices. Provide front-line support for mobile credential issues, including device compatibility, NFC access problems, and lost/stolen/new devices while delivering a high level of customer service. Other responsibilities include: Report notable activities, problems, questions, etc. to the appropriate individuals. Perform end-of-day audits when needed. Process credit card payments. Expected Salary: $14.00 hourly. Required Qualifications: Flexibility to work at least 2 hours in the mornings (between 8am-12pm) on Tuesdays and Thursdays is preferred. Valid Florida Driver License. Preferred: Excellent customer service skills, a positive attitude, professional demeanor, and problem-solving/conflict resolution skills. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Reliable, responsible, professional in appearance and able to work under pressure. Ability to organize and prioritize with a high attention to detail and confidentiality. Ability to maintain accuracy and efficiency in a fast-paced environment. Proficient in data entry, typing skills, and Microsoft Office products with advanced knowledge of Microsoft Excel. The ability to work independently, identify resources and make independent decisions. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Multiple vacancies will be filled from this posting. Position is being reposted, previous applicants still under consideration. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541567/assistant-scientist-instrument-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Scientist Instrument Science",
      "description": "Assistant Scientist Instrument Science Apply now Job no: 541567 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Scientist Instrument Science Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must hold a Ph.D. degree (or equivalent) in physics or a closely related field. Relevant postdoctoral research experience is required. Demonstrated potential for excellence in mentoring a diverse undergraduate and graduate student body, while establishing a well-recognized research program, is essential. Job Description: The Department of Physics at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time, 12-month Assistant Scientist position specializing in precision optical measurements, beginning January 1, 2027. We seek highly qualified candidates from all areas of precision optical measurements. The successful candidate should have demonstrated extraordinary experience in developing, implementing and/or applying optical systems with picometer accuracy. The University of Florida has been part of the LIGO and LISA experiments for almost 30 years. During that time, we have focused on instrument science and currently have projects measuring the length stability of prototype telescopes for space missions as well as the development of a 2-micron laser. The successful candidate will carry out research on precision measurements of optical instruments to be used in gravitational-wave detectors such as the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna (LISA) and the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory (LIGO). The work includes the design of optical systems capable of measuring the stability of telescopes to 1 part in a trillion (picometer stability), the design of optical benches to match properly laser beams to ultra-stable cavities. It also includes studies of single-mode non-planar ring oscillator (NPRO) lasers operating near 2-micron wavelengths for use in cryogenically cooled laser interferometers employing silicon optics. Relevant tasks include measuring the tunability of these lasers, designing mode-matching optics to optical cavities, studying methods for phase modulation at 2-micron wavelengths, and demonstrating locking of these lasers to the cavities. Salary & Benefits The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: A cover letter A curriculum vitae with full publication list A research statement Three letters of recommendation Reference providers will be notified of their upload link only after a completed application has been submitted. Early submission is strongly encouraged. To ensure full consideration, applications must be submitted by November 19, 2026. For more information, please contact Prof. David Tanner ( tanner@phys.ufl.edu ). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 20, 2026 and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541674/student-assistant": {
      "title": "Student Assistant",
      "description": "Student Assistant Apply now Job no: 541674 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 16620000 - LS-AFRICAN AMERICAN STUDIES Classification Title: Federal Work Study - Student Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: Current UF student and eligible for Federal Work Study. Job Description: Duties and Responsibilities include: Provide support for the Administrative Specialist and faculty in the program. Providing front desk coverage, greeting visitors, and directing inquiries. Assisting with the creation and distribution of the department newsletter. Managing and updating the department’s social media pages. Providing general administrative support, including organizing files and handling correspondence. Assisting with special projects as assigned. Triaging and directing questions to the appropriate faculty or staff members Expected Salary: $15.00 Required Qualifications: This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through Federal Work-Study. To be considered, you must upload your resume and a copy of your class schedule and Financial Aid Award Page. To locate your Financial Aid Award Page, go to https://one.uf.edu/financialaid/aidyear . Must maintain a 2.0-grade point average. Students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Preferred: Proficient with Microsoft Word and Excel Excellent communication skills Strong attention to detail Excellent organizational skills Positive, friendly and professional attitude Reliable, good follow through Special Instructions to Applicants: To apply, please include the following in your application: Class schedule Federal Work Study Award Resume Students seeking FWS jobs should print/screenshot a copy of their Financial Aid Award Page, which contains the Federal Work Study award as proof of the FWS eligibility. To locate your Financial Aid Award Page, go to https://one.ud.edu/financialaid/aidyear . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through Federal Work-Study (FWS) and must maintain a 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541683/transportation-resource-coordinator": {
      "title": "Transportation Resource Coordinator",
      "description": "Transportation Resource Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541683 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Allied Health, Transportation Services Department: 33030000 - HP-OCCUPATIONAL THERAPY Classification Title: OPS-Sponsored Projects Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: High School Diploma or equivalent. Job Description: Consumer Support: Operates the Find-A-Ride hotline, guiding callers through transportation research and resolution. Conducts comprehensive web-browser investigations to locate transportation options, service providers, and related resources for callers. Records caller interactions and identifies emerging trends or issues for the team. Provides timely information to callers and directs unresolved inquiries to appropriate program staff or service providers. Outreach and Promotion: Represents the Find-A-Ride program at professional conferences, community outreach events, and consumer-focused activities throughout Florida. Share program information through presentations demonstrations, and informational materials to increase public awareness and encourage use of the Find-A-Ride website. Identifies opportunities to exhibit the find-a-ride program. Collaborates with the program staff to coordinate outreach activities and works with the fiscal team to arrange travel logistics. Team Collaboration: Participates in program meetings, including Safe Mobility for Life coalition sessions, and contributes actionable insights. Provides regular progress updates to the Program Coordinator and Principal Investigator regarding consumer inquiries, outreach activities, and database maintenance. Identifies transportation barriers, service gaps, and customer concerns and inform the team to create actionable plans. Data Management and Quality Assurance: Conducts annual reviews of transportation providers to verify the accuracy and completeness of information maintained in the database. Updates provider records, including contact information, service areas, eligibility requirements, and available transportation services. Cross checks provider data to identify outdated records and missing information. Ensures all data collection meets program standards. Administrative Support: Maintains professional and timely communication with customers, transportation providers, program staff, and community partners through telephone and email correspondence. Maintains organized records of customer inquiries, outreach activities, and program communications. Prepares concise summaries of caller interactions, referrals, and resolution statuses to support program tracking and reporting. Expected Salary: $22.00 - $28.00 per hour; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: An associate degree or higher and at least one year of experience in customer service, telephone-based support, survey administration, or a related field. A valid driver's license is required. Preferred: Bachelor or higher degree Proficiency in Microsoft Office (Word, Excel, Outlook) Demonstrated ability to enter structured data quickly and accurately Excellent written and verbal communication skills Detail-oriented with strong organizational skills Ability to provide effective customer service. Experience with web browsers and investigation of resources Proven skills in drafting clear, concise summary notes Flexible schedule, including willingness to travel for events Ability to collaborate effectively within a team environment Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload a cover letter, resume, and a list of professional references. This is a part-time position at 20 hours per week. Hours may be flexible within the week but are expected to happen between normal business hours of M-F, 8am-5pm. Occasional overnight travel may be required. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. of the posting end date. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541691/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541691 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29150000 - MD-OPHTHALMOLOGY Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in Microbiology, Molecular Biology, Pharmacology, Neuroscience, Metabolism, Biomedical Sciences, or related field. Training in molecular biology, vision research, and genetic models of retinal diseases is desirable. Job Description: Responsible for all animal studies with rodent models, in vivo function evaluations, and tissue collections for biochemical, immunological, histological analyses. The work also requires extensive data analysis with molecular, cellular and biochemical assays on cultured cells and analysis of retinal samples. Demonstrated experience with rodent studies, oral gavage procedures, anaerobic bacterial culture, and qPCR. Experience with engineered probiotics, microbial metabolism of tryptophan, diabetic mouse models, and retinal biology is strongly preferred. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Ideal candidates are dynamic and self-motivated individuals that can take ownership of the projects. Excellent written and verbal communication skills are necessary. Candidates should have a strong publication record, ability to independently design experiments, analyze complex datasets, and contribute to manuscript and grant preparation. Preferred: Candidates with prior publications in one or more of the following areas would be highly desirable: Gut microbiome and metabolomics Host-microbe interactions, and/or engineered probiotic therapeutics Tryptophan metabolism Diabetes and diabetic complications Diabetic retinopathy Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload: Cover letter describing interest in the position Research statement detailing relevant experience, goals, and a list of publications. Curriculum Vitae. Three professional references. Letters should be addressed to Qiuhong Li, PhD, Associate Professor of Ophthalmology. If letters are not available, please provide a reference list. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. A health assessment for animal contact will be required. Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541692/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541692 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29150000 - MD-OPHTHALMOLOGY Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in Pharmacology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Biomedical Engineering, Microbiology, Molecular Biology, Immunology, or related field. Job Description: The successful candidate will lead preclinical development of genetically engineered probiotic therapeutics for treatment of diabetes and metabolic disorders. Responsibilities include design and execution of pharmacokinetic, bioavailability, toxicology, dose-response, and efficacy studies in rodent models; engineering and characterization of probiotic strains; bioanalytical assay development; data analysis; and preparation of manuscripts, grant applications, and regulatory documentation. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Ideal candidates are dynamic and self-motivated individuals that can take ownership of the projects. Excellent written and verbal communication skills are necessary. Candidates should have expertise in in vivo pharmacology, diabetes mouse models, PK/PD analysis, microbiome therapeutics, and peptide biologics. Preferred: 2–5 years of hands-on experience with rodent models of diabetes and metabolic disease. Strong publication record in metabolic disease, microbiome therapeutics, or drug delivery. Experience in interdisciplinary collaboration spanning microbiology, pharmacology, toxicology, and bioengineering. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload: Cover letter describing interest in the position Research statement detailing relevant experience, goals, and a list of publications. Curriculum Vitae. Three professional references. Letters should be addressed to Qiuhong Li, PhD, Associate Professor of Ophthalmology. If letters are not available, please provide a reference list. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. A health assessment for animal contact will be required. Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541693/biological-scientist-i": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist I",
      "description": "Biological Scientist I Apply now Job no: 541693 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 32060000 - PH-PHARMTHERAPY TRNSL RSCH Classification Title: Biological Scientist I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Job Description: The Biological Scientist I position will be responsible for clinical sample receiving and processing and shipping clinical samples to other research institutes and Genomic Centers. Additionally, this position will be responsible for DNA and RNA isolation, DNA and RNA quantitation and plating, genotyping DNA samples using the OpenArray protocol TaqMan, and Pyrosequencing, Gene Expression Analysis, isolating clinical samples for measurement on LC-MS for PK studies, PGx core laboratory safety. This position will also manage the labs, occasionally training students/trainees in various laboratory techniques as well as purchasing lab supplies as requested by staff/faculties. Clinical Coordination: Interacts and coordinate/provide scientific services for clients in a laboratory or research area. Participates in clinical research studies, manages and communicates with clinical sites and collection of data and lab work. Lab management: Organizes and manages labs for safety, manages lab inventory, coordinates equipment repair, places purchase orders, manages lab invoices, receives and logs in clinical samples. Sample processing: Processes and aliquots clinical samples for storage, conducts DNA and RNA extraction from different clinical samples, genotypes DNA samples using different genotyping platforms. Data Analysis: Preparing materials, methods and results with graphs and figures/tables for data results for publications. Reviews assays and protocols with lab director for accuracy, adequacy, or adherence to policies, regulations, or scientific standards. Other duties as assigned Expected Salary: $23.95 - 26.34 /hr Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF Faculty and Staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, maternity/paternity leave, and leave payouts. To learn more visit: hr.ufl.edu/benefits Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Preferred: -Experience in molecular biology laboratory skills -Experience in genomic sample preparation and DNA extraction -Scientific knowledge in molecular biology -Good computer skills -Good people skills Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and list of three professional references. As a part of our review for research and research support positions, we look for a full CV which includes all professional appointments/engagements, all post-secondary education, and all publications from post-secondary education and all respective dates. Please be sure to not use acronyms in your CV. This CV should include names of entities associated with any projects. This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541694/clinical-financial-counselor": {
      "title": "Clinical Financial Counselor",
      "description": "Clinical Financial Counselor Apply now Job no: 541694 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 34010703 - DN-FACULTY PRACTICE Classification Title: Clinical Financial Counselor Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of related experience and/or training, including customer service; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Determine insurance coverage and financial status of patients for the Restorative Dental Sciences (RDS) Faculty Practice (FP), according to procedures established by the College of Dentistry (COD) and the University of Florida (UF). Work with patients of RDS FP Care Center both at time of service and through accounts receivable to collect payment. Counsel patients regarding benefits of treatment plans, costs, and financing options. Process and follow through on third-party payer or agency claims. Maintain current dental terminology (CDT), and third party payer contract requirements. Responsible for collecting all balances due through patient contact in person, by telephone, by correspondence, or by working with an outside collection agency. Produce accounts receivable and other reports for faculty dentists. Perform receptionist and clerical duties related to the practice as needed. Facilitate and coordinate veterans appointments and work with VA and Community Care to gain treatment approvals- RFS and follow up on authorizations consistently to assure approvals are current. Request RFS as needed from VA. Interview and counsel all patients in the Restorative Dental Sciences, Faculty Practice Care Center to determine their financial status and/or insurance coverage and advise them of available payment options. Review insurance policies to determine deductible, co-payment, and extent of coverage. Prepare estimates of dental fees to discuss costs with patients and negotiate payment and collections with insurance companies. Collect balances on patient accounts and ensure accuracy of debits and credits for patient balances. Assist patients in establishing payment method for proposed treatment before treatment begins (i.e., cash payments, contracts, loans from financial institution, credit card payments, Lending Club, etc.). Initiate and follow through on patients’ applications for commercial payment plans. Maintain contact with patients throughout treatment to assist with payment problems, verify that payments are timely made, and to intervene on the patient’s behalf with third-party payers. Process and follow through on any third party or agency claims. File forms, monitor payments, maintain records, and produce reports. Work with members of the COD Business Office to collect balances, process claims, and troubleshoot all reimbursement problems for Faculty Practice(FP) and follow up on insurance claims monthly. Maintain current knowledge of third-party payer contracts; agency rules and regulations; Current Dental Terminology. Responsible for all doctor provider schedules and meeting production goals. Provide doctors with planned appointment list every two weeks. Follow-up with unscheduled patients. Review and work each doctor’s accounts receivable report. Report the doctor’s spreadsheet of findings to individual doctor, department director and practice manager monthly. Review schedule daily and inform front desk of balances to collect or any other financial responsibilities for patients. Meet with new patients and existing patients to discuss treatment plans, approximate insurance coverage and financial responsibility and ensure we have an electronically signed treatment plan on file. Serve as Restorative Dental Sciences, FP point-of-contact (POC) for all reimbursement, insurance, and coding issues: Receive all telephone calls and walk-in inquiries concerning billing, reimbursement, dental insurance or other dental payment questions. Maintain up-to-date knowledge in these areas and provide clear and appropriate information to questioners. Maintain updated patient information and documentation in Axium. Actively maintain communication with faculty members to provide current information regarding their patients’ financial status, especially as it affects treatment-planning choices. Respond to requests from dental faculty members to resolve payment issues as they arise. Provide timely feedback to faculty members regarding efforts towards resolution of financial concerns. Monitor upcoming patient schedules to determine any balances due prior to appointment and arrange to meet patients when necessary. Routinely alert doctors of balances due and discuss collection options. Using accounts receivable reports for all practitioners in the RDS FP Patient Care Center; follow collection procedures, which include telephone contact of patients and/or guarantors to request payment of balances due; generating collection letters; documenting collection action; and reporting collections outcomes to practice administrator, doctors, practice director and RDS chair monthly. Determine which accounts should be sent to an outside collection agency and present cases to doctors for approval monthly. Submit accounts to Billing Office for write-off or provide instructions for further follow-up monthly. Print, review, and analyze FP aging report monthly, adding comments to patient accounts relative to the same monthly. Follow up on any dental preauthorization’s sent on behalf of patients monthly. As member of front office team, routinely answer main telephone lines and provide information to callers on dental treatment choices available in the college as a whole. Responsible for obtaining or confirming required credentialing of dentists with third-party payers. Assist doctors with submitting required forms and documentation. In absence of Office Manager, ensure the front desk is adequately covered, filling-in when necessary. Perform other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $22.00 - $23.00 per hour; commensurate with skills and experience Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of related experience and/or training, including customer service; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Knowledge of Medical and Dental Codes Knowledge of UF and CoD policies and procedures related to patient collection Knowledge of basic dental and medical insurance terminology Analytical problem-solving capabilities; ability to identify/analyze problems quickly and develop solutions that best meet the objectives of the department and clinics Ability to communicate verbally with individuals of varied educational, social, and economic backgrounds effectively Skilled in utilizing the following computer programs: Axium, Microsoft (MS) Power Point, MS Excel, and MS Word Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and list of three references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541701/postdoctoral-associate-in-transportation-systems-safety-and-human-factors": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate in Transportation Systems, Safety, and Human Factors",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate in Transportation Systems, Safety, and Human Factors Apply now Job no: 541701 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Engineering Department: 19220300 - EG-TECHNOLOGY TRAINING CENTER Classification Title: Postdoc Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate by the appointment start date in civil or transportation engineering, electrical or computer engineering, industrial or systems engineering, human factors, experimental psychology, computer science, or a closely related field appropriate to the assigned research. Demonstrated experience conducting quantitative research and analyzing experimental, observational, or engineering data. Proficiency in at least one analytical or programming environment, such as Python, R, MATLAB, or equivalent software. Demonstrated technical writing and communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team. Job Description: Conduct literature reviews and synthesize research, technical standards, and agency practices relevant to transportation systems, safety, and accessibility. Develop experimental designs, research protocols, performance measures, sampling approaches, and statistical analysis plans. Support the development and operation of controlled test environments and field evaluations involving transportation technologies, infrastructure, and user behavior. Develop and apply methods for instrumentation, data acquisition, event logging, video analysis, and measurement of system performance. Support human-factors and pedestrian research, including study procedures, IRB documentation, recruitment coordination, and participant activities conducted under approved protocols and appropriate specialist supervision. Analyze engineering and behavioral data using reproducible methods that address repeated observations, measurement uncertainty, missing data, and data quality. Prepare technical reports, peer-reviewed manuscripts, presentations, implementation guidance, and practitioner training materials. Coordinate research activities with collaborating faculty, staff, agency partners, and technical specialists. Mentor student and hourly research assistants and support consistent data-collection and quality-control practices. Participate in research mentoring and professional development, and contribute to related funding proposals and research initiatives. Expected Salary: $60,000 commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate by the appointment start date in civil or transportation engineering, electrical or computer engineering, industrial or systems engineering, human factors, experimental psychology, computer science, or a closely related field appropriate to the assigned research. Demonstrated experience conducting quantitative research and analyzing experimental, observational, or engineering data. Proficiency in at least one analytical or programming environment, such as Python, R, MATLAB, or equivalent software. Demonstrated technical writing and communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team Preferred: Experience in one or more of the following areas is preferred. Applicants are not expected to possess every qualification. Traffic signal operations, intelligent transportation systems, transportation safety, vehicle detection, or traffic simulation. Instrumentation, synchronized measurements, data acquisition, networked sensing, or camera-based system evaluation. Human factors, pedestrian behavior, accessibility, wayfinding, or evaluation of transportation infrastructure from a user perspective. Human-subject research, IRB processes, participant recruitment, and accessible research procedures. Experimental design, repeated-measures or mixed-effects analysis, sampling methods, and measurement uncertainty. Video processing, computer vision, automated event extraction, or behavioral observation. Controlled testbed development and field data collection. Preparation of research publications, agency reports, technical manuals, or implementation guidance. Collaboration with public agencies, industry partners, or accessibility practitioners. Mentoring research assistants and coordinating concurrent research activities. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants should submit a cover letter describing their research interests and relevant qualifications, a curriculum vitae, contact information for three professional references, and one representative publication or research writing sample. The anticipated start date is negotiable, subject to University hiring requirements and available funding. Periodic travel within Florida and participation in approved laboratory and field research activities are expected. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541703/student-assistant-mouse-colony-management-and-laboratory-support": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Mouse Colony Management and Laboratory Support",
      "description": "Student Assistant - Mouse Colony Management and Laboratory Support Apply now Job no: 541703 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 28100000 - VM-INFECT DISEASE & IMMUNOLOGY Classification Title: Student Assistant - Mouse Colony Management and Laboratory Support Classification Minimum Requirements: - Current University of Florida undergraduate student with eligibility to participate in the Federal Work-Study program. - Ability to work approximately 10–15 hours per week in accordance with the student's approved Work-Study eligibility and scheduling requirements. - Ability to follow written and verbal instructions, maintain accurate records, and perform assigned duties reliably. - Willingness to complete required laboratory safety, animal handling, occupational health, and research compliance training before performing applicable duties. - Ability to work responsibly in a research laboratory environment and comply with institutional animal welfare and safety procedures. Job Description: The Department of Infectious Diseases and Immunology at the University of Florida College of Veterinary Medicine is seeking an undergraduate research assistant to support the Bafor Laboratory of Reproductive Bio-Immunology and Immune Engineering (ReBIE). The selected student will assist with the daily management of research mouse colonies and provide general laboratory support. The position requires strong attention to detail, dependable attendance, accurate documentation, and a commitment to animal welfare and laboratory safety. Mouse Colony Management Assist with routine management and monitoring of research mouse colonies. Maintain accurate records of breeding pairs, birth dates, litter sizes, weaning dates, and animal identification. Coordinate breeding schedules, weaning activities, and colony inventory updates under supervision. Assist with animal identification, tissue collection for genotyping, and PCR-based genotyping following appropriate training and authorization. Monitor colony breeding performance and report reproductive outcomes or abnormalities to laboratory personnel. Maintain colony databases and track animal availability for planned research experiments. Observe animal health and welfare and promptly report concerns to designated personnel. Perform all animal-related activities in accordance with approved Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) protocols and University of Florida policies. Laboratory and Research Support Assist with preparing reagents, supplies, and materials for laboratory experiments. Support routine molecular biology and immunology procedures under appropriate supervision. Assist with sample labeling, processing, storage, and documentation. Maintain laboratory inventory and assist with organizing research supplies and equipment. Support general laboratory organization, cleanliness, and other assigned research activities. The position is expected to require approximately 10–15 hours per week, with scheduling coordinated around academic responsibilities and laboratory needs. Occasional weekend or holiday availability may be necessary for animal-related responsibilities, as communicated by the supervisor. The position involves working in a biomedical research laboratory with research animals. All animal handling and research procedures will be performed only after completion of required training and authorization and under the supervision of qualified laboratory personnel. Expected Salary: $15.00 / hour Required Qualifications: - Current University of Florida undergraduate student with eligibility to participate in the Federal Work-Study program. - Ability to work approximately 10–15 hours per week in accordance with the student's approved Work-Study eligibility and scheduling requirements. - Ability to follow written and verbal instructions, maintain accurate records, and perform assigned duties reliably. - Willingness to complete required laboratory safety, animal handling, occupational health, and research compliance training before performing applicable duties. - Ability to work responsibly in a research laboratory environment and comply with institutional animal welfare and safety procedures. Preferred: - Interest in biomedical research, reproductive biology, immunology, veterinary medicine, or a related scientific field. - Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, reliability, and effective recordkeeping practices. - Strong communication skills and the ability to work cooperatively with faculty, graduate students, and laboratory personnel. - Previous laboratory experience, animal handling experience, or familiarity with research mouse colonies is beneficial but not required. - Familiarity with basic laboratory procedures, research documentation, or Microsoft Excel is beneficial. - Interest in developing technical skills related to animal colony management and laboratory research. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and proof of federal work study eligibility. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541704/univ-registrar-svcs-crd-iii-scheduling": {
      "title": "Univ Registrar Svcs CRD III (Scheduling)",
      "description": "Univ Registrar Svcs CRD III (Scheduling) Apply now Job no: 541704 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Student Services Department: 03030000 - EM-OFFICE OF REGISTRAR Classification Title: Univ Registrar Svcs CRD III. Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida is seeking a Registrar Services Coordinator III to support the Office of the University Registrar within the Division of Enrollment Management. This position plays a key role in coordinating academic scheduling, curriculum administration, and classroom utilization while supporting the development and maintenance of the Schedule of Courses. The successful candidate will supervise staff, resolve complex scheduling challenges, and collaborate with university departments to ensure efficient operations, accurate records, and high-quality service. Responsibilities will include, but are not limited to: Academic Scheduling and Curriculum Management Help drive the success of university scheduling operations by coordinating classroom assignments, course schedules, and instructional space utilization. Build strong working relationships with academic departments to resolve scheduling concerns and support university priorities. Oversee the accuracy of curriculum records supporting more than 8,800 approved courses. Coordinate assembly examinations, manage scheduling conflicts, and assist with complex course modifications. Use scheduling data and classroom utilization trends to support effective planning, accurate records, and informed academic scheduling decisions. Staff Supervision and Leadership Make a meaningful contribution as a supervisor by guiding two full-time employees and federal work-study students. Foster a productive team environment through training, coaching, workload management, and performance feedback. Serve as a reliable resource for resolving complex academic records inquiries and operational concerns. Training and Professional Development Put your knowledge to work by developing and delivering training for scheduling coordinators and registrar staff. Help colleagues strengthen their understanding of curriculum management, scheduling policies, and classroom utilization. Operational Improvement and Departmental Support Identify and recommend practical improvements that make daily operations more efficient and accurate. Collaborate on departmental projects, reporting, and operational initiatives. Provide additional support across registrar functions during peak periods. Performs other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $52,000 In addition, UF offers the following exceptional benefits: Health, Dental, and Vision Insurance State Retirement Plans Paid Time Off (approximately 22 days of vacation leave, 13 days of sick leave, and 11 paid holidays annually) Tuition Assistance (UF Employee Education Program) Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer For more information on benefits, please visit UFHR Benefits and Rewards (ufl.edu) Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Preferred: Experience working in a Registrar's Office or within a higher education environment, with an understanding of academic records administration, student services, and university policies and procedures. Ability to supervise, coach, and develop staff; provide clear direction, performance feedback, and support employee growth and accountability. Ability to establish and maintain professional and effective working relations with others; ability to supervise. Ability to establish work priorities and coordinate the timely completion of work assignments; strong organizational skills. Knowledge of effective written and oral communication; ability to articulate and interpret information. Ability to work independently, exercise good judgment, and work with minimal supervision. Ability to use a variety of computer programs such as Microsoft Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Ability to comprehend and interpret university and Registrar policies, procedures, and academic regulations. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must upload the following documents to be considered for the position: Cover Letter Resume List of References Applications must be submitted by 11:55p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541708/dean-college-of-public-health-and-health-professions": {
      "title": "Dean, College of Public Health and Health Professions",
      "description": "Dean, College of Public Health and Health Professions Job no: 541708 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 33010000 - HP-OFFICE OF THE DEAN KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain and enhance the stature of the College by ensuring that it continues to be at the forefront of research and in educating students who are prepared to be outstanding practitioners and leaders in public health and the health professions. Maintain high standards in faculty recruitment, appointment, promotion, and tenure in order to support excellence in research, teaching, and clinical practice in the professions supported by the College. Serve as a catalyst and advocate for innovative and impactful research. In collaboration with other UF and UF Health entities, create and implement exceptional and innovative programs of education, research, clinical practice, and service. Foster a student-centered culture that enhances effective teaching, engages students in faculty research, maintains a diverse and inclusive learning environment, builds strong relationships between students, faculty, staff, and alumni, and promotes leadership development. Maintain and enhance the financial health of the College through active representation with applicable state of Florida entities and raise the College’s visibility and obtain financial and other types of support from individuals, foundations, and corporations for initiatives, programmatic efforts, research, public service, faculty and student projects, student support, and discretionary activities. DESIRED OUTCOMES Develop, in partnership with others and in alignment with the University, a forward-looking strategic plan for the College. Grow the research output of the College and strengthen engagement with University of Florida and UF Health research elements. Enhance educational programs to continue to achieve exceptional learning. Enhance the College’s national reputation by growing and strengthening the research, clinical, and service enterprises. Attract top faculty and staff to further elevate the quality of the College, and ensure best-in-class hiring, retention, development, and engagement practices. The Dean, College of Public Health and Health Professions will be a proven academic leader with demonstrated ability to engage with faculty and staff to develop and execute a vision in support of the College’s broad educational, research, clinical, and public service missions. IDEAL EXPERIENCE Academic Leadership in a Multidisciplinary Setting Progressive executive management experience in a leading academic or multidisciplinary institution affiliated with public health and/or health professions. Demonstrated ability to lead across the diverse disciplines represented in the College, and to integrate the College's contributions within a broader academic health center. Recruitment, Retention, and Development of Talent Demonstrated success recruiting, retaining, and developing outstanding cohorts of faculty, staff, and students across a range of disciplines and career stages. Substantive experience leading faculty appointment, promotion, and tenure processes; building robust mentoring and professional development pathways; and supporting the engagement and advancement of all members of the College community. Research Enterprise Growth Proven ability to grow and sustain a high-performing research enterprise, including substantive experience competing for and managing NIH and other federal funding portfolios. Demonstrated success developing interdisciplinary research programs, supporting early-career investigators, and leveraging shared resources to advance translational impact at scale. Fundraising Success generating resources through foundation grants, corporate partnerships, and philanthropic outreach. Personal Scholarship and Achievement An earned doctoral or terminal degree with achievements commensurate with appointment at the full Professor level in a discipline represented within the College, with a preference for a public health professional. CRITICAL LEADERSHIP CAPABILITIES Strategic Vision Within a preeminent public university with a history of excellence and multidisciplinary collaboration, the successful candidate should be prepared to build on existing strengths of the College to create, develop, and expand initiatives and programs that will advance the College’s contributions by: Developing a clear vision and strategy consistent with the University of Florida and UF Health’s mission and best practices. Demonstrating a commitment to excellence in, and innovative approaches to, education, research, training, and service in the College’s mission areas. Demonstrating commitment to interdisciplinary activities. Adapting strategies and goals to political realities and institutional constraints, engaging broadly and seeking to bring a range of opinions and approaches to solve systemic and emerging issues. ​ Collaborating and Influencing The successful candidate will possess the ability to understand and thrive in a highly relationship- and consensus-driven organization, balancing competing needs to make the best strategic and tactical decisions for the College by: Possessing superior communication skills and the ability to connect with a variety of audiences; clear and effective written and oral presentation; and the experience, ability, and enthusiasm to be an energetic representative and spokesperson for the College. Maintaining effective liaisons with internal and external stakeholders. Leading People Within a large, complex academic setting, the next Dean will inspire, motivate, and direct the College’s senior leadership team, faculty, staff, and other stakeholders to their Dean’s individual team, as well as colleagues, stakeholders, and influencers throughout the organization. The Dean will do this by: Motivating and empowering direct reports and other team members to initiate and develop projects or strategic objectives. Providing vision, leadership, and management for the academic enterprise. Setting clear expectations and targets for scholastic and administrative contributions in collaboration with University of Florida and UF Health leaders, department chairs, and individual faculty members.​ FOR CANDIDATES The executive search firm Spencer Stuart has been retained to assist in this recruitment. Chuck Jordan and Shannon Yeatman of Spencer Stuart are lead consultants on the search. Inquiries, nominations, and applications are invited and should be submitted to search team member Ted Sorota via tsorota@spencerstuart.com . Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. If accommodations due to a disability are needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. As an Equal Opportunity Employer, the University is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, creed, color, religion, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity and expression, marital status, national origin, political opinions or affiliations, genetic information, and veteran status in all aspects of employment, including recruitment, hiring, promotion, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits, and training. Advertised: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541684/2027-spring-stephen-c-oconnell-center-internships-facility-intern-marketing-intern-employee-relations-hr-intern": {
      "title": "2027 Spring Stephen C. O’Connell Center Internships – Facility Intern, Marketing Intern, Employee Relations/ HR Intern",
      "description": "2027 Spring Stephen C. O’Connell Center Internships – Facility Intern, Marketing Intern, Employee Relations/ HR Intern Apply now Job no: 541684 Work type: Student Ast, Temp Full-Time, Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL), Out of the State of Florida Categories: Recreation/Event Services Department: 67010000 - OC-OPERATING ACCOUNT Classification Title: Internship - OPS HOURLY NON-SECRETARY/CLERICAL Classification Minimum Requirements: This position is open to anyone who is interested in an internship in an exciting environment. Minimum GPA of 2.5 or higher Strong written and verbal communication skills with careful attention to detail Comfort handling confidential information with discretion and professionalism Ability to manage competing priorities in a venue environment where schedules shift around events Comfort working in a fast-paced, event-based environment Job Description: FACILITY INTERN Gain hands-on experience with Gator Athletics, concerts, special events, facility operations, event setups, and event-day management. MARKETING INTERN Build experience in social media, audience engagement, promotions, advertising, photography, graphic design, and event marketing. EMPLOYEE RELATIONS INTERN Gain experience with hiring, interviewing, training, staffing, employee engagement, recognition, morale, and employee relations. INTERNSHIP DETAILS • Paid internship opportunities • Full-time internships of approximately 40 hours per week preferred (Part-time opportunities may be considered) • Semester-long internships available, with consideration given to longer-term commitments • Evening and weekend hours required based on the event schedule • Opportunities to work directly with experienced venue and event professionals • Housing is not provided; interns are responsible for transportation and parking BUILD YOUR EXPERIENCE. EXPAND YOUR NETWORK. START YOUR CAREER. An internship at the Stephen C. O’Connell Center provides students with the opportunity to apply classroom knowledge in a professional setting while gaining firsthand experience at one of the University of Florida’s premier event venues. Join the O’Connell Center team and gain the experience that can help prepare you for your future career. Expected Salary: $15.00/hour. $16.00/hour after a 120-hour probationary period. Required Qualifications: This position is open to anyone who is interested in an internship in an exciting environment. Minimum GPA of 2.5 or higher Strong written and verbal communication skills with careful attention to detail Comfort handling confidential information with discretion and professionalism Ability to manage competing priorities in a venue environment where schedules shift around events Comfort working in a fast-paced, event-based environment Preferred: Proficient computer skills; excellent people skills; able to perform duties with reasonable accommodation. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered for this position, applicants must attach a resume and cover letter (even if this is your first position). Selected applicants who move to the next step will be notified soon after the position has closed, so please check your emails! Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541686/parttime-oconnell-center-crew-member-ops-or-student-assistant": {
      "title": "Part-Time O'Connell Center Crew Member - OPS or Student Assistant",
      "description": "Part-Time O'Connell Center Crew Member - OPS or Student Assistant Apply now Job no: 541686 Work type: Student Ast, Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Recreation/Event Services Department: 67010000 - OC-OPERATING ACCOUNT Classification Title: OPS HOURLY NON-SECRETARY/CLERICAL Classification Minimum Requirements: This position is open to anyone who wants part-time employment in an exciting environment. (Note: UF Students are welcome to apply but must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average and will work up to 20 hours per week.) On top of the hiring requirement minimum number of shifts mentioned above, you will also be required to work during your time with the O'Connell Center: - A minimum of 25 hours per month during the fall and spring semesters - 1 Holiday shift per semester - 2 shifts on any home football game day - 1 Fall commencement shift, 2 Spring commencement shifts Additional crew member requirements, as listed on our website, will also apply (under job requirements): https://www.oconnellcenter.ufl.edu/part-time-employment/ Job Description: The O’Connell Center provides an exciting and dynamic environment that makes us unlike anything else. Being a crew member requires teamwork, dedication, and a friendly, positive attitude to contribute to the many events held at the facility. The O’Connell Center offers flexible scheduling by allowing crew members to self-schedule from a list of shifts based on our event schedule. Shifts range from before, during, and/or after events held inside and outside of the building. Shifts are categorized by three major divisions: Event Staff, Changeover, and Technical. Crew members can work shifts interchangeably within all 3 divisions. For Event Staff shifts, you will be working inside or outside the building upholding the policies of the building, the university, and the event for which you are working. You will also be assisting patrons by answering questions or helping them to their seats, scanning tickets, and maintaining a friendly and positive attitude throughout the crew. For Changeover shifts, you will assist the facilitation of the setup and breakdown of events, as well as helping maintain cleanliness during events. For Technical shifts, you will be assisting with the stage, electrical, and audio aspects of the event. You will be trained before you can work any of these shifts, so no prior knowledge or experience is necessary but is always welcomed. You must be available to work weeknights and weekends, especially home football game days in the fall, and commencements in the Spring. Additional requirements, as listed below or on our website, also apply: https://www.oconnellcenter.ufl.edu/part-time-employment/ We are currently looking for individuals who are able to start working and work a minimum of 15 shifts spread across the following dates (please note that some dates are optional whereas others are required and are indicated as such below): 11/21 (required), 11/24 (required), 12/1 (required), 12/2, 12/3, 12/4, 12/5, 12/6 (required), 12/11 (required), 12/12 (required), 12/13, 12/14, 12/17, 12/20, 12/21 (required), 12/22, 12/29 (required), 12/311/6 (required), 1/9 (required) Please note this is NOT a seasonal position. A criminal background will be conducted. Expected Salary: $16.00/hour after probationary period ($15.00/hour during probationary period). Required Qualifications: This position is open to anyone who wants part-time employment in an exciting environment. (Note: UF Students are welcome to apply but must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average and will work up to 20 hours per week.) On top of the hiring requirement minimum number of shifts mentioned above, you will also be required to work during your time with the O'Connell Center: - A minimum of 25 hours per month during the fall and spring semesters - 1 Holiday shift per semester - 2 shifts on any home football game day - 1 Fall commencement shift, 2 Spring commencement shifts Additional crew member requirements, as listed on our website, will also apply (under job requirements): https://www.oconnellcenter.ufl.edu/part-time-employment/ Preferred: The ability to work more than the minimum requirements; A willingness to learn and be taught by your peers; Ability to adapt quickly; Ability to learn on the job; Having advanced time-management skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered for this position, applicants must attach a resume and cover letter (even if this is your first position). Selected applicants who move to the next step will be notified soon after the position has closed, so please check your emails! Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541473/bioinformatics-research-scientist": {
      "title": "Bioinformatics Research Scientist",
      "description": "Bioinformatics Research Scientist Apply now Job no: 541473 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Computer Science, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 27320702 - HA-UFHCI BIOSTAT COMP BIOL SR Classification Title: Bioinformatics Analyst II Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of experience. Job Description: About UFHCI The University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI) is an NCI-designated cancer center comprising more than 350 basic, translational, and population-based scientists and clinicians who conduct research for the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer. A central collaborative research model encourages translation of basic laboratory findings into novel therapies, and the multidisciplinary membership of the Institute is its strength. Researchers are drawn from across UF, including six colleges within the Health Science Center — Medicine, Nursing, Dentistry, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine — as well as the Colleges of Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, Health and Human Performance, Journalism and Communications, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. Research is organized around four programs: Cancer Control and Population Sciences; Cancer Targeting and Therapeutics; Immuno-Oncology and Microenvironment; and Mechanisms of Oncogenesis. About the Position UFHCI invites applications for a Masters-level bioinformatics research scientist in the Biostatistics and Computational Biology Shared Resource (BCB-SR). The position reports to the Associate Director for Cancer Quantitative Sciences and Director of the BCB-SR and works within the Bioinformatics Unit alongside biostatisticians and other computational scientists. The Bioinformatics Unit provides analytical support to UFHCI members. The successful candidate will work directly with cancer researchers from experimental design through analysis, interpretation, and publication. The position carries a broad and changing project portfolio, substantial investigator contacts, and regular co-authorship on peer-reviewed publications. Duties and Responsibilities: Analyze high-throughput genomics data in support of UFHCI investigators, including bulk and single-cell RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, DNA methylation assays (WGBS, EM-seq, RRBS), ChIP-seq, and whole-genome or whole-exome sequencing. Consult with investigators on experimental design, feasibility, and analysis plans before data are generated, and contribute analysis plans, preliminary data, and written text to grant applications. Develop, adapt, and maintain reproducible analysis pipelines and software on UF's HiPerGator high-performance computing cluster, using workflow managers, containers, and version control. Integrate multi-omic, clinical, and publicly available data sets; develop or adapt statistical and computational methods where existing tools are inadequate. Interpret results in collaboration with investigators and contribute figures, methods sections, and manuscript text to publications and presentations. Maintain organized project documentation, code repositories, and data management practices, and track service requests and effort for shared-resource reporting. Contribute to training and outreach for cancer center members and trainees, including workshops, tutorials, and individual instruction. Maintain current knowledge of the field through seminars, journal clubs, and scientific meetings, and evaluate emerging methods and tools for adoption by the unit. This is a professional position ideal for candidates with advanced training, education, and experience in bioinformatics, computational biology, or similar fields. Employees in these roles operate as part of our collaborative research enterprise and are fundamental to the progress of cancer research being conducted at the University of Florida. A complete job description will be provided to candidates selected for interview. Expected Salary: Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. This is a TEAMS position, and is eligible for a suite of excellent benefits, retirement options, and paid leave . About the City of Gainesville Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, and farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds . Required Qualifications: Masters' in bioinformatics, computational biology, genetics, molecular biology, computer science, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience analyzing high-throughput sequencing data, evidenced by publications, preprints, dissertation work, or a public code portfolio. Proficiency in R and/or Python and working knowledge of the Linux command line and shell scripting. Strong written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to work effectively with a broad range of collaborators, including investigators, trainees, industry sponsors, partners at other institutions, and UFHCI staff. Preferred: Experience in a shared resource, or other collaborative setting managing multiple concurrent projects and deadlines. Experience with high-performance computing environments and job schedulers such as SLURM. Experience with workflow systems (Nextflow, Snakemake, or WDL), containerization (Apptainer/Singularity, Docker), and version control (Git). Experience with single-cell and spatial transcriptomics analysis. Working knowledge of applied statistics relevant to genomics, including generalized linear models, mixed models, multiple testing correction, and multi-omic integration. Familiarity with public data resources and repositories such as TCGA, GTEx, ENCODE, GEO/SRA, and dbGaP. Experience with compiled languages (C++, Rust), SQL, or relational database design. Experience developing and releasing software packages (Bioconductor, CRAN, PyPI, or equivalent). Experience contributing to federal grant applications. Familiarity with handling protected health information and controlled-access data under HIPAA and dbGaP data use agreements. Special Instructions to Applicants: The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Applicants must upload a copy of the following documents to be considered for this position: Cover Letter or Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae or Resume List of Three Professional References This is not a remote work position, and is located on the University of Florida's Main Campus in Gainesville, FL. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541458/ops-community-scientist": {
      "title": "OPS - Community Scientist",
      "description": "OPS - Community Scientist Apply now Job no: 541458 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 16010600 - LS-HEALTH DISPARITIES Classification Title: OPS - Community Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: Able to speak, read, and understand English At least 21 years of age or older Ability to work flexible hours, including occasional evenings and weekends All training will be provided. No experience required Job Description: Need for Community Scientists to Work As Needed in the UF Health Cancer Institute Community-Partnered Cancer Disparities Research Collaborative (CDRC) Dr. Carolyn M. Tucker directs and serves as the Principal Investigator for the CDRC. This collaborative consists of 13 churches and their pastors, community and faculty researchers, cancer survivors, undergraduate student interns in a Health Disparities and Health Promotion Research Internship, and health disparities and health promotion research staff. These groups work together to conduct community-based participatory research that will lead to interventions and large grants, with the aim of promoting cancer prevention, treatment and survivorship. The CDRC targets cancer survivors and people at risk for cancer who live in low-income communities in or near East Gainesville, or in rural and/or low-income communities in Alachua County. The CDRC’s goal is to reduce cancer incidence, cancer deaths and cancer disparities that have a disproportionately negative impact on Black, Hispanic and other minoritized groups. The CDRC is supported and funded by the NCI-designated UF Health Cancer Institute. Position Summary: Community Scientists engage with diverse communities to support recruitment of community members to serve as participants in health disparities- and cancer-related research studies conducted or supported by the CDRC. The ideal candidate is outgoing, articulate, creative, and comfortable initiating conversations with individuals they may not know. This role requires explaining research studies, answering questions, and helping eligible community members understand the benefits of participating in research. Community Scientists collect data from research participants, including height and weight, and are trained to obtain informed consent from participants. Candidates will complete IRB certification training, in addition to other job training. It would be useful for candidates to have connections within the Gainesville community, such as connections with local churches and community organizations. Although some meetings are virtual, Community Scientists should be able to attend in-person meetings, training sessions, and community events as needed. Key Responsibilities: Research and Study Involvement: Participant Recruitment and Consent: Recruit participants for health promotion and cancer prevention related research studies, and collaborate with CDRC partner churches Consent and Screening: Screen potential participants and obtain informed consent from participants involved in CDRC-led research studies Data Collection: Collect both baseline and post-intervention data from research participants in accordance with study protocols, including participant height and weight Incentive Distribution: Distribute gift cards to participants after completion of designated study activities Focus Group Facilitation : Conduct focus groups with select participants to gather qualitative data on group participants when applicable Meetings and Training: Meetings: Attend monthly CDRC planning meetings and virtual weekly Community Scientist meetings to stay updated on study developments and needs Training: Participate in approximately four hours of training to learn research tasks, participant interaction protocols, and data collection procedures Events : Attend CDRC community engagement events throughout the year and distribute flyers at these events Working Conditions and Expectations: Work Hours: Seasonal, project-based position with up to 15 hours per week based on participant availability and program needs. When no projects are active, no work hours will be available. Report To: Dr. Carolyn M. Tucker, Director of UF Health Cancer Institute Community-Partnered Cancer Disparities Research Collaborative and Ms. Miles Hamilton, CDRC Program Manager Expected Salary: $16 per hour Required Qualifications: Able to speak, read, and understand English At least 21 years of age or older Ability to work flexible hours, including occasional evenings and weekends Preferred: Community-based research experience or interest in conducting health-related research Ability to communicate effectively with community members from varied backgrounds Familiarity with the health concerns and needs of underserved communities Passion for reducing cancer disparities and improving community health Established connections within diverse Gainesville and Alachua County communities Demonstrates cultural sensitivity Learns quickly Has a high school diploma or GED Because community scientists should be reflective of the community as a whole, current UF students are not preferred for this position Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541432/agricultural-assistant-ii": {
      "title": "Agricultural Assistant II",
      "description": "Agricultural Assistant II Apply now Job no: 541432 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Hillsborough Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Agriculture Operations Department: 60752000 - AG-GCREC-FIELD OPERATIONS Classification Title: Agricultural Assistant II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Crop Maintenance: 1. Assist in punching holes for tomatoes, strawberries, or any other needs. 2. Assist in staking and tying tomatoes 3. Assist in calibration of spray equipment 4. Responsible for spraying herbicides, and pesticides as requested 5. Assist in planting different crops 6. Obtain within 3 months and maintain pesticide applicator license. Maintenance Operation: 1. Assist in routine repair of the different types of irrigation systems, including filters. 2. Assist in cleaning of equipment and work areas 3. Responsible for cleanliness, orderliness and maintenance of all working areas 4. Assist in preventative maintenance of tractors, state vehicles, and farm equipment. 5. Any unscheduled work related to field. Land Preparation: 1. Assist in land preparation, i.e., disking, harrowing, rototilling 2. Assist in bedding, mulching before planting 3. Assist in removing the plastic mulch, twine, and stakes from the fields at the end of the season Harvesting: 1. Assist in the harvest of research crops 2. Assist in grading the crops after the harvest, and weighing of the fruits. Ideal candidate will have: Familiarity with the farm equipment: knowledge, and skills to operate adequately, and safely (i.e. farm tractors and attached equipment, farm trucks, and motorized sprayers). Stand and walk for extended or continuous periods of time. Bend and stretch several times during the course of the day. Ability to work with faculty and other members of the Research Center staff Has a working knowledge of field plot research procedures. Able to recognize potential safety problems and correct them and to practice safety personally Flexible enough to change work methods if asked Ability to anticipate problems, inform supervisor of the situation, and take appropriate and timely action. Demonstrate commitment to contributing to a respectful and caring community for all, including individuals of diverse backgrounds, experiences, races, ethnicities, gender identities, sexual orientation, and perspectives. Expected Salary: $17-$19/ per hour commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Pesticide License preferred (must obtain within 3 months and maintain) Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541283/associate-dean-of-clinical-services": {
      "title": "Associate Dean of Clinical Services",
      "description": "Associate Dean of Clinical Services Apply now Job no: 541283 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28011100 - VM-DEAN Classification Title: ASO DEAN & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate Professor Candidates for this rank must hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Professor Candidates for this faculty rank must be qualified for the rank of associate professor and, in addition, be a recognized national or international authority or an outstanding teacher in their field or specialization. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the associate professor level. Job Description: The University of Florida (UF) College of Veterinary Medicine (CVM) is recruiting a positive, influential and visionary leader for the Associate Dean (AD) of Clinical Services (small animal emphasis) for the UF Veterinary Hospital Systems (UFVHS). This leadership position will report to and advise the CVM Dean on matters related to the management and operations of the Small Animal Hospitals and will be responsible for future development and implementation of strategic plans, service growth and the delivery of excellent patient-centric care. The AD will collaborate with CVM and UFVH leaders, including the Associate Dean for Clinical Services (large animal emphasis), academic department chairs, associate deans, chief medical officer, directors and service chiefs, to (1) ensure effective delivery of outstanding patient/client-centered care, (2) ensure that the UFVH meets the major needs for clinical training of professional students, interns, residents and fellows and (3) provide an effective environment to conduct clinical research and clinical trials within the UFVH. The College of Veterinary Medicine is part of the University of Florida Health Science Center, which houses six health-related colleges (Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health & Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine). It is the only health science center in the southeast and one of only three nationally, where all six health colleges are located on the same campus. The CVM has equally strong ties to the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences. These alliances create opportunities for vibrant research collaborations, academic depth in Artificial Intelligence, and a robust human clinical enterprise, which has aided the UF Veterinary Hospital system in becoming a leader in veterinary medical care. The campus is in Gainesville, a vibrant and growing community with numerous cultural amenities and outstanding natural beauty (see http://www.visitgainesville.com/arts-culture-history/ ). The UF Small Animal Hospital enterprise has the largest caseload in academic medicine. It spans two locations (one in Gainesville and one in Ocala), over 20 specialty services, 90 small animal clinicians, 95 small animal house officers, 240 small animal clinical support staff and approximately 65,500 small animal patients annually. The small animal hospital in Gainesville is a Level 1 Veterinary Emergency & Critical Care Society (VECCS) certified facility and an American Animal Hospital Association (AAHA) accredited hospital. The UFVH has a $68M annual operating budget. The UFVH supports a growing Clinical Trials program, a unique small animal open heart surgery program, and the most sophisticated and integrated Patient Quality and Safety program in veterinary medicine. Specific activities for which the AD will have primary or shared responsibilities include: Leadership - The AD will develop and execute strategic plans for the small animal hospital to lead academic veterinary clinical medicine in an increasingly competitive local clinical environment. The AD will actively establish programs to improve the quality of life and well-being of staff, house officers and faculty regarding the work environment. The AD will develop and provide mentorship to foster leadership skills for managers, service chiefs and new personnel. Administration – The AD will oversee operations of the UF small animal hospital in Gainesville. Management includes overseeing facilities and equipment and maintaining compliance with UF, state and federal regulations. The AD will work to improve efficiency in operations of this complex hospital system. Personnel Management – The AD will oversee the small animal clinical activities of faculty, locums, house officers and hospital staff. Financial Responsibility – The AD will work with the Director of Administration & Finance and CFO to develop and oversee the small animal annual service budgets, satellite business operation, fiscal performance, revenues, expenditures, fee structures and accounts receivables and with those individuals and the ADCS-LA to manage the overall UFVH financial operations. Clinical Service Activities – The AD will oversee the quality of veterinary medical care provided by small animal clinicians and staff. The AD will oversee the Assistant Dean/ Chief Medical Officer and the Patient, Quality and Safety Program. The AD is responsible for maintaining the highest level of patient care and exceptional client experience. The AD is expected to maintain VECCS and AAHA accreditation. Clinical Educational Activities – The AD will work with the CVM Associate Dean for Academic and Student Affairs and UFVH service chiefs to provide an exemplary clinical training program for DVM students and meet the American Veterinary Medical Association Council on Education accreditation standards. The AD is expected to foster innovations in clinical teaching. In concert with the CVM leaders, the AD is expected to develop new or enhanced clerkship training opportunities to meet the needs of a changing veterinary profession. In addition, the AD will support professional training of house officers, including interns, residents and fellows, in conjunction with the Director of House Officer Programs and the ADCS-LA. Clinical Research Activities – The AD will work with the CVM Associate Dean for Research & Graduate Studies, academic department chairs and CVM faculty to oversee the use of the small animal facilities, equipment and personnel in the conduction of clinical research and clinical trials and will be responsible for compliance with hospital rules and regulations about patient safety and confidentiality. Outreach, Public Relations and Marketing – The ADs (SA and LA) will oversee the organization of annual events and continuing education opportunities that pertain to and promote clinical services of the UFVH. In addition, the AD will contribute to outreach activities, including UFVH marketing operations, engagement with the community of referring veterinarians (RDVMs) and efforts to determine the level of satisfaction with UFVH clinical services among critical stakeholders, including RDVMs and clients. The AD will work with the Office of Advancement to cultivate philanthropic support that benefits small animal operations. The successful candidate will have an academic appointment as Associate Professor or Professor (Clinical or Tenure Track as appropriate) in the UF CVM and, accordingly, will be able to develop a focus area for scholarly activity. The successful candidate may contribute to the CVM teaching mission by participating in professional DVM student education. Potential areas for scholarly activities will be defined through negotiations with the Dean and may include one or more broad initiatives related to clinical research, clinical service, academic veterinary hospital management or other areas that are relevant to the AD's background and interests. Expected Salary: The rank and salary will be commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have an advanced degree in veterinary medicine (DVM or equivalent) and six or more years of professional experience in academic veterinary medicine or specialty practice. Preferred: Advanced clinical training and board certification in a clinical specialty is preferred. Applicants should demonstrate leadership skills in managing complex organizations with an understanding of delivering high-quality client/patient-centric care in a learning environment. Applicants must have excellent interpersonal and communication skills, a solid foundation in building a collaborative and transparent work environment and demonstrated experience in conflict resolution and team building. Special Instructions to Applicants: All applicants must apply online through Careers at UF to be considered. Applications for job number 541283 must submit: (1) a cover letter including a statement of the applicant's leadership philosophy, articulated vision and how the candidate's experience supports that vision (2) a curriculum vitae; and (3) the name and contact information of three professional references. Email inquiries to Dr. Chris Sanchez, Search Committee Chair sanchezl@ufl.edu are welcome, however for full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Review of applications will begin on October 16, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled. The successful candidate will be required to provide official transcript(s) upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is an equal-opportunity institution. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541271/systems-database-administrator-v": {
      "title": "Systems Database Administrator V",
      "description": "Systems Database Administrator V Apply now Job no: 541271 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology Department: 14300000 - IT-ICT INFRA COMM TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: Database Administrator V Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and ten years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: UF Information Technology (UFIT) is currently seeking a professional level Systems Database Administrator V to join Infrastructure and Communication Technologies (ICT), Database Platforms, a unit within UFIT. UF Information Technology (UFIT) enables teaching, learning, research, and service on campus and across the region with state-of-the-art enterprise IT systems, including SEC and SUS universities, and the opportunity to teach using HiPerGator. Infrastructure and Communication Technology (ICT) is a division of UF Information Technology that manages the UF Data Center and delivers hosted server, storage, virtualization, database, email, and related systems, in addition to connecting the University of Florida campuses to the world via high-speed data, video, Wi-Fi, telecommunications, and VoIP services. The Database Platform team is responsible for the architecture, engineering, and administration of UFIT’s core database infrastructure. They design, build, and maintain critical database services, such as PostgreSQL, MySQL, MSSQL, Oracle and more. This role serves as a senior technical designer for database infrastructure solutions and supporting software systems. You will provide expert-level administration, analysis, and maintenance for complex Enterprise Database Systems, ensuring the performance and reliability of critical database infrastructures. As a subject matter expert, you will provide technical guidance to other staff, assist with complex troubleshooting, and coordinate technical efforts for projects. Some key responsibilities and characteristics of this position are: Expert System Administration & High Availability (40%): Conduct expert administration of complex Enterprise Database Systems with a primary focus on MS SQL Server Always On Availability Groups and MS Windows Failover Clustering. Maintain secondary expectations for other team platforms including Linux OS, VMware, MySQL, PostgresSQL, and Oracle. Compliance & Secure Environments (10%): When required for specific environments, ensure database systems comply with strict security frameworks, including CJIS, FISMA . Technical Design & Engineering (25%): Serve as a senior designer for infrastructure solutions, developing technology requirements and conducting vendor solution analysis for on-premise and public cloud environments. Mentorship & Technical Coordination (10%): Mentor less experienced administrators, ensure implementation follows established procedures, and coordinate technical initiatives with other infrastructure support teams. Documentation, Training, & Quality Control (5%): Develop and maintain comprehensive system documentation to ensure smooth operation of database systems. Project Guidance & Strategy (10%): Provide technical guidance for special projects, participate in tactical and strategic planning, and assist management with capacity and budget analysis. About UF Information Technology Led by Senior Vice President and CIO Elias Eldayrie, the nine departments comprising UFIT are: Academic Technology; AI² Center; Applications, Development, and Integrations; Business Center; Customer Experience and Resource Planning; Data Platform and Analytics; Information Security Office; Infrastructure and Communication Technology; and Research Computing. UF Information Technology (UFIT) enables teaching, learning, research, and service on campus and across the region with state-of-the-art enterprise IT systems, including SEC and SUS universities, and the opportunity to teach using HiPerGator. For more information about UFIT and its goals and governance, visit https://it.ufl.edu/about-ufit/strategic-plan/ . About the University of Florida One of America’s all-around best universities, the University of Florida drives future-making education, eye-opening discoveries, life-saving health care, and community-building collaboration for our state, our nation, and our world. UF is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from Florida’s West Coast, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support various activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Exceptional Benefits UF provides various leave programs based on an employee’s salary plan, including vacation, sick leave, holidays, personal leave days, and paid family leave. In addition to paid time off, the University of Florida offers a very competitive benefits package. Expected Salary: $98,000 - $110,000; Commensurate based on education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and ten years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: EDUCATION & TRAINING Bachelor’s degree in computer science, business administration, or a related field. EXPERIENCE Expert-level administration and configuration of Microsoft SQL Server (MSSQL) and Windows OS environments (VM and Bare-metal). Advanced experience with MSSQL Always On High Availability (HA) technologies and Windows Failover Clustering. Experience managing database systems within CJIS and FISMA environments, or other highly regulated data frameworks, when required. Proficiency in secondary team platforms including Linux OS, MySQL, and Oracle Enterprise Database. Infrastructure deployment and configuration using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools such as Ansible, Chef, or Terraform. SKILLS Excellent written and verbal communication skills with the ability to interact effectively with all constituencies. Strong organizational skills and the ability to prioritize and complete simultaneous complex projects with minimal supervision. High level of accuracy, attention to detail, and a dedicated customer service-oriented approach. KNOWLEDGE Expert knowledge of Windows Core remote administration, including Group Policy and RSAT tools. Deep understanding of current IT standards, emerging trends, and federal/state privacy regulations (including FERPA, HIPAA, and PII). Knowledge of automation and scripting languages such as PowerShell, Bash, or Python for system optimization. Familiarity with virtualization technologies like VMWare and monitoring applications (e.g., Nagios, Zabbix, or SCOM). ABILITIES Ability to coordinate technical efforts and provide guidance for medium-to-large sized projects involving cross-functional teams. Think critically and creatively to provide solutions for complex, multi-layered infrastructure designs and planning. Work collaboratively to build strategic relationships with both internal and external clients while maintaining a high standard of integrity. Ability to perform in a fast-paced, customer-focused environment involving critical production support and development. Special Instructions to Applicants: Work visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This is not a remote work position and is located in Gainesville, FL. A Level 2 Criminal Background Screening is required. Applicants are required to submit the following with their application: Cover Letter Resume List of professional references with contact information (Minimum of 3 with one from a previous or current supervisor) The application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (E.T.) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540680/parttime-ufifas-4h-camp-timpoochee-environmental-education-educator": {
      "title": "Part-Time UF/IFAS 4-H Camp Timpoochee Environmental Education Educator",
      "description": "Part-Time UF/IFAS 4-H Camp Timpoochee Environmental Education Educator Apply now Job no: 540680 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Okaloosa Categories: Recreation/Event Services, Agricultural Sciences, Early childhood education/Childcare Department: 60330100 - AG-CAMP TIMPOOCHEE Classification Title: OPS Time-Limited Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: High School diploma or its equivalent is minimum requirements Job Description: Essential Tasks Florida 4-H environmental educators play a key role in creating engaging and hands-on environmental education experiences. Your responsibilities will include: Teach, organize, and evaluate educational programs, focused on Florida’s marine natural resources, to students in grades K-12 through day residential, and outreach programs Provide programmatic support to maintain and increase marine and environmental education classes, activities, and materials. Assist with animal care for education animals Help maintain clean facilities utilized for educational programs on-site May assist with dining hall duties as needed, for on-site educational programs Help with other programmatic responsibilities as needed Expected Salary: $16.50 per hour Required Qualifications: High School diploma or its equivalent is minimum requirements Preferred: Preferred qualification is a Bachelors Degree, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience, in sciences, education, natural resources, youth development, recreational leadership or other related fields is required. Must pass a Background Screening prior to hire. Valid driver’s license preferred. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities Teach pre-designed environmental education curricula to a variety of ages and abilities Effective oral communication with both large and small groups Effective written communication Customer service Experience working with youth Knowledge, experience and/or interest in teaching marine and environmental education Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is an OPS seasonal position, with two seasons available for employment: Fall Term: 9/15/26 – 11/25/26 Winter/Spring Term: 1/15/27-4/30/27 Weekly schedules vary, depending upon programming but would typically be M-F, between 8AM-5PM. Some night and weekend work. Average of 30 hours/week. Benefits of Accepting this Position Tangible: Hourly wage of $16.50/hour, with average work week of 30 hours/week Housing may be available on-site at no additional cost American Red Cross certifications provided, including: First Aid, CPR, AED, and waterfront lifeguarding Explore additional beneﬁts for University of Florida OPS positions at: Temporary Staff (OPS) | Current Employees Intangible: Be part of the world’s largest positive youth development program (4-H) and connected to a top 5 University (University of Florida). Make a difference in the future of Florida’s environment by educating our youth. Connect with a strong network of youth development and environmental professionals and fellow educators. To be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Jul 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Feb 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540681/parttime-ufifas-florida-4h-camp-cloverleaf-environmental-education-educator": {
      "title": "Part-Time UF/IFAS Florida 4-H Camp Cloverleaf Environmental Education Educator",
      "description": "Part-Time UF/IFAS Florida 4-H Camp Cloverleaf Environmental Education Educator Apply now Job no: 540681 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Highlands Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 60330300 - AG-CAMP CLOVERLEAF Classification Title: OPS Time Limited Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: High School diploma or equivalent Job Description: Essential Tasks Florid 4-H environmental educators play a key role in creating engaging and hands-on environmental education experiences. Your responsibilities will include: Teach, organize, and evaluate educational programs, focused on Florida’s marine natural resources, to students in grades K-12 through day residential, and outreach programs Provide programmatic support to maintain and increase marine and environmental education classes, activities, and materials. Assist with animal care for education animals Help maintain clean facilities utilized for educational programs on-site May assist with dining hall duties as needed, for on-site educational programs Help with other programmatic responsibilities as needed. Benefits of Accepting this Position Tangible: Hourly wage of $16.50/hour, with average work week of 30 hours/week Housing may be available on-site at no additional cost American Red Cross certifications provided, including: First Aid, CPR, AED, and waterfront lifeguarding Explore additional beneﬁts for University of Florida OPS positions at: Temporary Staff (OPS) | Current Employees Intangible: Be part of the world’s largest positive youth development program (4-H) and connected to a top 5 University (University of Florida). Make a difference in the future of Florida’s environment by educating our youth. Connect with a strong network of youth development and environmental professionals and fellow educators. Expected Salary: $16.50 per hour Required Qualifications: High School diploma or equivalent. Preferred: Preferred qualification is a Bachelors Degree, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience, in sciences, education, natural resources, youth development, recreational leadership or other related fields is required. Must pass a Background Screening prior to hire. Valid driver’s license preferred. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities Teach pre-designed environmental education curricula to a variety of ages and abilities Effective oral communication with both large and small groups Effective written communication Customer service Experience working with youth Knowledge, experience and/or interest in teaching marine and environmental education Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is an OPS seasonal position, with two seasons available for employment: Fall Term: 9/15/26 – 11/25/26 Winter/Spring Term: 1/15/27-4/30/27 Weekly schedules vary, depending upon programming but would typically be M-F, between 8-5. Some night and weekend work. Average of 30 hours/week. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Jul 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Feb 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540361/apopka-ops-maintenance-generalist": {
      "title": "Apopka OPS Maintenance Generalist",
      "description": "Apopka OPS Maintenance Generalist Apply now Job no: 540361 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Orange Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Agriculture Operations Department: 60310000 - AG-FAC PLANNING - OPERATIONS Classification Title: OPS Classification Minimum Requirements: A high school diploma (or equivalent) and zero (0) to two (2) years of experience in the field or in a related area. Job Description: Position Summary The Maintenance Generalist I provides essential maintenance support to the UF/IFAS Mid-Florida Research & Education Center by performing a variety of routine maintenance, repair, and groundskeeping duties to ensure facilities, greenhouses, and research spaces remain safe, functional, and well-maintained. This position supports the Center's research and extension mission by maintaining buildings, equipment, and greenhouse infrastructure while responding to routine maintenance needs. Responsibilities Perform routine maintenance and minor repairs to buildings, facilities, and equipment, including basic plumbing, electrical, carpentry, and painting tasks within the scope of the position. Conduct preventative maintenance on facility systems and equipment, including replacing HVAC filters, inspecting building components, lubricating equipment, and reporting deficiencies requiring specialized repairs. Perform routine inspections of buildings, greenhouses, and surrounding facilities to identify maintenance needs, safety concerns, or operational issues. Assist with greenhouse maintenance by performing preventative maintenance on cooling pads, exhaust fans, vents, irrigation components, shade systems, and environmental control equipment to help maintain proper operating conditions. Support the maintenance and repair of research infrastructure, including benches, workspaces, irrigation systems, fencing, walkways, and other facility assets. Operate and maintain hand tools, power tools, and light maintenance equipment in a safe and effective manner. Perform routine groundskeeping activities, including mowing, trimming, edging, pressure washing, debris removal, and maintaining clean, safe, and accessible work areas around buildings and greenhouses. Respond to maintenance work orders in a timely manner and communicate identified repair needs or safety concerns to supervisors. Maintain maintenance records, complete assigned work orders, and document completed preventative maintenance activities. Follow University of Florida safety policies and procedures, utilize appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE), and maintain a safe work environment. Perform other duties as assigned to support the operational needs of the Mid-Florida Research & Education Center. Expected Salary: $16 - $18/hr. Licenses/Certifications: Must maintain a valid FL Driver's License Preferred: One (1) year or more of experience performing general building, facilities, or maintenance work. Experience with preventative maintenance and minor repairs involving plumbing, electrical, carpentry, painting, or HVAC systems. Experience maintaining greenhouse facilities, environmental control systems, irrigation systems, or agricultural/research support facilities. Knowledge of the safe operation and maintenance of hand tools, power tools, and light maintenance equipment. Ability to troubleshoot maintenance issues, identify repair needs, and recommend appropriate corrective actions. Experience utilizing computerized work order or maintenance management systems. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume and preferably three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. *Preference is a full-time employee; however, we would consider a part-time employee to work 24 hours per week. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 15 Jul 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538913/counselor-developmental-pediatrics": {
      "title": "Counselor - Developmental Pediatrics",
      "description": "Counselor - Developmental Pediatrics Apply now Job no: 538913 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Social/Behavioral Science, Counseling/Community Outreach/Social Work, Early childhood education/Childcare Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Counselor - Developmental Pediatrics Classification Minimum Requirements: Professionals with one of the following credentials are qualified to provide psychosocial counseling: A Social worker with a masters degree or a Ph.D. in social work from a Certified Social Work Education-accredited school of social work. A Registered nurse with advanced specialized counseling education and training as a clinical nurse specialist or certified psychiatric nurse. A Professional licensed to provide clinical, counseling, and psychotherapy services by the Florida Department of Health, Division of Medical Quality Assurance. A Counselor with a masters degree in counseling. A Psychologist with a masters or Ph.D. in psychology. A Masters or doctoral level graduate student intern from an accredited school of social work or a psychology or counseling program, under the supervision of a licensed practitioner qualified to supervise such interns. Job Description: Assess a child’s cognitive abilities and administer testing to children using a variety of instruments and techniques that are appropriate for the individual child. Recommend challenging yet achievable cognitive and academic goals for children with different abilities, disabilities, strengths and needs; and develop interventions to achieve these goals. demonstrate strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work, communicate, and collaborate effectively with families, co-workers, schools and community agencies. Apply sound principles of data-based decision making to all aspects of practice. Demonstrate sensitivity and skills needed to work with families, children and staff from various cultures and backgrounds. Ability to drive for satellite clinics, provide trainings and support groups to the community. Provide internal trainings to the staff. Participate in multidisciplinary assessments and team integration meetings. Provide counseling services to children and adolescents. Administer testing to children using a variety of instruments and techniques appropriate to the individual child’s needs. Provide counseling services such as PCIT or CBT to children and adolescents. Interpret evaluation and assessment results and recommend challenging yet achievable cognitive and academic goals for children with different abilities, disabilities, strengths and needs; and develop interventions to achieve these goals. Provide trainings and support groups to families, staff, school personnel, therapists and professional organizations. Participate in developing appropriate developmental outcomes and strategies as part of the multidisciplinary team. Expected Salary: $65,000 - $72,000 depending on education and experience Required Qualifications: Must have active School Psychologist certificate Preferred: 3 to 5 years as a certified School Psychologist. Knowledge of policies and procedures related to the general Education and Exceptional Student Education Services and Eligibility requirements. Knowledge of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Improvement Act (IDEIA) Knowledge of test administration and interpretation Knowledge of behavioral assessment procedures Knowledge of general child development Experience working with children who exhibit learning, behavior, and emotional difficulties. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Background Check is Required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 19 May 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/537307/clinical-case-manager-early-steps": {
      "title": "Clinical Case Manager - Early Steps",
      "description": "Clinical Case Manager - Early Steps Apply now Job no: 537307 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Allied Health, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Clinical Case Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Job Description: Participates in/conducts developmental evaluations/screenings, consultations, direct services, and family assessments in collaboration with the multidisciplinary team when appropriate for the purpose of completing intake, determining eligibility, monitoring progress, updating IFSPs, and planning/providing intervention. Works with IFSP team to ensure State and Federal timelines are met with regard to timely service delivery, transition, IFSP development/updates, and LEA/SEA notification. Recommends, coordinates, and makes referrals to community services to meet identified outcomes on Individualized Family Support Plans. Maintains current and accurate client records including program plans, intervention notes, case notes, and billing information timely and accurately for payment. Participates in local and statewide meetings and trainings to ensure best practice and compliance; Completes trainings/modules as required by the program and organization. Expected Salary: $46,000 to $55,000 annually depending on education and experience. Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Valid driver's license. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in Early Childhood or Early Childhood Special Education, Social Work, Psychology, or Sociology. Two years of appropriate experience with children birth to five with special needs. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Level II Background Check is Required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 16 Oct 2025 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
