    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541793/lecturerasst-instr-prof-of-meteorology": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Asst. Instr. Prof of Meteorology",
      "description": "Lecturer/Asst. Instr. Prof of Meteorology Apply now Job no: 541793 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16220000 - LS-GEOGRAPHY Classification Title: Lecturer/Assistant Instructional Professor Job Posting Title: Lecturer/Assistant Instructional Professor of Meteorology Job Description: The Department of Geography at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for a full-time, 9-month, renewable, non-tenure track Lecturer/Assistant Instructional Professor position in Meteorology , beginning in August 2027. We seek a dedicated and innovative instructor who will substantially contribute to the department’s instructional mission and key focus areas in Meteorology. Other responsibilities include engaging in service activities within the university and college. Service to the profession is also recognized. Primary responsibilities include teaching a sequence of advanced meteorology courses at the undergraduate level. The candidate will teach several of our existing advanced courses, such as Radar and Satellite, Atmospheric Dynamics, Atmospheric Thermodynamics, Synoptic Meteorology, Mesoscale Meteorology, Atmospheric Physics, and Weather Briefing. The candidate will also create and deliver new elective courses to enhance the quality of the degree program, preferably with attention to machine learning and artificial intelligence applications and experiential learning opportunities. Courses will be designed to build progressively from core skills to advanced applications, complementing existing course offerings and ensuring students are well prepared for opportunities in public research, academic, private business, and communication sectors. Faculty in the Department of Geography also contribute new courses to the CLAS Beyond120 program, which is encouraged with this position. Additional duties include contributing to program and curriculum development, outreach and recruitment, mentorship of undergraduate students, and engagement with university resources such as HiPerGator. Applicants should be committed to long-term excellence in instruction and program development. The appointment is renewable and eligible for promotion. At UF, Lecturers/Instructional Professors have a well-defined promotion structure, a professional development leave program, professional development opportunities, and access to potential funding for professional travel. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain a record of high-quality pedagogical professional development. This position is not transferable to a tenure track research appointment. The University of Florida is an R1 university ranked nationally as the #8 public university by US News & World Report. UF supports dynamic cohorts of 41,000 undergraduate students and 14,000 graduate students. The university is home to the HiPerGator 3.0 Supercomputer and HiPerGator AI, one of the most powerful GPU-enabled AI systems in U.S. higher education. The Department of Geography has 22 active faculty members, with excellence in teaching and research across a broad spectrum of focus areas. It offers Bachelor of Science specializations in General Atmospheric Science, Applied Meteorology, and Broadcast Meteorology. The department additionally offers a Meteorology minor and a Meteorology and Climatology certificate program. Classrooms in the department include two instructional computer labs and an immersive classroom featuring an OmniGlobe. The department also offers BS/BA, MS/MA, and PhD degree tracks in Geography, including online and distance learning options for coursework. Visit UF Geography’s website ( http://geog.ufl.edu/ ) to learn more about the department. Minimum Requirements: PhD degree in Meteorology, Atmospheric Science, Climatology, or related field. Demonstrated track record of high-quality college- or university-level teaching experience of advanced courses in Meteorology with broad knowledge across courses. Preferred Qualifications: BS degree in Meteorology in a program that satisfies the US Office of Personnel Management’s Meteorology Series 1340 (GS 1340) requirements and American Meteorological Society (AMS) guidance on the composition of Bachelor Degree programs. Experience with course development and instruction using active learning and experiential learning techniques. Ability to develop and teach one or more new advanced Meteorology elective courses. Demonstrated use and instruction of machine learning techniques related to data analysis and research methods. Undergraduate mentoring experience. Advertised Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant’s qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A complete curriculum vitae, including all college degrees, relevant bachelor-level meteorology courses completed. A statement of teaching experiences, philosophy, and potential course offerings. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., evaluations, awards, etc.) Sample syllabi from relevant Meteorology courses taught as the instructor of record. The names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning November 20, 2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Inquiries about the position should be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Mr. Stephen Mullens, at stephen.mullens@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541795/laboratory-technician-i": {
      "title": "Laboratory Technician I",
      "description": "Laboratory Technician I Apply now Job no: 541795 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 29081100 - MD-PATH - UF PATH LABS Classification Title: Laboratory Technician I Classification Minimum Requirements: Associates degree in an appropriate area; or a high school diploma or equivalent and two years of related experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The Laboratory Technician works to support research for an International Diabetes research network. Primary focus is the receipt, processing, and distribution of human specimens to laboratories as well as specimen archival and retrieval. Serves as backup for unit Program Assistant as necessary Specimen processing including, labeling, aliquoting, shipping, and archival of specimens for TrialNet and Radiant research projects. Unit responsibilities include receipt, accessioning, and labeling of human samples for the NIH funded TrialNet Type I Diabetes Research project and Radiant. Database entry of HIPAA compliant specimen receipt, documenting courier air bill, shipping information, study identifier, physician submitters, all test numbers, and specific identification information. Operation and maintenance of UF TrialNet/Radiant database including data transfers to/from the TrialNet/Radiant Coordinating Center at the University of South Florida. Client services interactions for the TrialNet Type I Diabetes Research Project/Radiant, including but not limited to sample status inquiry by telephone or facsimile from all physician submitters and/or designated Affiliate Clinical Centers, locating and resolving any discrepant and/or missing samples or data inconsistencies through creative problem solving. Quality assurance procedures for specimen identifiers which involve the proofreading and editing of all specimen submissions. Monthly creation of established quality assurance data related to TrialNet/Radiant research project operations. Maintain Diabetes specimen repository including assuring equipment maintenance and maintenance of archive database. Additional specimen retrieval projects including the distribution (shipment) of specimens as required by TrialNet/Radiant project. Other duties as assigned Expected Salary: Starting salary is between $18.50 - $19.68 Required Qualifications: Associates degree in an appropriate area; or a high school diploma or equivalent and two years of related experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Communication skills which will allow the employee to interact with UF physicians, clients, and other health professionals in a professional manner. Ability to work in a potentially stressful environment. Ability to quickly learn various aspects of laboratory operations. Knowledge of various tests offered by the DRL. Experience with TrialNet, Radiant or other Diabetes research. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541796/ops-femors-logistics-coordinator": {
      "title": "OPS - FEMORS Logistics Coordinator",
      "description": "OPS - FEMORS Logistics Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541796 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29080900 - MD-PATHOLOGY-LABDOC Classification Title: OPS - FEMORS Logistics Coordinator Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The role oversees the inventory and readiness of the Disaster Portable Morgue Unit, or DPMU, coordinates training sessions, develops operational protocols, provides logistical support during deployments, and maintains a state of 24/7 readiness funded by an extramural grant. Logistics - Oversee the routine inventory, maintenance, and resupply of the Disaster Portable Morgue Unit, or DPMU, cache, ensuring equipment is operational, compliant with safety standards, and deployment ready. Marshals equipment including procurement, inventory management, warehouse organization, vehicle storage and maintenance options, transportation load planning for both activations and training exercises. During deployment (PRN), in consultation with local authorities, duties shall include initial scene response and evaluation of personnel and equipment required for: processing of the scene, establishing temporary morgue operations and support of the forensic units within the morgue, establishing a Victim Information Center (VIC) to receive reports of missing persons, and managing responder administration. Evaluates locations for facility sizes, layout, accessibility, and support infrastructure. Supervises and administers the FEMORS Disaster Portable Morgue Unit (DPMU) to include site set-up, inventory, cache maintenance, and disaster response. Distributes equipment as needed for Administrative Command Post, field recovery operations, the morgue, the VIC, and the MIC Strategic Communications Planning and Management Coordinates with command staff to execute annual training sessions to enhance team preparedness, develops maintains and performs annual reviews of internal protocols and quality assurance (Field Operations Guide - FOG), reviews new applicant submissions to offer advice on FEMORS classification options, and provide hands-on instruction on equipment use and safety Develop and maintain internal protocols and quality assurance (Field Operations Guide - FOG). Develop and manage training manuals and handouts. Coordinates with command staff to provide input for completion of annual status reports and deliverable reports in sustainment, management, and training. Assists with internal monitoring of all aspects of Administration Monitors compliance with University of Florida, DOH, and OSHA regulations. Negotiates with vendors and follows proper bid 'solicitation procedures’. Researches equipment and stays abreast of the latest technology and updates. Performs educational outreach to local emergency operations centers and District Medical Examiners to educate them on the activities of FEMORS and how to become involved or request assistance. Maintains Go-team response, 24-7 on-call readiness. Promotes the FEMORS program in educational opportunities to relevant audiences. Create and distribute targeted marketing content to promote FEMORS team membership. Channels may include, but are not limited to, mail, e-mail, social media, signage, events, open houses, and presentations. Expected Salary: Starting salary is $38.31, this position is 20 hours a week. Required Qualifications: Administration and Mechanical Aptitude Experience Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541802/clinical-research-coordinator-iii-neurology-multiple-sclerosis-division": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator III - Neurology - Multiple Sclerosis Division",
      "description": "Clinical Research Coordinator III - Neurology - Multiple Sclerosis Division Apply now Job no: 541802 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration Department: 29181100 - MD-NEURO-MULTIPLE SCLEROSIS Classification Title: Clinical Research Coord III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Department of Neurology , Division of General Neurology, subdivision of Multiple Sclerosis (MS) is seeking candidates with at least three years of human subject research experience for a Clinical Research Coordinator III position . The Clinical Research Coordinator III is responsible for the daily operations of clinical trial studies run by the MS Section. Responsible for ensuring regulatory documentation is completed and submitted as appropriate for all research/clinical trial activity which includes, but is not limited to, new submissions, continuing reviews, and revisions through the University of Florida Health Professions IRB and a central IRB. More About this Position Coordination of Protocol Subjects: Incumbent is responsible for the coordination of relevant care and protocol required testing for subjects enrolled on their assigned trials. This includes the following tasks: Educates subjects on topics related to the informed consent document and confirms that informed consent documents are appropriately executed, registers and/or randomizes subjects with appropriate sponsor Educates subjects and families on the medical aspects of protocols, testing procedures, visit schedules, and side effects of chemotherapy and/or other therapeutic drugs Follows subject hospitalizations, clinic/office visits, treatments and scheduled activities Order protocol tests and communicates these test results to investigators, treating providers, subjects, and their families, as applicable Communicates with subjects on a regularly scheduled basis and assesses progress, evaluates problems and determines appropriate action or investigator/physician consultation as needed Communicates and interacts with family members, investigators, physicians, providers, nurses and other health care agencies involved with the patients care and follow-up status Prepares pre-printed and electronic study orders and study information sheets and secures appropriate approvals as required Communicates with investigational pharmacy staff regarding subject enrollment, ensuring an adequate supply of investigational agent is available prior to subject appointments Attends research meetings, preparing and/or presenting requested information Attends Investigator meetings and represents the University of Florida in a professional manner Follows each subject’s clinical course during inpatient hospitalizations and at clinic visits, confirming that all protocol-specific tests and procedures are completed within study timelines Enters and tracks all subject status changes in the Clinical Trials Management System (CTMS) Chart Review & Data Interpretation: Incumbent is responsible for the interpretation and collection of medical and clinical data of potential and existing subjects on assigned clinical trials. This includes the following tasks: Collects and interprets data from medical records, including medical history, diagnosis, pathology, laboratory, radiology, specialty test results, progress notes, reported side effects or adverse reactions and dose modifications. Transcribes clinical data into sponsor-specific case report forms, delegating to Clinical Research Assistants (CRA) as appropriate Identifies possible protocol candidates and confirms subject eligibility with the investigator for protocol participation by attending patient care conferences/clinics, interviewing prospective participants, and reviewing medical records Provides accurate and complete data to study sponsors according to protocol timelines Protects subject privacy by maintaining complete case report forms and/or research charts in compliance with privacy laws Evaluates, interprets and grades clinical toxicities noted in the medical record and subject diaries using the grading criteria specified in the protocol Provides guidance and assistance to Study Coordinator Is and IIs during onboarding and provide protocol assistance Compliance: Incumbent is responsible for all compliance with all internal and external regulatory and institutional requirements related to the trials assigned to them. Responsible for attending all relevant trainings monthly as required and provides in-service education related to assigned protocols to ensure compliance. Overall compliance responsibilities can be broken out into the following sections and tasks: Monitor Audits and QA: Collaborates with monitors and/or with sponsors’ data management units to resolve case report form discrepancies Answers data queries, monitors for data delinquencies, and replies to special requests as necessary Participates in internal and external inspections and audits of subject medical records to ensure compliance with the protocol Acts as resource for Study Coordinator Is and IIs in the administration of protocol assessments Directs concerns of protocol violations to PI and/or appropriate treating provider. IRB and Regulatory Collaborates with internal and external IRB staff to ensure all regulatory documents are appropriately submitted Communicates serious adverse events and both planned and unplanned subject deviations to the IRB, sponsor, and/or FDA according to established reporting requirements Acts as liaison for institution with clinical trial sponsors and data managers involved with protocols Works with internal IRB Specialists to submit continuing review/study closure reports, serious adverse event reports, protocol revisions and other documents to the IRB within appropriate timelines to prevent project expiration or sponsor deficiencies Prepares cumulative adverse event tables and deviation reports as required for continuing review or closure reports Fiscal: Participates in fiscal audits to ensure compliance with established billing procedures Facilitates the submission of research study orders for all sponsor/study-paid services and maintains department copies of all submitted orders Records the service dates for all protocol-related study visits using the appropriate tracking log or database system Laboratory: Incumbent is responsible for the coordination of all sample collection, analysis, and shipping for assigned trials, includes the following tasks (may be delegated to CRAs as appropriate): Coordinates research sample collection with clinical and/or research collection personnel and confirms samples are appropriately labeled following collection Adheres to pertinent safety and quality policies and procedures for the handling, processing, disposal, and shipping of biological specimens Ensures that research samples are processed according to protocol or sponsor requirements Verifies that research samples are stored appropriately until shipment Prepares samples for shipment as required by the protocol and ship samples in accordance with protocol, local, state and federal requirements Obtains, processes, stores and ships clinical samples according to sponsor requirements Strategy: Provides mentorship to new CRAs and SCs, including in-service training related to neurology, clinical research, and patient care topics. May be assigned to one-on-one mentorship and training tasks for onboarding and retraining purposes Acts as resource for clinical staff with the implementation of clinical research protocols and review of subject cases Serves on internal committees for the creation of SOPs, workflows, and metrics. Collaborates with leadership and support staff to implement and change policies and/or procedures Participates in working groups and coordinates internal quality control activities in conjunction with internal Monitors and other related staff Any other duties, as needed, to fulfill the mission and to abide by the values of the UF Department of Neurology Expected Salary: $59,700 - $70,000 annually Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: 3 years or more of human subject research Knowledge of NIH and FDA procedures Knowledge of state and federal regulations regarding clinical research Knowledge of accepted procedures and ethical, confidential practices Proficient in the use of personal computer software, including Microsoft Office Demonstrated ability to interpret complex guidelines Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and to meet deadlines as demanded by the position Highly resourceful team-player, with the ability to also be extremely effective, independent, interact professionally, and utilize effective writing and organizational skills Demonstrate understanding and effective use of Emotional Intelligence strategies and skills Proven ability to handle confidential information with discretion, be adaptable to various competing demands, and demonstrate the highest level of customer/client service and response Demonstrate ability to achieve high performance goals and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment Forward-thinking mentality, actively seeking opportunities and proposing creative solutions with strong decision-making capability Special Instructions to Applicants: This position requires a valid driver’s license and dependable transportation. To be considered, please upload the following documents with your application: Cover Letter Resume Contact information for three professional references This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541804/clin-svc-representative-ii": {
      "title": "Clin Svc Representative II",
      "description": "Clin Svc Representative II Apply now Job no: 541804 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Collier Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 34060700 - DN-COMMUNITY DENTISTRY Classification Title: Clinical Service Rep II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant office experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Patient Care Advocate -Supports pediatric patients and their families by strengthening communication between dental providers, schools, and community partners to ensure children receive timely, appropriate, and effective oral-health care. -Assist children and families in understanding dental diagnoses, treatment options, and recommended follow-up care. --Provide compassionate, trauma-informed emotional support to families navigating complex dental needs. -Coordinate appointments, referrals, and communication between pediatric oral-health agencies, community clinics, residency programs, and school-based partners. -Work collaboratively with school nurses and health liaisons at partner schools to ensure successful follow-up for students identified with urgent dental needs. -Monitor treatment progress, track adherence to care plans, and maintain ongoing communication with families to support optimal outcomes. -Identify and address barriers to care such as transportation, insurance, language, or health-literacy challenges. -Attend health and community fairs to promote community-based clinical dental navigation and to stress establishing a dental home for all family members. -Maintain accurate, comprehensive pediatric patient records, including referrals, treatment progress, and follow-up outcomes. -Collaborate with dental providers, residency programs, schools, and community organizations to strengthen pediatric care strategies and improve system-wide coordination. Community-Based Clinical Navigation -Consult and cooperate with community systems to facilitate linkage, referral, advocacy, and follow-up, with a focus on achieving recommended pediatric treatment goals. -Establish and maintain close working relationships with safety-net organizations, demonstrating strong knowledge of key contacts, eligibility criteria, referral processes, and follow-up expectations. -Organize transportation for pediatric patients who are transferred to or seeking services at the NCF Pediatric Dental Center or Healthcare Network facilities. -Support families in navigating community-based care pathways to ensure continuity of treatment and improved oral-health outcomes. -Strengthen communication channels between community partners and oral-health providers to reduce gaps in care and improve referral completion. Administration -Attend meetings as appropriate and meet regularly with supervisor to exchange pertinent information and receive supervision. -Prepare and maintain client and program records in accordance with applicable standards, regulations, grant requirements, and data-collection protocols. -Verify patient Medicaid eligibility and assist families in navigating Medicaid-related processes. -Participate in Medicaid Navigator events, helping clients understand required paperwork and procedural steps. -Participate in continuing education activities, outreach events, and community engagements that promote available services and resources for families in Collier County. -Maintain ongoing professional development, remaining knowledgeable in areas of expertise and completing all required training and certifications. -Perform additional marginal functions of the job as assigned, with time allocation documented according to program needs. Expected Salary: $24.74/hr, commensurate with education/experience Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant office experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: - CPR certification - Ability to drive to various locations within Collier County - Good communication skills to foster relationships with agency partnerships - Experience working with pediatric populations - Exhibits cultural sensitivity with diverse racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic populations - Experience working in the Medicaid online system - Familiarity with community resources, social services, and healthcare in Collier County - Ability to properly demonstrate and communicate health education activities - Fluent in Spanish or Haitian Creole Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See the Qualified Positions link on our applicant FAQs page for a list of eligible positions. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
