    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541811/accountant-i": {
      "title": "Accountant I",
      "description": "Accountant I Apply now Job no: 541811 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 64801000 - FA-UNIVERSITY BURSAR-ADMIN Classification Title: Accountant I Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization Job Description: This position is part of the Sponsored Student Billing team within the University Bursar, located in the Finance & Accounting unit of the CFO Division. This position supports the team by resolving complex payment issues on student and sponsor accounts, monitoring of aging on sponsor accounts, and in assisting the team with processing during peak periods. The incumbent in the position will deliver excellent customer service to students and ensure the timely resolution of issues. Expected Salary: $22.03 - $24.90 Required Qualifications: A bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization Preferred: Ability to plan, organize and coordinate files, records, and work assignments. Ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to work independently and as a team. Ability to utilize problem-solving techniques. Experience in Microsoft Office, including Excel and Word. Ability to determine work priorities, assign work and ensure proper completion of work assignments. Ability to analyze and interpret accounting data Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541813/school-director-professor-engineering-school-of-sustainable-infrastructure-environment": {
      "title": "SCHOOL DIRECTOR & PROFESSOR, Engineering School of Sustainable Infrastructure & Environment",
      "description": "SCHOOL DIRECTOR & PROFESSOR, Engineering School of Sustainable Infrastructure & Environment Apply now Job no: 541813 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management, Engineering Department: 19070000 - EG-ENG SCH SUSTAIN INFRST ENV Classification Title: SCHOOL DIRECTOR & PROFESSOR Classification Minimum Requirements: • An earned doctorate in civil, coastal, environmental, oceanographic engineering, or a closely related field. • A distinguished record of scholarship, education, mentoring, and professional contribution that merits appointment as a tenured full professor at the University of Florida. • Evidence of successful leadership in a research-intensive university or comparably complex organization, including building collaborative, high-performing organizational cultures and developing faculty, staff, and emerging leaders. • Demonstrated ability to develop and articulate a compelling strategic vision to advance the reputation and ranking of the department by translating that vision into clear priorities, strategic investments and measurable outcomes. • Is skilled in the development and expansion of sponsored research programs, including fostering collaborative research and advancing high-impact programs. • Excellent communication, judgment, integrity, and relationship-building skills; ability to communicate and work effectively with faculty, staff, students, administrators, and external stakeholders. • Demonstrated commitment to student success and to a scholarly and working environment where people with varied backgrounds and perspectives can contribute and thrive. • Experience with external engagement and development, such as industry partnerships, alumni outreach, and fundraising. Job Description: The Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering at the University of Florida invites nominations and applications for the position of Professor and Director of the Engineering School of Sustainable Infrastructure and Environment (ESSIE). Reporting to the Dean of the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering, the Director will provide strategic, intellectual, and administrative leadership for an internationally visible research and education enterprise. The successful candidate will be an accomplished scholar and an effective organizational leader with a strong record of externally funded research who can substantially improve the department’s recognition and ranking, develop a clear vision for international impact and connect the department’s strengths to consequential opportunities for Florida and the world. The selected leader will join the Dean’s leadership team and will be expected to advance both the School and the college, contribute to university priorities, and engage constructively with the state, nation, and profession. The Director will have the opportunity to shape the future of a multidisciplinary School that integrates expertise across civil, coastal, environmental, sustainability and resilience related engineering and science disciplines; strengthen a culture of collaboration and excellence; elevate the School’s national and international visibility; and position ESSIE to address some of the most consequential civil, coastal, and environmental challenges facing Florida, the nation, and the world. The Opportunity ESSIE brings together the Department of Civil & Coastal Engineering ( https://essie.ufl.edu/cce/ ) and the Department of Environmental Engineering Sciences ( https://essie.ufl.edu/ees/ ), creating a distinctive academic community that connects built and natural environments. The School includes approximately 65 faculty and 966 students , with approximately $19 million in annual research expenditures and $22.6 million in research awards. Its research and educational activities extend well beyond traditional disciplines to pioneer research, education, and environmentally responsible engineering technologies that advance innovative solutions that enhance the resilience and sustainability of communities, infrastructure and environmental resources. Through a nexus of the civil, coastal, environmental and oceanographic programs, ESSIE fosters interdisciplinary collaboration across engineering, science, policy and practice. The School houses and manages a robust network of researchers, research centers, and core facilities (https://essie.ufl.edu/about/research-centers-facilities/), providing exceptional opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration, external partnerships, technology development and high-impact research. These assets represent a major institutional strength and will be central to advancing the School strategic vision and impact under its next Director. The Director will join ESSIE at a moment of significant opportunity. Urgent challenges and emerging opportunities in infrastructure systems, climate resilience, environmental systems, environmental health engineering, artificial intelligence and automation, advanced materials, mobility, and data-driven engineering are creating new possibilities for research, education, and societal impact. At the same time, UF’s breadth, scale, land-grant mission, statewide presence, and interdisciplinary research ecosystem provide an exceptional platform for building ambitious programs and partnerships. The Director will be expected to articulate a compelling vision for its future and translate that vision into measurable outcomes that strengthens ESSIE’s reputation and impact, at the state, national and global levels. Leadership Priorities The Director will work with faculty, staff, students, College leadership, alumni, and external partners to advance a focused set of strategic priorities. In collaboration with center directors, facility managers and faculty, the Director will help leverage ESSIE’s extensive network of research centers and facilities to attract and strengthen external funding, partnerships, and workforce development opportunities. ● Provide visionary and strategic leadership. Develop and communicate a bold, forward-looking vision for ESSIE that builds on its existing strengths while identifying emerging areas in which the School can achieve distinctive national and international leadership. Translate that vision into clear priorities, measurable goals, strategic investments, and sustained execution. ● Forge a transdisciplinary, cutting-edge research environment by facilitating active collaboration among researchers and units. Provide strategic guidance in identifying and pursuing emerging research opportunities across disciplines. School-wide priorities and the long-term success of ESSIE should guide decisions regarding investments, opportunities, and resources. The Director will encourage ambitious interdisciplinary research, enable faculty to pursue large and complex sponsored programs, strengthen connections among ESSIE’s departments, centers and facilities, expand collaborations with UF institutes and centers, and develop strategic partnerships with federal and state agencies, industry, communities, and other universities. ● Recruit, develop, and retain talent. Lead efforts to recruit, develop, and retain outstanding faculty and staff and foster an environment in which people at all career stages can thrive. The Director will promote effective mentoring, professional development, recognition, succession planning, and leadership development while maintaining high expectations for excellence. The Director will also work with College leadership to identify opportunities for strategic faculty growth in areas that strengthen the School’s long-term research and educational mission. ● Advance educational innovation and student success. Provide leadership for undergraduate, graduate, and professional education across ESSIE. The Director will support continuous improvement and accreditation; encourage curricular innovation; expand experiential, field-based, interdisciplinary, and research-centered learning opportunities; strengthen graduate education and mentoring; and prepare students to lead in a rapidly changing engineering profession. ● Expand external engagement and advancement. Serve as a visible and persuasive ambassador for ESSIE. The Director will cultivate strong relationships with alumni, industry, government agencies, professional organizations, communities, donors, and other external partners. The Director will work closely with College leadership and advancement professionals to expand philanthropic support and develop partnerships that advance the School’s strategic goals. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: • An earned doctorate in civil, coastal, environmental, oceanographic engineering, or a closely related field. • A distinguished record of scholarship, education, mentoring, and professional contribution that merits appointment as a tenured full professor at the University of Florida. • Evidence of successful leadership in a research-intensive university or comparably complex organization, including building collaborative, high-performing organizational cultures and developing faculty, staff, and emerging leaders. • Demonstrated ability to develop and articulate a compelling strategic vision to advance the reputation and ranking of the department by translating that vision into clear priorities, strategic investments and measurable outcomes. • Is skilled in the development and expansion of sponsored research programs, including fostering collaborative research and advancing high-impact programs. • Excellent communication, judgment, integrity, and relationship-building skills; ability to communicate and work effectively with faculty, staff, students, administrators, and external stakeholders. • Demonstrated commitment to student success and to a scholarly and working environment where people with varied backgrounds and perspectives can contribute and thrive. • Experience with external engagement and development, such as industry partnerships, alumni outreach, and fundraising. Preferred: • Experience recruiting, mentoring, evaluating, and retaining faculty and staff across career stages and appointment types. • Experience developing multidisciplinary initiatives, major collaborative proposals, centers, institutes, or other large-scale research activities. • Experience building productive industry partnerships, including sponsored research, consortia, or public-private collaborations. • Experience using industry engagement to strengthen education, experiential learning, workforce preparation, and research opportunities. • The ability to identify emerging funding opportunities and organize faculty and external partners around them. • Experience working with College and University leadership and across academic units. • Experience in advancement, philanthropy, alumni engagement, or development of external resources. • Professional leadership and service in civil, coastal, environmental, oceanographic engineering, or a closely related field or a related discipline. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to receive applications until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted through UFCareers at: https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/listing/ (Search Job 541813). Complete applications must include the following files in PDF format: (1) A cover letter describing the candidate's interest in the position, relevant academic leadership experience, and scholarly background; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) the names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of five professional references; (Additional required documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” section in the application.) ; (4) a leadership vision statement of no more than 3 pages detailing your leadership approach, a preliminary vision for the department, and two or three strategic priorities the candidate would explore with the community. Competitive candidates are expected to clearly complement the overall mission of the department and the college. The anticipated start date for this position is August 16, 2027. Priority consideration will be given to applications received by December 1, 2026. Please direct questions about the position to Senior Associate Dean John M. Shea, Faculty Search Chair, email: jmshea@ufl.edu or Professor Nina Stark, Faculty Search Co-Chair, email: nina.stark@essie.ufl.edu About the University of Florida and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering The University of Florida is the flagship institution of the State University System of Florida, a member of the Association of American Universities, and consistently ranked among the nation’s leading public universities. UF is currently ranked as one of the top 10 best public universities, 8th according to the 2026 U.S. News & World Report of Best Colleges and is recognized by Forbes as one of 10 public “New Ivies” for producing exceptional graduates. With 16 colleges, more than 150 research centers and institutes, and over 60,000 undergraduate and graduate students, UF is one of the largest universities in the nation. UF offers broad opportunities for interdisciplinary work across engineering, medicine, agriculture, the sciences, and other fields. In FY2026, University of Florida research spending reached a record $1.34 billion. UF is also home to HiPerGator, the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education. The Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering has over 400 faculty members and combines disciplinary depth with cross-cutting institutes, centers, shared facilities, industry partnerships, and a statewide mission. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541814/ops-laboratory-courier-i": {
      "title": "OPS - Laboratory Courier I",
      "description": "OPS - Laboratory Courier I Apply now Job no: 541814 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Transportation Services Department: 29081100 - MD-PATH - UF PATH LABS Classification Title: OPS - Laboratory Courier I Classification Minimum Requirements: Completion of 9th grade Current classified Florida driver license appropriate to the type of vehicle operated. Possession of a classified driver license (CDL) may be required bases on the type of vehicle operated. Job Description: The Courier will provide pickup, transport, and delivery of medical specimens, lab supplies, and reports, while providing excellent service to our clients They are the trusted liaison between the client and PathLabs. Maintain assigned vehicle in proper working condition assuring all maintenance, safety, and required equipment are compliant with Department guidelines. Provide and/or arrange for appropriate maintenance of Department vehicle(s) and coordinate for coverage or back up vehicle. Maintain database daily (provided route sheets) of stops, mileage, start and end times. Courier Services: Responsible for ensuring the verification of specimens picked up at various locations and optimum transport of specimens at correct temperatures to maintain sample integrity. Responsible for recording, monitoring, and maintaining the temperature ranges for frozen, ambient, incubated, and refrigerated laboratory specimens. Delivers mail and materials to clients as designated by the supervisor or lead courier on a daily basis. Fills client supply orders on a daily basis and meets 24 hours turn-around for consumables and 48 hours for printed material. Enforces the laboratory specimen rejection / acceptance policy by confirming patient identification on specimens and requisitions. Maintains timeliness of assigned routes with associated timetables for each site to ensure turnaround times and optimal specimen receipt and reporting are met. Create and maintain database (provided route sheets) of stops, mileage, start and end times. As directed by supervisor or lead courier, assists with new hire training, safety, policies and procedures. Vehicle Maintenance: Performs basic vehicle maintenance (ie: all necessary fluids, fuel, and normal lubricants kept at required operating levels). Lights, as well as air pressure in tires, should be checked periodically. Maintains vehicle in top operational condition and appearance. Operates in a non-destructive and careful manner through utilization of primary roadways, slowing for rough roadways, railroad crossings, and construction sites, etc. Adheres to posted speed limits. Reports any potential vehicle malfunctions immediately to designated person. Reports any accident or vehicle issues immediately and completes appropriate reports. Reports to Supervisor any medication used or illnesses requiring medications which could cause drowsiness, or any other reason for potential impairment. Drivers are responsible for maintaining the cleanliness of vehicles. Client Compliance: Have the ability to speak conscientiously to existing and potential customers and represent the company in the most professional manner. Escalate customer concerns or new customer opportunities to management. Laboratory processes: Have strong organizational and critical thinking skills and a customer-focused mindset. Able to identify potential customers they may discover along the way and effectively communicate those discoveries to PathLabs Client Relations team. Work to better the customer experience by helping to solve problems, brighten days and elevate concerns to management. Provide support to Laboratory personnel as required. Expected Salary: Salary is between $16.75 - $17.00 per hour Required Qualifications: Completion of 9th grade Current classified Florida driver license appropriate to the type of vehicle operated. Possession of a classified driver license (CDL) may be required bases on the type of vehicle operated Preferred: Completion of 12th grade or equivalent. Good verbal and writing skills Knowledge of medical terminology. Medical courier experience Background in customer service Experience with product deliveries and fulfillment Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541816/clinical-assistantassociate-professorfamily-medicine": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Apply now Job no: 541816 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30160000 - JX-COMMUNITY MEDICINE-JAX Classification Title: Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must be MD/DO and have a GA Medical License Job Description: The Department of Community Health and Family Medicine at the University of Florida College of Medicine-Jacksonville seeks a family medicine/internal medicine physician, MD/DO, to join us. The position is for Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor level on a clinical non-tenure accruing track at our outpatient care office - UF Health Family Medicine. This position is up to fulltime and will report to the medical director and chair of the department at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville. Base salary is based on experience and guaranteed with significant additional productivity incentives and benefits package. UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Negotiable based on Qualifications and Experience Required Qualifications Candidates must be MD/DO and have a GA Medical License Preferred: Board certified or board eligible. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications November 24, 2025. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and contact information for 3 References. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541818/astasofull-prof-palliative-care": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Palliative Care",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Palliative Care Apply now Job no: 541818 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29051800 - MD-PALLIATIVE CARE DIVISION Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Palliative Care and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. M.D. degree or equivalent is required. Job Description: The University of Florida Department of Medicine is seeking a board-eligible or board-certified Palliative Care Physician to become an active and integral member of the team. This is a part-time 0.5 FTE appointment in the Department of Medicine. The successful candidates may perform inpatient attending duties, including consultations, as well as teaching and supervising medical students and residents. We enjoy a close collaborative relationship with UF Health Hospital and pride ourselves on professional and cooperative interdisciplinary care. Join an established interdisciplinary palliative care team that includes physicians, advanced practice providers, social work, and collaborating disciplines. Provide specialty palliative care inpatient consultation for a medically complex adult population at UF Health Shands Hospital. Collaborate across oncology, hospital medicine, critical care, cardiology, neurology, surgery, geriatrics, and other specialties. Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other health professional trainees. Participate in quality improvement, program development, and educational initiatives based on individual interests. Opportunities to collaborate with established investigators and participate in palliative care and serious illness research across the University of Florida. Faculty appointment at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level, commensurate with qualifications and academic experience. Requisite attributes include a commitment to service, a strong sense of teamwork and a desire to train tomorrow's doctors. The Gainesville area has superb weather, inexpensive living, nationally ranked schools, and multiple year-round recreational opportunities. We offer generous compensation. UF Health is one of the Southeast’s most comprehensive academic health centers and includes UF Health Shands, an 1,100-bed quaternary referral hospital, which consistently ranks among the top 50-hospitals by U.S. News & World Report in multiple specialties. In addition, the University of Florida is a top 5 public research university and includes substantial resources to support teaching, research, and collaboration, including one of only two Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI) hubs in the state of Florida. The CTSI features a Training and Research Academy for Clinical and Translational Science for early career faculty seeking to develop research skills and pursue careers as clinical investigators. About the area: The Gainesville, Florida region has an inexpensive cost of living, nationally ranked public middle and high schools, and superb weather with multiple year-round recreational opportunities including biking, hiking, kayaking, snorkeling, and cave diving. Pristine wilderness areas, coastal communities, famous theme parks, and other attractions are within a short driving distance. MSA Population: 330,000 Average High Temp in January: 66 F No State Income Tax Strong public and private schools 1 hour to Gulf of Mexico and Atlantic Ocean 2 hours or less to Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa Expected Salary: Academic rank and compensation will be commensurate with qualifications, years of experience, and applicable academic and specialty compensation benchmarks. Our compensation plan includes the opportunity to earn RVU-based incentives, allowing faculty to increase their compensation over time through increased clinical productivity. The University of Florida College of Medicine offers a comprehensive faculty benefits package, including health insurance and employer-supported retirement programs. Eligible College of Medicine clinical faculty have access to GatorCare health coverage, with premiums for the GatorCare Premium plan, including dependent coverage, paid by the College of Medicine. Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Palliative Care and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. M.D. degree or equivalent is required. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae, cover letter, and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541825/coordinator-of-programs-and-events": {
      "title": "Coordinator of Programs and Events",
      "description": "Coordinator of Programs and Events Apply now Job no: 541825 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 17050300 - BA-PURC Classification Title: Education/Training Spec II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Manage all aspects of admissions of PURC’s flagship 8-day training program, the PURC International Training Program on Utility Regulation and Strategy and One-Day Leadership Workshop, held jointly twice a year in Gainesville, Florida. The incumbent will manage all aspects of the admissions and extracurricular activities in collaboration with PURC faculty, staff and students. Duties include: Manage all aspects of applications and selection processes of participants in an accurate and timely manner: Maintain engaging, professional, informative, clear and timely communications with program applicants, potential applicants, participants and alumni before, during and after our courses. Manage initial contact with applicants, potential applicants, and continued follow-up to questions and concerns during the application and registration processes Interpret verbal and written communications with applicants and participants from different countries, different cultures and backgrounds, and different levels of written and oral knowledge of the English language Maintain applicant and participant databases for current and previous programs. Issue appropriate reports and interpret relevant data for other team members, faculty, associate director and director. Create and/or update all other associated documents for this purpose Contribute to session and program evaluation processes Work with other project team members, OPS staff and student assistants as needed to complete all project tasks such as applicant data entry, accurate and timely production of acceptance letters, invoicing, etc. for accepted participants, adjunct services or extra-curricular activities for programs Negotiate contracts and pricing with program vendors Responsible for extra-curricular activities for the program Serve as on-site coordinator and work to ensure participants’ needs are met during program deliveries. Requests range from assistance making medical appointments, arranging transportation, things to do in the area, etc. Contribute to program marketing with e-mail communications, mailers, some website updates, and/or other targeted campaigns such as WhatsApp, LinkedIn, etc. Track participant payments and related program expenses Manage all aspects pertaining to program speakers, program preparation and delivery logistics of PURC’s flagship training program, the PURC International Training Program on Utility Regulation and Strategy and PURC’s One-Day Leadership Workshop held twice a year, working in collaboration with PURC faculty, staff and students. Duties include: Responsible for general program management and setting project/program goals, milestones, tracking progress, etc. Manage course calendars, program time lines, on-site staff schedule, program evaluation reports, forms, and procedures in consultation, as needed, with the PURC program faculty Evaluate training effectiveness and achievement of training goals and make recommendations as needed in collaboration with other PURC team members Manage all aspects pertaining to program speakers, coordinating all communications with speakers, their travel, honoraria, reimbursements, presentation materials, hotel accommodations, etc. Create and update speaker tracking method for each program delivery Set speaker materials deadlines, compile training materials, format properly and ensure that presentations, handouts, etc. are ready for distribution for each program Schedule, organize and run program meetings, and ensure that all meeting decisions are carried out in an appropriate and timely manner Track overall program income and expenses, and update overall program income statement Negotiate contracts and pricing with program vendors, including meeting facilities Set up meeting needs ahead of time with training facilities. This may includes the review and update hotel banquet event orders, submit rooming list and attend pre-conference meetings with the hotel, etc. Serve as on‐site coordinator for the program and, in collaboration with PURC team, ensure that speaker and participant needs are met during the training deliveries. Requests range from assistance with presentations to making medical appointments, arranging transportation, etc. Manage on‐site program logistics such as loading presentations ready for display, ensure handouts and evaluations are distributed, meeting rooms are set up as needed and ready for the sessions, audio visual equipment is working properly, etc. Consult with Warrington College of Business instructional designers on teaching technologies and techniques Manage areas of or lead (other) existing or future PURC training events, research projects, and other PURC initiatives, as assigned. Duties include: Liaise with host institution’s representative(s) in an engaging and caring way to ensure that the project/event develops as agreed by PURC associate director and/or research faculty Contribute to the design, implementation, and follow up of all aspects of PURC initiatives, projects, programs, and events outside the content area Create and manage all aspects of event, project, or initiative to ensure that PURC standards of quality are met, and all deliverables, timelines, goals, etc. are met Create and update the project income statement, and track all income and expenses pertaining to the project/event Coordinate with event speakers or project researchers regarding all event/project/initiative materials, presentation materials, travel arrangements, etc. Compile and produce event/project/initiative materials as needed Contract and negotiate with training facilities as needed, and with other vendors Serve as on-site coordinator for event as required Coordinate all aspects of event attendee registration and registration process Coordinate office functions as needed Expected Salary: $53,000-$60,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will be engaging and client-focused, have excellent oral and written communications skills, great attention to detail, ability to plan well and meet deadlines, and have experience on training program/event administration. International experience or knowledge of other cultures is a plus. The Education and Training Specialist II will be required to work well independently and as part of a team. PURC often engages in new experimental initiatives. Adaptability and flexibility are important in this position and at PURC. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 11 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541828/assistant-professor-in-anglophone-poetry-studies": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Anglophone Poetry Studies",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Anglophone Poetry Studies Apply now Job no: 541828 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Humanities Department: 16200000 - LS-ENGLISH Classification Title: Assistant Professor in Anglophone Poetry Studies Minimum Requirements: PhD, and record of research/publication and teaching experience in any field or period of American, British, and/or global Anglophone poetry. Job Description: The Department of English at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor position to teach in the B.A. and Ph.D. programs in the field of Anglophone Poetry Studies, beginning August 16, 2027. We seek candidates who can contribute to the excellence of our community. The successful candidate will be expected to 1) maintain an active writing and publication career, 2) teach four courses per academic year at the undergraduate and graduate level, and mentor and advise undergraduate and graduate students, 3) contribute service to the Department, the University, and the profession. The Department offers innovative courses and programs at all levels and enjoys productive relationships with other units housed within and beyond the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, including the Center for the Humanities and the Public Sphere. UF is a major comprehensive, land-grant, research-intensive university, ranked in recent years as among the nation’s top public universities. Gainesville is a city of approximately 150,000 residents in Alachua County. It sits 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville. Its many nearby parks, springs, and recreational areas offer year-round outdoor activities such as hiking, biking, diving, and nature photography. Learn more at Visit Gainesville Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: PhD, and record of research/publication and teaching experience in any field or period of American, British, and/or global Anglophone poetry. Preferred: We are especially interested in candidates with expertise across periods, genres, and national literature. Our ideal candidate would also have demonstrated interest in critical and theoretical approaches to literature and culture. This is not a creative writing position, but courses in Anglophone poetry studies are likely to attract MFA students as well as undergraduates taking poetry workshops. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. For full consideration, applications must include: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae , including a list of three references, (3) a statement of teaching goals and practice. Application review will begin on December 1, 2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and/or hiring authority. After initial review, writing samples will be requested from selected applicants, as well as letters of recommendation from the listed references. We anticipate Zoom interviews in December or January and campus visits for finalists in the spring term. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
