    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541788/postdoctoral-associate-pulmonary": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Pulmonary",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Pulmonary Apply now Job no: 541788 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29050900 - MD-PULMONARY MEDICINE Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: The candidate will hold a Ph.D. in computer science, biomedical engineering, computational biology, systems biology, applied mathematics, or a closely related field. The candidate will have experience in scientific software development, computational modeling, simulation, digital twins, or workflow modeling as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications. Experience in the Python programming language is a requirement. Job Description: Dr. T.J. Sego is recruiting for one postdoctoral position in computational biological modeling. The postdoctoral position will develop, implement, calibrate, and validate digital twin architectures and computational workflows in critical care applications. The work will include developing reusable scientific software, model-format libraries, evolutionary ensemble methods, workflow twin testbeds, and dual-timescale refinement frameworks. The postdoctoral position is available immediately for a duration of one year and subject to annual renewal based on performance and availability of funds. The candidate will carry out research tasks aligned with project goals to produce deliverables following project timelines. They will lead development of resulting submissions to academic journals for scholarly publication. The candidate will also assist with scoping future projects and identifying opportunities for follow-up work based on project findings. They will contribute to the development of subsequent research questions, proposals, and related deliverables to support continued scholarly activity. The University of Florida is situated in beautiful Gainesville, FL, a thriving town with a low cost of living, outstanding community, access to numerous parks and recreational facilities, and year-round outdoor weather. Dr. Sego’s research focuses on development of computational biological models, simulation methods and software, and their application to problems in critical care, respiratory disease and immune response. As a member of the Laboratory for Systems Medicine, the candidate will work in a highly collaborative research environment that closely integrates quantitative, clinical and experimental research. Expected Salary: $65,000 annually Preferred Qualifications: The candidate will also have experience with collaborative software development practices, including version control, automated testing, continuous integration and continuous delivery pipelines, and reproducible computational workflows. Experience with systems biology model formats, including SBML or Antimony, evolutionary or ensemble-based computational methods, model calibration and validation, or performance and risk assessment is preferred. The candidate must have strong communication skills in the form of effective public speaking and clear, concise writing. Minimum Requirements: The candidate will hold a Ph.D. in computer science, biomedical engineering, computational biology, systems biology, applied mathematics, or a closely related field. The candidate will have experience in scientific software development, computational modeling, simulation, digital twins, or workflow modeling as demonstrated by peer-reviewed publications. Experience in the Python programming language is a requirement. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications should include a cover letter, updated curriculum vitae and contact information for three references. Relevant peer-reviewed publications by the applicant are appreciated. For more information or to apply, please contact Dr. T.J. Sego, timothy.sego@ufl.edu. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541789/certified-medical-assistant": {
      "title": "Certified Medical Assistant",
      "description": "Certified Medical Assistant Apply now Job no: 541789 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 35010800 - SH-NURSING STAFF Classification Title: Certified Medical Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: Successful completion of the Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) exam offered by an appropriate accredited institute. Appropriate institutes include the American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA), the National Center for Competency Testing (NCCT), the American Medical Technologists (AMT), the National Healthcareer Association (NHA), and the American Registry of Medical Assistants (ARMA). Job Description: The Opportunity The Student Health Care Center at the University of Florida is seeking a Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) for its ambulatory clinic. The CMA will assist health care professionals in the examination and treatment of ambulatory patients, such as preparation of the patient and environment for treatment, knowledge of conducting basic laboratory and electrocardiogram tests, care of the patient post-treatment functions. They will perform Point-of-Care testing as directed and will be responsible for general cleanliness of desks, counters, examination tables, equipment, and for ensuring appropriate cleaning, packaging (wrapping) and sterilization of surgical instruments. The Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) will assist the team RNs with patient care, obtaining vital signs, visual screening, heights and weights, peak flow readings and other objective data as appropriate for incoming patients and will assist clinicians with special procedures and examinations. Clinical Support Duties: • Performs CMA duties under the direct supervision of the Team RN and responsibility of a Physician • Standards Universal Precautions • Defers all medical questions to the RNs and/or medical providers. • Check Daily Temperature logs. • Order, stock, and maintain inventory of routine supplies and special items needed for the exam and • procedure rooms, patient care and assure update of sterile supplies. • Apply infection control guidelines in the clinic setting. • Application of age specific care to clinical practice. • Emergency equipment use clinic –specific emergency response plan • Assist in exams • Clean /sterilize equipment and exam rooms • Basic patient instructions per provider approved handouts • Telephone messaging • Alerts Team RNs or providers of any abnormal findings. • Performs point-of-care tests as requested. • Assists providers with procedures (minor surgery/pelvic exams). • Assists RNs with observation of patients. • Responsible for cleaning clinic area including counter tops, desks, exam tables, etc. • Responsible for autoclaving and wrapping instruments as needed. • Responsible for picking up clean linens and returning dirty linens for cleaning weekly from clinic area. • Order supplies from Central Supply and Pharmacy as needed. • Assist in training new CMAs. • Wear PPE (personal protective equipment) as appropriate, such as an N95 with level 2 surgical mask over, gown, gloves, googles or face shield. • Clean and disinfect exam rooms after each patient visit per SHCC policy/guidelines. PPE must be worn during this process. This includes everything that touches the patient: (surfaces, exam tables, BP cuff, Otoscope & Ophthalmoscope, Thermometers, Pt. chair etc., before bringing another patient to the room or area of screening. • Performs point-of-care tests as requested • Performs blood drawn requested by providers. • Performs and collects specimens for Drive-by patients. Medication Administration: • Pend and call in prescriptions • Prepare and administer medications as directed by a physician. • Explain treatment procedures, medications diets, or physician’s instructions to patients. • Medication administration which may include the following (except IV): • Authorize drug refills and provide prescription information to pharmacies. • Non-vaccine IM & SC injections (No dose calculations allowed) • Immunizations • Oral medications-simple dose calculations allowed • TST (PPD)administration and reading of result • Eye Drops • Nebulizer Treatment Procedures: • Sterile dressings • Sterile technique • Phlebotomy –routine, clinic specific • Remove sutures/staples • Specimen collection and processing • Ear Irrigations Other duties: • Responsible for routinely re-stocking exam rooms, cleaning exam rooms including desks, exam tables, and counters, and keeping rooms neat throughout the day. Assists with ordering supplies and equipment and restocking reserves as needed • Performs EKG's in Radiology department during athlete's physical exams PRN • Responsible for cleaning, wrapping and autoclaving instruments as needed, and participating in cleaning and maintaining the autoclave and autoclave room as per OSHA • Expected to huddle daily with RN, Physicians, and staff to anticipate and plan the Team’s needs; communicates with co-workers in a respectful and courteous manner • Rotates to other areas of the SHCC, provides Break Week coverage in SMAC Team and performs other tasks as assigned. Attends and participates in the HST / MA staff monthly meeting. Typical Schedule: • Monday through Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm OR weekdays adjusted per FTE needs • 40 hours/week • Hours and days can vary according to SHCC’s needs • Provides coverage for weekends and other clinical areas as assigned About the Student Health Care Center (SHCC) The mission of the SHCC is to help every student achieve optimal health in pursuing personal and academic success. Our vision is to be the preferred healthcare provider for University of Florida students as a nationally recognized leader in the collegiate health specialty. The SHCC is fully accredited by the AAAHC and is located in a state-of-the-art WELL-certified building on UF’s campus. Medical services offered at the SHCC include primary care, sports medicine, urgent care, gynecology, psychiatry, allergy/immunization clinic, and travel medicine. Radiology, pharmacy, lab, and physical therapy are also on site. Learn more about SHCC here https://shcc.ufl.edu/ About the University of Florida UF is one of the nation’s top universities and a dynamic and rewarding place to work. UF is in Gainesville, one of Florida’s most livable cities. Known for its natural attractions (wetlands, forests, springs, wildlife) and cultural offerings (music, theater, visual arts, and historic districts), the area also is gaining a reputation as a thriving hub for high-tech start-ups, as well as invention and research collaborations. We are excited about what’s happening here and know you will want to be a part of it. The City of Gainesville The Guide to Greater Gainesville shares all that you need to learn about the Heart of Florida, including neighborhood and city information, schools and zoning, culture and leisure, sports and fitness, healthcare and much more. Learn more at https://guidetogreatergainesville.com Expected Salary: $19.85 - $21.13 per hour Exclusive Perks and Benefits Enjoy stability and support with UF’s unique range of benefits and opportunities. With a robust healthcare package, generous leave policies, retirement planning, and professional development , you can achieve a healthy work-life balance, plan for your future, and advance your career. Full-time TEAMS employees will accrue vacation leave at a rate of 6.769 hours biweekly/22 days annually and sick leave at a rate of 4 hours biweekly/13 days annually. In addition, employees are eligible for eleven paid holidays each year. Required Qualifications: Successful completion of the Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) exam offered by an appropriate accredited institute. Appropriate institutes include the American Association of Medical Assistants (AAMA), the National Center for Competency Testing (NCCT), the American Medical Technologists (AMT), the National Healthcareer Association (NHA), and the American Registry of Medical Assistants (ARMA). Certified Medical Assistant (CMA) with at least one-year experience. BLS/CPR Certificate Preferred: A high school diploma and one year of appropriate experience. Emergency Medical Technician (EMT) licensure may be used in lieu CMA certification in special circumstances. One year of appropriate experience Knowledge of patient care. Knowledge of basic computer skills. Knowledge of sterile technique mandatory. Knowledge of services functions, policies and confidentiality procedures. Knowledge of phlebotomy. Knowledge of splits, braces, casting, and other equipment used in clinic. Skilled in basic math. Skills to perform CPR. Skills to maintain patient care equipment in operational order. Ability to communicate efficiently in writing and verbally. Ability to interact with patients. Ability to take vital signs (e.g. TPR, Blood Pressure, visual acuity, peak flow meters, height and weight). Ability to draw blood. Ability to give equipment instructions to patients. Ability to perform point of care lab tests and run quality controls. Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments. Special Instructions to Applicants: Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and a list of at least 3 references. Applications must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539795/fire-safety-coordinator": {
      "title": "Fire Safety Coordinator",
      "description": "Fire Safety Coordinator Apply now Job no: 539795 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Environmental/Occupational Safety Department: 71010000 - EHS-ENVR - HLTH - SAFETY-ADMIN Classification Title: Fire Safety Coordinator Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate in Science degree in Fire Science Technology and two (2) years of responsible experience with a governmental agency or private organization, including enforcement or design work to meet required fire regulations, fire prevention inspections or development, and fire plan check or design of fire systems, or 10 years as a Fire Marshal with a local municipality or state agency. Must be certified as a Fire Safety Inspector 1 in accordance with Florida Statute 633. Job Description: Inspects UF Buildings, Fraternities and Sororities for Fire Code Compliance and prepares reports to be submitted for correction of code violations. Reviews Fire Alarm Activity Logs to ensure proper maintenance of fire alarm systems in accordance with UF’s False Alarm Reduction program Supervises and assists with the Enhanced Radio System monitoring of all campus buildings. Provides assistance and oversight to the inspections of fire safety related items in new construction projects. Conducts public outreach for fire safety including providing fire extinguisher use training. Other duties as assigned. Must have a valid Florida Driver’s License. This position requires a post offer health assessment. Must be certified as a Fire Safety Inspector 1 in accordance with Florida Statute 633. This position is designated as an Essential Employee for the University, as the employee’s response immediately following an emergency event in which the continuation of critical and essential services is needed. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: Monday – Friday, 8 AM to 5 PM, or as approved by supervisor. 40 hours per week Occasional travel to offsite locations. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education & Experience ; $72,000 - $75,000 per year. Required Qualifications: Associate in science degree in Fire Science Technology and two (2) years of responsible experience with a governmental agency or private organization, including enforcement or design work to meet required fire regulations, fire prevention inspections or development, and fire plan check or design of fire systems, or 10 years as a Fire Marshal with a local municipality or state agency. Must be certified as a Fire Safety Inspector 1 in accordance with Florida Statute 633. Preferred: EDUCATION & TRAINNG: Bachelor’s degree in engineering, Architecture, Building Construction or Business Administration. NICET certification. EXPERIENCE: Five years supervisory experience. KNOWLEDGE: A thorough knowledge of the standard fire codes, blueprint reading and interpretation, construction terminology and techniques. SKILLS: Excellent written and oral communication skills. ABILITIES: Ability to interface with professionals such as architects, engineers, and construction workers. Ability to work with database reporting systems to ensure compliance with required codes. PHYSICAL QUALIFICATIONS: Repeated bending Climbing Areas of excessive noise Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540415/fiscal-assistant-ii-office-of-graduate-education": {
      "title": "Fiscal Assistant II - Office of Graduate Education",
      "description": "Fiscal Assistant II - Office of Graduate Education Apply now Job no: 540415 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29010801 - MD-GRADUATE STUDENT PROGRAMS Classification Title: Fiscal Assistant II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for required experience. Job Description: This position provides centralized business administration support for the Office of Graduate Education (OGE) including the Biomedical Sciences (BMS) program within the College of Medicine, functioning in a shared services capacity that supports all graduate education programs, trainees, and participating departments across the entire College. The role serves as a key operational resource for fiscal, payroll, and administrative functions, helping ensure consistent, efficient, and compliant business processes that support the Graduate School's mission. The position also supports Office of Graduate Education fiscal operations through procurement activities, invoice processing, purchasing, requisitions, and PCard reconciliation. Additional responsibilities include maintaining financial records, monitoring funding activity, supporting reporting and reconciliation processes, and assisting with onboarding and operational coordination activities for graduate students and trainees. As a central point of coordination for the college, this role partners with departmental administrators, COM-HR, COM Finance and Administration, the Graduate School, vendors, graduate students, trainees, and university offices to facilitate business operations and support graduate education activities across the College of Medicine. The position helps promote standardized processes, provides guidance on administrative procedures, and ensures compliance with university and college policies while delivering responsive customer service to a diverse group of stakeholders. About This Role: Payroll Distribution and Funding Administration Centralized Support Centralized support for the college to create and manage payroll distributions for BMS-supported graduate assistants (GAs) and OGE employees across the college. Monitor, reconcile, and update GA and pre-doctoral payroll distributions across all BMS cohorts. Coordinate cross-departmental and cross-college payroll support related to OGE and BMS funding allocation, including temporary funding coverage. Administer special funding sources and scholarship support, including Graduate School programs such as McKnight, McNair, FAMU, NSF, and other sponsored funding mechanisms. Process payroll distribution updates related to GA stipend increases, fringe benefit rate changes, and annual Pre-Doc compensation adjustments. Support the onboarding and offboarding of graduate assistant (GA) students by coordinating required documentation, facilitating appointment and payroll setup or termination, and ensuring compliance with university processes. Incorporate AI funding support allocations for participating departments and funding initiatives. Procurement, Finance, and Accounts Payable Serve as a centralized resource to process and reconcile invoices, purchase orders, requisitions, journal entries, and fiscal transactions in accordance with University of Florida and College of Medicine guidelines. Coordinate procurement activities and ordering for program and departmental operational needs. Perform PCard reconciliation and ensure timely and accurate documentation of expenditures. Coordinate travel, develop itineraries, and manage communication and expense reimbursement for graduate student and postdoctoral candidate recruiting visits. Monitor fiscal transactions, maintain supporting documentation, and assist with account reconciliation and expense tracking. Partner with vendors, departmental administrators, fiscal staff, and university offices to resolve procurement, payment, and transaction-related issues while serving as a primary point of contact for business operations support. Assist with monitoring available funding balances and ensuring expenditures align with approved budgets and funding sources. Tuition Waiver Administration Initiate, process, update, and monitor tuition waivers for BMS-supported GAs and Pre-Docs receiving institutional tuition support. Serve as the centralized resource to coordinate tuition waivers across departments and colleges to ensure accurate funding and student eligibility. Manage tuition waiver allocations associated with special funding programs and Graduate School scholarship initiatives, including McKnight, McNair, FAMU, NSF, and related programs. Incorporate AI funding support into tuition waiver processing for participating departments. Reporting Prepare reports, maintain financial records, and support audits related to payroll distributions, tuition waivers, and funding activity. Assist with fiscal planning and funding analysis to support OGE program operations and compliance requirements. Provide support for additional fiscal and administrative projects as assigned. Special Projects and Administrative Support Manage office necessities including supplies, mail, equipment, and security. Support the OGE Administrator with the annual asset management and space report. Collaborate with the Graduate Engagement and Outreach Coordinator to maintain and update the website, social media account(s), event calendar, and listservs related to activities with OGE. Collaborate with the Graduate Engagement and Outreach Coordinator to assist with planning and supporting student-centered events such as commencement, recruitment, and professional development activities. Support the overall operation of the Office of Graduate Education by performing a variety of tasks and responsibilities as assigned About the College of Medicine: The University of Florida's College of Medicine is committed to advancing health through education, research, and patient care. With a focus on innovation and excellence, the college prepares future healthcare leaders through a rigorous curriculum that combines basic sciences with hands-on clinical experience. The College of Medicine emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement, fostering and environment where students, faculty, and staff work together to improve healthcare outcomes. The College is home to cutting-edge research initiatives and state-of-the-art facilities, providing an exceptional training ground for aspiring medical professionals. Dedicated to enhancing health care, the College of Medicine plays an impactful role in shaping the future of medicine. For more information about the College of Medicine and its programs, visit College of Medicine . We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities: Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! Expected Salary: $20 - $22 per hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for required experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Knowledge of graduate assistant funding models, tuition waiver processes, and graduate education support programs preferred. Ability to analyze information, problem-solve, and exercise sound judgment in a complex operational environment. Experience supporting fiscal, payroll, and operational functions, preferably within a higher education, academic medicine, or research setting. Strong organizational skills with exceptional attention to detail and a commitment to accuracy and compliance. Ability to collaborate effectively with diverse stakeholders and provide high-quality administrative support across multiple programs or departments. Experience contributing to the efficient administration of graduate education, trainee support programs, or other complex academic operations. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships across organizational levels. Experience writing, documenting, or improving operational processes and procedures. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. This position is being reposted. All applicants are still being reviewed and need not reapply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541436/astasofull-prof-cardiology": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiology",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiology Apply now Job no: 541436 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050400 - MD-CARDIOLOGY Classification Title: Ast/Aso/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have a medical degree and Florida Medical License. Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Medicine, and Division of Cardiovascular Medicine is seeking a General Non-Invasive Cardiologist to become an active and integral member of the Division. Rank and Tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. Applicants should be board eligible or certified in Cardiovascular Medicine and echocardiography. Responsibilities will include general cardiology services at UF Health Hospital and Clinics. Clinical duties will include performance and teaching in general cardiology, outpatient clinic duties as well as cardiac imaging. Special areas of interest, such as cardio-oncology or sports cardiology, can be supported. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have a medical degree and Florida Medical License. Preferred Qualifications: Applicants must have an MD degree. Candidates must be U.S. or naturalized citizens and be proficient in spoken and written English. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541730/student-administrative-assistantmcguire-center": {
      "title": "Student Administrative Assistant—McGuire Center",
      "description": "Student Administrative Assistant—McGuire Center Apply now Job no: 541730 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 56021300 - NH-LEPIDOPTERA Classification Title: STU AST-NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements Current UF student Experience with Outlook, Excel, and Word Job Description: The McGuire Center for Lepidoptera & Biodiversity at the Florida Museum of Natural History is seeking a highly organized, dependable UF student to provide administrative support to the Center’s director, Dr. Akito Kawahara. Essential duties include: Coordinating calendars and meetings Organizing travel receipts and reimbursement documentation Maintaining digital files Tracking deadlines and follow-up tasks Approximately 10–15 hours/week, with flexibility around classes. Located at the McGuire Center, Gainesville. Expected Salary: $17-20/hour, depending on experience Required Qualifications: Current UF student Experience with Outlook, Excel, and Word Preferred Strong attention to detail Clear communication skills Discretion with confidential information Ability to work independently In-person work Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resumé, a brief statement of interest, and your weekly availability. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541737/communications-support-internship": {
      "title": "Communications Support Internship",
      "description": "Communications Support Internship Apply now Job no: 541737 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology Department: 14300000 - IT-ICT INFRA COMM TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: OPS Intern or Student Intern Classification Minimum Requirements: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: UF Information Technology (UFIT) is currently seeking a talented and highly motivated, part-time Communications Support Intern to join ICT, a unit within UFIT, for a year-long appointment. The intern will be responsible for supporting the setup, troubleshooting, and deployment of Cisco VoIP devices, performing analog troubleshooting and repairs, and maintaining thorough documentation of deployed devices across campus. Employment with UFIT provides an excellent opportunity to work with other dedicated employees focused on understanding and interpreting the technological needs of the University of Florida’s innovative and inspirational community. Based in Ayers Medical Plaza, the UFIT Communications Support team consists of four experienced Infrastructure Technicians and one Customer Service Representative. Together, they manage and support the comprehensive Cisco VoIP phone network across campus and within UF Health, along with analog devices, voice gateways (VGs), and essential emergency notification equipment, including classroom speakers, elevator phones, and all Blue Light Emergency phones on campus. The team also supports UF REC sites off-campus, collectively maintaining approximately 23,000 devices. Some key responsibilities and characteristics of this position are: Assist in phone configuration changes. Assist in the physical setup for new phone deployments. Assist in documentation for new phone deployments and phone updates. Assist in troubleshooting VoIP and analog issues. About UF Information Technology Led by Senior Vice President and CIO Elias Eldayrie, the nine departments comprising UFIT are: Academic Technology; AI² Center; Applications, Development, and Integrations; Business Center; Customer Experience and Resource Planning; Data Platform and Analytics; Information Security Office; Infrastructure and Communication Technology; and Research Computing. UF Information Technology (UFIT) enables teaching, learning, research, and service on campus and across the region with state-of-the-art enterprise IT systems, including SEC and SUS universities, and the opportunity to teach using HiPerGator. For more information about UFIT and its goals and governance, visit https://it.ufl.edu/about-ufit/strategic-plan/ . About the University of Florida One of America’s all-around best universities, the University of Florida drives future-making education, eye-opening discoveries, life-saving health care, and community-building collaboration for our state, our nation, and our world. UF is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from Florida’s West Coast, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support various activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: $15.00 per Hour Required Qualifications: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess the following education, experience, and skills: Proficiency in Microsoft Excel Exemplary communication/customer service skills Team collaboration Attention to detail Problem-solving skills Special Instructions to Applicants: A Level 2 Criminal Background Screening is required. Work visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This is not a remote work position and is located in Gainesville, FL. Applicants are required to submit with their application: Cover Letter Resume or CV Professional References (Minimum of 3) Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541738/microsoft-365-administration-internship": {
      "title": "Microsoft 365 Administration Internship",
      "description": "Microsoft 365 Administration Internship Apply now Job no: 541738 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology Department: 14300000 - IT-ICT INFRA COMM TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: OPS Intern or Student Intern Classification Minimum Requirements: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: UF Information Technology (UFIT) is currently seeking a talented and highly motivated, part-time Microsoft 365 Administration Intern to perform entry-level work on Microsoft 365 services within Infrastructure and Communication Technology, a unit within UFIT. This one-year, paid internship is designed to provide students currently enrolled in college an opportunity to learn practical skills and gain experience to propel them into a successful career in Information Technology. Employment with UFIT provides an excellent opportunity to work with other dedicated employees focused on understanding and interpreting the technological needs of the University of Florida’s innovative and inspirational community. This position is part of the ICT Collaboration Platforms Services team, which is responsible for administering the Microsoft 365 platform (including Exchange Online, OneDrive, Teams, SharePoint) for the UF community. This position is primarily focused on performing entry-level work on service request tickets for the Microsoft 365 services. The incumbent will work collaboratively with a team of experienced administrators on the delivery of services, and provide excellent customer service to the UF community. Some key responsibilities and characteristics of this position are: Working on assigned service request tickets related to Microsoft 365 services. About UF Information Technology Led by Senior Vice President and CIO Elias Eldayrie, the nine departments comprising UFIT are: Academic Technology; AI² Center; Applications, Development, and Integrations; Business Center; Customer Experience and Resource Planning; Data Platform and Analytics; Information Security Office; Infrastructure and Communication Technology; and Research Computing. UF Information Technology (UFIT) enables teaching, learning, research, and service on campus and across the region with state-of-the-art enterprise IT systems, including SEC and SUS universities, and the opportunity to teach using HiPerGator. For more information about UFIT and its goals and governance, visit https://it.ufl.edu/about-ufit/strategic-plan/ . About the University of Florida One of America’s all-around best universities, the University of Florida drives future-making education, eye-opening discoveries, life-saving health care, and community-building collaboration for our state, our nation, and our world. UF is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from Florida’s West Coast, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support various activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: $15.00 per hour Required Qualifications: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess the following education, experience, and skills: Punctuality Attention to detail Interpersonal and customer service Team collaboration Special Instructions to Applicants: A Level 2 Criminal Background Screening is required. Work visa sponsorship is not available for this position. This is not a remote work position and is located in Gainesville, FL. Applicants are required to submit with their application: Cover Letter Resume or CV Professional References (Minimum of 3) Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541749/neurologist-all-subspecialties-clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Neurologist (All Subspecialties) - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Neurologist (All Subspecialties) - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541749 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 30180000 - JX-NEUROLOGY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates should have a MD/DO in Adult Neurology Job Description: The Department of Neurology at the University of Florida College of Medicine-Jacksonville, seeks full-time faculty members at the non-tenure accruing level of Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor. The successful candidates will be BC/BE adult neurologists with or without specialty training (stroke, dementia, neurotrauma, neurohospitalist, epilepsy, neuromuscular, movement disorder, neuroimmunology, neurocritical care). Faculty positions are available for the Jacksonville campus. There are opportunities to modify patient-mix to subspecialty interests. The successful candidate is expected to participate in shared departmental clinical and teaching responsibilities. There are also opportunities to participate in various leadership roles, and advance in academic rank. This position will report to the Chairman of the Department of Neurology at the University of Florida College of Medicine-Jacksonville. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/. Expected Salary: Salary and academic appointment are negotiable and benefits are excellent. Required Qualifications: Candidates should have a MD/DO in Adult Neurology Preferred: Board eligible or board certified. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on a rolling basis until a competitive candidate pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541753/astasofull-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF Apply now Job no: 541753 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29051400 - MD-GASTROENTEROLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Fellowship trained in Gastroenterology Board certified or board eligible in Gastroenterology Eligible for medical licensure in the State of Florida Qualified for faculty appointment at the University of Florida at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level Job Description: The University of Florida Division of Gastroenterology, Hepatology & Nutrition is seeking Gastroenterologists to join our growing faculty in Gainesville, Florida. This is a full-time faculty appointment in the Department of Medicine at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. Academic rank and tenure status will be commensurate with the candidate's qualifications, experience, and accomplishments. The successful candidate will join a collaborative and growing division providing comprehensive care across the full spectrum of gastrointestinal disease. The position offers the opportunity to develop a gastroenterology practice that includes outpatient consultation, endoscopic procedures, and care of patients with a broad range of gastrointestinal conditions. Innovation and continued program growth are important priorities for the Division. Physicians interested in new approaches to gastrointestinal care, artificial intelligence, advanced technology, data-driven clinical care, quality improvement, or emerging diagnostic and therapeutic approaches will have access to the broader research and innovation infrastructure of the University of Florida. Faculty participate in the education and mentorship of gastroenterology fellows, residents, and medical students. Opportunities for clinical research, innovation, program development, and leadership are available based on the candidate's interests and experience. Why UF GI? The UF Division of Gastroenterology, Hepatology & Nutrition combines the breadth and complexity of a major referral-based GI program with the opportunity to shape what comes next. Our multidisciplinary programs span general and advanced gastroenterology, IBD, hepatology and liver transplantation, motility, pancreaticobiliary disease, and nutrition. With continued growth in clinical and endoscopy capacity, physicians can develop their practice and subspecialty interests while pursuing opportunities in education, research, innovation, program development, and leadership. Why UF Health? UF Health is one of the Southeast's most comprehensive health systems, anchored by a major quaternary referral center and supported by a growing network of hospitals, physician practices, and outpatient services across Florida. This expanding clinical enterprise provides access to a large and diverse patient population, complex referrals, multidisciplinary expertise, and opportunities to grow specialized programs and services. Physicians benefit from the resources of an academic health system while practicing within a rapidly growing clinical enterprise. Why the University of Florida? The University of Florida is one of the nation's leading public research universities, with more than $1 billion in annual research expenditures and extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration. UF has made artificial intelligence a university-wide priority, supported by HiPerGator, the nation's fastest university-owned and operated AI supercomputer. This environment connects medicine with expertise across AI, engineering, data science, technology, and other disciplines, creating exceptional opportunities for physicians interested in innovation, research, and the future of health care. Why Gainesville? Gainesville combines the energy and culture of Gator Nation with the quality of life of an accessible, family-friendly Florida community. Residents enjoy SEC athletics, a vibrant food, arts and music scene, strong schools, manageable commutes, and year-round outdoor recreation among North Central Florida's springs, rivers, trails, and state parks. Jacksonville, Orlando, Tampa, and both Florida coasts are within easy driving distance. Add year-round sunshine and no individual state income tax, and Gainesville offers plenty to enjoy beyond work. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Fellowship trained in Gastroenterology Board certified or board eligible in Gastroenterology Eligible for medical licensure in the State of Florida Qualified for faculty appointment at the University of Florida at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor level Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541755/assistant-vice-president-for-engagement": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice President for Engagement",
      "description": "Assistant Vice President for Engagement Apply now Job no: 541755 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management Department: 50010000 - AD-VP ADVANCEMENT Classification Title: Ast Vice President Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and eight years of appropriate experience. Job Description: The Assistant Vice President (AVP) for Engagement serves as the university's chief architect of constituent experience and the donor continuum. This executive leader is responsible for designing and governing a comprehensive strategy that guides alumni, donors, parents, students, volunteers, and friends through a lifelong journey of connection, engagement, philanthropy, and advocacy. The AVP leads the development of a modern, data-driven engagement ecosystem that leverages constituent insights, analytics, technology, artificial intelligence, and personalized engagement strategies to create meaningful experiences at scale. Through the integration of alumni relations, annual giving and donor relations, the AVP develops and manages a coordinated engagement strategy designed to strengthen institutional affinity, increase philanthropic participation, and help build a sustainable pipeline of future major and principal gift donors. As a senior Advancement leader, the AVP is responsible for ensuring that constituent engagement serves as a strategic driver of campaign success, donor pipeline development, revenue growth, and long-term philanthropic growth. The AVP partners closely with development, operations, communications, and academic leadership to create a seamless constituent journey that advances institutional priorities while delivering measurable engagement and fundraising outcomes. With the importance of engaging alumni and friends with meaningful communication, experiential, volunteer or philanthropic touchpoints, this position plays a critical role in strengthening the prospect pipeline, enhancing the constituent experience, and achieving institutional fundraising goals leading to the ability for Advancement to identify major gift prospects earlier, cultivate them more effectively, and allocate fundraising resources where they are most likely to produce results. To help foster deeper collaboration efforts and meaningful engagement with alumni, donors, and leadership, as well as members of our advancement staff, this will be an onsite position at our main campus in Gainesville, Florida. Strategic Leadership & Constituent Experience Provide vision, leadership, and strategic direction for the university's constituent experience strategy and engagement ecosystem Provide oversight of Alumni Relations, Annual Giving, and Donor Relations teams in alignment with the Engagement Ecosystem and the established organizational mission, vision and values. Serve as the institutional leader responsible for designing and optimizing the constituent journey across the entire donor continuum. Develop a comprehensive engagement strategy that aligns constituent experiences with advancement goals, campaign objectives, and institutional priorities. Establish a framework for guiding individuals from awareness and affinity to engagement, philanthropy, volunteer leadership, advocacy, and transformational giving. Create scalable engagement models capable of delivering personalized experiences to hundreds of thousands of alumni, donors, and friends. Ensure that engagement investments generate measurable advancement outcomes, including increased participation, donor retention, leadership annual giving growth, prospect identification, and major gift pipeline development. Position constituent engagement as a strategic enterprise function that supports fundraising success across the university. Champion the use of artificial intelligence in the day-to-day operations of the teams. Foster a collaborative and constituent-centered culture that promotes innovation, teamwork, service excellence, and alignment with the university’s mission and advancement priorities. Lead organizational planning and talent development efforts, including recruitment, performance management, succession planning, employee engagement, and leadership development to build and sustain high-performing teams. Oversee budgets, resource allocation and operational effectiveness. Champion a culture of continuous improvement by evaluating processes, implementing best practices, leveraging technology and constituent data, and driving operational efficiencies that enhance engagement, fundraising, and stewardship results. Engagement Strategy & Individualization Lead the design and execution of a bold, industry leading, integrated engagement ecosystem vision to scale individualized engagement across the University’s hundreds of thousands of constituents. Ensure alignment of alumni relations, annual giving, and donor relations within a lifecycle-based engagement continuum. Establish governance for data integrity, engagement definitions, and KPI tracking consistent with CASE standards. Leverage constituent data, behavioral insights, analytics, and emerging technologies to create relevant and timely engagement experiences. Champion the use of artificial intelligence, automation, predictive modeling, and digital engagement tools to enhance constituent relationships and organizational effectiveness. Establish audience segmentation, engagement scoring, constituent personas, affinity modeling, and journey orchestration strategies that improve engagement outcomes. Ensure that engagement experiences are coordinated across channels, programs, and constituent populations to create a seamless institutional experience. Donor Continuum & Pipeline Development Lead the development and governance of a comprehensive engagement strategy that intentionally cultivates future philanthropic support along the donor continuum. Design and oversee engagement pathways that move constituents from initial connection to increasing levels of philanthropic commitment. Develop strategies that increase the identification, qualification, and progression of future major prospects. Partner with development leadership to ensure engagement activities contribute directly to pipeline generation and fundraising performance. Establish systems for measuring the relationship between engagement behaviors and philanthropic outcomes. Work collaboratively to develop engagement and participation strategies that support comprehensive campaign goals and long-term fundraising objectives. Work collaboratively to ensure alignment between annual giving, leadership annual giving, major gifts, stewardship, and volunteer engagement strategies. Increase revenue through annual giving and alumni programs. Enterprise Collaboration & Influence Serve as a strategic partner across the university to advance institutional engagement and philanthropic goals. Collaborate with academic and administrative leadership to strengthen constituent engagement across the university. Partner with advancement operational teams to ensure accurate reporting using industry standards. Collaborate with Strategic, Communications, Marketing & Engagement (SCME) team on coordinated messaging across the engagement lifecycle. Partner with Development to ensure alignment of engagement strategies with campaign initiatives, major gifts pipeline and institutional priorities. University of Florida At the University of Florida , we are feeding communities, healing coastlines, exploring space, curing diseases, powering cities, building industries, and imagining wonders. As the state’s most comprehensive university, UF is a land, sea, and space grant institution with ties to every Florida county, a top academic medical center, and much more. The ideas born here gather steam and make tangible impacts. Florida is among the nation’s leaders in technology transfer, turning discoveries into companies, products, and jobs. Come join us—let’s see where we can go together. Gainesville, Florida Gainesville, Florida is a vibrant, welcoming city known for its natural beauty, rich culture, and strong sense of community. As home to the University of Florida and UF Health, it is a hub for education, healthcare, and innovation. With award-winning parks, a thriving arts scene, and a variety of dining and entertainment options, Gainesville offers something for everyone. Ranked the #1 city to move to in Florida by USA Today in 2024, Gainesville combines small-town charm with big-city amenities—making it a great place to live, work, and thrive. Visit the city’s website for community information. Expected Salary: Annual salary commensurate with education and experience. The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package including: Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future. Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave. Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and eight years of appropriate experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Minimum of seven to ten years of progressively responsible leadership experience in advancement, alumni relations, fundraising, donor relations, constituent engagement, or related fields. Experience in higher education, nonprofit, or mission-driven organizations. Experience managing complex constituent engagement and fundraising programs. Demonstrated success in strategic planning, team leadership, and program management. Demonstrated experience leveraging technology to increase organizational efficiency or effectiveness. Strong understanding of annual giving, stewardship, donor engagement, and alumni relations best practices. Familiarity with advancement CRM systems, donor analytics, and constituent engagement platforms as well as Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, various internet browsers and email platforms. Ability to respond effectively to change in a creative, demanding and fast-paced environment. Ability to be self-motivated and self-managing while collaborating closely with senior leadership and delivering the division and university vision. Demonstrated excellent attention to detail and work quality including the ability to deliver creative, high-quality work quickly, in a dynamic organization under time pressure. Ability to listen carefully and take into account stakeholder needs and react appropriately. Ability to manage multiple projects and prioritize work to maximize productivity. Effective time management and organizational skills are essential for success in this position. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement programs and activities for stakeholders, along with a talent for motivating staff, partners and volunteers. The ability to meet deadlines and work independently. Competency in problem solving with exceptional written and verbal communication skills. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, you must upload a cover letter and resume with completed application form. All requested materials must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work visa sponsorship is not available for this position Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541759/emergency-critical-care-assistant-care": {
      "title": "Emergency & Critical Care Assistant (CARE)",
      "description": "Emergency & Critical Care Assistant (CARE) Apply now Job no: 541759 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311001 - VM-ICU AND PCW SERVICES Classification Title: Veterinary Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: As part of our 24/7 service, this full-time (40-hour/week) role is essential to maintaining the “Gator Standard” of care across our ICU, ER, and Progressive Care wards. You will: • Support infection control through cleaning of all areas and equipment, sterilization, and stocking • Provide hands-on patient care (feeding, walking, grooming) • Assist DVMs and technicians with triage, restraint, intake, and documentation You’ll also gain unique experience with wildlife cases (overnights) and rotate through our Blood Bank service, learning the operations behind life-saving transfusions and working with our donor dogs. Why Join Us? We are committed to your growth. This role is an excellent foundation for the following but not limited to careers in: • Veterinary Technology (CVT/VTS) • Shelter/Charity work • Animal Behavior & Care Schedule The expectation is to work a 40-hour week comprising typically of 1 x 13hr shift and 2 x 13.5hr shifts. • The available schedule is Thursday, Friday, and Saturday daytime (6am – 8pm) • Alternating holiday shifts required - these are fairly shared amongst ECC staff Expected Salary: $15 Preferred Certified Veterinary Assistant (CVA) Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs , bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541760/associate-director-of-development-college-of-dentistry": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Development, College of Dentistry",
      "description": "Associate Director of Development, College of Dentistry Apply now Job no: 541760 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Dentistry Department: 34010501 - DN-DEVELOPMENT Classification Title: ASO DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in an appropriate area and one year of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience. Job Description: The Associate Director of Development is responsible for implementing a broad range of fundraising activities, primarily focused on soliciting and closing major gifts ($100K+) in support of the College of Dentistry. Reporting to the Senior Director of Advancement, the incumbent is responsible for assisting in developing and executing the fundraising program to fulfill the financial needs as identified by the Dean of the college, and in alignment with the strategic initiatives of UF Advancement. Portfolio Management Responsible for identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of mid-range gift prospects. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift level ($100K+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to close. Qualify prospects by contacting them, both in person and virtually, to evaluate their readiness to engage with the dental school and their interest in making a gift. Increase knowledge and grow support for UFCD campaign priorities. Develop support for UFCD by effectively involving stakeholders, such as faculty, staff, and volunteer, in effort to support fundraising and engagement initiatives. Collaborate with offices of Estate & Gift Planning and Corporate and Foundation Relations teams to develop and implement a giving strategy for qualified prospects/donors. Strategic Initiatives Work closely with the college’s Development Coordinator on development and alumni events. Initiate, plan and support activities and events involving volunteers, donors and prospects, both on campus and regionally. In coordination with the Senior Director of Advancement, attend college and UF Advancement functions to identify new individuals and move prospects through the donor continuum. These events include but are not limited to: Florida Dental Association Programming, Dental Fall Weekend, Grand Guard, Gator 100, President’s Suite, etc. Project management of Dentistry’s role in Gator Nation Giving Day. Coordinate all annual fund strategies and solicitations via central Annual Giving and internally with direct mail projects. Assist the Senior Director of Advancement in managing and executing plans and policies for the administration of the College’s overall development and alumni affairs program. Assist with writing content for proposals, annual giving campaigns, alumni magazine articles, and other development materials as needed. Administrative Effectively perform prospect tracking, portfolio management, record keeping, file maintenance, and reporting using the Ascend database. Work with development team to ensure all donors are properly recognized for their contributions. Draft correspondence to donors, prospects, alumni, and corporations for the development office and Dean. Ensure best practices in office systems, files, and record keeping. Participate in the planning and execution of Departmental alumni association events, including those focused on cultivating prospects and donors for college initiatives. Provide research, market, and prospect information for entry into the Foundation’s integrated database, and handle confidential prospect information in a sensitive, ethical manner. Expected Salary: $65,000 - $75,000, commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and one year of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Two to three years of major gift fundraising experience with proven success soliciting gifts of $25,000 and above strongly preferred. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively as a team player. Competency in problem solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; and excellent organizational abilities. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver’s license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541762/extension-agent-iii-family-and-consumer-sciences-health-and-nutrition": {
      "title": "Extension Agent I-II – Family and Consumer Sciences (Health and Nutrition)",
      "description": "Extension Agent I-II – Family and Consumer Sciences (Health and Nutrition) Apply now Job no: 541762 Work type: Permanent Status Faculty Location: Hendry Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60355026 - AG-HENDRY Classification Title: EXT AGENT I-II Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall hold a master’s degree and/or bachelor’s degree with relevant experience and accomplishments. Job Description: You Belong at a University That's Leading the Way The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (UF/IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Opportunity This full-time, 12-month, permanent status-accruing Extension Agent I or II position in Family and Consumer Sciences will deliver comprehensive programming focused on nutrition, chronic disease prevention and management, food literacy, and community health improvement across Hendry County. The successful candidate will also apply their expertise to strengthen youth nutrition education and food literacy in collaboration with 4-H programming (5%). This position is jointly funded by UF/IFAS Extension and the Hendry County Board of County Commissioners. Program Development and Delivery Design and implement research-based educational programs addressing adult nutrition, chronic disease prevention, and lifestyle management. Deliver hands-on food and nutrition education through teaching kitchen demonstrations and food preparation classes. Develop and deliver food literacy programming focused on budgeting, food safety, and meal planning. Promote efficient use of food resources to strengthen household and community resilience. Community Engagement and Partnerships Establish strong collaborations with County Health Departments, food distribution sites, senior centers, schools, faith-based organizations, and community organizations. Partner with Hendry County and UF/IFAS Extension faculty and stakeholders to explore development of a local farmer’s market in Hendry County. Build initiatives that promote local food systems and improve access to fresh, nutritious foods. Scholarship and Professional Responsibilities Document program impacts using established evaluation methods. Maintain accurate records and reports in compliance with UF/IFAS Extension standards. Document program outcomes and impacts using UF/IFAS Extension reporting systems. Participate in professional development, interdisciplinary teams, and applied scholarship appropriate to rank. Contribute to county and state Extension initiatives as assigned. About UF/IFAS UF Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences Extension is a federal, state, and county partnership dedicated to delivering science-based education that enhances quality of life and economic sustainability. Through research-driven programming, Extension connects university expertise with local communities to address critical issues in agriculture, natural resources, family and consumer sciences, and youth development. About UF/IFAS Extension UF/IFAS Extension has a longstanding tradition of empowering individuals and communities through practical, research-based education. County Extension offices serve as trusted local hubs, providing programming that strengthens families, improves health outcomes, and supports resilient food systems. Faculty collaborate with community partners, local governments, and agencies to ensure programming is responsive, innovative, and impactful. About Hendry County Hendry County is located in south Florida, on the southwest side of Lake Okeechobee and characterized by a strong agricultural heritage and rural landscape. Hendry County has a population of approximately 42,000 residents, with Clewiston and LaBelle serving as key population centers. The region is known for sugarcane production, cattle ranching, and vegetable farming, which shape both the economy and community life. Hendry County presents unique opportunities for Extension programming focused on food systems, nutrition, and health due to limited food access in some areas and a high need for chronic disease prevention education. Community institutions such as schools, senior centers, and health departments play a central role in outreach efforts. UF/IFAS Extension maintains a presence in the county, working collaboratively with nearby research and education centers to deliver impactful programming tailored to local needs. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in Family and Consumer Sciences, nutrition, public health, dietetics, health education, food science, or a closely related field (for Extension Agent I), or a master’s degree or at least 50% completion of a master’s program in a related field (for Extension Agent II). Ability to develop and deliver educational programs using research-based information. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to build collaborative partnerships with community organizations and stakeholders. Strong written and verbal communication skills with ability to produce scholar output and document program impacts. Ability to work independently and manage multiple responsibilities. Willingness to work flexible hours, including evenings and weekends. Ability to obtain a Florida driver’s license and ability to travel within the county. Successful completion of a Level 2 background screening through the Florida Department of Children and Families (DCF) due to youth programming responsibilities. Candidates must also have a commitment to the University of Florida’s core values. Preferred: A master’s degree in nutrition, public health, dietetics, family and consumer sciences, food science, or a closely related discipline. Experience delivering nutrition education or chronic disease prevention programs. Experience working with adult learners and rural populations. Demonstrated success in securing grants or external funding and conducting program evaluation. Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish is highly desirable given county demographics. Special Instructions to Applicants: Apply at Careers at UF by 11:55 p.m. (Eastern) of the posting end date, and attach: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541763/biological-scientist-i": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist I",
      "description": "Biological Scientist I Apply now Job no: 541763 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 28100000 - VM-INFECT DISEASE & IMMUNOLOGY Classification Title: Biological Scientist I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelors degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Job Description: The Aria Eshraghi Laboratory in the Department of Infectious Diseases and Immunology at the University of Florida College of Veterinary Medicine is seeking a highly motivated Research Technician to join a growing research program investigating bacterial pathogenesis, host-pathogen interactions, antimicrobial discovery, and vaccine development. Current projects examine the molecular mechanisms governing Francisella pathogenesis, respiratory metabolism, bacterial adaptation to arthropod vectors, and the genetic basis of bacterial host adaptation and vector colonization. The successful candidate will perform molecular biology, microbiology, and cell biology experiments; conduct bacterial genetic screens; characterize mutant phenotypes; and perform mammalian and arthropod cell culture and vector-infection studies. Additional responsibilities include analyzing experimental data, contributing to manuscript and grant preparation, maintaining research records, preparing reagents, assisting with laboratory organization and equipment maintenance, and supporting the training of junior laboratory members. The position is well suited for a detail-oriented and intellectually curious candidate with experience in microbiology, bacterial culture, molecular cloning, PCR, DNA/RNA extraction, gel electrophoresis, cell culture, infectious disease, immunology, host-pathogen interactions, or vector biology. The laboratory provides opportunities to develop skills in experimental design, data analysis, scientific writing, grant development, and collaborative research. Expected Salary: $22.03 / hourly Required Qualifications: Bachelors degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Preferred: • Experience with microbiology, bacterial culture, molecular cloning, PCR, DNA/RNA extraction, gel electrophoresis, or cell culture. • Experience with infectious disease, host-pathogen interactions, immunology, or vector biology. • Experience with quantitative data analysis and scientific writing. • Demonstrated initiative, attention to detail, and enthusiasm for research. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is time-limited. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541764/laboratory-technician-iii": {
      "title": "Laboratory Technician III",
      "description": "Laboratory Technician III Apply now Job no: 541764 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 28060000 - VM-COMP, DIAG & POP MEDICINE Classification Title: Laboratory Technician III Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of related experience or a high school diploma and six years of related experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Job Description: Research Related Activities: Conduct biological research in live specimens in both controlled and clinical settings and will assist in the dissection of cadaver specimens. Collect, process, and store blood and tissue samples. Maintain knowledge of various equipment within the laboratory and monitor maintenance of all laboratory equipment to ensure accuracy and quality of experimental results. Manage various laboratory experiments by assisting with data collection and analysis. Coordinate sample collection and organize data on spreadsheets. Assist with grant proposals and preparing publications and presentations. Assist with clinical research, which may include client-owned animals. Communicate appropriately with clients when performing clinical research. Responsible for monitoring the health status of research animals, providing routine care as dictated by research projects, and coordinating and supervising support personnel to help with animals and projects. Act as a liaison between the PI’s and Animal Care Services, and IACUC. Assist with drafting IACUC applications and following them through the approval process. Maintaining accurate records of IACUCs. Maintain accurate records of grants’ budget and purchases. Teaching Related Activities: Assist in training of veterinary students in several laboratories involving anesthesia, including labs for the small and large animal surgery courses (VEM5402 and VEM5422) and anesthesia courses (VEM5470 and VEM5473) for both spring and fall semesters. Ensure all necessary equipment is in proper working condition and paperwork is provided to the students. Oversee students with drug calculations, and provide drugs to students in an organized fashion while logging all controlled substances. Assist students with handling of dogs and horses for small procedures, such as intravenous catheterization and orotracheal intubation. Order materials needed for laboratories, assist with budget requests for student labs, and complying with both safety standards and IACUC standards for all labs involving the use of animals. Assist in the development and execution of anesthesia simulation training for both veterinary students and house officers. Assist veterinary students assigned to independent research projects under faculty mentorship with the execution of their projects. Assist in the instruction of students to conduct research, with emphasis on suitable care of animals in a lab setting and safe and proper use of laboratory methods. Administrative Related Activities: Assist in general management of two laboratories and supervise graduate students, postdocs, house officers, and other students working within the labs. Maintain a rapport with various regulatory offices on campus, including IACUC, EH&S, and DSR. Maintain familiarity with interactive websites associated with these regulatory offices, such as myIACUC, AALAS Learning Library, and Gator TRACS. Act as liaison between lab and fiscal office. Ensure compliance with UF’s accounting and purchasing policies and procedures. Assist with management of grants, course budgets, and other sources of funding. Maintain precise records. Manage all aspects of ordering for labs, including placing orders, ensuring proper arrival, and charging to the appropriate account. Manage lab codes and safety regulations, including disposal and record keeping of hazardous chemicals and frozen animal tissues. Maintain a detailed log of laboratory activities. Reorganize the laboratory space and keeping the laboratory organized. Updating the inventory of equipment, instruments, and chemicals. Expected Salary: $22.03 - $22.99/hr Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of related experience or a high school diploma and six years of related experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Preferred: 1. Prefer experience handling and anesthetizing domestic animals (dogs, cats, small and large ruminants, pigs, ponies, and horses), laboratory animals (non-human primates, rats, guinea pigs, ferrets, rabbits, hamsters), exotic animals, and wildlife (iguanas, alligators, snakes, birds, cheetahs, cougars, bears, wild ruminants). 2. Knowledge of various anesthetic machines and monitoring equipment. Knowledge of anatomy, dissection techniques, and animal tissue handling. 3. Previous experience in a teaching role instructing undergraduate 4. Excellent communication, time management, and organizational skills. Experience with Windows and MacOS; Microsoft Word; Excel; Power Point; and statistical analysis software. 5. Ability to oversee execution of lab experiments and assist with reviewing and analyzing results. 6. Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments 7. Ability to conduct scientific experiments with minimal supervision, in accordance to stipulated protocols while adhering to safety standards. 8. Ability to maintain accurate records and data 9. Ability to work and solve problems with minimal supervision, but also to follow detailed written and verbal instructions. 10. Ability to multitask. 11. Ability to establish an effective working relationship with other members of the Department and with peers outside of the Department. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541765/assistant-associate-full-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate/ Full Professor",
      "description": "Assistant / Associate/ Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541765 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Engineering Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Assistant / Associate/ Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: A PhD in Biomedical Engineering or a related field. Job Description: The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering (BME) in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering (HWCOE) at the University of Florida invites applications for up to two full-time, nine-month tenure-track faculty positions. This position is targeted for the rank of associate and full professor; however, exceptional faculty with experience close to this level may be considered. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Florida is a leading institution dedicated to pioneering innovative biomedical solutions for healthcare improvement in Florida and globally. UF BME uniquely benefits from UF's exceptionally fertile environment, as one of only a handful of universities with co-located, top-ranked colleges of engineering, medicine, veterinary medicine, and dentistry, along with robust infrastructure in engineering, biotechnology, and artificial intelligence. UF BME faculty are working in highly innovative research areas that fully leverage recent advancements in biotechnology, AI, and clinical care. The activities of our faculty range from collaborations with clinicians and the translation of their products to patients to discovery-based work creating new materials and approaches to more effectively treat or cure various pathologies. For this recruitment cycle, key research areas of emphasis include: 1) Holistic, AI-Driven Precision Healthcare (e.g., AI-driven population segmentation for therapeutics, AI approaches in omics, computational pathology & imaging); 2) Engineering Healthspan (e.g., engineering approaches addressing aging, mobility, biomechanics, and pain); and 3) Immune instructive biomaterials (e.g., cancer immunotherapy, autoimmunity, anti-inflammatory). Our department already has a strong international presence in these research areas; thus, recruited faculty will join a comprehensive research ecosystem that will further amplify their work. Faculty members will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses within Departmental curricula, initiate and sustain externally sponsored research programs, recruit and supervise graduate students, and engage in service activities for the University and the profession. Applicants must possess a PhD in Biomedical Engineering or a related field. Evidence of dedication to excellence in research and scholarly achievements, teaching, and service is required. The candidate should have a record of successful proposal writing, PhD student mentoring, scholarship, and classroom teaching. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Florida (https://bme.ufl.edu/) is home to 30 primary and more than 70 affiliated faculty members advancing every area of biomedical engineering, with continued growth expected in the coming years. The department supports over 500 students, including more than 130 pursuing Ph.D. degrees. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: A PhD in Biomedical Engineering or a related field Preferred: Evidence of dedication to excellence in research and scholarly achievements, teaching, and service is required. The candidate should have a record of successful proposal writing, PhD student mentoring, scholarship, and classroom teaching. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to receive applications until the position is filled. A priority deadline is December 7 th . Applications must be submitted through UFCareers at: https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/listing/ (Search Job 541765 ). Complete applications must include the following files in PDF format: (1) cover letter specifying research area; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) the names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of no less than three and up to five references. ( Additional required documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” section in the application .); (4) a research program vision statement detailing short- and long-term goals; (5) a teaching statement describing teaching experience and vision for developing a teaching program at the University of Florida; (6) up to three representative refereed publications (co-)authored by the applicant. Competitive candidates are expected to clearly complement the overall mission of the department. The anticipated start date for this position is as early as Fall 2027, with some flexibility for a later start date based on individual needs. Priority consideration will be given to applications received by December 5 th , 2026 . Please direct questions about the position to Mingzhou Ding, Faculty Search Chair, email: MDing@bme.ufl.edu The University of Florida (UF) is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities. UF is home to HiPerGator , the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education, and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering has undergone a major expansion in faculty, research, and facilities with a strong focus on artificial intelligence. The new Malachowsky Hall for Data Science & Information Technology is a 260,000-square-foot facility that serves as the hub for AI. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Additional Requirements The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. Transcripts marked “Issued to Student” will not be considered official. Degrees earned outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). Additional Information The University and College count among its greatest strengths a community of faculty, students and staff having diverse perspectives who support a culture of respect and dignity where learning and research thrive. UF is committed to equal educational opportunity and access. If you require a reasonable accommodation to apply, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) . Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. All searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541766/data-scientist-i": {
      "title": "Data Scientist I",
      "description": "Data Scientist I Apply now Job no: 541766 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Computer Science Department: 29140600 - MD-SURG-POPULATION HEALTH SCI Classification Title: Data Scientist I Classification Minimum Requirements: A Bachelor’s Degree in computer or physical science, statistics, bioinformatics, analytics, or similar field and two years of experience; Master’s Degree in computer or physical science, statistics, bioinformatics, analytics, or similar field. Job Description: Responsible for designing, developing, maintaining, and supporting software applications, computational workflows, and data systems for biomedical and clinical research. The position will apply principles of computer science, software engineering, data engineering, and computational methods to support the PLuS2 project and future research initiatives. Key responsibilities include developing research software and data-processing pipelines, integrating heterogeneous research datasets, automating analytical workflows, implementing computational and statistical methods, creating data visualizations, conducting quality assurance activities, and developing reproducible research tools. The individual will collaborate closely with investigators, biostatisticians, clinicians, and research staff to translate research needs into reliable, scalable, and efficient computational solutions that support research operations and scientific discovery. Duties will include: Research Software Development and Computational Tools Design, develop, test, maintain, and document research software applications, scripts, APIs, and computational tools that support biomedical and clinical research projects, including the PLuS2 project and related future research initiatives. Ensure software solutions are reliable, scalable, secure, and well documented. Research Data Engineering and Computational Pipelines Develop, implement, and maintain automated and reproducible data-processing pipelines for integrating, transforming, cleaning, validating, and managing clinical, electronic health record, registry, survey, imaging, and other research datasets. Ensure data quality, integrity, security, and reproducibility in accordance with research and institutional standards. Natural Language Processing and Artificial Intelligence Develop and apply natural language processing, machine learning, and artificial intelligence methods to research data, including extraction and analysis of information from clinical notes, medical records, survey responses, and other unstructured data sources. Create computational models, AI-based applications, and text-processing pipelines to support research objectives and improve research efficiency. Computational Analysis and Research Applications Collaborate with investigators, biostatisticians, and research staff to implement computational and statistical methods that address research questions and study objectives. Develop analytical workflows, predictive models, data visualizations, and other computational solutions to support data analysis, interpretation of findings, and dissemination of results. Software Automation and Research Infrastructure Develop, maintain, and enhance automated research workflows and computational infrastructure to improve the efficiency, scalability, and reproducibility of research activities. Integrate databases, APIs, cloud-based resources, and research systems as needed to support ongoing and future projects Expected Salary: $61,000 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in computer or physical science, statistics, bioinformatics, analytics, or similar field and two years of experience; master’s degree in computer or physical science, statistics, bioinformatics, analytics, or similar field. Preferred: Master's degree in computer science, Computer Engineering, Data Science, Bioinformatics, Computational Science, or a related field. Experience developing software or computational tools for biomedical, clinical, scientific, or research applications. Proficiency in one or more programming languages such as Python, R, Java, C++, or JavaScript/TypeScript. Experience with software engineering principles, Git/version control, testing, debugging, APIs, databases, and software documentation. Experience designing and implementing data-processing or ETL pipelines and working with large or heterogeneous datasets. Experience with relational databases and SQL, including database design, querying, and data integration. Experience with machine learning, statistical computing, data visualization, or other computational research methods. Experience developing reproducible research workflows and automated computational pipelines. Experience working in Linux, cloud computing, high-performance computing, containers, or research computing environments is desirable. Experience collaborating with researchers, biostatisticians, clinicians, or interdisciplinary research teams Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: no Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541768/ops-harn-museum-security-visitor-engagement-assistant": {
      "title": "OPS Harn Museum Security + Visitor Engagement Assistant",
      "description": "OPS Harn Museum Security + Visitor Engagement Assistant Apply now Job no: 541768 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums Department: 57801300 - HN-SECURITY Classification Title: OPS Hourly Non-Secretary/Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma, or its equivalent, and one year of related experience. Job Description: Greet and welcome guests to the museum Professional and positive interaction with all visitors and staff of the museum Provide excellent customer service and provide general and directional information to guests Perform routine patrol of galleries and public spaces Monitor guest compliance with museum policies and report non-compliance through verbal and written communication Provide front desk coverage on an as-needed basis Provide escorts as-needed to secure areas within the museum Assist with setup and breakdown of equipment, chairs, and tables for museum activities Practice de-escalation techniques when dealing with aggressive or disruptive behavior Professionalism, reliability, dependability and adherence to schedules are required Other duties as assigned Expected Salary: $15 per hour Required Qualifications: Flexibility for varied schedules to include nights, weekdays, weekends, and holidays based on the needs of the museum. Preferred: Previous experience in a security and/or customer service role Ability to work as part of a team and project a positive attitude Excellent verbal and written communication skills Experience working and communicating with individuals of diverse backgrounds Knowledge of basic first aid, or First Aid/AED certification Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload a resume with your online application. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541771/lecturersenior-lecturermaster-lecturer": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Master Lecturer",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Master Lecturer Apply now Job no: 541771 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Engineering Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Master Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in biomedical engineering or bioengineering, or a closely related discipline. Job Description: The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering in The Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering at the University of Florida seeks to hire a twelve-month, full-time, non-tenure track faculty to teach undergraduate and graduate students in core Biomedical Engineering courses. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Florida seeks instructional faculty members to contribute to our rapidly growing and evolving undergraduate (ABET accredited) and graduate programs through excellence, innovation, and leadership in engineering education. This 12-month, non-tenure track, full-time position is at the rank of either Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, or Master Lecturer with working titles of Instructional Assistant Professor, Instructional Associate Professor, or Instructional Full Professor. For more information on our department, please visit: https://bme.ufl.edu/ . The University of Florida (UF) is a major public research university. The state’s oldest and most comprehensive university, UF is among the nation’s most academically diverse public universities. As a land-, sea- and space-grant institution, UF is dedicated to serving the interests of society and is an economic powerhouse behind Florida’s economy. The Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering (HWCOE) at the University of Florida houses one of the largest and most dynamic engineering programs in the nation, producing leaders and problem-solvers who take a multidisciplinary approach to innovative and human-centered solutions. HWCOE also has a new Department of Engineering Education, which specializes in engineering education research and the delivery of innovative and effective instructional methods in engineering undergraduate courses, as well as assessment. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering at the University of Florida is a leading institution dedicated to pioneering innovative biomedical solutions for healthcare improvement in Florida and globally. The Department has strengths in the areas of biomaterials and regenerative medicine, biomedical data science, imaging diagnostics and therapeutics, and neural engineering. BME faculty benefit from close collaborations with the College of Medicine, College of Veterinary Medicine, and the College of Liberal Arts and Life Sciences adjacent to our building. Faculty and students in BME also interact with clinicians at the Malcolm Randall VA Medical Center, located south of campus. As a faculty member at UF-BME, you will benefit from access to a wealth of resources, including state-of-the-art research facilities and a wide array of internal and external professional development programs. Institutes such as the Institute for Excellence in Engineering Education (IE3), the Center for Instructional Technology and Training (CITT), and the Department of Engineering Education provide a host of resources in engineering education research, the delivery of innovative and effective instructional methods, and engineering education assessment. Together, these programs and facilities provide a vibrant environment supportive of cutting-edge biomedical engineering education and research. UF also offers competitive benefits packages, including affordable, high-quality health care and retirement plans, and a family-friendly work-life to ensure faculty success and well-being. The faculty member will instruct selected biomedical engineering courses, engage in service, and explore research initiatives associated with engineering education. Preference will be given to candidates whose expertise and/or teaching abilities fit the current needs of the department. Our highest priority is lab-based instruction in cell culture, tissue engineering, biomaterials, and microscopy. Other core biomedical engineering courses are of interest, including biomedical instrumentation, biosystems and signals, engineering design, and programming. This position offers a unique opportunity to shape the next generation of biomedical engineers by participating in curriculum development and maintaining ABET accreditation, engaging in innovative teaching methods, and collaborating across the campus on impactful educational research. Successful submission and funding of engineering education grants will be encouraged. The faculty member will fully participate in department, university, and professional service activities, which are recognized and celebrated with clear promotion tracks available for non-tenure instructional ranks. Although applications will be accepted until the position is filled, the priority deadline for full consideration is December 1 st , 2026. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in biomedical engineering or bioengineering, or a closely related discipline. Preferred: Candidates with an undergraduate degree in biomedical engineering or a closely related discipline are highly desirable. Applicants must have an outstanding record of academic accomplishments, a strong interest in undergraduate teaching in biomedical engineering, and a commitment to professional service (e.g., through participation in professional societies). The successful candidate will be expected to teach biomedical engineering undergraduate lectures and laboratory courses, collaborate with faculty in and outside the department, and be involved in service to the university and the profession. Previous experience with teaching core laboratory courses typical to an undergraduate biomedical engineering curriculum instruction is highly desirable. Industry experience is also valued. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to receive applications until the position is filled. All applications must be submitted through UFCareers at: https://jobs.ufl.edu/ ( Please see Job Requisition #541771 ) . Complete applications must include the following files in PDF format: (1) cover letter (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) Teaching Philosophy statement, and (4) the names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of no less than three and up to five references. Any other supplemental materials in support of the application may be uploaded as a PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. The University of Florida (UF) is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities. UF is home to HiPerGator , the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education, and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering is undergoing a major expansion in faculty, research, and facilities with a strong focus on artificial intelligence. In Fall 2023, UF opened Malachowsky Hall for Data Science & Information Technology , a 260,000-square-foot facility that serves as the hub for AI. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Additional Requirements The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. Transcripts marked “Issued to Student” will not be considered official. Degrees earned outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Additional Information The University and College count among its greatest strengths a community of faculty, students and staff who support a culture of respect and dignity where learning and research thrive. If you require a reasonable accommodation to apply, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) . Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. All searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541772/academic-faculty-affairs-support-coordinator-college-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Academic & Faculty Affairs Support Coordinator - College of Nursing",
      "description": "Academic & Faculty Affairs Support Coordinator - College of Nursing Apply now Job no: 541772 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 31000000 - COLLEGE-NURSING Classification Title: Academic & Faculty Affairs Support Coordinator - College of Nursing Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Academic & Faculty Affairs Support Coordinator provides comprehensive administrative, operational, and programmatic support for faculty affairs, academic operations, clinical education, and instructional programs within the College of Nursing. This position serves as a central point of coordination among faculty, academic leadership, students, clinical partners, and university stakeholders to ensure the effective delivery of academic programs, seamless clinical placement processes, and compliance with college, university, regulatory, and accreditation standards. The coordinator supports a broad range of academic and faculty-related functions, including faculty workload administration, course and instructional scheduling, clinical education coordination, faculty appointment and evaluation processes, recognition and promotion activities, accreditation support, and data management. The role also contributes to continuous quality improvement initiatives and operational excellence that advance the College's educational, clinical, and strategic mission. FACULTY SCHEDULING, WORKLOAD & INSTRUCTIONAL SUPPORT • Coordinate faculty teaching assignments, instructional schedules, and workload activities across didactic, simulation, laboratory, online, and clinical learning environments. • Support instructional planning, course staffing, workload reporting, and coverage strategies to ensure effective course delivery and efficient resource utilization. • Maintain faculty workload systems, instructional records, and related reporting while serving as a resource for faculty and leadership on scheduling and instructional operations. • Assist with instructional continuity planning, course readiness activities, and communication of teaching-related schedules, deadlines, and updates. ACADEMIC OPERATIONS, COURSE COORDINATION & ACCREDITATION SUPPORT • Coordinate academic operational activities including course scheduling, academic calendars, examinations, instructional logistics, and curriculum-related initiatives. • Collaborate with faculty, program leadership, simulation teams, clinical partners, and academic support units to ensure effective course delivery and operational efficiency. • Support accreditation, assessment, regulatory compliance, and program review activities through coordination of documentation, data collection, and reporting. • Assist with implementation of new academic initiatives, process improvements, and operational projects that support student success and program effectiveness. FACULTY AFFAIRS, EVALUATION & RECOGNITION SUPPORT • Coordinate faculty evaluation, Faculty Evaluation and Advancement (FEA), promotion, recognition, and awards processes. • Support faculty affairs initiatives in collaboration with academic leadership and university partners. • Maintain faculty records, submission calendars, and tracking systems. • Serve as a liaison among faculty, departments, and administrative offices. CLINICAL PLACEMENT & ACADEMIC PARTNERSHIP COORDINATION • Coordinate student clinical placement activities, including assignment processes, scheduling, tracking, and compliance requirements. • Serve as a liaison with healthcare systems, clinical agencies, preceptors, and internal stakeholders. • Maintain clinical placement records, data systems, and reports. • Assist with affiliation agreement coordination, agency communications, and operational activities related to clinical education. Expected Salary: $53,000 - $63,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred Qualifications: • Master's degree in a related field. • Demonstrated experience supporting faculty scheduling, instructional coordination, or academic operations. • Familiarity with faculty affairs processes, instructional appointments, or academic workforce management. • Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple schedules, deadlines, and priorities simultaneously. • Experience with faculty workload tracking, effort reporting, or academic labor allocation. • Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective partnerships. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541774/research-computing-support-and-applications-internship": {
      "title": "Research Computing Support and Applications Internship",
      "description": "Research Computing Support and Applications Internship Apply now Job no: 541774 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology Department: 14500000 - IT-RC RESEARCH COMPUTING Classification Title: OPS Intern or Student Intern Classification Minimum Requirements: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: UF Information Technology (UFIT) is seeking two talented and highly motivated, part-time Support and Applications Interns to join Research Computing, a unit within UFIT for the summer semester. This semester-long, with a possibility of extension, paid internship is designed to provide students currently enrolled in college an opportunity to learn practical skills and gain experience to propel you into a successful career in Information Technology. Employment with UFIT provides an excellent opportunity to work with other employees dedicated and focused on understanding and interpreting the technological needs of the University of Florida’s innovative and inspirational community. The internship location is the New Physics Building on the main campus. Some key responsibilities and characteristics of this position are: Development of support-related scripts/tools/databases/web applications Installation, troubleshooting, and HPC adaptation of research applications on HiPerGator Support service request triage Basic HPC user support via the ticketing system and Slack Some key learning outcomes for the student intern are: Programming experience (support tool and web app development) Practical understanding of contemporary research computing infrastructure and applications HPC application installation skills Application troubleshooting and modification Identification, documentation, and execution of project tasks and performance objectives Developing customer service and communication skills that demonstrate openness, sensitivity, and the ability to interact respectfully with all people About UF Information Technology Led by Senior Vice President and CIO Elias Eldayrie, the nine departments comprising UFIT are: Academic Technology; AI² Center; Applications, Development, and Integrations; Business Center; Customer Experience and Resource Planning; Data Platform and Analytics; Information Security Office; Infrastructure and Communication Technology; and Research Computing. UF Information Technology (UFIT) enables teaching, learning, research, and service on campus and across the region with state-of-the-art enterprise IT systems, including SEC and SUS universities, and the opportunity to teach using HiPerGator. For more information about UFIT and its goals and governance, visit https://it.ufl.edu/about-ufit/strategic-plan/ . About the University of Florida One of America’s all-around best universities, the University of Florida drives future-making education, eye-opening discoveries, life-saving health care, and community-building collaboration for our state, our nation, and our world. UF is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from Florida’s West Coast, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support various activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: $18.00 per hour Required Qualifications: MUST BE AT LEAST 18 YEARS OF AGE UF Student Assistant This position is open to all students eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. OPS Intern Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess the following education, experience, and skills: ● Strong communication skills ● Familiarity with Linux and command-line use ● Research or programming experience in a variety of programming languages, python knowledge is required ● HiPerGator use experience Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants are required to submit with their application: ● Cover Letter ● Resume or CV ● Professional References (Minimum of 3) A (Level 1) Criminal Background Screening is required. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541775/harn-museum-ops-visitor-services-assistant": {
      "title": "Harn Museum OPS Visitor Services Assistant",
      "description": "Harn Museum OPS Visitor Services Assistant Apply now Job no: 541775 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 57808100 - HN-VISITOR ENGAGEMENT ADMIN Classification Title: OPS Hourly Secretary/Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma, or its equivalent, and available to work Sundays, 1:00 - 5:00 pm Job Description: Job responsibilities include: Providing support for visitor‑access operations at the museum Engaging daily with guests both on-site and by phone Offering courteous customer service and sharing information about the museum and the city of Gainesville Assisting with coat‑check and visitor property procedures manage lost‑and‑found items Help museum staff gather demographic and attendance data, keeping accurate daily logs and survey records. Expected Salary: $15 per hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma, or its equivalent, and available to work Sundays, 1:00 - 5:00 pm Preferred Qualifications: The successful candidate will possess the following qualities: Excellent customer service skills Ability to answer questions and deal with awkward and stressful situations Friendly, enthusiastic and flexible Excellent oral and written communication skills Computer fluency in the Windows operating system Ability to work with individuals of diverse backgrounds and abilities Must have professional appearance and demeanor and maintain confidentiality of all information entrusted to this position. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload your cover letter and resume with your online application. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541776/associate-full-professor": {
      "title": "Associate / Full Professor",
      "description": "Associate / Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541776 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Engineering Department: 19020000 - EG-MECHANICAL - AEROSPACE ENG Classification Title: Associate / Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must possess a Ph.D. in a related field by the start date. Job Description: The Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering (MAE) in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering (HWCOE) at the University of Florida invites applications for full-time, nine-month faculty positions at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. The positions have an anticipated start date of August 2027. We are seeking candidates with established reputation and research programs in engineering and technology development for space exploration and scientific missions. Candidates should have demonstrated experience with NASA space flight programs and engagement with other agencies critical to program development in these areas. The faculty member will be expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses within the Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering; initiate and sustain externally sponsored research programs; recruit and supervise graduate students; and engage in service activities for the University of Florida and the profession. Dedication to excellence in research, teaching, and service, and evidence of scholarly achievements are expected. The Department of Mechanical & Aerospace Engineering at the University of Florida (https://mae.ufl.edu/) has approximately 60 faculty members working in all aspects of mechanical and aerospace engineering and is growing. The combined undergraduate and graduate student population is approximately 3000 with nearly 200 in the Ph.D. program. Recent US News & World Report has MAE ranked as 14th and 21st for the aerospace and mechanical engineering graduate programs, respectively, among public universities in the US. The University of Florida is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is currently ranked as one of the best public universities, 8th in 2026 according to U.S. News & World Report. The university has unique resources for research and education, such as the Mission Operations Center in the Astraeus Space Institute, and HiPerGator, the most powerful academic supercomputer in the United States for AI development and applications. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must possess a Ph.D. in a related field by the start date. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to receive applications until the positions are filled. All applications must be submitted through UFCareers at: http://jobs.ufl.edu ( Search Job #541776 ). Complete applications must include the following files in PDF format: (1) cover letter which specifies the candidates interest in the position, (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) the names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of no less than three and up to five references. Additional required documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. (4) A research program vision statement detailing short- and long-term goals; (5) a teaching statement describing the applicant’s teaching experience and vision for developing a teaching program at the University of Florida; and (6) up to three refereed journal or conference articles (co-)authored by the applicant. Questions about this position may be directed to the Faculty Search Chair, maefacultysearch@mae.ufl.edu . The University of Florida (UF) is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities. UF is home to HiPerGator , the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education, and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering is undergoing a major expansion in faculty, research, and facilities with a strong focus on artificial intelligence. In Fall 2023, UF opened Malachowsky Hall for Data Science & Information Technology , a 260,000-square-foot facility that serves as the hub for AI. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Additional Requirements The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. Transcripts marked “Issued to Student” will not be considered official. Degrees earned outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Additional Information The University and College count among its greatest strengths a community of faculty, students and staff having diverse perspectives who support a culture of respect and dignity where learning and research thrive. UF is committed to equal education, opportunity, and access. If you require a reasonable accommodation to apply, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) . Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. All searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539600/director-of-development-grateful-patient-college-of-medicine": {
      "title": "Director of Development, Grateful Patient - College of Medicine",
      "description": "Director of Development, Grateful Patient - College of Medicine Apply now Job no: 539600 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management Department: 29011500 - MD-ALUMNI AFFAIRS Classification Title: DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor's degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Job Description: The Director of Development, Grateful Patient is responsible for identifying, qualifying, cultivating, soliciting, and stewarding major gift prospects for UF Health with a primary emphasis on grateful patient fundraising and a focus on gifts of $100,000 and above. Reporting to the Senior Director of Development, this position coordinates strategic research, data collection, and planning to identify and advance new major gift prospects in support of the College of Medicine and UF Health. The role works in close collaboration with clinical partners, including physicians and medical staff, to identify and engage grateful patient prospects and align fundraising efforts with clinical priorities. Outreach may also include alumni, parents, friends, and faculty in support of broader College of Medicine initiatives. About this role: Development • Identify, qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward major gift prospects for the College of Medicine and UF Health, with a primary emphasis on grateful patient fundraising and gifts of $100,000 and above. The portfolio may also include alumni, parents, friends, and faculty, in coordination with College of Medicine departments and units. • Grow and refine an existing portfolio of prospects and donors, strategically advance relationships and bring gifts to closure through face-to-face visits, proposals and solicitations. • Develop and present concept papers and major gift proposals and acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the mission, vision and values of and Advancement. • Develop support for UF Health and College of Medicine priorities by engaging stakeholders, including clinical partners such as physicians and medical staff, as well as volunteers, faculty, and staff, to advance grateful patient fundraising and related engagement initiatives. Increase knowledge of and grow support for COM campaign priorities, including scholarships and faculty endowments, as well as department-specific priorities and initiatives. Strategic Initiatives • Support physicians, researchers, administrators and medical staff in identifying and referring prospects, and in presenting needs and possibilities effectively. • Collaborate with clinical partners and other development officers to identify and develop strategies that increase grateful patient and house staff engagement opportunities and solicitations. • Prepare research and reports for the attention of appropriate College staff, volunteers and stakeholders in support of targeted relationship and partnership building initiatives. • Initiate, plan and support activities and events involving volunteers, donors, grateful patients and prospects, both on campus and regionally. Administrative • Effectively perform prospect tracking, portfolio management, record keeping, file maintenance, and reporting using the Ascend database. • Ensure best practices in office systems, files and record keeping. • Participate in College of Medicine events, including those focused on cultivating prospects and donors for scholarship, faculty support and research initiatives. • Provide research, market, and prospect information for inclusion into the Foundation’s integrated database, and handle confidential prospect information in a sensitive, ethical manner. Perform related duties as required or deemed appropriate to the accomplishment of the responsibilities and functions related to this position. We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Package including over 10 paid holidays, as well as paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! About the City of Gainesville Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds Expected Salary: $85,000 - $100,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Master's degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor's degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Preferred Qualifications: •Four to six years successful prior fund-raising experience in a medical center, college or university setting is strongly preferred. Prior work with volunteers and soliciting gifts in the $100,000+ range would be beneficial.• Experience working with or supporting clinical partners such as physicians, care teams, or medical staff, or demonstrated transferable skills that indicate readiness for productive collaboration in a healthcare setting. • Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. • Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. • Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. • The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. • Ability to work collaboratively and a team player • Competency in problem solving. • Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. • Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. • Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. • The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position requires a valid driver’s license and dependable transportation, as responsibilities include travel within the surrounding area to support program activities and site visits. To be considered, please upload the following: Cover Letter Resume Contact information for three professional references The University of Florida has partnered with Cooper Coleman search firm on the Director of Development, Grateful Patient role. You may view the position prospectus or apply via this link: https://www.coopercoleman.com/current-searches Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540530/assistant-associate-full-professor-rheumatology": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor - Rheumatology",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor - Rheumatology Apply now Job no: 540530 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050300 - MD-RHEUMATOLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Rheumatology Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Rheumatology, have an MD degree or equivalent and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: Responsibilities will include patient care in the inpatient and outpatient settings as well as supervision/teaching of Rheumatology Fellows, Residents, and Medical Students. Along with General Rheumatology clinics, successful applicants will be expected to participate in a weekly half-day multidisciplinary clinic specializing in either muscle disease or vasculitis and to collaborate with a basic scientist in the capacity of \"clinical mentor\" for one or more research trainees in our NIH-funded T32 training program. An interest in translational research is highly desirable. We enjoy a collaborative relationship with Shands Hospital and pride ourselves in professional and cooperative interdisciplinary care. The UF Department of Medicine is a national leader in Cancer, Cardiology, Gastroenterology, Kidney Disorders, and Pulmonology. In addition, UF Health Shands Hospital is ranked among the top-50 in eight specialties in the 2010 edition of America's Best Hospitals, published by U.S. News & World Report. Resources available for professional development at the University of Florida Health Sciences Center include leadership, education, and research tracks within a Clinical Translational Science Institute(CTSI), formal mentorship programs, and supported opportunities for teaching and research. These faculty positions will be based at our University teaching hospital with a potential option for part-time patient care duties at our immediately adjacent VA Hospital. Both hospitals serve a designated shortage area and/or a medically undeserved area/population. Requisite attributes include a commitment to service, a strong sense of teamwork and a desire to train tomorrow's doctors. Substantial start-up packages are available for appropriate research applicants. The Gainesville area has superb weather, inexpensive living, nationally ranked schools, and multiple year-round recreational opportunities. Foreign national candidates whose employment conditions meet federal and University requirements under an immigrant classification are eligible to apply. Expected Salary: Starting salary is negotiable; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Rheumatology, have an MD degree or equivalent and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540861/progressive-care-ward-veterinary-technician": {
      "title": "Progressive Care Ward Veterinary Technician",
      "description": "Progressive Care Ward Veterinary Technician Apply now Job no: 540861 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311001 - VM-ICU AND PCW SERVICES Classification Title: Veterinary Technician I/II (CARE) Classification Minimum Requirements Veterinary Technician I: High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II: Credentialed veterinary technician. Job Description: The Progressive Care Ward (PCW) is the perfect gateway for new and experienced technicians to become better acquainted with the hospital’s culture and practices. The PCW acts as the stable patient care hub for the entire small animal hospital, ensuring each new day offers an exciting and varied array of patient cases. Our technicians work in a fast-paced, teaching-driven environment alongside other experienced veterinary technicians, volunteers, students, and clinicians. Applicants must be a motivated, well-organized, and caring individuals who are knowledgeable and skillful in the nursing care of general and critically ill animal patients. A person in this position must possess excellent communication skills and be proactive in determining the needs of the hospital. A successful applicant should enjoy learning new things and can quickly adapt to a changing environment. They need to possess a good base knowledge of veterinary medicine and express a positive attitude in their daily work. The individual must be able to communicate effectively, and demonstrate critical thinking and professionalism. Individual is considered essential personnel and part of the Emergency response team which may include reporting to work during Main Campus closures. Schedule: The expectation is to work a 40-hour week comprising typically of 1 x 13hr shift and 2 x 13.5hr shifts. • Daytime (6am – 8pm) and night shifts (6pm – 8am) available • Schedules available typically involve at least 1 weekend day • Alternating holiday shifts required - these are fairly shared amongst ECC technician staff The expectations is to work a 40 hour week comprising typically of 1 x 13hr shift and 2 x 13.5hr shifts. The individual's schedule may change, with a 2-week notice, to accommodate staffing and caseload needs. This position is required to cover assigned holidays throughout the year. Expected Salary: Non-credentialed Technician: $18 - $22 Credentialed Technician: $20 - $25 Required Qualifications Veterinary Technician I: High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II: Credentialed veterinary technician. Preferred 1+ year of experience working with hospitalized inpatients in an emergency or specialty clinic setting Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541223/clinical-financial-counselor": {
      "title": "Clinical Financial Counselor",
      "description": "Clinical Financial Counselor Apply now Job no: 541223 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Dentistry, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 34080100 - DN-ORAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Financial Counselor Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of related experience and/or training, including customer service; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Dentistry is accepting applications for a Clinical Financial Counselor. This position is assigned to the Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery to provide financial counseling to patients for both clinical and operating room procedures, pre-certify and schedule all OR surgeries. Scheduling/Coordination of Care • Arranges the coordination of care with associated services at UF Health Shands, including the scheduling and pre-certification of all OR surgeries. This will include, but is not be limited to coordinating scheduling history and physical, pre-orthognathic work-up, and pre-surgical anesthesia appointments. • Responsible for scheduling history and physical, pre-orthognathic workup appointments, diagnostic testing (MRI, CT scan, etc.) for both clinical and hospital grade requests. • Responsible for posting OR cases on the SNAP board. • Responsible for updating the faculty Outlook calendars with OR cases. Financial Counselor • Interview and counsel patients who will or have been treated in Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery and Center for Implant Dentistry to determine financial status and/or insurance coverage. • Verify insurance coverage for each patient and determine if services to be provided are covered and/or non-covered according to individual insurance policies. • Verify account information in our computer system to ensure accuracy and update accounts as necessary. • Print proposal treatment plans for patients, knowledge of electronic billing, and print dental claim forms. • Responsible for entering treatment plans and charges into the system. • Responsible for communicating with various insurance companies to obtain all inpatient and outpatient surgery authorization approvals. • Works with Medicaid Portals for direct billing of all Medicaid dental or medical billing for the department. • Determines patients who are covered by HMO, PPO, or Worker’s Compensation. • The incumbent will insure that each HMO, PPO or Worker’s Compensation visit is pre-certified. • Verifies patient’s insurance information on Availity, MMA Medicaid plans, and commercial plans. • Determines deductible, co-payment and extent of coverage. • Notifies patients of any deductibles due and/or any co-payment not covered by their policy. • Notifies students, residents, and faculty of services which are authorized and monitors treatment plan to ensure that all procedures are performed according to agency guidelines. • Notifies students, residents and faculty of the amount of payment to be collected from the patient prior to treatment being provided. • Gather and verify copies of the patient’s insurance card and completes all forms required by third party payers. • Inputs insurance information into the computer system to ensure correct billing for treatment provided. • Create medical record number for dental patient in the UF Health Shands Epic scheduling network system. • Responsible for emailing residents the CPT codes, DX codes, and surgery dates to enter an Epic encounter to create the OR case. Answering OMFS Financial Office Phone lines. Assisting caller with financial questions regarding their accounts with both the oral surgery resident clinic and implant center (as needed). Assist with department insurance explanation of benefits review for posting accuracy, providing requested information timely and efficiently, claims filing in Inovalon, submitting adjustment and refund requests, as needed Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $20.00 - $22.00 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of related experience and/or training, including customer service; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Knowledge of general office procedures and practices Working knowledge of basic math Able to plan, organize and coordinate work activities Able to follow instructions, written and verbal Able to communicate effectively Able to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others Able to review data for accuracy and completeness Knowledge of dental and medical terminology (basic terms at least) Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and a list of three references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541287/administrative-spec-i": {
      "title": "Administrative Spec I",
      "description": "Administrative Spec I Apply now Job no: 541287 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29120000 - MD-PSYCHIATRY Classification Title: Administrative Spec I Job Description: The Administrative Specialist I provides comprehensive administrative, operational, and financial support to the Addiction Division within the Department of Psychiatry. This position serves as a critical liaison between faculty, referral sources, healthcare professionals, and external agencies, ensuring efficient day-to-day office operations and the successful coordination of Independent Medical Evaluations (IMEs), forensic evaluations, faculty scheduling, travel arrangements, billing activities, and divisional administrative functions. The incumbent is responsible for maintaining highly confidential records, supporting forensic evaluation processes, coordinating faculty and fellowship schedules, processing travel and timesheet documentation, tracking revenue and billing activities, and providing general office support. The position requires exceptional organizational skills, attention to detail, discretion, and the ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced academic healthcare environment. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Forensic Evaluations and Independent Medical Evaluations Coordinate and maintain documentation related to Independent Medical Evaluations (IMEs) and forensic evaluations. Assist faculty with preparation, formatting, editing, and quality review of IME and forensic reports, including ASAM Criteria and Dimensional Assessments. Ensure toxicology reports and laboratory results are properly identified, linked, and maintained within participant records. De-identify Department of Health (DOH) IME reports and submit finalized reports to the Florida Prosecution Services Unit. Provide administrative support for agency-referred IMEs involving healthcare professionals. Schedule evaluations and maintain faculty calendars, schedules, itineraries, and travel arrangements for comprehensive and forensic evaluations. Coordinate evaluation logistics with in-state and out-of-state referral sources, including professional monitoring organizations. Communicate referral information, collateral documentation, and scheduling details with faculty, referral agencies, and front desk staff. Download, organize, and maintain laboratory and toxicology reports within participant files. Answer telephone inquiries and serve as a point of contact for evaluation scheduling and coordination. Administrative Support for Addiction Division Prepare and transcribe correspondence, reports, and administrative documents for faculty and division leadership. Coordinate faculty participation in educational programs, lectures, Continuing Medical Education (CME) activities, and Grand Rounds presentations. Arrange meeting logistics, room setup, and communication activities related to educational events. Maintain and coordinate faculty on-call schedules and ensure accurate EPIC system updates. Process and monitor faculty, fellow, and contracted provider timesheet submissions for multiple clinical locations. Verify contractual coverage obligations and resolve discrepancies in reported work hours or assignments. Maintain accurate records of faculty leave and ensure appropriate reporting through departmental systems. Coordinate all faculty travel arrangements, including: Travel Authorization Requests (TARs) Airfare, lodging, rental cars, and parking Conference registrations Travel reimbursements and expense reporting Departmental purchasing card (P-Card) expense reconciliation Faculty Scheduling and Operational Coordination Develop and maintain quarterly faculty on-call schedules for divisional programs and services. Ensure equitable distribution of call assignments, holiday coverage, and adherence to divisional requirements. Review scheduling conflicts related to leave, conferences, and other obligations. Submit schedules for leadership approval and communicate approved schedules to stakeholders. Enter approved faculty and fellowship call schedules into EPIC for access by hospital and nursing personnel. Coordinate quarterly travel for faculty members providing off-site clinical coverage. Manage monthly timesheet reporting and verification for contracted faculty and fellows across multiple clinical locations. Review and reconcile work schedules, coverage arrangements, travel time, and mileage reporting. Maintain divisional leave records in SharePoint following leadership approval. Financial Administration, Collections, and Reconciliation Process and track daily revenue collections associated with forensic evaluations and IMEs. Create invoices and maintain billing records. Monitor accounts and track payment status for evaluations and services. Reconcile monthly reports and related vendor invoices. Maintain comprehensive IME logs and databases. Compile and prepare IME activity reports for departmental leadership. Review financial documentation to ensure accuracy and compliance with departmental procedures. Office Operations and Administrative Support Support daily office operations, including: Answering phones Managing email and fax communications Filing and document management Ordering and maintaining office supplies Coordinate service requests and troubleshoot office equipment issues. Serve as liaison with Information Technology and equipment service providers for maintenance and repairs. Provide administrative backup support to the Administrative Specialist II as needed. Serve as the on-site administrative contact for fellows and faculty regarding urgent operational matters. Perform additional administrative duties and special projects as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of procedures for University purchasing Knowledge of Microsoft Excel, Microsoft Access, general office practices, basic arithmetic, bookkeeping, and basic filing practices Accurate typing skills Ability to use a calculator; maintain fiscal records; organize and file materials; review fiscal data for accuracy; and post, balance, and reconcile fiscal records Knowledge of UF compliance requirements Ability to use excellent organizational skills Knowledge of spreadsheet and word processing computer software Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Expected Salary: $22.03 - $26.82 ($46,000 -$56,000) Benefits: As one of the nation’s top public research universities, the University of Florida provides robust benefits options to support the wellbeing of their employees. The university offers a range of complementary and affordable benefit options to meet your family’s financial, educational, and health needs. Eligibility varies by position and FTE. Health, Dental & Vison Insurance. Staff Retirement Plans. Paid Time Off (approximately 22 days of vacation leave, 13 days of sick leave and 11 paid holidays annually. Tuition Assistance (UF Employee Education Program) Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PLSF) Eligible Employer. About the University of Florida (UF), College of Medicine: The Department of Psychiatry at the University of Florida are engaged in groundbreaking projects covering a variety of interests through our Centers, labs, and collaborations with other departments. The Department of Psychiatry includes a large outpatient center with various specialty clinics within (including one associated with our Center for Autism and Neurodevelopment), a very active inpatient service, a collaborative psychiatry program for primary care practitioners, school consultation program, and community consultation services. The Department of Psychiatry spans services across adult, addiction, and child/adolescent specializations, including a freestanding psychiatric hospital, a large consultation liaison service at the main UF Shands Hospital, the Florida Recovery Center operated by our Addiction Division, and multiple research centers including the Center for Addiction Research & Education and the Center for OCD and Related Disorders (COARD). About Gainesville: The City of Gainesville holds many distinctions. Located in the northern part of Florida, Gainesville is home to the state’s largest and oldest university (UF), and is one of the state's centers of education, innovation, healthcare, arts & culture, sports, and more. You can find the city routinely listed as one of the best college towns, the best places to live, and the best places to retire. Known for its preservation of historic buildings and the beauty of its natural surroundings, Gainesville's numerous parks, museums, and lakes provide endless entertainment to thousands of visitors and locals every year. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants should submit the following items as part of the online application: - Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualification relative to the credentials listed above. - Curriculum Vitae. - Reference Names/Contact Information (3). The cover letter should include the names and contact information of three individuals who can provide an evaluative appraisal of the applicant’s work, potential in the field, and capacity to be an effective colleague in an academic health sciences center. References will be requested from the Department should the applicant be considered. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please contact UF Human Resources at 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541408/clinical-programs-coordinator-ii-department-of-pediatrics": {
      "title": "Clinical Programs Coordinator II - Department of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Clinical Programs Coordinator II - Department of Pediatrics Apply now Job no: 541408 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29091600 - MD-PEDS-NEPHROLOGY Classification Title: Clinical Programs Coord II Classification Minimum Requirements Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: This position serves as a Clinical Programs Coordinator II and is responsible for supporting multidisciplinary care coordination, facilitating patient transitions, coordinating program activities, and ensuring compliance with quality improvement and programmatic requirements. The position will coordinate services across providers, patients, families, and community resources to enhance continuity of care, support effective workflows, and optimize patient outcomes. About This Role: Multidisciplinary Care Coordination Coordinate care activities among pediatric nephrology providers and specialists. Facilitate communication between patients, families, and the healthcare team to promote timely access to services. Coordinate referrals, outpatient services, follow-up needs, and transition planning activities between pediatric and adult providers. Support continuity of care through effective coordination of clinical services across healthcare settings. Assist patients and families with navigating insurance requirements, authorization processes, and healthcare access barriers. Coordinate resources needed to support access to medications, procedures, and specialty care services. Connect families with financial assistance programs, community resources, and support services as appropriate. Identify and address barriers that may impact successful participation in the patient’s care plan. Program Coordination and Support Coordinate patient and family support services for pediatric nephrology patients with chronic kidney disease and complex healthcare needs. Assess barriers impacting access to care, treatment adherence, follow-up, and successful transition to adult nephrology services. Facilitate connections to appropriate clinical, behavioral health, and community resources to support patient care plans. Coordinate support services to address the medical, psychosocial, and logistical needs of patients and families living with chronic kidney disease. Education, Engagement, and Quality Improvement Support Coordinate and reinforce patient and family education related to disease management, treatment expectations, and transition readiness. Support patient engagement and self-management by coordinating educational resources and reinforcing individualized care plans. Participate in Pediatric Nephrology quality improvement activities, including monitoring program goals, supporting programmatic requirements, and contributing to process improvement initiatives. Expected Salary: $49,000-$55,000; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in social work (MSW) Licensed Social Worker (state licensure preferred) Experience in healthcare or pediatric chronic disease care Strong organizational and communication skills Experience with pediatric chronic illness populations Experience in care coordination or case management Knowledge of insurance authorization processes Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541463/administrative-specialist-i-department-of-orthopaedic-surgery-and-sports-medicine": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist I - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Sports Medicine",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist I - Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Sports Medicine Apply now Job no: 541463 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29170000 - MD-ORTHOPAEDICS - REHAB Classification Title: Administrative Spec I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and appropriate experience. Job Description: The Administrative Specialist I provides administrative and operational support to physicians, staff, and departmental programs. The position coordinates schedules, correspondence, meetings, travel, records, communications, and other administrative activities while serving as a professional point of contact for internal and external stakeholders. The Administrative Specialist I work collaboratively with faculty, staff, patients, and other departments to ensure timely completion of administrative tasks and efficient departmental operations. About this role: Administrative Operations Provides administrative support to assigned faculty physicians, Advanced Practice Providers (APPs), and Division leadership to support efficient daily operations. Assists with coordinating routine administrative activities related to clinical, academic, research, and departmental responsibilities. Maintains provider calendars, clinic schedules, meetings, conferences, and departmental events using QGenda. Assists with coordinating weekly and monthly divisional conferences, including scheduling meetings, preparing agendas and supporting materials, coordinating logistics, and distributing follow-up communications. Performs routine administrative tasks, maintains accurate information, communicates schedule and meeting updates, and follows established departmental procedures and guidelines. Provides administrative support for faculty and APP licensure, DEA registration, hospital credentialing, clinical privileges, regulatory requirements, and related compliance activities. Maintains records and tracks, required documentation and renewal deadlines in accordance with established procedures. Assists with tracking faculty and APP leave, communicating schedule changes to appropriate departments, and coordinating routine provider coverage needs. Assists with domestic and international travel arrangements, conference registrations, reimbursements, and routine budget tracking in accordance with institutional policies and procedures. Reviews and routes incoming correspondence and refers matters requiring additional review or leadership attention to the appropriate individual. Provides administrative support with confidential legal and administrative documentation related to medical malpractice cases, expert witness requests, subpoenas, and attorney communications, following established departmental procedures. Manages incoming mail, correspondence, faxes, and electronic communications; prioritizes and routes information appropriately, responds to routine requests, and coordinates follow-up as needed. Provide administrative support for legal and medical-legal matters, including depositions, document requests, correspondence, billing, and preparation of supporting materials. Maintains confidentiality and follows applicable University policies and requirements. Clinical Operations Coordination Provides administrative support for clinical scheduling activities, including provider clinic schedules, operating room schedules, trauma coverage, administrative assignments, and provider availability using scheduling systems such as Epic and QGenda. Assists with maintaining 24-hour trauma call schedules, rounding assignments, weekend and holiday coverage, trauma operating room coverage, and backup schedules in accordance with established departmental procedures. Updates and maintains scheduling information and communicates schedule changes to providers, clinic administrators, hospital personnel, operating room staff, and other appropriate departments. Assists with identifying routine scheduling conflicts and refers issues requiring additional review or decision-making to the appropriate supervisor or leadership. Provides administrative support for coordinating provider coverage to help maintain appropriate staffing and continuity of clinical operations. Prepares charts for local and regional clinics; and coordinates follow-up activities. Provide administrative support to referring physicians, patients, families, and healthcare professionals regarding referrals, scheduling, and routine coordination of care. Assists with scheduling telemedicine appointments and coordinating patient follow-up activities and related administrative tasks. Responds to routine patient-related administrative inquiries involving scheduling, medical documentation, imaging requests, prescriptions, healthcare forms, and other clinical support needs, following established policies and procedures, and refers issues requiring additional review to the appropriate staff. Assists with receiving, uploading, tracking, and returning diagnostic imaging to support physician review and patient care. Prepares and distributes patient census reports and routine operational reports to support physician scheduling and daily clinical activities. Maintains accurate records and communicates updates to appropriate staff and departments in accordance with established procedures. Program Coordination and Compliance Follows departmental, institutional, and clinical policies and established procedures when performing administrative activities and maintaining compliance with applicable requirements. Provides administrative support and serves as a point of contact among faculty physicians, APPs, hospital departments, administrative offices, and other appropriate individuals to facilitate routine communication and coordination. Assist with maintaining administrative procedures, workflows, systems, and databases to support accurate and timely completion of assigned activities. Updates and maintains administrative records and information, ensuring accuracy, completeness, and appropriate documentation. Maintains confidential personnel, clinical, financial, and operational information in accordance with university, hospital, HIPAA, and other applicable requirements. Provide routine administrative information to faculty and staff regarding established administrative processes, scheduling procedures, credentialing requirements, travel policies, and departmental procedures, and refers questions requiring additional interpretation or guidance to the appropriate supervisor or department. Assists with departmental initiatives, process improvement activities, and special projects by completing assigned administrative tasks and providing support as needed. Expected Salary: $22 - $24 per hour, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and appropriate experience. Preferred: Experience in a healthcare setting Demonstrates comprehensive knowledge of advanced administrative practices, academic medicine operations, healthcare administration, and physician practice support Proficiency in Microsoft Office, including Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint, etc. Demonstrate project management skills Ability to compose own correspondence and edit/proofread that of others Capable of working under pressure and producing within deadlines Ability to prioritize own work and proceed with minimum supervision Ability to always maintain a professional approach Demonstrate ease with learning new programs Always maintain strict confidentiality Interacts professionally with the public in a sensitive and professional manner; screens requests and materials effectively and efficiently; and readily handles a variety of situations Works independently and as a team member Articulate; has a good command of the English language Punctual; maintains an excellent attendance record Highly organized Consistently demonstrates personal initiative Displays interest in the overall well-being of the health system Takes initiative to assist whenever possible in the success of the institution Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. To claim veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Form or equivalent certification with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541632/data-engineer-senior-coordinator": {
      "title": "Data Engineer Senior Coordinator",
      "description": "Data Engineer Senior Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541632 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology, Grant or Research Administration, Artificial Intelligence Department: 18300000 - ED-LASTINGER CENTER Classification Title: Application Database Admin III Job Description: The University of Florida Lastinger Center for Learning’s mission is to improve the quality of teaching, learning, and childcare. We research, develop, and scale educational innovations for adults and children that put all learners on trajectories for lifelong success. Specifically, the Center targets kindergarten readiness, 3rd grade reading proficiency, and algebra proficiency as critical milestones in children's educational trajectory that are predictive of success in school and life. The Center works at the nexus of research, practice, and policy to develop sustainable systems that leverage educational technologies and improve the implementation of research-based practices. Housed in one of the nation's elite public research universities, the Center operates as a dynamic, entrepreneurial workplace focused on executing UF's service mission to strengthen the human condition and improve the quality of life. The Lastinger Center is committed to attracting, hiring, and retaining top talent by valuing a broad range of thought, skill, and experience. We believe in fostering environments where team members feel respected, valued, and empowered to do their best work. Data Management Prepares data infrastructure for data science research by building out data collection and reporting infrastructure Plays a role in the development and design of databases and data warehouses/lakes across multiple platforms and computing environments. Plays a role in the development of an overall data architecture that supports the information needs of the business in a flexible but secure environment. Assists developers on the Center's data system to streamline and expand analysis and reporting for internal use and client reporting. Mentors others in the develop and design of database and warehouse applications across multiple platforms and computing environments. Responsible for developing, implementing, and overseeing database policies and procedures to ensure the integrity and availability of databases and their accompanying software. Data Coordination Creates actionable high-value analysis and reporting priorities using available data in collaboration with the research, evaluation, and data teams for each Lastinger Center Product, in alignment with OKRs. Analyzes data requirements, application and processing architectures, data dictionaries, and database schema(s), and then designs, develops, amends, optimizes, and certifies database schema design to meet system(s) requirements. Gathers, analyzes, and normalizes relevant information related to, and from business processes, functions, and operations to evaluate data credibility and determine relevance and meaning. Responsible for developing, implementing, and overseeing database policies and procedures to ensure the integrity and availability of databases and their accompanying software. AI Development Collaborates with product and research teams to identify opportunities where AI can improve data processing, coordination, or reporting efficiency. Develops standards and best practices for prompt engineering, model output validation, and AI-assisted code/query generation to ensure accuracy and reliability. Evaluates and selects appropriate AI tools, models, and frameworks based on data sensitivity, performance requirements, and long-term maintainability. Develops backend structure to support AI tools utilized at the data layer in support of Center-wide initiatives Other Duties as Assigned Expected Salary: $105,000-$120,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and five years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Familiarity with data-intensive technologies and practices. Knowledge of SAS, R, Python, and SQL, or data visualization software. Understanding of compliance relating to disclosure laws, regulations and other requirements. Ability to communicate clearly in multiple venues and formats with non-technical audiences. Expertise in large-scale education data and data from application use Experience with data infrastructure and data visualization Interest in transforming education through the application of data science Commitment to the responsible use of data for the benefit of society Proactive, precise, and persistent Organization and planning skills to ensure timely follow through Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is soft-funded and time-limited. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541727/assistant-associate-or-full-professor-health-education-and-behavior-university-of-florida": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor – Health Education and Behavior, University of Florida",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor – Health Education and Behavior, University of Florida Apply now Job no: 541727 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 26050100 - HH-HEB-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must have earned their doctorate in health education, health behavior, public health, social and behavioral sciences, or related field prior to faculty appointment. Applicants must show evidence of a strong scientific background and the ability to secure external funding to support their research program. A track record of extramural funding is expected for consideration of Associate or Full Professor rank. Job Description: The Department of Health Education and Behavior (HEB) at the University of Florida (UF) invites applications for an open rank (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor) tenured or tenure track faculty position. This is a 9-month, full-time (1.0 FTE), tenure-accruing, on-campus appointment scheduled to begin August 16, 2027 . The Department of HEB ( https://hhp.ufl.edu/academics/health-education-behavior-heb/) is home to 18 full-time faculty members and currently enrolls over 400 undergraduate majors and 115 graduate students. Established research programs include behavioral aspects of substance misuse; obesity, physical activity, and nutrition; and sexual health, including HIV/AIDS, other STIs, violence prevention, and reproductive health. Preferred candidates will have research programs in these existing areas or in areas that closely complement them. Applications are encouraged from candidates with an established research record in the modification of health related behavioral risk factors, strong quantitative or mixed methods research skills, experience with multidisciplinary collaboration, a focus on culturally appropriate methods, use of digital technologies for health promotion, and implementation/dissemination of evidence-based health behavior change programs. We seek applications from individuals who will: (a) conduct an independent research program that complements department and college research initiatives, (b) participate in collaborative research programs, (c) contribute significantly to the teaching mission of the department, including undergraduate and graduate education, and (d) mentor MS and PhD students. HEB faculty members are active research collaborators and contribute to research training in several university-wide centers, including the Center for Behavioral Economic Health Research (housed within HEB), Center for Addiction Research and Education, and Southern HIV and Alcohol Research Consortium. Candidates must have earned their doctorate in health education, health behavior, public health, social and behavioral sciences, or related field prior to faculty appointment. Applicants must show evidence of a strong scientific background and the ability to secure external funding to support their research program. A track record of extramural funding is expected for consideration of Associate or Full Professor rank. The position is supported by a competitive salary and a start-up package commensurate with rank and experience. Rank and tenure status will be determined based on qualifications and experience of the identified finalist. UF has a competitive benefits package, and department faculty have opportunities for summer salary. The University of Florida is one of the top ranked Public Universities in the United States and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 61,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. There is a vibrant culture of research, with over $1 billion in research expenditures last fiscal year. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 145,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 85 miles from the Georgia border, 50 miles from the gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean. It has abundant wildlife, mature forests, prairies, lakes, and opportunities for many outdoor activities. UF is an equal opportunity employer. The selection process will be conducted in accordance with Florida's 'Government in the Sunshine' and Public Records Laws. Call (352)392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD) if a disability accommodation is needed to apply. Expected Salary: Competitive salary and a start-up package commensurate with rank and experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates must have earned their doctorate in health education, health behavior, public health, social and behavioral sciences, or related field prior to faculty appointment. Applicants must show evidence of a strong scientific background and the ability to secure external funding to support their research program. A track record of extramural funding is expected for consideration of Associate or Full Professor rank. Preferred: Preferred candidates will have research programs in behavioral aspects of substance misuse; obesity, physical activity, and nutrition; or sexual health, including HIV/AIDS, other STIs, violence prevention, and reproductive health or in areas that closely complement them. Applications are encouraged from candidates with an established research record in the modification of health-related behavioral risk factors, strong quantitative or mixed methods research skills, experience with multidisciplinary collaboration, a focus on culturally appropriate methods, use of digital technologies for health promotion, and implementation/dissemination of evidence-based health behavior change programs. Special Instructions to Applicants: Review of applications will begin November 16, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. Submit applications for this position (Requisition #541727) online at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/listing/ and include: (a) an application letter summarizing qualifications; (b) a research statement and evidence of research proficiency; (c) a teaching statement and evidence of teaching proficiency; (d) a curriculum vitae; and (e) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. References will be contacted if applicant is a finalist in the process. Send email inquiries to Dr. Amy Lynn Rossi , Search Committee Chair, at amy.mobley@ufl.edu . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541731/associate-or-full-professor-chair-of-department-of-economics": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor – Chair of Department of Economics",
      "description": "Associate or Full Professor – Chair of Department of Economics Apply now Job no: 541731 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16030000 - LS-ECONOMICS Classification Title: Associate or Full Professor Job Posting Title: Associate or Full Professor – Chair of Department of Economics Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field. Distinguished record of scholarship, teaching, and service that qualifies the candidate for appointment as an Associate Professor or Professor in the Department of Economics. Demonstrated leadership and administrative experience in higher education. Job Description: The Department of Economics at the University of Florida invites applications for the position of Department Chair. This is a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing faculty appointment. The successful candidate will be appointed at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor and will be eligible for tenure upon appointment, subject to the University's approval process. The Chair appointment is expected to be a three- to five-year term, with the possibility of reappointment. In addition to providing leadership and oversight of the Department's academic, administrative, and financial operations, the successful candidate will demonstrate a vision for advancing research excellence, strengthening the national and international reputation of the Department's doctoral program, and effectively advocating for strategic faculty growth. The Department welcomes applications from candidates in all fields of economics. The Department of Economics comprises 22 faculty members, including 5 instructional faculty, 10 Assistant Professors, and 7 Full Professors. The Department has experienced substantial growth in recent years across its research, graduate, and undergraduate missions and is poised for continued advancement. The Department's established strengths in Industrial Organization, International Trade, Macroeconomics, and Public Economics provide a strong foundation for future growth. In addition, a talented group of junior faculty is rapidly expanding the Department's research profile through innovative scholarship and publications in leading economics journals. The Department offers doctoral, master’s, and undergraduate programs: Ph.D.: A small program admits 10 students every two years. Students typically finish in five years, and most have published in respected field journals by graduation. M.A. in Econometrics and Data Analysis: A residential program (25 students per year) and a growing online program (60 students per year). Undergraduate: More than 800 majors and 425 minors. Service teaching: Two general education introductory economics courses that serve students from all UF majors. About the Candidate: The Department seeks a visionary leader who can elevate the research enterprise, improve the external reputation of doctoral program, and grow the faculty size. The successful candidate will be expected to improve the Department’s research reputation and standing in external rankings. They will foster research productivity and mentor a large cohort of early-career faculty. Additionally, they will oversee the Department's academic programs, budget, hiring, and faculty evaluation. The successful candidate will conduct independent research leading to publication in leading peer-reviewed economics journals and must teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Advertised Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: •Ph.D. in Economics or a closely related field. •Distinguished record of scholarship, teaching, and service that qualifies the candidate for appointment as an Associate Professor or Professor in the Department of Economics. •Demonstrated leadership and administrative experience in higher education. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in academic leadership and administration. Demonstrated ability to support faculty success, research excellence, and graduate education. Experience with strategic planning and resource management. Commitment to a collaborative and inclusive academic environment. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Applicants should submit a 2–3-page cover letter that describes their vision and priorities. CV, and a research paper. Evidence of effective leadership and/or impactful academic service is helpful. Finalists will be required to submit three letters of reference. Questions may be directed to Mark Rush, Search Committee Chair ( markrush@ufl.edu ). The position will remain open until filled, and the search committee encourages applicants to apply by November 15, 2026 , for full consideration. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2027. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 May 2027 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541741/assistant-professor-in-ancient-medicine-department-of-classics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Ancient Medicine, Department of Classics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Ancient Medicine, Department of Classics Apply now Job no: 541741 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 16140000 - LS-CLASSICS Classification Title: Assistant Professor Job Description: The Department of Classics at the University of Florida invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor specializing in Ancient Medicine, to begin August 16, 2027. This is a nine-month, full-time (1FTE) position. The Department of Classics seeks a candidate who will have a PhD in Classical Studies, History or a related field in hand by the start of the appointment, with a specialization in the area of Ancient Medicine with a primary focus of study on the Greek and Roman worlds, and a strong research and teaching record in the area of Ancient Medicine in its wider social and cultural context. The successful candidate will contribute to our general education curriculum by teaching classes in the areas of Ancient Medicine and science in the Greek and Roman worlds, relevant general education course offerings, as well as Greek and/or Latin, as needed. Preference will be given to the candidates who have demonstrated an active research agenda and are developing an international reputation for expertise in the field, as well as a record of outstanding teaching, particularly in the areas of Ancient Medicine and classical languages (Greek and/or Latin). The successful candidate will be expected to: Maintain an active research agenda and demonstrate a clear trajectory for publication, building an international reputation and potential for attracting external funding. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Ancient Medicine in its wider social and cultural context, relevant offerings in Greek and Roman civilization, and Greek and/or Latin languages, as needed. Supervise and mentor diverse students in a public, land-grant, research-intensive institution. Contribute to departmental, college, and university service. Collaborate with interdisciplinary research and teaching programs across campus, including, but not limited to, the Quest university-wide undergraduate program, the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences Beyond 120, the Center for the Humanities and the Public Sphere and the Center for Greek Studies. The successful candidate will have opportunities to engage with the diverse activities of the Center for Greek Studies and its interdisciplinary and collaborative programs. The Center for Greek Studies also provides competitive funding opportunities to support faculty research activities. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: PhD in Classical Studies, History or a related field with a specialization in Ancient Medicine. Ability to contribute to the department’s teaching curriculum in the area of Ancient Medicine in its wider social and cultural context in the Greek and Roman worlds and maintain an active research agenda in the field. Preferred: Desirable research interests/experiences: It is desirable that the candidate can connect their research and teaching of Ancient Medicine to Medical Humanities and the History of Science. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement and evidence of teaching effectiveness Names and contact information of three references (letters will be requested later) Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) December 15th, 2026. Review of applications will begin after the closing date and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Questions may be directed to Search Committee Chair: Dr Ifigeneia Giannadaki giannadaki.if@ufl.edu All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541742/assistant-professor-for-astronomical-instrumentation": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor for Astronomical Instrumentation",
      "description": "Assistant Professor for Astronomical Instrumentation Apply now Job no: 541742 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16060000 - LS-ASTRONOMY Classification Title: Assistant Professor Job Description: The Department of Astronomy at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time, nine-month tenure-track faculty position at the level of Assistant Professor to begin August 16, 2027, in the area of Space Astronomical Instrumentation and Technology Development. Candidates should already have a strong established research program that complements ongoing research in the Department of Astronomy, a compelling plan for future research, and the ability to supervise both graduate and undergraduate research projects. The successful applicant will gain access to the new, state-of-the-art space instrumentation laboratory currently under construction at UF. The Astronomical Space Technology Research Applications Lab (ASTRAL) includes ISO 6+ cleanroom facilities with ISO 4-5 flow bench; an integration vacuum chamber (<1.0E-6 Torr) for space instrument and component performance testing; electrostatic discharge (ESD) flooring and stations; dedicated, ultra-high purity (UHP) gas fixtures; and silicone-free cleanroom spaces for sensitive space experimentation. The Department of Astronomy includes a core team of engineers through the Astronomy Instrumentation Service Center that the successful applicant will have access to support their program. UF is home to the Nanoscale Research Facility (NRF; https://nrf.aux.eng.ufl.edu/), which offers renowned fabrication and characterization equipment, as well as expert staff and engineering. The successful applicant is encouraged to participate in and contribute to UF’s newly established Astraeus Space Institute, which supports space-based research across many disciplines ( https://astraeus.ufl.edu/ ). UF's world-class research facilities and telescopes ( www.astro.ufl.edu/research/telescopes ), including the 10.4-m Gran Telescopio Canarias in Spain, the Dharma survey telescope in Arizona, and the MINERVA Australis exoplanet RV array. Faculty at UF also carry out research using the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument and the Sloan Digital Sky Survey V. UF is home to HiPerGator, one of the most powerful high-performance computers at a US public university ( www.rc.ufl.edu/services/hipergator/ ). UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked 8 th in the country among public universities (US News and World Report 2026 rankings), and 1 st among public institutions in the Wall Street Journal 2023 survey. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families, and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, parental leave, and leave payouts. To learn more about benefits visit: UF Benefits Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. degree in Astronomy, Astrophysics, Physics, or a closely related field is required Preferred Post-Doctoral research experience. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a cover letter summarizing the applicant's interest in, and suitability for the position; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a list of publications; (4) a statement summarizing the applicant’s past, current, and future research programs and future plans, including how the candidate's current and planned instrumentation and technology development program reinforces their scientific research program (6 page limit) (5) a statement of experience and/or interest in teaching and mentoring (3 page limit); (6) a statement describing the broader impacts of the applicant's professional activities (2 page limit). Broader impacts may include, for example, efforts to increase public engagement and literacy, develop a competitive STEM workforce, improve STEM education and other positive benefits for society; (7) the names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning December 11, 2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Questions may be addressed to the Search Committee Chair, Professor Keri Hoadley ( khoadley@ufl.edu ). All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541744/lecturersenior-lecturer-in-communication-studies": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Communication Studies",
      "description": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Communication Studies Apply now Job no: 541744 Work type: Instructional Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16580000 - LS- SPEECH & COMM STUDIES Classification Title: Lecturer/Senior Lecturer Minimum Requirements: •A Ph.D. in Communication Studies or a closely related field (Ph.D. must be completed by August 15, 2027; an M.A. will be considered if other qualifications are outstanding). •Demonstrated excellence as a teacher, with evidence of incorporating diverse perspectives into teaching. •A track record of teaching public speaking and other human communication courses. •Experience teaching small, large, and (online) undergraduate classes. •The ability to work collaboratively with faculty and staff in the unit and across campus. •A record of scholarly research and conference presentations. Job Description: The William & Grace Dial Center for Speech and Communication Studies at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for the position of Communication Studies Lecturer or Senior Lecturer (Instructional Professor Track) who will serve as the Coordinator of the Public Speaking Lab, beginning August 16, 2027. The position is a full-time, nine-month, renewable, non-tenure-accruing appointment at the rank of Lecturer or Senior Lecturer, with an opportunity for advancement in UF’s Lecturer/Instructional Professor track. This position will have a focus on Public Speaking instruction and administration. This candidate will coordinate the Public Speaking Lab. Duties of the Coordinator include training, managing, and assessing lab staffers; coordinating lab workshops and events; managing lab hours, appointments, technology, and security protocols; and coordinating Dial Center-related events and workshops that are public-speaking-centered. The individual in this position works very closely with the Director of Public Speaking and our undergraduate and graduate assistants. The successful candidate will be expected to teach a minimum of two courses per semester in addition to their administrative duties as the Public Speaking Lab Coordinator and will be expected to teach our foundational courses in public speaking, as well as the communication practicum for undergraduate students. Candidates will also be expected to contribute to the development of courses for the UF Quest Initiative; support the UF Beyond 120 Initiative; work with the Center’s student groups; provide outreach for the Center through connections and collaborations with other campus units and the local community; participate in Center activities; and provide service to the university and the communication studies discipline. The University is a major comprehensive, land-grant, research-intensive university, ranked in recent years as among the nation’s top public universities. Gainesville, Florida is a city of approximately 150,000 residents in Alachua County. It sits 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to the major cities of Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville. Its many nearby parks, springs, and recreational areas offer year-round outdoor activities such as hiking, biking, diving, and nature photography. Cultural activities abound and local schools are highly rated. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Communication Studies or a closely related field (Ph.D. must be completed by August 15, 2027; an M.A. will be considered if other qualifications are outstanding). Demonstrated excellence as a teacher, with evidence of incorporating diverse perspectives into teaching. A track record of teaching public speaking and other human communication courses. Experience teaching small, large, and (online) undergraduate classes. The ability to work collaboratively with faculty and staff in the unit and across campus. A record of scholarly research and conference presentations. Preferred: Experience with coordinating a communication lab and/or public speaking program is preferred. Familiarity with podcasting, VR, and other technological tools used in public speaking labs is a plus. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: A letter summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A statement of teaching philosophy. A complete curriculum vitae. Recent course evaluations demonstrating excellence in teaching. Three reference letters. Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026, and will remain open until the position is filled. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. You may send email inquiries about the position to Dr. Michael Harmon, Chair of the Search Committee ( michael.harmon@ufl.edu ), but please be aware that all materials must be submitted via the online application as outlined above. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541746/federal-work-study-student-assistant-division-of-radiology": {
      "title": "Federal Work Study - Student Assistant (Division of Radiology)",
      "description": "Federal Work Study - Student Assistant (Division of Radiology) Apply now Job no: 541746 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Dentistry, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 34160400 - DN-RADIOLOGY Classification Title: Federal Work Study - Student Assistant (Division of Radiology) Classification Minimum Requirements: Must have Federal Work Study Award. Job Description: The Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Diagnostic Sciences, Division of Radiology in the College of Dentistry, is recruiting for a Federal Work Study Student Assistant. This position will assist the Clinic supervisor and Radiology technicians in the Oral Radiology Clinic. The principal duties and responsibilities assigned to this position are: Answering phones calls. Making and confirming appointments. Assisting in inputting charges and closing out radiology requests. Obtaining insurance claims for patients. Verifying patient’s insurance and authorizations for CT scans. Cleaning and stocking operatories. Perform other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $15.00 per hour Required Qualifications: Must have Federal Work Study Award. Preferred: Excellent interpersonal and communications skills, both written and oral. Strong computer skills including proficiency with MS Word, Excel, and Outlook. Exercise discretion in handling confidential and privileged information. Excellent organizational, time management and follow-through skills. High-level attention to detail, accuracy is essential. Ability to handle a large volume of detailed information. Must be able to work independently, be self-motivated and be able to multi-task. Professional demeanor and dress. Reliability is critical and eager to learn. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and FWS Award. This is a time-limited position Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 02 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541748/chair-professor-chemical-engineering": {
      "title": "CHAIR & PROFESSOR, Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "CHAIR & PROFESSOR, Chemical Engineering Apply now Job no: 541748 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management, Engineering Department: 19030000 - EG-CHEMICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Chair & Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate in chemical engineering or a closely related field. A distinguished record of scholarly research, education, mentoring, and professional contribution that merits appointment as a tenured full professor at the University of Florida. Evidence of successful leadership in a research-intensive university or comparably complex organization, including building collaborative, high-performing organizational cultures and developing faculty, staff, and emerging leaders. Demonstrated ability to develop and articulate a compelling strategic vision to advance the reputation and ranking of the department by translating that vision into clear priorities, strategic investments and measurable outcomes. Is skilled in the development and expansion of sponsored research programs, including fostering collaborative research and advancing high-impact programs. Excellent communication, judgment, integrity, and relationship-building skills; ability to communicate and work effectively with faculty, staff, students, administrators, and external stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to student success and to a scholarly and working environment where people with varied backgrounds and perspectives can contribute and thrive. Experience with external engagement and development, such as industry partnerships, alumni outreach, and fundraising. Job Description: The next Chair will join the Department at an important point in its development. The Department has 25 tenured and tenure-track faculty members, 6 instructional faculty members, and 17 affiliate faculty members. It enrolls more than 70 Ph.D. students, approximately 15 M.S. students, and over 475 undergraduate students in an ABET accredited program. Faculty conduct experimental and computational research in materials science and engineering, biotechnology, and energy systems, with computational as well as experimental focus and with annual research expenditures of approximately $6.5 million. The Department’s primary building is undergoing a comprehensive renovation scheduled for completion in Summer 2027, with more than $44 million in support from the State of Florida. The renovated building will include research laboratories, teaching laboratories, shared facilities, and collaboration spaces. The next Chair will be able to establish an active research program in the renovated building while helping align the new space, faculty strengths, University investments, and external partnerships with the Department’s next phase of growth. The successful candidate will develop a shared vision for the Department and provide the leadership needed to translate that vision into action. This includes identifying areas in which UF Chemical Engineering can lead; developing faculty and future leaders; strengthening research and educational programs; expanding engagement with industry and other external partners; managing human, financial, and physical resources; and positioning the Department to pursue major opportunities beyond the work of any individual faculty member. Leadership and Department Development The Chair will work effectively with faculty who bring broad expertise and experience, identify opportunities, establish priorities, build support for those priorities, and create the conditions for faculty, staff, and students to succeed. Key responsibilities include: Developing and implementing a strategic vision that builds on existing strengths and identifies areas in which UF Chemical Engineering can grow and lead during the next five to ten years. Translating that vision into priorities for faculty recruitment, research development, educational programs, facilities, partnerships, advancement, and resource allocation. Recruiting, mentoring, retaining, and supporting outstanding faculty and staff while developing leadership capacity across career stages. Fostering shared governance, effective communication, collaboration, and accountability while advocating for the Department within HWCOE and across UF. Research Leadership and External Engagement The Chair will help increase the scale, impact, and visibility of the Department’s research. The successful candidate will bring an established record of competitive federal funding and an understanding of how to develop and sustain strong individual and collaborative programs. Key responsibilities include: Maintaining an active, externally funded research program while serving as Chair. Supporting competitive faculty research programs and creating opportunities for multidisciplinary collaboration. Recognizing emerging opportunities, connecting complementary expertise, and building teams able to compete for major collaborative programs, centers, and institutes. Facilitating participation in interdisciplinary UF initiatives, including the UF Health Cancer Center, UF Astraeus Space Institute, Florida Semiconductor Institute, and other University research programs. Strengthening connections with University research infrastructure, other academic units, national and international laboratories, government agencies, foundations, and external partners. Industry Partnerships and Engagement The Chair will expand industry engagement as an important component of research growth and student education, strengthening relationships with industries important to Florida and the Southeast while also developing national and international partnerships. Key responsibilities include: Growing industry-sponsored research and sustained university-industry partnerships that create meaningful opportunities for faculty and students. Supporting consortia, federal programs, public-private partnerships, and other models that connect academic and industrial expertise. Expanding industry participation in internships and co-ops, sponsored projects, research experiences, seminars, mentoring, and professional development. Working with faculty, College leadership, alumni, and industry partners to keep degree programs responsive to evolving technical and workforce needs. Developing national and international industry relationships that broaden research, education, student placement, and professional opportunities. Faculty Student and Community Engagement The Chair will recognize and develop the varied contributions of faculty and staff, create opportunities for individuals to advance shared goals, and cultivate the Department’s next generation of academic leaders. Key responsibilities include: Supporting the professional growth and success of faculty, staff, graduate students, and undergraduate students. Identifying leadership potential and creating opportunities for rising faculty to contribute to Department, College, University, and professional leadership. Promoting excellence and innovation in undergraduate and graduate education, mentoring, recruitment, and professional development. Supporting undergraduate research and meaningful engagement between students and faculty. Recognizing contributions in research, teaching, mentoring, outreach to Florida schools and communities, professional service, and leadership. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate in chemical engineering or a closely related field. A distinguished record of scholarly research, education, mentoring, and professional contribution that merits appointment as a tenured full professor at the University of Florida. Evidence of successful leadership in a research-intensive university or comparably complex organization, including building collaborative, high-performing organizational cultures and developing faculty, staff, and emerging leaders. Demonstrated ability to develop and articulate a compelling strategic vision to advance the reputation and ranking of the department by translating that vision into clear priorities, strategic investments and measurable outcomes. Is skilled in the development and expansion of sponsored research programs, including fostering collaborative research and advancing high-impact programs. Excellent communication, judgment, integrity, and relationship-building skills; ability to communicate and work effectively with faculty, staff, students, administrators, and external stakeholders. Demonstrated commitment to student success and to a scholarly and working environment where people with varied backgrounds and perspectives can contribute and thrive. Experience with external engagement and development, such as industry partnerships, alumni outreach, and fundraising. Preferred: Experience recruiting, mentoring, evaluating, and retaining faculty and staff across career stages and appointment types. Experience developing multidisciplinary initiatives, major collaborative proposals, centers, institutes, or other large-scale research activities. Experience building productive industry partnerships, including sponsored research, consortia, or public-private collaborations. Experience using industry engagement to strengthen education, experiential learning, workforce preparation, and research opportunities. The ability to identify emerging funding opportunities and organize faculty and external partners around them. Experience working with College and University leadership and across academic units. Experience in advancement, philanthropy, alumni engagement, or development of external resources. Professional leadership and service in chemical engineering or a related discipline. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to receive applications until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted through UFCareers at: https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/listing/ (Search Job 541748 ). Complete applications must include the following files in PDF format: (1) A cover letter describing the candidate's interest in the position, relevant academic leadership experience, and scholarly background; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) the names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of five professional references. ( Additional required documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” section in the application .); (4) a leadership vision statement of no more than 3 pages detailing your leadership approach, a preliminary vision for the department, and two or three strategic priorities the candidate would explore with the community. Competitive candidates are expected to clearly complement the overall mission of the department and the college. The anticipated start date for this position is August 16, 2027. Priority consideration will be given to applications received by December 1, 2026 . Please direct questions about the position to Senior Associate Dean John M. Shea, Faculty Search Chair, email: jmshea@ufl.edu About the University of Florida and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering The University of Florida is the flagship institution of the State University System of Florida, a member of the Association of American Universities, and consistently ranked among the nation’s leading public universities. UF is currently ranked as one of the top 10 best public universities, 8th according to the 2026 U.S. News & World Report of Best Colleges and is recognized by Forbes as one of 10 public “New Ivies” for producing exceptional graduates. With 16 colleges, more than 150 research centers and institutes, and over 60,000 undergraduate and graduate students, UF is one of the largest universities in the nation. UF offers broad opportunities for interdisciplinary work across engineering, medicine, agriculture, the sciences, and other fields. In FY2026, University of Florida research spending reached a record $1.34 billion. UF is also home to HiPerGator, the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education. The Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering has over 400 faculty members and combines disciplinary depth with cross-cutting institutes, centers, shared facilities, industry partnerships, and a statewide mission. About the Department The Department of Chemical Engineering offers ABET accredited B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in Chemical Engineering. The Department includes 25 primary faculty members, including 6 instructional faculty members, and 17 affiliate faculty members. Current graduate enrollment includes more than 70 Ph.D. students and approximately 15 M.S. students, with over 475 students enrolled in the undergraduate program. Faculty research spans 7 research thrusts that cover major areas in materials, biotechnology, and energy systems using both computational and experimental methods. UF Chemical Engineering primary faculty include 3 active NIH MIRA Awardees and 3 active NSF CAREER Awardees. Current research is supported by federal agencies including NSF, NIH, DOE, NASA and DARPA, as well as industry, foundations, and other sponsors. These current efforts result in average annual research expenditures are approximately $6.5M. The Department has substantial opportunities for interdisciplinary research and education across the University of Florida. Growing areas of University investment relevant to Chemical Engineering include biomedical and health-related research through both UF and UF Health initiatives, such as the UF Cancer Center, agricultural efforts though UF IFAS and Extension Offices, space research and technology through the UF Astraeus Space Institute, and semiconductor research, manufacturing, and workforce development through the Florida Semiconductor Institute. Faculty also collaborate broadly across HWCOE and with researchers throughout UF. For more information about college or department initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu or che.ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541750/maintenance-generalist-ii": {
      "title": "Maintenance Generalist II",
      "description": "Maintenance Generalist II Apply now Job no: 541750 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Alachua Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Agriculture Operations Department: 60093000 - AG-BEEF RESEARCH UNIT Classification Title: Maintenance Generalist II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of experience in the field or in a related area. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Job Description: The Department of Animal Sciences is seeking a Maintenance Generalist II. This position is primarily responsible for maintaining and repairing the equipment, vehicles, and facilities located at the Santa Fe Beef Research Unit. Duties include maintenance and upkeep of tractors, vehicles, heavy equipment, sprayers, and other livestock and agricultural equipment. May be called upon to assist in all areas of farm operations and animal husbandry. Duties include but are not limited to: - Inspect, operate, and test heavy trucks and equipment to include dump trucks, farm loaders, backhoes, and farm tractors to diagnose malfunctions and conduct repairs. Load and secure equipment to transport to different parts of the farm unit or other farm units. Utilize farm equipment to maintain fences, structures, irrigation equipment, roads, ditches, culverts, etc. - Perform maintenance of grounds, facilities, and equipment including mowers, trimmers, blowers, chainsaws etc. in a manner appropriate for public viewing. - Ensure compliance of vehicles, equipment, and mechanics' facilities with safe, environmental and regulatory standards. - Assisting with cultivation of pastures, hayfields: mowing, fertilizing, planting, harvesting. - Administer the safety and training programs related to shop safety, hazardous waste, and related matters. Assist with developing specifications for equipment purchase. - Maintain fuel facilities and records. Order fuels and lubricants. Maintain vehicle travel logs. Maintain farm maintenance shop by keeping tools, and equipment in an orderly fashion. - Perform other duties as needed. Normal Work Schedule: Monday - Friday 7:00 A.M. - 3:30 P.M. Occasional overtime may be required to complete projects This is considered an essential position and may need to report to work when the University is closed during emergency weather closing events. Expected Salary: $19.16 - $21 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. Build your retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of experience in the field or in a related area. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Valid driver's license Preferred: Experience with maintenance and operation of farm machinery and heavy equipment Familiar with cattle operations CDL and SAE preferred Experience with diesel, gas, and small motor mechanics. ASE certifications would be beneficial ARC and MIG welding training or experience Experience with construction and maintenance of facilities, fences, roads Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume, and names and contact information for three references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541752/assistant-professor-or-associate-professor-in-musical-theatre": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Musical Theatre",
      "description": "Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Musical Theatre Apply now Job no: 541752 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 13040100 - COTA-THEATRE-CHAIR Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for the rank of Assistant Professor shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Candidates for the rank of Associate Professor shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Job Description: The University of Florida School of Theatre and Dance (SoTD) seeks an Assistant or Associate Professor of Musical Theatre to provide collaborative leadership for the BFA Musical Theatre program through teaching, creative/professional practice, student mentorship, recruitment, and service. The successful candidate will bring professional performance experience in musical theatre, including experience in established regional, national, touring, commercial or comparable professional markets along with a demonstrated ability to support the artistic, academic and professional development of undergraduate students through teaching, coaching, directing, mentorship, professional training or related experience. The successful candidate will teach a range of courses from introductory through advanced levels, with responsibilities that may include Musical Theatre performance and scene study, professional preparation, audition techniques, musical theatre history, acting through song, and other courses appropriate to the candidate's expertise and the instructional needs of the School. The faculty member may also contribute to beginning and intermediate acting and introductory directing. This position requires substantial engagement with performance curricula and student mentorship across the program. The successful candidate will guide the coordination and continued development of the Musical Theatre program in collaboration with faculty colleagues while mentoring BFA Musical Theatre and Acting students in their academic, artistic, and professional development. The candidate will value collaborative, integrated Musical Theatre training and work effectively across acting, voice/music, and dance curricula in support of the development of well-rounded contemporary musical theatre artists. The candidate will also contribute to curriculum development and assessment, as well as student recruitment and retention. The successful candidate will contribute to the School’s production season through directing the fall musical and/or concert musical productions, as assigned, and will work collaboratively across both production and academic areas of the School. This includes collaboration with directors, choreographers, designers, accompanists, production personnel, guest artists, and student artists, as well as close collaboration with the School Director, SoTD Producing Director, Degree Coordinators, Theatre Studies and Performance faculty, SoTD Accompanist, and other faculty and staff within the School of Theatre and Dance. The successful candidate should be able to work effectively within the School’s existing resources and production structures while maintaining an active professional practice and connections to regional and national professional markets. The position requires a strong commitment to collegiality, collaborative work, and excellence in undergraduate education within a broad-based liberal arts university. The faculty member will actively recruit promising students for the Musical Theatre program and participate in School of Theatre and Dance recruitment activities, which may require travel. The faculty member will also participate in School, College, University, and professional service; shared governance; academic advising; campus outreach; and donor-relations activities. The School values faculty who maintain an active professional practice while contributing fully to the educational mission of the University. Candidates should demonstrate an interest in helping shape the evolving future of musical theatre training and in preparing students for the changing professional landscape. The ideal candidate will demonstrate technological fluency and adaptability across contemporary digital environments, both in preparing students for the professional musical theatre industry and in effectively navigating the teaching, communication, collaboration, and administrative responsibilities of a major research university. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a change-maker. We seek a colleague who will position emerging artists and researchers as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities Teaching and Student Mentorship Teach undergraduate Musical Theatre courses at beginning through advanced levels, which may include: Musical Theatre performance and scene study Introduction to Musical Theatre Studies Musical Theatre Professional Preparation Musical Theatre audition techniques Acting through song Musical Theatre history Musical Theatre workshops Other courses within the candidate's expertise and the instructional needs of SoTD Teach beginning and intermediate acting as appropriate. Teach introductory directing as appropriate. Mentor BFA Musical Theatre and Acting students in academic, artistic, and career planning. Prepare students for professional auditions, including traditional Musical Theatre, contemporary Musical Theatre, pop/rock, monologue work, and acting through song. May have opportunity to teach courses in theatre studies and/or UF Quest courses Contribute to curriculum development, assessment, and program development. Active participation in our learning community, finding innovative ways to connect the arts to critical discourse in other disciplines. Prepare students to navigate contemporary digital tools and platforms used in professional auditioning, self-promotion, networking, portfolio development, and career development. Creative and Professional Practice Direct the fall musical and/or concert musical productions, as assigned. Contribute to the School's production season through coaching or other professional expertise. Maintain an active professional practice and professional connections appropriate to the field. Bring current professional practices and expectations into the classroom and production environment. Contribute to student preparation for professional rehearsal, audition, and performance environments. Recruitment, Advising, and Program Development Recruit promising students to the Musical Theatre program and participate actively in SoTD recruitment activities. Contribute to recruitment and retention initiatives. Develop and maintain relationships with professional and regional markets relevant to Musical Theatre students. Provide collaborative leadership in the coordination, strategic development, and continued growth of the Musical Theatre program, building upon the strengths and expertise of existing faculty. Participate in campus outreach and donor-relations activities as appropriate. Collaboration and Service Work collaboratively with the School Director, Degree Coordinators, Theatre Studies and Performance faculty, SoTD Accompanist, Design & Production faculty, Dance faculty and staff. Collaborate effectively with directors, choreographers, designers, accompanists, production personnel, guest artists, and student artists. Participate in School of Theatre and Dance, College of the Arts, University, and professional service activities. Attend School of Theatre and Dance, College of the Arts, and full faculty meetings. Participate in shared governance and other University responsibilities appropriate to the position. Contribute to a collegial, collaborative, and inclusive School community. Perform other duties appropriate to the position and assigned by the School Director. SCHOOL OF THEATRE + DANCE: The light-filled Nadine McGuire Theatre and Dance Pavilion is home to the School of Theatre + Dance (SoTD). The School’s international full-time faculty of 35 recognized artists and researchers, complemented by adjunct and guest faculty and staff, including full- and part-time professionals. The School offers MFA curricula in acting and in design, which includes scene design, lighting design and costume design; BFA programs in acting, musical theatre, and theatrical production with concentrations in costume, digital media, lighting, scenic, and sound; and a BA with concentrations in general theatre, stage management, and theatre management. The School features two degree programs in dance designed to develop the individual voice of the dance artist, a BFA and a BA. Approximately 200 students major in the artistically diverse curricula of the School of Theatre + Dance. Through its production program, outreach and innovative programming, the School of Theatre + Dance provides multiple cultural opportunities annually to thousands of members of the University and the greater community. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Dance and the National Association of Schools of Theatre. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/theatreanddance . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, an eclectic community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. Nine-month faculty employees accrue approximately 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which can provide savings at various business and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: Assistant Professor: $70,000-$78,000 – commensurate with experience Associate Professor: $84,000-$92,000 – commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following Terminal degree (MFA or PhD) in Musical Theatre, Theatre, Acting, or a closely related field. Potential for record of achievement in the field at a national or international level, in alignment with the unit’s tenure and promotion criteria. Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate Musical Theatre performance. Demonstrated ability to prepare students for Musical Theatre auditions and professional performance, including traditional Musical Theatre, contemporary Musical Theatre, pop/rock, acting through song, and monologue coaching. Demonstrated experience directing Musical Theatre productions. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, guest artists, production personnel, and student artists. Demonstrated ability to mentor undergraduate students in their academic, artistic, and professional development. Demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate education and student-centered mentorship. Demonstrated commitment to teaching practices that reflect current and evolving professional practices and prepare students to navigate a changing field. Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following Three to five years of university-level teaching experience in Musical Theatre beyond graduate assistantship, or comparable substantial teaching experience. Demonstrated ability to teach in a secondary area such as acting, directing, Musical Theatre history, or related areas. Demonstrated professional experience and established connections within regional, national, and/or professional Musical Theatre markets. Evidence of artistic/professional development through ongoing creative or professional activity. Demonstrated experience with curriculum development, program assessment, recruitment, retention, or academic program growth. Demonstrated leadership ability, including the capacity to contribute to the coordination and development of a Musical Theatre program. Demonstrated ability to recruit and retain School of Theatre + Dance majors. Evidence of a commitment to innovative and cross-disciplinary curricula Experience navigating a multi-disciplinary higher education setting. Demonstrated aptitude for working toward structural and strategic change. Organizational, management and communication experience in the higher education setting Demonstrated technological fluency and adaptability, including the ability to independently navigate contemporary digital tools and platforms used in higher education, academic administration, communication and collaboration, musical theatre training, auditioning, professional preparation, and the performing arts industry. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, all application documents must be submitted by December 14, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: : (1) a detailed letter of application that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a research narrative; (4) statement of teaching philosophy; (5) links to research, production and/or teaching sample (Note that the UF application system only allows for one 5 MB maximum file size PDF document); (6) names and contact information of three professional references. The UF system will give you the option to upload letters of reference. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Dr. Colleen Rua Associate Dean of Research and Strategic Initiatives and Associate Professor of Theatre Studies Chair, Assistant or Associate Professor of Musical Theatre Search Committee Email: crua@arts.ufl.edu The search committee does not utilize AI or other software to provide any level of applicant assessment. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541756/senior-director-dev-administration": {
      "title": "Senior Director, Dev Administration",
      "description": "Senior Director, Dev Administration Apply now Job no: 541756 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement Department: 19011700 - EG-DEVELOPMENT Classification Title: SR DIR, Dev Administration Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor's degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. Job Description: Portfolio Management Responsible for implementing fundraising initiatives, identifying, and cultivating prospects. Focus on individual prospects and will work with leadership, corporate and foundation prospects where gifts are assessed at $500,000 and above. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring major gift solicitations to closure. Develop and present major gift proposals by acknowledging and recognizing gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the mission, vision and values of the unit and UFA. Responsible for articulating, communicating, and prioritizing within the context of the overall Advancement mission, goals, and objectives. Strengthen alumni and donors’ awareness of, and interest in giving opportunities and collaborate with academic unit-based development officers across campus. Strategic Initiatives & Leadership Develop support for the program by effectively involving stakeholders, such as volunteers, faculty, staff, and members of the advisory board, in efforts supporting the fundraising program. Assist in developing and implementing a comprehensive communications plan to inform constituents and identified markets about the mission, vision, and values of the unit, its funding priorities, and gift opportunities. Initiate, plan and support activities and events involving volunteers and prospects. Create a structured process for the identification, recruitment, evaluation, recognition, and replacement of volunteers to strengthen fundraising effectiveness. Participate in recruiting experienced leadership for boards and/or committees in order to ensure that these groups are representative of, and responsive to, the communities served. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty, and administration. Manage staff using talent management best practices in alignment with UF Advancement’s mission and vision; ensure best practices in office systems, practices, files and record keeping; ensure effective use of strategic tools and resources such as contact reports, prospect management, research, and analysis, and the CRM database. Build, manage and lead a strong team of development professionals. Identify and recruit top talent and provide leadership, counsel on matters relating to fundraising, and accountability including establishing goals in alignment with unit objectives, developing metrics and strategic planning. In addition, incumbent will attend senior development meetings. Program Coordination In partnership with the Executive Director of Advancement, assist with the formation and implementation of fundraising plans and policies related to the College, interprets policy to faculty and other staff, as necessary. Work closely with the Executive Director of Advancement and members of the Dean’s Advisory Board to identify new prospects and establish fundraising priorities for HWCOE. Assist with the administration of the development programs for HWCOE promoting potential donor priorities as they relate to the overall priorities. Assist with planning and implementing short- and long-term development and alumni programs and strategies. Assist with designing solicitation strategies on a case-by-case basis, being cognizant of development and college office needs and procedures. Create a structured process for the identification, recruitment, evaluation, recognition, and replacement of volunteers to strengthen fundraising effectiveness. Assist in developing and implementing a comprehensive communications plan to inform constituents and identified markets about the mission, vision, and values of the unit, its funding priorities, and gift opportunities. Ensures best practices in office systems, practices, files, and record keeping; ensures effective use of strategic tools and resources such as contact reports, prospect management, research, and analysis, and the Advance database. Perform related duties as required or deemed appropriate to the accomplishment of the responsibilities and functions related to this position. Expected Salary: Up to $130,000 annually; Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Master's degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor's degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. A valid driver’s license is required. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Five to eight years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $500,000 and above from individual and corporate prospects with campaign experience. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Experience in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and as a team player. Competency in problem solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver’s license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. UF Advancement has defined the following critical competencies for organizational and position specific success: Relationship Mastery - Identifies, targets, and strategically builds relationships with colleagues, prospects, donors, and volunteers. Establishes and maintains effective relationships, demonstrating genuine interest in the well-being and opinions of others and recognizing common ground with individuals from varying backgrounds. Anticipates conversational direction with ability to swiftly adapt and diffuse high tension situations. Successfully identifies the proper timing, medium and consistency to engage stakeholders. Exhibits a high level of emotional intelligence in all interactions. Donor Focus - Ability to establish, cultivate and steward prospects/ donors to build and maintain lifelong partnerships and trust on behalf of the institution. Consistently searches for ways to improve the donor experience centralizing all efforts and tasks around meeting their needs. Matches donor/prospect passions with university priorities, involving the right people and resources at the right time. Places prospect/ donor needs ahead of personal success. Utilizes resources, remains flexible, and creatively suggests individualized engagement activities. Tenacity - Ability to demonstrate the discipline and resilience necessary to successfully achieve goals and strategies. Identifies and seizes opportunities, setting “stretch” goals and exceeding them (always looking for the next rung on the ladder) Ability to re-direct prospects from distractions and non-productive requests (in control but not seemingly in control) Strong commitment to close gifts (every day is a domino) Does not give up in the face of obstacles (master of “No”) Credibility - Displays responsible, reliable, and trustworthy actions. Acts in an ethical and authentic manner to support the mission of the university, demonstrating a “can do” spirit, sense of ownership, urgency, and commitment. Stay current on functional and technical aspects of one’s job seeking opportunities to acquire new knowledge and skills. Promotes and establishes trust, respect and rapport utilizing diplomacy and tact. Fulfill commitments made to donors, colleagues, and university partners. Entrepreneurial Spirit - Ability to generate creative solutions and take risks. Understands current environment and possesses the ability to adapt to new opportunities and challenges. Generates innovative ideas, solutions, and opportunities to positively impact results (doesn’t order from the menu) Learns from success and failure with a drive to succeed (hates to lose) Accepts, embraces, and leads change. Engaging Communication - Ability to attentively listen and express oneself clearly and empathetically in interactions with others in all forms of communication. Possesses the ability to engage and inspire. Successfully reads verbal and non-verbal cues adapting communication style and strategy as needed. Demonstrates active listening and understands the art of asking questions to skillfully obtain more complete information. Commands attention when speaking and presenting to individuals or groups. Strategic Thinking - Ability to develop strategies and plans that integrate into the organization’s mission, priorities, and goals. Ability to effectively synthesize information and data to evaluate and prioritize prospects/donors in a larger context (knows their prospects) Ability to document and illustrate a comprehensive engagement strategy with realistic timelines. Collaboratively engages colleagues and academic partners and integrates their knowledge into strategy. Ability to think big, and create bold and inspiring tactics. Mission Driven - Fundamentally identifies with the core mission to create awareness, build relationships, generate support, and recognize donors for the purpose of advancing the university. Considers national trends and best practices of advancement within higher education. Possesses a solid appreciation of UF history, culture, and tradition. Understands the power of philanthropy and how to engage donors toward achieving their goals. Creates advocates for the university through the ability to “visualize dreams,” “ask,” and “close.” Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, you must upload a cover letter and resume with application. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541757/astasofull-prof-cardiovascular-surgery-icu": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Cardiovascular Surgery ICU",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Cardiovascular Surgery ICU Apply now Job no: 541757 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29141710 - MD-SURGERY-TCV-CARDIOVASC Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery as well having completed a critical care fellowship (board certification or eligibility in surgical critical care). Excellent communication skills and evidence of collaborative work are required. Job Description: The Department of Surgery at the University of Florida, College of Medicine is currently seeking applicants for an open rank, tenure or non-tenure track faculty member for our Cardiovascular Intensive care Unit. As a faculty member in the Department of Surgery, you will join an outstanding multi-disciplinary surgical and critical care team as an intensivist in the Cardiovascular Intensive Care Unit in the New Heart and Vascular Surgery Tower. There are opportunities for rotations in the Thoracic and Vascular Units on a rotational basis with your colleagues. Your primary clinical mission is provide critical care for the Cardiac Surgery patients. UF Health is a major referral Center for Complex Cardiac Surgery for the SouthEast including aneurysms, dissections, minimally invasive and transcatheter valves etc. The team is supported with excellent mid-level providers and residents as part of the Hospital Critical Care Organization. In addition, if you wish you can participate in extracorporeal membrane oxygenation patient cannulation and coverage (ECMO) Surgical/operative time, research opportunities and educational focus are all negotiable. Specific educational duties include medical student and resident education rotating on your service. You will also be expected to assist with the teaching of surgical residents through the level-specific, didactic curriculum and medical students through the 3rd year rotation small group sessions. Finally, you will also participate in the training of the Surgical Critical Care, Acute Care Surgery and TCV fellows, as well as all Critical Care Fellows that rotate through your ICU/s. Significant opportunities are potentially available for clinical, translation or basic science research with the University of Florida Sepsis and Critical Illness Research Center and other Laboratories in the University Department of Surgery. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery as well having completed a critical care fellowship (board certification or eligibility in surgical critical care). Excellent communication skills and evidence of collaborative work are required. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541758/veterinary-community-outreach-program-veterinary-assistant-client-services-focus": {
      "title": "Veterinary Community Outreach Program Veterinary Assistant (Client Services Focus)",
      "description": "Veterinary Community Outreach Program Veterinary Assistant (Client Services Focus) Apply now Job no: 541758 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28314000 - VM-VETERINARY COMM OUTRCH PROG Classification Title: Veterinary Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: The University of Florida’s Veterinary Community Outreach Program (VCOP) is seeking a Veterinary Assistant with a primary focus on administrative and client service support. This position serves as a key point of contact for the shelters and rescue groups and plays an essential role in day-to-day clinic operations through effective communication, coordination, scheduling, and customer service. In addition to client-facing and administrative responsibilities, the role includes limited assistant duties to support clinical operations as needed. VCOP is designed to expose students to the realities of veterinary practice in both community and shelter medicine settings, while supporting high-volume spay/neuter services and preventive care for canine and feline patients. Example of Veterinary Assistant Duties and Responsibilities include but not limited to: • Coordinate communication with shelter/rescue partners , community members, and hospital staff • Enter, update, and maintain accurate client and patient information in medical record, including scanning and documenting in records • Manage invoices, enter charges, and adhere to all financial policies • Collaborate with the team to create and manage the weekly surgery schedule • Maintain an organized inventory system and order supplies on a regular basis • Assist with processing laundry • Handle patients safely as needed The service receives Monday - Friday. May require working earlier than 7:45am or later than 5:00pm This is a full-time, benefited position and is considered essential personnel. A post-offer education verification, background check, and health assessment are required. Expected Salary: $15-16 /hr Preferred • 1-year experience in Veterinary Medicine Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs , bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540157/directorsenior-director-of-advancement-student-life": {
      "title": "Director/Senior Director of Advancement, Student Life",
      "description": "Director/Senior Director of Advancement, Student Life Apply now Job no: 540157 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management Department: 04010000 - SL-STUDENT LIFE Classification Title: SR DIR, Advancement Classification Minimum Requirements: Director of Advancement: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Senior Director of Advancement: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. Job Description: The Director/Senior Director of Advancement for the Division of Student Life is responsible for implementation of a broad range of fundraising activities, focused primarily on acquiring major gifts. Reporting to the Executive Director of Advancement (ExD) and the Vice President (VP) for Student Life, the incumbent is the primary fundraiser for the Division of Student Life and will develop and execute the fundraising program to fulfill the financial needs as identified by the ExD and VP. Additionally, this role will serve as a strategic partner with Student Life departments and Advancement ensuring conformity with the central fundraising efforts and alumni programs and goals, and in promoting a culture of philanthropy by broadening constituents’ understanding of the value of giving. Work with the Division of Student Life units to identify prospects and cultivate donors in alignment with University protocols; maintain and enhance relationships with past donors and philanthropic partners. Portfolio Management Actively work with the VP of Student Life and Executive Director of Advancement, implement and sustain a multi-year fundraising plan; establish the major gift program for the Division of Student Life by effectively building a pipeline of major-giving prospects to ensure philanthropic goals are met.. Manage a portfolio of 60-80 major gift prospects. Identify, cultivate, solicit and maintain all major gift prospects for the division, strategically managing relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure, focusing on gifts of $100,000 or more and oversee the division’s participation in the University’s annual giving activities. Build and manage stewardship relationships, utilizing a strategic moves management approach to include identification, cultivation, solicitation and stewardship of prospects. Responsible for articulating and communicating priorities within the context of the overall Division of Student Life mission, goals and objectives. Strengthen alumni and donors’ awareness of, and interest in, giving opportunities within Student Life and collaborate with academic unit-based development officers across campus as appropriate. Strategic Initiatives Oversee plans for campaign readiness to ensure all campaign goals are achieved, in alignment with the strategic priorities of the Division of Student Life. Work with and advise the Vice President of Student Life and senior leadership in the analysis, formation and implementation of fundraising and alumni activities plans and policies. Advise the Vice President of Student Life on strategic and annual fundraising and alumni affairs goals and prioritization of these efforts. Initiate, plan and support activities and events involving prospects, donors, volunteers and staff. Participate in University events and serve on committees as needed. Foster productive and collaborative professional relationships with Advancement colleagues and throughout the University, demonstrating a commitment to the One UF philosophy. Work in partnership with the Division of Student Life’s Marketing and Communications team to implement a comprehensive communications plan as well as create print and digital collateral to be used for fundraising efforts. With the alumni association taking the lead, support their efforts in student philanthropy initiatives, affiliate groups (Cicerones, ABA, AHA, etc) and annual giving efforts. Maintain strong partnership and collaborate with the Parent & Family Giving Program within UF Advancement to expose parents and family members to the Division of Student Life and philanthropic opportunities. Develop and implement a robust stewardship strategy involving divisional and departmental leadership. Management of Accounts Serve as a liaison to the Director of Finance in Student Life to help monitor and manage foundation accounts held in Student Life. Discuss fund usage and future plans with Student Life Directors regularly. Partner to appropriately steward endowment donors during the Annual Endowment Reporting period led by Central Advancement Donor Relations team. Help ensure scholarship allocation is cultivated and provided in alignment with Division of Student Life priorities and donor intent. Serve as a resource to DSL fund administrators. Leadership Responsible for managing employees including, but not limited to recruitment and hiring, onboarding, training, certifying time and attendance, goal setting, performance management, and creating action plans. Evaluate staff and develop a strong sense of teamwork and establish an environment of mentoring and support. Coach the team and bring cohesion to the department. Student Life General Duties Serve on University and/or Divisional committees at the request of the Vice President for Student Life. Serve as a member of the Vice President's Student Life Leadership Team. To help foster deeper collaboration efforts and meaningful engagement with donors, leadership, and Student Life staff as well as members of our advancement community, this will be an onsite position based in Gainesville, Florida. Student Life The University of Florida Division of Student Life serves as the cornerstone of the student experience, dedicated to supporting, engaging and empowering every Gator throughout their academic and personal journey during their time at UF and beyond. Through more than 25 areas and units, Student Life champions meaningful experiences that foster community connection, career design and lifelong wellbeing. Our dedicated team members believe every Gator should have an individualized, excellent student experience that prepares them for lifelong success. University of Florida At the University of Florida , we are feeding communities, healing coastlines, exploring space, curing diseases, powering cities, building industries, and imagining wonders. As the state’s most comprehensive university, UF is a land, sea, and space grant institution with ties to every Florida county, a top academic medical center, and much more. The ideas born here gather steam and make tangible impacts. Florida is among the nation’s leaders in technology transfer, turning discoveries into companies, products, and jobs. Come join us—let’s see where we can go together. Gainesville, Florida Gainesville, Florida is a vibrant, welcoming city known for its natural beauty, rich culture, and strong sense of community. As home to the University of Florida and UF Health, it is a hub for education, healthcare, and innovation. With award-winning parks, a thriving arts scene, and a variety of dining and entertainment options, Gainesville offers something for everyone. Ranked the #1 city to move to in Florida by USA Today in 2024, Gainesville combines small-town charm with big-city amenities—making it a great place to live, work, and thrive. Visit the city’s website for community information. Expected Salary: Director: $95,000 – 105,000 annually Senior Director: $115,000 - $125,000 annually Commensurate with education and experience. The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package including: Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future. Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave. Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Director of Advancement: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Senior Director of Advancement: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and six years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and eight years of experience. Valid driver's license. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Master’s degree; degree specialization in Student Personnel or a related field. Four to six years of philanthropic and strategic planning experience in complex higher education organizations. Three to five years of major gift fundraising experience (preference with higher education fundraising, preferably Student Life fundraising efforts) with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual and corporate prospects with campaign experience. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards as well as expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with competency in problem solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and work collaboratively with a wide range of individuals (students, faculty, staff, alumni, and external constituents) are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions. Experience in a data-driven environment; proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541739/extension-agent-iiii-commercial-horticulture": {
      "title": "Extension Agent I-III – Commercial Horticulture",
      "description": "Extension Agent I-III – Commercial Horticulture Apply now Job no: 541739 Work type: Permanent Status Faculty Location: Collier Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60354011 - AG-COLLIER Classification Title: EXT AGENT I-III Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall hold a master’s degree and/or bachelor’s degree with relevant experience and accomplishments. Job Description: You Belong at a University That's Leading the Way The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (UF/IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Opportunity The University of Florida/IFAS Extension and Collier County seek to fill a 12-month, permanent status-accruing Extension Agent I-III position in Commercial Horticulture. This faculty position provides leadership for a comprehensive commercial horticulture Extension program serving Collier County's landscape, nursery, arboriculture, pest management, turfgrass, and green industry sectors. The successful candidate will develop, implement, evaluate, and report innovative, research-based educational programs addressing current and emerging issues affecting commercial horticulture businesses, environmental stewardship, workforce development, pesticide licensing, and landscape sustainability. This position is jointly supported by UF/IFAS Extension and Collier County and reports to the County Extension Director with programmatic guidance from appropriate state and regional Extension leadership. Program Development and Delivery Conduct formal and informal needs assessments to identify priorities affecting commercial horticulture stakeholders throughout Collier County. Develop, deliver, evaluate, and report science-based educational programs addressing integrated pest management, plant health care, irrigation efficiency, water conservation, fertilizer best management practices, arboriculture, invasive species management, storm resilience, landscape sustainability, worker protection safety, and emerging horticultural issues. Plan and deliver in-person and virtual educational programs including workshops, field demonstrations, field days, webinars, certification courses, professional development programs, and industry conferences. Deliver Green Industries Best Management Practices education and related workforce development programming. Provide continuing education opportunities supporting pesticide applicator licensing and other professional credentials in partnership with industry associations and regulatory agencies. Translate university research into practical recommendations that improve profitability, environmental stewardship, operational efficiency, and risk management within commercial horticulture operations. Industry and Community Engagement Establish and maintain productive working relationships with landscape professionals, nursery operators, arborists, pest management professionals, turf managers, municipalities, and public agencies. Develop and maintain a stakeholder advisory committee representing commercial horticulture interests to assist with program planning, evaluation, and accountability. Collaborate with industry organizations, local governments, educational institutions, and community partners to address horticultural and environmental priorities. Serve as a trusted source of research-based information and technical assistance for commercial horticulture clientele throughout Collier County. Scholarship and Collaboration Demonstrate Extension scholarship through educational innovation, applied demonstrations, publications, presentations, impact reporting, and other scholarly activities. Collaborate with county, regional, and state Extension faculty on multidisciplinary initiatives addressing agriculture, horticulture, natural resources, urban landscapes, and water quality. Pursue grants, contracts, sponsorships, and other external funding opportunities that enhance program effectiveness and reach. Maintain active participation in relevant professional associations and continuing education opportunities. Organizational Responsibilities Maintain accurate records and complete plans of work, reports of accomplishment, impact documentation, and accountability reports in accordance with University and Extension policies. Utilize data-driven evaluation methods to document program effectiveness, stakeholder behavior change, and economic, environmental, and social impacts. Participate in Extension office initiatives, county activities, and emergency management responsibilities as assigned. Support youth development programming as a minor component of the position (approximately 5%). About UF The University of Florida is a premier public research university and a member of the Association of American Universities. UF is dedicated to excellence in teaching, research, and service and consistently ranks among the nation's top public institutions. The University serves as a catalyst for discovery, innovation, and community engagement throughout Florida and beyond. About UF/IFAS The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is one of the nation's most comprehensive land-grant systems. Through integrated teaching, research, and Extension programs, the institute generates knowledge and solutions that advance agriculture, protect natural resources, support economic development, and improve communities across Florida. About UF/IFAS Extension UF/IFAS Extension connects research and education to the practical needs of Florida's residents and industries. With faculty located throughout the state, Extension delivers science-based information through educational programs, consultations, demonstrations, applied research, and community partnerships. Extension faculty help stakeholders solve problems, adopt best practices, and make informed decisions grounded in the latest research. The University of Florida offers a comprehensive benefits package that supports employees' health, financial security, professional growth, and work-life balance. Additional information regarding benefits is available on the UF Human Resources benefits website . About Collier County Collier County is located in Southwest Florida along the Gulf Coast and encompasses more than 2,300 square miles, making it one of the largest counties in Florida by land area. Home to more than 417,000 residents, the county includes Naples, Immokalee, Marco Island, Golden Gate, and rapidly growing unincorporated communities. The county's economy is supported by tourism, construction, real estate, agriculture, and professional services. Agriculture remains an important economic sector, with vegetable production, citrus, ornamental plant production, and livestock operations contributing significantly to regional agricultural activity. Water conservation, nutrient management, invasive species prevention, urban tree management, hurricane resilience, and sustainable landscape practices are increasingly important priorities for Southwest Florida. The Commercial Horticulture program is well positioned to provide leadership through education, workforce development, applied demonstrations, and collaborative partnerships that address local and regional needs. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates must possess the academic credentials required for appointment at the appropriate Extension Agent rank. A bachelor's degree in horticulture, plant science, agronomy or a closely related discipline is required for Extension Agent I applicants. A master's degree, or at least 50 percent progress toward a master's degree, is required for Extension Agent II applicants. A master's degree is required for Extension Agent III applicants. Demonstrated ability to design, deliver, and evaluate educational programs. Strong written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Effective organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities. Commitment to providing timely, high‑quality customer service. Ability to work independently and as part of a collaborative team. Proficiency with educational technology, data management, and digital communication tools. Willingness to work evenings, weekends, and travel as needed. Ability to obtain and maintain a valid Florida driver’s license. Ability to obtain required Green Industries Best Management Practices Instructor and pesticide applicator certifications consistent with the Commercial Horticulture program area. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Master’s degree or higher in horticulture, plant science, agronomy or related discipline. Professional experience in Extension education or an equivalent applied education setting. Background in landscape pest management, arboriculture, irrigation, turf, or ornamental plant production. Experience securing grants or external funding. Special Instructions to Applicants: Apply at Careers at UF by 11:55 p.m. (Eastern) of the posting end date, and attach: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541740/postdoctoral-associate-plant-pathology": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Plant Pathology",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Plant Pathology Apply now Job no: 541740 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Dade Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60790000 - AG-TREC-HOMESTEAD Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The UF/IFAS Tropical Research and Education Center is seeking a postdoctoral associate in plant pathology. This is a 12-month, non-tenure track full-time position with potential of extension based on performance and funding availability. The incumbent will conduct lab, greenhouse, and field studies on sustainable management of complexes of soilborne diseases and nematodes in snap bean and other vegetables. The incumbent is expected to design and conduct experiments, collect and analyze data, prepare reports, and develop articles for publications. In addition, the incumbent will be expected to: 1) be involved in extension activities; 2) assist in securing extramural grants; 3) work as a team with scientists, industry representatives, county extension agents and specialists; and 4) mentor students and supervise technical personnel when needed. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: An earned Ph.D. in plant pathology, microbiology, or a closely related field; be highly self-motivated, hardworking, and goal-oriented; ability and willingness to work in fields; ability to function within a team of faculty, staff, and students with different backgrounds and under pressure; and strong written and oral communication skills. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Experience with plant diseases and management; skills in molecular biology; a strong publication record; willingness to collaborate with co-workers and others; ability to interact with growers and other stakeholders. Special Instructions to Applicants: Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 4 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541502/pt-occupational-therapist-pediatrics-early-steps": {
      "title": "PT Occupational Therapist - Pediatrics Early Steps",
      "description": "PT Occupational Therapist - Pediatrics Early Steps Apply now Job no: 541502 Work type: Staff Part-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: PT Occupational Therapist - Pediatrics Early Steps Classification Minimum Requirements: Graduation from an accredited Occupational Therapy Program and successful completion of American Occupational Therapy Association certification examination. Job Description: Completes developmental evaluations, screenings, and assessments in collaboration with the multidisciplinary team for the purpose of determining eligibility, monitoring progress, updating IFSPs, and planning intervention. Provides intervention/consultations as authorized on each child’s Individualized Family Support Plan in the natural environment utilizing the Primary Service Provider approach. Develops Plan of Care and obtains authorization for services as required by third party Payers and program policies. Maintains clinical materials, supplies, and work are, and submits request for Replacement or repair as needed. Expected Salary: $39.27-$51.25 per hour Required Qualifications: Licensed as a Occupational Therapist in accordance with Section 468. Florida Statutes, and Rule Chapter 64B11, F.A.C. Must have a valid Driver’s License and be eligible for a Medicaid Provider Number. Requires Level II Criminal Background Screening and be eligible for enrollment with Children’s Medical Services. Must enroll as a Medicaid Early Intervention (EI) Services provider. Completion of Early Steps online orientation modules (1-3) within 90 days of application date. Applications must be renewed every three (3) years and document thirty-six (36) hours of CEU/In-service training. All in service hours must be related to at least one of the early intervention professional competency areas. Preferred: Two to five years’ experience working with children birth to age 3 with special needs. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Background Check is Required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and contact information for 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: yes Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
