    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541350/veterinary-biomedical-equipment-technician": {
      "title": "Veterinary Biomedical Equipment Technician",
      "description": "Veterinary Biomedical Equipment Technician Apply now Job no: 541350 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28300000 - VM-VMTH Classification Title: Operations Specialist I Classification Minimum Requirements Bachelors degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Veterinary Hospitals is recruiting for a Biomedical Equipment Technician. This position serves as the first point of contact for veterinary medical equipment maintenance, troubleshooting, and repair, helping ensure critical equipment remains safe, functional, and available for patient care. The Biomedical Equipment Technician performs preventive maintenance, calibrations, inspections, and repairs on a variety of veterinary medical equipment while coordinating vendor supported service when necessary. The Biomedical Equipment Technician collaborates closely with clinicians, area managers, facilities staff, and vendors to minimize equipment downtime, support equipment installations and replacements, and maintain compliance with safety and regulatory standards. Responsibilities include assisting with equipment lifecycle management, documentation of maintenance and repair activities, equipment recalls and safety notices, and training staff on proper equipment operation and care. Critical competencies for this position include strong analytical and troubleshooting skills, excellent organizational and communication abilities, attention to detail, and the capacity to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced healthcare environment. The University of Florida is committed to supporting employees both professionally and personally. Eligible employees receive competitive health and retirement benefits, paid time off, educational and professional development opportunities, wellness resources, and exclusive employee discounts. This position requires a post-offer health assessment and background check. Expected Salary: $58,000 - $64,000, Salary based on education and experience Required Qualifications Bachelors degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred • Knowledge and experience working in a clinical veterinary environment • Experience in equipment management and maintenance • Strong understanding of safety regulations and compliance standards related to equipment. • Excellent organizational skills with a keen attention to detail. • Degree or courses in Biomedical Equipment Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 04 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541817/postdoctoral-associate-in-polymer-science-and-biocatalysts": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate in Polymer Science and Biocatalysts",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate in Polymer Science and Biocatalysts Apply now Job no: 541817 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate in Polymer Science and Biocatalysts Classification Minimum Requirements: PhD in chemistry or a closely related field. Able to start between January and August 2027. Strong publication record or evidence of research productivity. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Excellent problem-solving, analytical, and critical-thinking abilities. Demonstrated ability to work independently and as a collaborative team. Job Description: This position focuses on the development of non-natural biocatalytic reactions for the synthesis, modification, and deconstruction of synthetic plastics. The project involves a combination of small molecule synthesis, polymer synthesis, and protein engineering to tackle long-standing selectivity challenges in the field of polymer science. Prior experience in protein engineering and/or gene manipulation is strongly preferred but not required for this position. Applicants wanting to gain training in any of these subfields are implored to apply. This position will be initially awarded for one year, and, contingent upon strong performance and conduct and availability of funds, may be renewed for up to one year. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. To see more, visit, benefits.hr.ufl.edu. Required Qualifications: PhD in chemistry or closely related field. Able to start between January and August 2027. Strong publication record or evidence of research productivity. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Excellent problem-solving, analytical, and critical-thinking abilities. Demonstrated ability to work independently and as a collaborative team. Preferred: Research experience in one or more of the following areas: Polymer synthesis and characterization Organic chemistry and asymmetric synthesis High-throughput experimentation Enzyme discovery, gene manipulation, and protein engineering. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae, (3) a statement on teaching and research goals, and (4) a list of at least three references. After initial review, letters of recommendation will be requested from the references for selected applicants. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 May 2027 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541831/human-resources-intern-jupiter-fl": {
      "title": "Human Resources Intern - Jupiter, FL",
      "description": "Human Resources Intern - Jupiter, FL Apply now Job no: 541831 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Human Resources, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 37023000 - SR-HUMAN RESOURCES Classification Title: OPS Special Projects Classification Minimum Requirements: Must be currently enrolled or recently graduated within the last twelve months from an accredited college or university degree program. Job Description: The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology is seeking a motivated and detail-oriented individual to join our Human Resources team as an HR Intern. This paid internship provides college students and recent graduates with hands-on experience supporting HR operations in a biomedical research and academic environment. The intern will gain exposure to HR processes, employee support, international services, and process improvement projects while working closely with The Wertheim UF Scripps Institute HR team. Responsibilities may include: Supporting HR administrative functions, including recruitment, onboarding, benefits, and records management. Assisting with special projects designed to improve HR processes, workflows, and overall service to The Wertheim UF Scripps community. Supporting The Wertheim UF Scripps International Services with immigration-related HR processes as needed. Collaborating with HR team members to identify opportunities for process improvement. Researching, compiling, and analyzing HR-related information, data, and inquiries. Monitoring and triaging incoming HR emails and routing requests to the appropriate team member. Expected Salary: $20 - $22 per hour. Required Qualifications: Strong verbal and written communication skills. Interest in human resources, higher education, biomedical research administration, or related fields. Ability to maintain confidentiality and handle sensitive HR information with professionalism. Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Strong organizational skills with the ability to prioritize tasks, meet deadlines, and manage time effectively. Attention to detail and willingness to learn new systems and processes. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Preferred: Bachelor's degree in human resources management. Interest in pursuing a career in Human Resources, higher education administration, research administration, or related fields. Experience using Microsoft Excel, Outlook, or other office software. Meticulous attention to detail and ability to follow established processes. Ability to handle confidential information with discretion and professionalism. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, please upload your cover letter and resume. Please provide names and email addresses of three to five references as part of the application process. When ready, the hiring department will contact the listed references via email requesting their reference letters to be uploaded directly to the application website. This is an in-person position located at The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology in Jupiter, Florida. The work schedule is Monday through Friday between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. This position will work up to 20 hours per week , depending on workload, business needs, and the intern’s availability. T his position is time-limited. This position is open until filled, and application review will begin November 3, 2026. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541838/student-financial-aid-coordinator-i-federal-programs": {
      "title": "Student Financial Aid Coordinator I (Federal Programs)",
      "description": "Student Financial Aid Coordinator I (Federal Programs) Apply now Job no: 541838 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Student Services Department: 03060000 - EM-STUDENT FINANCIAL AID ADMIN Classification Title: Student Financial Aid CRD I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Office of Student Financial Aid and Scholarships is currently seeking an organized and detail-oriented individual to join our team as a Financial Aid Coordinator I. In this role, you will be responsible for verifying and awarding financial aid programs, including evaluating loan eligibility for Federal Direct Loan borrowers who are near or at their maximum limits. You will help ensure that students have access to the resources they need to succeed. Key responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Monitoring and Processing Federal Loan Programs Analyzing reports for the Alternative Educational Loan program. Monitoring automated processing of loans. Assisting in manual adjustments to financial aid packages. Communicating with borrowers and lenders to resolve discrepancies as necessary. Evaluating Loans Determining award eligibility based on enrollment status and cost of attendance. Calculating loan awards based on academic level and dependency status. Assessing eligibility for borrowers nearing or at loan maximums. Providing operational support to the Manager in reviewing student awards for eligibility. Other Duties Corresponding with students within Gator360 CRM about required information. Performing additional duties as assigned by the Manager. Expected Salary: $43,888 annually In addition, UF offers the following exceptional benefits: Health, Dental, and Vision Insurance State Retirement Plans Paid Time Off (approximately 22 days of vacation leave, 13 days of sick leave, and 11 paid holidays annually) Tuition Assistance (UF Employee Education Program) Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer For more information on benefits, please visit HR Benefits and Rewards (ufl.edu) Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Prior student financial aid experience Microsoft Office Suite proficiency Excellent organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must upload the following documents to be considered for the position: Cover Letter Resume List of References Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541842/director-and-professor-school-of-natural-resources-and-environment-snre": {
      "title": "Director and Professor - School of Natural Resources and Environment (SNRE)",
      "description": "Director and Professor - School of Natural Resources and Environment (SNRE) Apply now Job no: 541842 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60170000 - AG-SNRE-ACADEMIC PROGRAMS Classification Title: Dir & Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for the rank of professor shall be qualified for the rank of associate professor and, in addition, be a recognized national or international authority or an outstanding teacher in their field or specialization. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the associate professor level. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month tenure-accruing position that will be 80% administration and 20% teaching, available in the School of Natural Resources and Environment (SNRE), College of Agricultural and Life Sciences (CALS), Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS), at the University of Florida. Tenure will be in an IFAS Department most relevant to the selected candidate’s professional area. Though this assignment may change in accordance with the needs of the unit, typical duties will include providing the following: (1) Vision, support and coordination in planning, developing, and implementing SNRE programs in accordance with the Land-Grant Mission (teaching, research, and extension); (2) Leadership in recruiting and fostering academic growth and professional development of faculty, staff, and students; (3) Leadership in building partnerships with other departments and units across the University of Florida and IFAS. (4) Facilitation and management of multiple degree programs (B.A., B.S., M.S., Ph.D.) and affiliate faculty engagement with SNRE students; (5) Supervision and evaluation of program faculty and staff performance; (6) Oversight of the School’s human and financial resources; (7) Promotion of programs across UF, statewide, nationally, and globally; (8) Engagement with alumni and fundraising opportunities; (9) Service as the Graduate Coordinator; and (10) Instruction in courses (Expected to teach at least one class per year in the SNRE curricula). The next Director of SNRE will have the opportunity to build on the School’s strong campus-wide, interdisciplinary, student-focused programs. Opportunities include strengthening collaborations with departments and building new ones for interdisciplinary environmental and ecological scholarship across campus, increasing the visibility of the program, enhancing relationships with external supporters, and expanding the SNRE’s impacts across campus colleges, schools, departments, and off-campus research and education centers across Florida. Other opportunities include leading efforts to grow the online self-funded M.S. program started in 2025, leading efforts to establish new programs (e.g., certificates) and courses as needed, and providing leadership in assessment and development of student recruitment strategies. The successful candidate will engage in scholarly activities related to instruction, including teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, advising and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, seeking funding for the teaching program, supervising undergraduate and graduate research and creative work, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the CALS Honors Program, distance education, and international education. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching, and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The School of Natural Resources and Environment (SNRE) offers campus-wide, interdisciplinary degree programs at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. The school operates horizontally across UF's elaborate structure of academic disciplines. The school has three full-time lecturers and one dually appointed faculty position, which is shared with another academic unit. Inspired by the globally renowned legacy left by UF faculty members H.T. Odum ( 1987 Crafoord Prize in Biosciences winner - Pioneer of Ecosystem Ecology) and C.S. 'Buzz' Holling ( 2008 Volvo Environment Prize winner - Father of Resilience Theory), SNRE offers degrees in Environmental Science (B.S. and B.A.) and Interdisciplinary Ecology (M.S. and Ph.D.). The Interdisciplinary Ecology M.S. degree program is also offered as an online option for place-bound environmental sciences professionals. The student body includes 157 graduate students and 155 undergraduate students. SNRE builds on the University's existing strengths by encouraging select academic departments and other organizational units of the University to participate in SNRE's activities. Currently, more than 350 affiliate faculty from 50 academic departments and 35 UF Research Centers and Institutes in 12 academic colleges participate in the SNRE programs. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in environmental, ecological, agricultural, and/or life sciences, or a closely related discipline is required. Evidence of effective administrative and/or leadership experience, with an emphasis on interdisciplinary work to address major environmental and/or natural resource management challenges. Candidates should have a distinguished record of scholarship and teaching, and/or engagement in natural resources, and demonstrated ability to identify and develop resources and opportunities. Candidates should demonstrate ability to foster interdisciplinary scholarship, education, and collaboration across organizational boundaries. They should also have demonstrated skills in: effective verbal and written communication, and establishing and maintaining professional relationships. Candidates should have experience advising graduate thesis and dissertation research programs and must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system and must also be committed to UF core values. Preferred: Experience in curriculum development and/or management at a land grant institution. Evidence of effective administrative experience in an interdisciplinary program. Record of building and sustaining partnerships. Demonstrated commitment to innovative, rigorous, and student-centered undergraduate and graduate education, and consultative leadership style with demonstrated ability to build consensus. Experience leading undergraduate and graduate teaching programs Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by December 1, 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. Employment Conditions This position is available August 2027, and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541842 P. Chris Wilson Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida Department of Soil, Water, and Ecosystem Sciences 3167 McCarty Hall A POB 110290 Gainesville, FL 32611-0290 Telephone: 352.294.3166 Facsimile: 352.392.3399 Electronic Mail: pcwilson@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Administrative/Teaching Philosophy Statement Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three (3) individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541846/parking-and-traffic-logistics-coordinator": {
      "title": "Parking and Traffic Logistics Coordinator",
      "description": "Parking and Traffic Logistics Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541846 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Transportation Services Department: 68040000 - BS-TRANSPORTATION - PARKING Classification Title: Operations Specialist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Schedules and manages the deployment and retrieval of parking control equipment (e.g., cones, barricades, signage) for events, construction projects, and maintenance activities. Ensures timely closure and reopening of parking spaces and communicates with stakeholders to minimize disruptions. Maintains an inventory of signage and field equipment, ensuring availability and condition for deployment. Maintains a deployment calendar and ensures timely communication with affected departments and vendors. Responds to on-site operational issues, including signage discrepancies, blocked spaces, or vehicle-related concerns. Tracks university capital projects to coordinate parking or transportation impacts with PDC, including relocation of parking spaces and route modifications. Maintains safe and clean parking facilities by reporting hazards, debris, graffiti through work orders. Schedules parking facility closures for maintenance repairs. Reports inconsistencies in signage, parking space allocations and makes recommendations on solutions. Tracks progress of work orders. Audits parking signage across lots and garages to ensure consistency with the digital inventory and space assignments. Coordinates updates, replacements, or removals of signage to reflect current operational needs. Works in conjunction with the ADA office to manage disabled reserved parking requests. Coordinates parking coverage for large-scale events such as concerts, football games, and other campus-wide activities that requires multiple parking and roadway closures. Secures residential and ADA parking spaces during events. Reserves parking spaces for events to include deployment of cones and barricades. Strategically provides blockades with lots and garages that allow for maximum vehicular traffic flow and pedestrian guidance. Relays parking capacities to Police during events. Assists with parking regarding high-profile dignitaries through the request of UF Police. Facilitates parking occupancy counts at the start of each semester and generates a comprehensive report from collected data. Monitors departmental KPIs and recommends continuous improvement measures. Serves as the department’s asset custodian. Supports enforcement operations, facilitates access to gates and investigates reported issues with parking equipment, identifies and implements prompt solutions. Supports other duties as assigned. Valid Class E Driver’s License is required. Criminal Background Check required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 7:30am – 4:30pm, M-F. Some nights and weekends for special events. Classified as Essential Personnel and on-call 24/7. Expected Salary: $60,000.00 to $65,000.00 Annually based on experiene. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience with managing parking and transportation operations for large-scale events with 5,000 or more attendees. Detailed experience with managing parking and transportation operations at a large public university. Knowledge of industry parking software applications. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541848/clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor-comprehensive-otolaryngology": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor – Comprehensive Otolaryngology",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor – Comprehensive Otolaryngology Apply now Job no: 541848 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29210000 - MD-OTOLARYNGOLOGY-HEAD & NECK Classification Title: CLIN AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicant must be board certified or board eligible, and license (or eligible) to practice in Florida. Job Description: The Department of Otolaryngology - Head & Neck Surgery at the University of Florida is seeking applicants interested in pursuing an academic career in Comprehensive Otolaryngology at the rank of Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor. The department has 17 faculty members and 15 residents. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to clinical practice and resident education, with a specialized interest in vestibular disorders. This position will provide patient care by overseeing their individual outpatient clinic. In addition, they will participate in resident education through hands-on clinical teaching, didactic instruction, and formal lectures. Applicants must be board certified or board eligible in Otolaryngology and licensed or eligible for licensure to practice in Florida. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable and will be commensurate with education and experience. Preferred: Fellowship training or significant comparable clinical experience in comprehensive otolaryngology and/or vestibular disorders is preferred. Strong interest in clinical care, teaching and research. Specialized interest in vestibular disorders is preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications should include a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation or contact information for three references. Academic rank and tenure status will be determined based on the candidate's qualifications, experience, and scholarly achievements. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcripts to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541849/biological-scientist-i": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist I",
      "description": "Biological Scientist I Apply now Job no: 541849 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Agricultural Sciences, Agriculture Operations Department: 60740000 - AG-EREC-BELLE GLADE Classification Title: Biological Scientist I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Job Description: Laboratory Conduct molecular experiments to assess plant pathogens in insects and plants. Help conduct insect bioassays and related experiments. Operate, calibrate, and maintain laboratory equipment. Process insect and plant samples, count and identify insects under a microscope. Maintain plant and insect colonies. Maintain chemical inventory and ensure compliance with UF laboratory safety guidelines. Field and Greenhouse Conduct field insect surveys and plant sample collections, coordinating dates and routes. Support greenhouse trials and data collection. Collect and organize field data for subsequent analysis. Operate state approved vehicles to reach external work sites and maintain driving logs. Interpersonal Collaborate with graduate students, student interns, volunteers, OPS, and TEAMS employees to coordinate work activities in labs, greenhouses, and fields. Provide training and guidance to new graduate level researchers and laboratory staff. Participate in hiring panels, conduct interviews, and onboard new hires under PI guidance. Office Source, purchase, and manage procurement of laboratory, greenhouse, and field supplies. Review, edit, and proofread presentations, manuscripts, and grant reports for students and PI. Keep up to date on UF required trainings. Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $22.75 - $25.00 hourly salary; commensurate with skills and experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in biology or a closely related life science field. Valid FL driver's license. Preferred: Master’s degree in entomology, biology or a closely related field. Prior experience in insect bioassays, molecular techniques, conducting field, greenhouse, and laboratory experiments preferred. Good computer skills (word processing, spreadsheet), and motivation to learn new skills are preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541854/academic-program-specialist-ii": {
      "title": "Academic Program Specialist II",
      "description": "Academic Program Specialist II Apply now Job no: 541854 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Student Services Department: 57301000 - HS-HAMILTON SCHOOL ADMIN Classification Title: Academic Program Spec II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Coordination of professional development opportunities for undergraduates. This includes assistance with the process for semester-long internship placement (application and selection); organizing professional development workshops and events each semester; liaising between faculty and students regarding research opportunities; communication to students about prestigious award opportunities, coordinating student outreach initiatives; and measuring and analyzing student outcomes. Facilitate one asynchronous internship class each Fall, Spring, and Summer semester for a total of three classes per academic year. Responsible for assisting with the development and implementation of various study abroad programs including proposal writing. This includes working with Hamilton faculty and UFIC to design, implement and manage these programs, as well as doing advance work to plan the programs, and serve as point-of-contact during the program. Develop and administer program policies and procedures. Responsible for oversight of Hamilton School student organizations. Duties to include providing direct guidance to student leaders regarding school policies and procedures; coordinating faculty advisor timely communication and assistance; and serving as liaison between Hamilton Finance, Events, and Marketing teams and student organization leaders. Established in 2022, the Hamilton School is a multidisciplinary academic unit whose purpose is to support teaching and research concerning the ideas, traditions, and texts that shape Western civilization and American values and institutions. The School offers four undergraduate degrees, five undergraduate minors and two graduate degrees. Applicants are encouraged to visit the Hamilton School website ( Hamilton School ) to learn more about the Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education. UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked the Number 1 public university in the United States by Wall Street Journal in its “2024 Best Colleges in the U.S.” report. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of America, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts centers support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families, and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, maternity/paternity leave and leave payouts. Expected Salary: $55,000 - $65,000 annually Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience working with student program development, experiential education programs, and event management. Strong communication abilities and interpersonal relationship-building skills in a customer-centric focus. Work collaboratively with great attention to detail and precision. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must upload the following documents to be considered for the position: Cover Letter Resume Three References Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540067/motor-vehicle-operator-ii-ops": {
      "title": "Motor Vehicle Operator II (OPS)",
      "description": "Motor Vehicle Operator II (OPS) Apply now Job no: 540067 Work type: Staff Full-Time, Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades Department: 63520500 - FS-OPER EG GROUNDS-REFUSE Classification Title: Motor Vehicle Operator II (OPS) Classification Minimum Requirements: Completion of the ninth grade and one year of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Safely operates large state vehicles in excess of 30,000 lbs. to remove waste and recycling from UF campus. Drives front-load refuse truck and empties contents of dumpsters and carts into the collection truck. Will also drive to some off-campus locations for collection and to disposal or processing sites operated by Alachua County and various private partners. Maintains collection information in electronic format via tablet noting the collection stops as they are completed. A commercial driver’s license (CDL) is required in order to operate a truck of this size and maintaining a valid CDL is a requirement of the position. This includes annual CDL physical as well as CDL required random drug screenings as required by the Department of Transportation (DOT). Functions as part of the Recycling & Solid Waste Management team. Able to communicate effectively to the supervisor as well as other team members on a daily basis. This is a flexible work environment in which the team members fill in for different routes as needed. Operates external equipment such as cart tippers in order to safely collect recyclable materials. Will move and relocate food compost carts, paper recycling carts, bottle & can recycling carts, and other collection containers in order to facilitate collection into the larger vehicle. May also operate smaller carrier truck capable of moving front-load dumpsters and collecting baled cardboard, furniture, and metals as well as other large items. May work campus events to provide recycling collection assistance during normal business hours and non-business hours such as nights and weekends. During daily route on campus, driver will record all collection stops completed and document and report any issues encountered. Driver will immediately phone or radio Supervisor to notify of any situations where immediate attention or action is required. Performs daily pre- and post-trip vehicle inspections and keeps trip logs as required. At the end of the shift cleans cab and washes down collection vehicle. Notes any preventive maintenance or work that needs to be performed on the vehicle that might prevent its safe operation. Reports any concerns, issues, or defects to the Supervisor in a timely manner. Attends departmental meetings and other university related training sessions. Performs related work and general departmental work as required. This position requires a post-offer health assessment as required in order to hold a CDL. Valid CDL Driver’s License Required. This position is covered by the Federal Omnibus Transportation Employee Test Act of 1991. Individuals applying for this position or incumbents of this position are required to hold a valid Class “B” or higher Florida commercial drivers' license as a condition of employment. The Act requires that all such individuals be subject to pre-employment and continuing post-employment drug and alcohol testing. Failure to comply with such testing is grounds for immediate dismissal from employment with the University. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 40 hours per week, 6:00 am to 2:30 pm Monday – Friday is the typical schedule. Adjustments to this schedule may be required by the Supervisor during heavy volumes such as on campus housing move-in and move-out periods. A monthly Saturday rotation is also a part of the normal schedule. During large events such as football games, weekend work may be required. During periods of storm debris collection or other emergencies this position will be classified as essential personnel and may be required to work to facilitate campus clean up. Some overtime may be required. Expected Salary: $21.28 Hourly Required Qualifications: Completion of the ninth grade and one year of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: At least one year of experience driving front-load recycling and solid waste trucks. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Position has been reposted, previous applicants are still under consideration. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 09 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541481/maintenance-generalist-i": {
      "title": "Maintenance Generalist I",
      "description": "Maintenance Generalist I Apply now Job no: 541481 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 63571300 - FS-OPER FACILITIES-MNT MCH Classification Title: Maintenance Generalist I Classification Minimum Requirements: A high school diploma (or equivalent) and zero (0) to two (2) years of experience in the field or in a related area. Job Description: Assists and performs semi-skill and unskilled maintenance and repairs in accordance with industry/trade practices. Provides daily maintenance support to building users through responses to calls for service, scheduled preventative maintenance inspections and similar service. Performs tasks not limited to minor plumbing, electrical, HVAC repairs, changing filters, light bulbs, light carpentry tasks to include replacing ceiling tiles, patching and painting, flooring materials such as VCT, plank, and carpet tiles, stair treads, minor decorative maintenance and other tasks not requiring a licensed skilled trades person or journeyman. Performs scheduled preventative maintenance and unscheduled or special work. Paint walls and/or equipment and make minor painting repairs. Inspect all mechanical/electrical equipment in machine rooms, penthouses, and basements for proper operation. Assists in the operating or testing machinery or equipment to diagnose machine malfunctions. Assists in emergency repairs to pumps, motors, compressors, valves, plumbing pipes and fixtures, and all mechanical components. Enter maintenance data via the work order system for work requests (WR) and time. Provide support services to other trades (e.g., properly route or phase WR) as may be required for the timely and efficient repair of buildings and/or equipment. Obtain parts from Centrals Stores for assigned WR. Clean and maintain shop, work areas, and personal equipment. With oversight, monitor inventory, research and order supplies and parts. Provide support service to other trades, as may be required for timely and efficient repair of buildings and/or equipment. Read, interpret, and work from construction blueprints, renovation, and floor plan drawings. Diagnose mechanical problems and determine how to correct them, checking blueprints, repair manuals, or parts catalogs, as necessary. Promotes safety and environmental protection by immediately reporting unsafe situations, spills, and accidents. Follow University safety plans/procedures/protocols; and participating in appropriate safety training. Assists in building refurbishing to include new ceilings, walls, doorways, masonry work, case work installation and carpentry work. Inspects electric door operators, elevators, and reports any malfunctions to work management. Proof of a State of Florida Class E operator’s license required Criminal Background Check and post offer health assessment is required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 3 PM- 1:30 AM M-Thurs Total hours per week: 40 Emergencies as needed; swing shift, evening shift, weekends, after hours shift, on-call, etc., as required Expected Salary: $16.00 to $17.65 Hourly based on experience Required Qualifications: A high school diploma (or equivalent) and zero (0) to two (2) years of experience in the field or in a related area. Preferred: Experience in multiple trades. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Position reposted, previous applicants still under consideration. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
